E["item:ambercorn"] = {t="item",n="Ambercorn",z="mulgore",s="Across Mulgore, Ambercorn falls beneath the ancient trees. Tauren gather it for the Water of the Seers, a brew used in the Rite of Vision.",h="Across Mulgore, Ambercorn falls beneath the ancient trees.",sec={{t="Gifts of the Canopy",b="Ambercorn falls beneath Mulgore's great trees, where tauren gather it from the grass. To the tauren, these nuts carry the land's spiritual vitality and latent magic. Tauren mystics have understood and used that power for generations. Young hunters and wanderers learn to watch the ground beneath the canopy and honor the Earth Mother's gifts.",sp=0},{t="The Seer's Brew",b="In Bloodhoof Village, seer Zarlman Two-Moons prepares young tauren for the Rite of Vision with ambercorn. He combines it with stones gathered from the plains' water wells to make the Water of the Seers. Initiates drink the ceremonial brew before the ancestral flames. Their senses shift, revealing a guiding spirit animal meant to lead them to their next destination.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is an ambercorn?",a="Ambercorn is a nut that falls from the large, ancient trees of Mulgore. It carries natural magic tied to the spirits of the land.",al={"ambercorn","what are ambercorns"},it="identity"},{q="Where can ambercorn be found?",a="Ambercorn lies on the ground beneath the canopies of Mulgore's mighty trees, especially around Bloodhoof Village.",al={"ambercorn location","where to find ambercorn","how to get ambercorn"},it="place",gp=true},{q="What is ambercorn used for?",a="Tauren seers combine ambercorn with well stones to brew the Water of the Seers. Initiates drink it during the Rite of Vision to reveal spiritual guides.",al={"ambercorn purpose","what does ambercorn do"},it="purpose"},{q="Who asks for ambercorn in Bloodhoof Village?",a="In Bloodhoof Village, seer Zarlman Two-Moons asks initiates to gather ambercorn as part of trials meant to prove their connection to the Earth Mother.",al={"who needs ambercorn","ambercorn quest giver"}},{q="Where does Ambercorn come from, and who made it?",a="Ambercorn is not crafted by mortal hands; it is a natural nut or cone that drops from the mighty trees standing across the plains of Mulgore. The tauren revere these seeds as gifts from the sacred earth, holding inherent magical elements woven directly into the land.",al={"where does ambercorn come from","ambercorn origin","who made ambercorn","where to find ambercorn lore"},it="origin",add="af22a474"},{q="Why does anyone want Ambercorn?",a="Tauren shamans like Zarlman Two-Moons seek Ambercorn for its latent magical properties. When combined with sacred well stones and brewed into the potent Water of the Seers, it induces a spiritual trance before the tribal fire, granting young tauren the visions required to complete the sacred Rite of Vision.",al={"why is ambercorn needed","why collect ambercorn","who wants ambercorn","ambercorn uses"},it="why_wanted",add="c9693aa2"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a young tauren, you learn to recognize ambercorn as an ingredient in the Rite of the Earth Mother. The brew helps reveal your spiritual path.",["class:Shaman"]="As a shaman, you honor natural catalysts like ambercorn. Tauren tradition holds that the Earth Mother places spiritual magic in the harvest of her trees and soil."},kw={"ambercorn","bloodhoof village","mulgore","rite of vision","tauren trial","water of the seers","zarlman two-moons"},rel={"quest:767","item:water-of-the-seers","item:well-stone","npc:zarlman-two-moons","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore"},src={"Ambercorn","Rite of Vision (2)"}}
E["item:battleboar-flank"] = {t="item",n="Battleboar Flank",z="mulgore",s="In Mulgore, young hunters gather Battleboar Flanks from beasts threatening tauren hunting grounds. The meat feeds the tribe's youth.",h="In Mulgore, young hunters gather Battleboar Flanks from beasts threatening tauren hunting grounds.",sec={{t="Sustenance from the Bristleback Threat",b="Bristleback quilboar train battleboars in Brambleblade Ravine to enter tauren hunting grounds. Their attacks threaten the young tauren at Camp Narache. Grull Hawkwind sends novice hunters to take the flanks and snouts from the beasts they kill. The flank meat is cooked into stew for the tribe's youth, feeding them with what the quilboar sent against their people.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is a Battleboar Flank used for?",a="Young hunters at Camp Narache gather Battleboar Flanks at Grull Hawkwind's direction. The meat is cooked into stew for the village's young tauren.",al={"battleboar flank use","what is battleboar flank","why collect battleboar meat"},it="item",gp=true},{q="Where are Battleboar Flanks found?",a="Hunters carve Battleboar Flanks from Battleboars and Bristleback Battleboars near Brambleblade Ravine, east of Camp Narache in Red Cloud Mesa.",al={"battleboar flank source","where do battleboars drop flanks","who drops battleboar flanks"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why are the battleboars encroaching on tauren land?",a="Bristleback quilboar breed and train the battleboars to be hostile. As tensions rise between the Bristlebacks and tauren, the quilboar drive the beasts into traditional tauren hunting grounds to harass the newcomers.",al={"why are battleboars attacking","quilboar boars mulgore","brambleblade battleboars"},it="conflict"},{q="What is a Battleboar Flank?",a="It is a hearty cut of meat harvested from the battleboars that roam the red plains and ravines of Mulgore. Though these fierce swine are bred and trained for war rather than livestock, their meat is wholesome and substantial enough to sustain the tribes when game runs lean.",al={"what is battleboar flank","battleboar flank info","battleboar meat"},it="identity",add="c29b6530"},{q="Where does Battleboar Flank come from?",a="These flanks are carved from battleboars and Bristleback battleboars prowling near Brambleblade Ravine. The creatures are raised and trained to be aggressive by the hostile Bristleback quilboar, turning what would normally be wild game into ferocious territorial beasts.",al={"battleboar flank origin","where do battleboar flanks come from","who has battleboar meat"},it="origin",add="4a352cac"},{q="Why does Grull Hawkwind want Battleboar Flanks?",a="Grull Hawkwind gathers the flanks to feed the tribe's youth at Camp Narache. Cooking the meat into nourishing stew ensures that nothing taken from the hunt is wasted, all while punishing the Bristleback quilboar for driving their aggressive beasts into ancestral tauren hunting grounds.",al={"why collect battleboar flank","why does grull want flanks","who wants battleboar flanks"},it="why_wanted",add="f9e62759"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren, hunting battleboars threatening your homeland teaches you to defend it while respecting and using the Earth Mother's bounty.",["profession:Cooking"]="As a cook, you can boil the flank slowly into a filling communal camp stew."},kw={"battleboar","battleboar flank","brambleblade ravine","bristleback","camp narache","grull hawkwind","mulgore","red cloud mesa"},rel={"quest:780","item:battleboar-snout","npc:grull-hawkwind","subzone:camp-narache","subzone:brambleblade-ravine","topic:quilboar","zone:mulgore"},src={"Battleboar Flank","The Battleboars"}}
E["item:battleboar-snout"] = {t="item",n="Battleboar Snout",z="mulgore",s="Young tauren hunters gather Battleboar Snouts near Red Cloud Mesa as the Bristleback press into their hunting grounds. The snouts also go into stews for the young in Camp Narache.",h="Young tauren hunters gather Battleboar Snouts near Red Cloud Mesa as the Bristleback press into their hunting...",sec={{t="Trophies and Provisions",b="Bristleback quilboar train battleboars in Brambleblade Ravine, in the shadow of Red Cloud Mesa, to probe tauren hunting grounds. Grull Hawkwind sends young braves to push back against the encroachment. A snout proves that a battleboar has been brought down. The tauren use the snouts with cuts of flank in rich stews for their young.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What are Battleboar Snouts used for?",a="Grull Hawkwind asks young hunters in Camp Narache to gather Battleboar Snouts and meat flanks. The snouts prove that threatening boars have been slain, and the tauren cook them into stew for the tribe's youth.",al={"battleboar snout use","why keep snouts","what does grull do with snouts"},it="why_wanted"},{q="Where do these snouts come from?",a="Battleboar Snouts come from Battleboars and Bristleback Battleboars near the fringes of Red Cloud Mesa and throughout Brambleblade Ravine to the east.",al={"where to find battleboars","who drops snouts","battleboar location"},it="origin"},{q="Why are the battleboars encroaching on tauren land?",a="Hostile Bristleback quilboar have trained the battleboars to be exceptionally vicious. The boars push out from Brambleblade Ravine into traditional tauren hunting grounds.",al={"why are boars attacking","quilboar boars","brambleblade battleboars"},it="conflict"},{q="What is a Battleboar Snout?",a="It is the severed snout of a battleboar raised and trained by the Bristleback quilboars near Brambleblade Ravine. Grull Hawkwind and the tauren of Camp Narache value these cuts alongside boars' flanks as hearty ingredients to boil down into stew for the tribe's youth, while simultaneously serving as physical proof that their hunters are culling the encroaching beasts.",al={"battleboar snout","what is battleboar snout","what is this item"},it="identity",add="a852a9b9"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren, gathering the snout with the meat honors the Earthmother's balance. It curbs an encroaching predator and provides food for the young in Camp Narache.",["profession:Cooking"]="As a cook, you can boil tough cuts like snouts slowly over a fire for tribal campfire stews."},kw={"battleboar","battleboar snout","brambleblade ravine","bristleback","camp narache","grull hawkwind","red cloud mesa"},rel={"quest:780","item:battleboar-flank","npc:grull-hawkwind","subzone:camp-narache","subzone:brambleblade-ravine","topic:quilboar","zone:mulgore"},src={"Battleboar Snout","The Battleboars"}}
E["item:bristleback-attack-plans"] = {t="item",n="Bristleback Attack Plans",z="mulgore",s="Bristleback Attack Plans warn of a planned siege on Camp Narache. Quilboar of Brambleblade Ravine drew crude maps and tactical notes for an assault meant to overrun the tauren settlement.",h="Bristleback Attack Plans warn of a planned siege on Camp Narache.",sec={{t="A Plot Against the Mesa",b="The Bristleback quilboar plan a sustained assault on Camp Narache. Their leadership means to push beyond skirmishes along the borders of the Red Cloud Mesa and wipe out the young tauren training there. The plans lie deep within a dirt cave in Brambleblade Ravine. Chief Hawkwind can send word to Bloodhoof Village for reinforcements, giving Camp Narache a chance to blunt the invasion before it begins.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What do the Bristleback Attack Plans contain?",a="The plans contain a tactical map and crude instructions for a siege on Camp Narache, showing that the quilboar are organizing a prolonged assault rather than sporadic raids.",al={"what are the attack plans","what is on the map","bristleback battle plans"},it="identity"},{q="Where are these plans found?",a="The plans lie on the dirt floor of an elevated cave in Brambleblade Ravine, just up a side path from the Bristleback chieftain's hut.",al={"where are the plans","where do i find the map","bristleback attack plans location"},it="origin",gp=true},{q="What does Chief Hawkwind do once he receives the plans?",a="Chief Hawkwind uses the plans to summon aid and warriors from Bloodhoof Village to reinforce Camp Narache's defenses.",al={"what does hawkwind do","camp narache response","chief hawkwind reaction"},sp=1,it="next_step"},{q="Why are the Bristleback attacking Camp Narache?",a="The Bristleback claim the thorny ravines as their territory and see the tauren on the mesa as intruders. They aim to drive the settlement out.",al={"why are quilboar attacking","bristleback motive"},it="motive"},{q="Where did the Bristleback Attack Plans come from?",a="The plans originated deep within Brambleblade Ravine, drafted by the warchief of the Bristleback quilboar. Placed upon a crude map hidden within their cave network, they detail the tribe's coordinated scheme to mount an extended siege against the tauren settlement of Camp Narache.",al={"who made bristleback attack plans","bristleback attack plans origin","who wrote bristleback attack plans"},it="origin",add="166f21b1"},{q="Why does Chief Hawkwind need the Bristleback Attack Plans?",a="Chief Hawkwind needs these plans because they expose the quilboar's scheme to lay siege to Camp Narache. With proof of the looming threat in hand, the chief can immediately call on reinforcements from nearby Bloodhoof Village to prepare defenses and thwart the attack before the settlement is overrun.",al={"why want bristleback attack plans","why does hawkwind want attack plans","bristleback attack plans purpose"},it="why_wanted",add="aea2975f"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren, uncovering the plot helps defend the training grounds where young tauren take their first rites, and protects elders and novices from a quilboar siege.",["faction:Horde"]="As a member of the Horde, thwarting the Bristleback offensive helps keep a secure foothold across Mulgore's sacred plains."},kw={"attack plans","brambleblade ravine","bristleback","camp narache","chief hawkwind","mulgore","quilboar"},rel={"quest:781","subzone:camp-narache","npc:chief-hawkwind","subzone:brambleblade-ravine","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore"},src={"Bristleback Attack Plans","Attack on Camp Narache"}}
E["item:bristleback-belt"] = {t="item",n="Bristleback Belt",z="mulgore",s="In Camp Narache, young tauren gather Bristleback Belts to prove their valor. The belts come from quilboar in Brambleblade Ravine and mark a step in the Rite of Strength.",h="In Camp Narache, young tauren gather Bristleback Belts to prove their valor.",sec={{t="Proof of the Rite",b="The Red Cloud tribe tests youths who seek respect in Thunder Bluff through the ancient Rites of the Earthmother. The first trial, the Rite of Strength, asks each initiate to protect the tribe from threats. Bristleback quilboar raid food supplies and ambush hunting parties outside Camp Narache. Chief Hawkwind demands their belts in return. Bringing him twelve proves an initiate can face danger and help defend the tribe.",sp=0},{t="Quilboar Craft",b="Bristleback warriors and shamans in Brambleblade Ravine wear belts made from rough hide, twine, and scrap. A tauren warrior has little use for one as apparel, but its quilboar make marks it as evidence of a slain enemy. Bringing the belts back to the camp circle fulfills the rite's proof.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What are Bristleback Belts used for?",a="Chief Hawkwind in Camp Narache asks for twelve Bristleback Belts as proof that an initiate has completed the Rite of Strength, the first of the traditional Rites of the Earthmother.",al={"why do i need bristleback belt","bristleback belt purpose","what is this belt for"},it="purpose",gp=true},{q="Where do Bristleback Belts come from?",a="Bristleback quilboar and shamans in Brambleblade Ravine, just east of Camp Narache in the Red Cloud Mesa, wear the belts.",al={"where to get bristleback belt","who drops bristleback belts","bristleback belt location"},it="origin",gp=true},{q="Why does Chief Hawkwind want belts instead of heads or weapons?",a="The belts provide portable proof that an initiate has slain enemies of the tribe. The rite calls for belts but does not explain why they were chosen over other trophies.",al={"why belts","hawkwind proof"},it="why_wanted"},{q="Why are the Bristlebacks being targeted?",a="Bristlebacks have encroached on tribal land from Brambleblade Ravine. They raid Camp Narache by night and ambush tauren hunting parties across the mesa.",al={"why kill bristlebacks","quilboar threat mulgore"},it="conflict"},{q="What is a Bristleback Belt?",a="A Bristleback Belt is a simple piece of raiment worn by the quilboar dwelling in Brambleblade Ravine. Stripped from fallen raiders, these rough leather or corded girdles serve as undeniable trophies of battle, proving to tribal elders that an encroaching threat has been confronted and slain.",al={"bristleback belt","what is bristleback belt","bristleback belt item"},it="identity",add="69faf7fa"},{q="Where does a Bristleback Belt come from, and who makes it?",a="These belts are fashioned by the Bristleback quilboar who have established a thorn-choked settlement within Brambleblade Ravine, just east of Camp Narache. Both their frontline warriors and shamans wear them into skirmishes against the tauren across the Red Cloud Mesa.",al={"where do bristleback belts come from","who makes bristleback belts","bristleback belt origin"},it="origin",add="d50579da"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren youth on the Red Cloud Mesa, taking a fallen Bristleback's belt marks your first step toward recognition by the elders in Thunder Bluff.",["faction:Horde"]="As a member of the Horde, curbing Mulgore's aggressive quilboar helps secure the southern borders of the Horde's heartland in central Kalimdor."},kw={"belt","brambleblade ravine","bristleback","camp narache","chief hawkwind","quilboar","red cloud mesa","rite of strength"},rel={"quest:757","npc:chief-hawkwind","subzone:camp-narache","subzone:brambleblade-ravine","quest:763","topic:tauren","topic:quilboar","zone:mulgore"},src={"Bristleback Belt","Rite of Strength (Classic)"}}
E["item:broken-tools"] = {t="item",n="Broken Tools",z="mulgore",s="At the dig northwest of Bloodhoof Village, dwarven picks lie broken on the forge. Baine Bloodhoof wants proof that the excavation threatening Mulgore's land and wildlife has been stopped.",h="At the dig northwest of Bloodhoof Village, dwarven picks lie broken on the forge.",sec={{t="Sabotaging the Dig",b="Northwest of Bloodhoof Village, dwarven excavators dig into Mulgore's hills in search of ancient history. The tauren see their work as a defilement of the Earth Mother. The digging's noise and destruction agitate the local wildlife, especially the roaming kodo herds. Young Baine Bloodhoof orders defenders to take the prospectors' iron picks and break them at a forge. With their picks ruined, the expedition cannot gouge further into the earth.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What are these Broken Tools?",a="Broken Tools are the ruined heads and splintered shafts of Prospector's Picks, taken from the dwarven excavators in Mulgore and crushed at a forge.",al={"broken tools","what are broken tools","shattered picks"},it="identity"},{q="Why does Baine Bloodhoof want them destroyed?",a="Baine believes digging into the soil defiles the land and angers Mulgore's wildlife, especially the kodos. Breaking the picks stops the excavation and warns that the land will not yield its wisdom to reckless digging.",al={"why destroy picks","why baine want broken tools","baine dwarven digging"},it="why_wanted"},{q="Where can these picks be broken?",a="You can break the picks at any working forge. One stands at the dwarven dig site, and others can be found in Bloodhoof Village or Thunder Bluff.",al={"how to break picks","where is forge","break prospector pick"},gp=true},{q="Why are dwarves digging in Mulgore?",a="The dwarves believe Mulgore's soil holds deep secrets and relics of antiquity. Searching for their origins, they hollow out foreign lands without regard for the people who live there.",al={"why dwarves in mulgore","dwarf digsite mulgore"},it="motive"},{q="Where do these broken tools come from?",a="These mangled implements were originally sturdy mining picks forged and carried by dwarven prospectors. Seeking titan relics and ancient secrets beneath Mulgore, the expedition brought their iron tools to tear into the earth to the northwest. By hauling those intact picks to a forge and smashing them to pieces, you reduce their mining gear to unusable scrap before returning it to the tauren as proof the excavation has been halted.",al={"broken tools origin","who made broken tools","where did broken tools come from"},it="origin",add="76aab525"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren, bringing ruined tools to Baine helps defend the Earth Mother from digging that torments the native beasts.",["profession:Blacksmithing"]="As a blacksmith, you can turn your forge and anvil against dwarven ironwork to defend the land."},kw={"baine bloodhoof","broken tools","dig site","dwarven digging","forge","mulgore","prospector's pick"},rel={"quest:746","npc:baine-bloodhoof","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore","zone:thunderbluff"},src={"Broken Tools","Dwarven Digging"}}
E["item:chief-sharptusk-thornmantle-s-head"] = {t="item",n="Chief Sharptusk Thornmantle's Head",z="mulgore",s="Chief Sharptusk Thornmantle's Head marks the end of a threat to Red Cloud Mesa. The Bristleback leader threatened the young tauren braves, and his head was recovered from deep in Brambleblade Ravine as proof of victory.",h="Chief Sharptusk Thornmantle's Head marks the end of a threat to Red Cloud Mesa.",sec={{t="Trophy of the Hunt",b="The Bristleback quilboar of Brambleblade Ravine have attacked the tribes of Camp Narache. Their chieftain, Sharptusk Thornmantle, urges them on. The tauren seek to protect their homeland in Mulgore with vigilance and strength. Sharptusk's head proves he is dead and warns the surviving quilboar clans against encroaching on the mesa borders.",sp=0},{t="A Lesson in the Hunt",b="Brave Windfeather demands Sharptusk's head as a rite of passage for young hunters and tribal defenders. Facing an enemy clever enough to strike back and lead a war band tests a young combatant's martial skill. Presenting the head fulfills that trial and earns the young fighter a reputation among the braves of Red Cloud Mesa.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why does Brave Windfeather want the head of Sharptusk Thornmantle?",a="Brave Windfeather wants proof that the Bristleback chieftain has fallen. Sharptusk can no longer direct raids against Camp Narache, and the head warns the remaining quilboar that their aggression will meet decisive force.",al={"why take his head","reason for sharptusk head","windfeather head request"},it="why_wanted"},{q="Who was Chief Sharptusk Thornmantle?",a="Chief Sharptusk Thornmantle led the Bristleback quilboar in Brambleblade Ravine, on Red Cloud Mesa's eastern edge. Under his command, the Bristlebacks harassed and attacked young tauren braves.",al={"who is sharptusk","sharptusk thornmantle lore","quilboar chief mulgore"},it="identity"},{q="Where is this trophy obtained?",a="You obtain the head by killing Chief Sharptusk Thornmantle in his makeshift tribal village, deep inside Brambleblade Ravine east of Camp Narache.",al={"where do i find the head","sharptusk head location"},it="origin",gp=true},{q="Where does Chief Sharptusk Thornmantle's Head come from?",a="This grim trophy is taken directly from the chieftain of the Bristleback quilboar who nest within Brambleblade Ravine, just east of Camp Narache. Sharptusk Thornmantle rallied his makeshift settlement against the tauren of Red Cloud Mesa, prompting the Braves to demand his severed head as undeniable proof that the Bristleback war effort has been broken.",al={"sharptusk head origin","where did sharptusk head come from","who has sharptusk head"},it="origin",add="27fbe344"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren, claiming a formidable enemy's head marks an important step for a young defender. It proves you have the resolve and skill to protect your people's new home.",["class:Hunter"]="As a hunter, Sharptusk is prey that can hunt back. Tracking and striking such an intelligent foe tests your skill."},kw={"brambleblade","bristleback","head","mulgore","sharptusk","thornmantle","windfeather"},rel={"quest:3376","subzone:brambleblade-ravine","topic:quilboar","topic:tauren","zone:mulgore"},src={"Chief Sharptusk Thornmantle's Head","Break Sharptusk!"}}
E["item:cougar-claws"] = {t="item",n="Cougar Claws",z="mulgore",s="Across Mulgore’s central plains, tauren hunters gather Cougar Claws for a cleansing ritual. The claws come from flatland cougars, among the grasslands’ apex hunters.",h="Across Mulgore’s central plains, tauren hunters gather Cougar Claws for a cleansing ritual.",sec={{t="A Vessel of Natural Balance",b="Flatland cougars rank among the apex hunters of Mulgore’s grasslands, stalking prey by instinct. For the tauren, taking their claws is neither casual slaughter nor trophy hunting; it gathers the beast’s natural spirit. When goblin operations defile the plains’ ancestral waters, Mull Thunderhorn asks young hunters to collect Cougar Claws alongside prairie stalker claws. The tauren believe native beasts’ pure predatory instincts can be woven into a ritual totem. They mean to use it to scour unnatural toxins and foul sorcery from the poisoned Thunderhorn Water Well.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What are Cougar Claws used for?",a="Mull Thunderhorn uses Cougar Claws with stalker claws to craft the Thunderhorn Cleansing Totem. The tauren weave the spirit of native predators into ritual objects to purify wells poisoned by goblin industry.",al={"cougar claws use","why get cougar claws","cougar claws purpose"},it="purpose"},{q="Where do Cougar Claws come from?",a="Flatland cougars prowl Mulgore’s rolling plains, especially east and west of Bloodhoof Village, and provide Cougar Claws.",al={"where to get cougar claws","cougar claw source","who drops cougar claws"},it="origin"},{q="Why does Mull Thunderhorn need predator claws to cleanse a well?",a="Tauren shamanic and druidic traditions honor the purity of wild beasts’ hunting. Native predators live in balance with the land, unlike goblins who exploit and defile it, so their essence can counter artificial corruption.",al={"why predator claws","mull claws reason","totem predator claws"},it="why_wanted"},{q="Are Cougar Claws useful for anything else?",a="Beyond crafting the ritual totem for the Thunderhorn Water Well, Cougar Claws have no specialized use. They are typically sold to local traders.",al={"other uses cougar claws","sell cougar claws"},gp=true},{q="What are Cougar Claws?",a="These are the natural talons taken from the paws of flatland cougars stalking the plains of Mulgore. Rather than mere trophies, these claws are viewed by tauren shamans as emblems of the natural hunt, embodying the pure and untainted balance of the beasts that prowl the wilderness.",al={"what is cougar claws","cougar claws item lore","what are these claws"},it="identity",add="ad1b83a1"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Gathering Cougar Claws teaches young tauren that hunting serves balance and spiritual need. That stands against the pollution caused by goblin enterprises.",["class:Shaman"]="As a shaman, you know local predators’ essence remains tied to the spirits of the plains. It can serve as a conduit for purifying wards and cleansing totems."},kw={"cleansing totem","cougar claws","flatland cougar","mulgore","mull thunderhorn","thunderhorn totem"},rel={"quest:756","item:thunderhorn-cleansing-totem","npc:mull-thunderhorn","item:stalker-claws","subzone:thunderhorn-water-well","quest:758","zone:mulgore","topic:goblin"},src={"Cougar Claws","Thunderhorn Totem (Classic)"}}
E["item:etched-note"] = {t="item",n="Etched Note",z="mulgore",s="At Camp Narache, the Etched Note calls a young tauren hunter to begin training. Lanka Farshot, the camp's hunter trainer, welcomes the novice to the traditions of the hunt.",h="At Camp Narache, the Etched Note calls a young tauren hunter to begin training.",sec={{t="A Call to the Hunt",b="At Camp Narache, a runner brings young shu'halo a message from Lanka Farshot, the camp's hunter trainer. The note names the hunter the heart of the tribe. It also warns that natural instinct needs rigorous instruction and deep respect for Mulgore's wilderness.",sp=0},{t="Traditions of the Shu'halo",b="For the tauren, hunting is a sacred discipline and a way of survival, woven into tribal life. Hunters join the work of warriors, shamans and druids, and the tribe looks to them for guidance and protection. The student presents the note to Lanka to begin learning weaponcraft and how to understand beasts, preparing to defend and sustain the tribes.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of the Etched Note?",a="The Etched Note calls a novice tauren hunter to present themselves to Lanka Farshot at Camp Narache. There, formal training begins in marksmanship and beast lore.",al={"what does the note do","etched note use","why did i get etched note"},it="purpose"},{q="Who wrote this note?",a="Lanka Farshot, the hunter trainer at Camp Narache, wrote the note. He sends couriers across the mesa to find young hunters ready to learn.",al={"who sent etched note","who wrote it","etched note author"},it="origin"},{q="Why is hunting so important to the tauren?",a="Hunting sustains tauren survival, spirituality and tribal tradition. Skilled hunters provide food and materials while living in balance with the land and honoring the spirits of the beasts they fell.",al={"tauren hunter lore","why do tauren hunt","hunting in tauren culture"}},{q="Where should this note be brought?",a="Bring the Etched Note to Lanka Farshot near the pavilion and training grounds at Camp Narache on the Red Cloud Mesa.",al={"where do i take the note","who do i turn this in to","etched note turn in"},it="next_step",gp=true},{q="What is the Etched Note?",a="The Etched Note is a written summons directed specifically to novice tauren hunters arriving at Camp Narache. Carved with a brief message from the camp's hunter trainer, Lanka Farshot, it reminds you that hunters are the heart of the tribe and bids you to seek him out in Camp Narache to begin mastering the art of the hunt.",al={"what is etched note","etched note item","etched note mulgore"},it="identity",add="10b0f74a"},{q="Why does Lanka Farshot want you to bring him the Etched Note?",a="Lanka Farshot has the note sent to bring promising young hunters directly under his wing. He intends to test your readiness and ground you in the vital traditions of the hunt—from handling ranged weaponry to taming beasts—ensuring you can serve and protect the tribe across the plains of Mulgore.",al={"why deliver etched note","why does lanka want etched note","etched note purpose"},it="why_wanted",add="bd70af04"}},ang={["class:Hunter"]="As a hunter, the note marks your formal entry into the hunter tradition. Lanka's lessons teach you to handle ranged weapons and commune with the beasts of the plains.",["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren, you carry the expectations of your elders and bloodline. The note calls you to master Mulgore's rolling grasslands before traveling to distant lands."},kw={"camp narache","etched note","lanka farshot","mulgore","red cloud mesa","tauren hunter","trainer summons"},rel={"quest:3092","npc:lanka-farshot","subzone:camp-narache","topic:hunter","zone:mulgore"},src={"Etched Note","Etched Note (quest)"}}
E["item:flatland-prowler-claw"] = {t="item",n="Flatland Prowler Claw",z="mulgore",s="Across Mulgore's plains, Flatland Prowler Claws mark a young hunter's trial. Hunters gather them to prove their resolve and fieldcraft to the elders of Thunder Bluff.",h="Across Mulgore's plains, Flatland Prowler Claws mark a young hunter's trial.",sec={{t="A Hunter's Proof",b="Flatland prowlers stalk Mulgore's plains, quick to ambush prey in tall grass. Their cunning and territorial nature makes them dangerous to travelers and a hard test for young hunters. Gathering their claws is a recognized tradition. Skorn Whitecloud sends promising wanderers to bring the claws to Melor Stonehoof at Hunter Rise in Thunder Bluff, proof that they have the resolve and fieldcraft to follow the hunter's way.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What are Flatland Prowler Claws used for?",a="Young hunters bring four Flatland Prowler Claws to Melor Stonehoof at Hunter Rise in Thunder Bluff. The claws prove their skill and courage, and their readiness to follow the hunter's path.",al={"prowler claw use","why get prowler claws","hunter's way claws"},it="item",gp=true},{q="Who asks you to collect them?",a="Skorn Whitecloud asks promising trackers to gather claws from Mulgore's prowlers before presenting themselves to Melor Stonehoof.",al={"who wants prowler claws","quest giver prowler claws"},gp=true},{q="Why are flatland prowlers considered a fitting hunt?",a="Flatland prowlers are tough, stealthy carnivores native to Mulgore's plains. Tracking and defeating these alert predators tests the patience and combat instincts tauren tradition asks of a hunter.",al={"why flatland prowlers","are prowlers dangerous"}},{q="What is a Flatland Prowler Claw?",a="It is a claw harvested from a flatland prowler, one of the predatory wild cats roaming the grassy plains of Mulgore. Among tauren hunters, presenting these claws serves as tangible proof of a young hunter's cunning, patience, and emerging tracking prowess.",al={"flatland prowler claw","what is prowler claw"},it="identity",add="cc11ac90"},{q="Where do Flatland Prowler Claws come from?",a="These claws come directly from the predatory flatland prowlers that stalk the windswept grasslands of Mulgore. Rather than being crafted or manufactured, they must be cleanly harvested by a hunter after tracking and defeating the agile beasts in the wild.",al={"where to get prowler claws","how are prowler claws obtained"},it="origin",add="a7640831"},{q="Why does anyone want Flatland Prowler Claws?",a="Skorn Whitecloud directs aspiring wanderers to gather these claws to prove their mettle to Melor Stonehoof atop the Hunter's Rise in Thunder Bluff. Hunting is one of the highest honors in tauren culture, and offering the claws of such tough, cunning predators demonstrates that you possess the burgeoning skill and spirit required to walk the hunter's path.",al={"why collect prowler claws","who needs prowler claws","why does melor stonehoof want claws"},it="why_wanted",add="b4c8d82b"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren, you honor the hunt as a spiritual tradition. Presenting the claws of a cunning plains predator marks your first steps toward serving your tribe as a tracker.",["class:Hunter"]="As a hunter, you test your field awareness against stealthy prowlers. Their claws mark earned respect among the marksmen at Hunter Rise in Thunder Bluff."},kw={"flatland prowler","flatland prowler claw","hunter rise","melor stonehoof","mulgore","skorn whitecloud","the hunter's way","thunder bluff"},rel={"quest:861","npc:melor-stonehoof","npc:skorn-whitecloud","zone:mulgore","zone:thunderbluff","topic:tauren","topic:hunter"},src={"Flatland Prowler Claw","The Hunter's Way (Classic)"}}
E["item:plainstrider-feather"] = {t="item",n="Plainstrider Feather",z="mulgore",s="On Red Cloud Mesa, young hunters gather Plainstrider Feather from the birds roaming Mulgore’s plains. Camp Narache uses them for clothing and tribal adornments, honoring the creatures that provide for the village.",h="On Red Cloud Mesa, young hunters gather Plainstrider Feather from the birds roaming Mulgore’s plains.",sec={{t="A Hunter's First Offering",b="Young tauren on Red Cloud Mesa learn to track and hunt for what the tribe needs. Plainstriders roam the grassy plateau, providing food and useful feathers. Camp Narache makes garments and gear from their plumage. Young hunters bring feathers and fresh meat to Grull Hawkwind to meet the camp’s needs. The offering is their first test before Chief Hawkwind recognizes them and welcomes them in Thunder Bluff.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What are Plainstrider Feathers used for?",a="Camp Narache uses Plainstrider Feathers to make clothing and ceremonial items. Grull Hawkwind gathers them from young hunters to replenish tribal supplies.",al={"feather purpose","plainstrider feather use","why collect feathers"},it="item"},{q="Who gathers Plainstrider Feathers on Red Cloud Mesa?",a="Young hunters gather Plainstrider Feathers on Red Cloud Mesa at Grull Hawkwind’s request. He replenishes Camp Narache’s stores and tests their discipline in providing for the tribe.",al={"who wants feathers","who needs feathers","grull hawkwind feathers"},it="people"},{q="Where can plainstriders be found?",a="Plainstriders roam the open grasslands of Red Cloud Mesa and the rolling plains of Mulgore.",al={"where to find plainstriders","plainstrider location","feather farm"},it="origin",gp=true},{q="Why does Grull Hawkwind require these feathers for tribal rites?",a="Hunting provides for survival and sport in tauren culture. Bringing meat and plumage back to Chief Hawkwind shows that a young hunter can sustain the tribe before going on to Thunder Bluff.",al={"the hunt begins lore","proving hunt tauren","why hunt plainstriders"},it="why_wanted"},{q="What is a Plainstrider Feather?",a="A Plainstrider Feather is plumage gathered from the large, flightless birds that wander the grasslands of Mulgore. The tauren of Red Cloud Mesa utilize every harvest from the land with deep respect, and these sturdy, colorful plumes serve as practical materials for crafting everyday garments as well as traditional adornments.",al={"what is plainstrider feather","plainstrider feather info","plainstrider feathers"},it="identity",add="f2ff74a3"},{q="Where do Plainstrider Feathers come from?",a="These feathers are naturally grown plumage harvested directly from the wild plainstriders roaming Red Cloud Mesa and the broader plains of Mulgore. They are not crafted by mortal hands, but instead gathered by hunters after a successful hunt upon the open prairie.",al={"plainstrider feather source","where to get plainstrider feathers","who made plainstrider feather"},it="origin",add="8bb68bd8"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a young tauren, gathering a plainstrider’s feathers teaches you to value the Earth Mother’s creatures and use each part of the kill for food and clothing.",["class:Hunter"]="As a hunter, stalking swift plainstriders across Red Cloud Mesa tests your patience and tracking, and teaches you to honor your quarry."},kw={"camp narache","feather","grull hawkwind","mulgore","plainstrider","red cloud mesa","the hunt begins"},rel={"quest:747","item:plainstrider-meat","npc:grull-hawkwind","subzone:camp-narache","zone:mulgore","npc:chief-hawkwind","topic:tauren","zone:thunderbluff"},src={"Plainstrider Feather","The Hunt Begins"}}
E["item:plainstrider-meat"] = {t="item",n="Plainstrider Meat",z="mulgore",s="Across Red Cloud Mesa, Plainstrider Meat helps feed Camp Narache. Young tauren hunt the birds for food and feathers, proving their skill and self-reliance.",h="Across Red Cloud Mesa, Plainstrider Meat helps feed Camp Narache.",sec={{t="Sustenance for the Mesa",b="Camp Narache on Red Cloud Mesa depends on game from the surrounding grasslands. Plainstrider meat helps feed the village when food stocks run low. The birds also provide feathers for clothing and ceremonial adornments. Tauren life depends on the land, and survival means taking what is needed while respecting nature.",sp=0},{t="A Young Hunter's Trial",b="Grull Hawkwind sends young hunters across Red Cloud Mesa to track the swift plainstriders. They gather meat and feathers to prove their tracking skill and self-reliance. Then they appear before Grull's father, Chief Hawkwind.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Plainstrider Meat used for?",a="Young hunters gather Plainstrider Meat on Red Cloud Mesa to replenish Camp Narache's food stores for Grull Hawkwind.",al={"why plainstrider meat","plainstrider meat quest","what do i do with plainstrider meat"},it="item",gp=true},{q="Who asks for this meat?",a="Grull Hawkwind asks young hunters to hunt plainstriders and replenish the tribe's food supplies while proving their worth as providers.",al={"who wants plainstrider meat","grull hawkwind meat"},it="why_wanted"},{q="Where can Plainstrider Meat be found?",a="Plainstriders roam Mulgore's grasslands, including Red Cloud Mesa, where their meat is gathered.",al={"where to get plainstrider meat","plainstrider locations","farm plainstrider meat"},it="origin",gp=true},{q="Why is hunting plainstriders considered important to the tauren?",a="Tauren hunt plainstriders for food and as a spiritual discipline. Bringing game to camp shows respect for the Earth Mother, self-reliance, and the ability to sustain the tribe.",al={"tauren hunting culture","why hunt plainstriders"},it="motive"},{q="What is Plainstrider Meat?",a="Plainstrider Meat is fresh game harvested from the flightless birds wandering the plains of Mulgore. For the tauren of Red Cloud Mesa, it serves as a vital staple food, gathered through traditional hunts that sustain the settlement and honor the bond between hunter and prey.",al={"plainstrider meat info","what is plainstrider meat","plainstrider meat lore"},it="identity",add="f404715e",nx={6}},{q="Where does Plainstrider Meat come from?",a="Plainstrider Meat comes from the wild plainstriders native to Mulgore. Young hunters harvest it directly from the beasts roaming Red Cloud Mesa during their first hunting rites for the tribe.",al={"where does plainstrider meat come from","plainstrider meat origin","how to get plainstrider meat"},it="origin",add="00c8b7f8"},{q="Why is Plainstrider Meat wanted in Camp Narache?",a="Camp Narache's stores of food have run low, and the tribe relies on keen hunting to replenish its supplies. Grull Hawkwind asks young hunters to collect Plainstrider Meat to feed the village and prove their skill to Chief Hawkwind.",al={"why need plainstrider meat","plainstrider meat purpose","why collect plainstrider meat"},it="why_wanted",add="f1043112"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren youth on Red Cloud Mesa, hunting plainstriders is an early step toward earning Chief Hawkwind's respect and the right to walk into Thunder Bluff.",["class:Hunter"]="As a hunter, you can test your tracking and marksmanship against swift plainstriders across Mulgore's open plains."},kw={"camp narache","chief hawkwind","grull hawkwind","mulgore","plainstrider","plainstrider meat","red cloud mesa","the hunt begins"},rel={"quest:747","item:plainstrider-feather","npc:grull-hawkwind","subzone:camp-narache","npc:chief-hawkwind","topic:tauren","zone:mulgore","zone:thunderbluff"},src={"Plainstrider Meat","The Hunt Begins"}}
E["item:plainstrider-talon"] = {t="item",n="Plainstrider Talon",z="mulgore",s="Across Mulgore, tauren shamans gather Plainstrider Talon for cleansing totems. The sharp claws come from large, flightless plainstriders native to the region, and help cleanse corrupted water.",h="Across Mulgore, tauren shamans gather Plainstrider Talon for cleansing totems.",sec={{t="Bonds of the Plains",b="Large, flightless plainstriders roam Mulgore’s open grasses, using powerful legs and sharp claws to forage through tough prairie earth and defend against predators. To the tauren of Bloodhoof Village, every part of a hunted beast carries spiritual resonance. When goblin ventures tainted sacred sources such as the Winterhoof Water Well, shaman Mull Thunderhorn sought plainstrider talons alongside prairie wolf paws. In tauren ritual crafting, reagents from native predators and foragers channel the land’s spiritual essence into totems that can purge unnatural poisons.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What creatures drop a Plainstrider Talon?",a="Adult plainstriders, elder plainstriders, and the notorious strider Mazzranache yield Plainstrider Talons across Mulgore’s grasslands.",al={"who drops plainstrider talon","where to get plainstrider talons","plainstrider talon source"},it="origin",gp=true},{q="What are Plainstrider Talons used for?",a="Mull Thunderhorn in Bloodhoof Village needs plainstrider talons and prairie wolf paws to craft a cleansing totem for purifying the corrupted Winterhoof Water Well.",al={"plainstrider talon use","why do i need talons","plainstrider talon quest"},it="why_wanted",gp=true},{q="Why does Mull Thunderhorn need animal parts to clean the water?",a="Tauren shamanism relies on harmony with Mulgore’s natural spirits. Binding local wildlife into a ritual totem calls on the land’s raw power to fight unnatural pollutants introduced by Venture Company goblins.",al={"why animal paws and claws","shaman cleansing ritual talons"},it="motive"},{q="Who poisoned the water wells in Mulgore?",a="Agents of the Venture Company operating in Mulgore, along with their lackeys, tainted the sacred tribal water wells. Their acts disrupt the local environment and threaten the tauren tribes.",al={"who tainted the wells","who poisoned mulgore water","poison water culprit"},it="threat"},{q="What is a Plainstrider Talon?",a="A Plainstrider Talon is the sharp claw harvested from the large, flightless plainstriders roaming Mulgore. While naturally used by these swift birds for defense and foraging across the plains, the talons also carry an innate connection to the natural world. To the tauren, such materials represent the land's strength and can be channeled through shamanic ritual.",al={"what is plainstrider talon","plainstrider talons lore","plainstrider claws"},it="identity",add="fdecb439"},{q="Where do Plainstrider Talons come from?",a="These talons are taken from the mature plainstriders that roam the open plains of Mulgore, southwest of Bloodhoof Village. Hunters gather them from adult and elder plainstriders, or even rare beasts like Mazzranache, as offerings of the land's bounty.",al={"plainstrider talon source","where to get plainstrider talons","plainstrider talon origin"},it="origin",add="07c33870"},{q="Why does Mull Thunderhorn want Plainstrider Talons?",a="Mull Thunderhorn in Bloodhoof Village requires these talons to craft a cleansing totem for the Winterhoof Water Well. Goblins and their Venture Company lackeys have tainted the sacred water, and Mull must call upon the primal essence of Mulgore's native beasts to counteract the poison and restore purity to the well.",al={"why does mull want talons","why need plainstrider talon","plainstrider talon purpose"},it="why_wanted",add="826885d3"}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="As a shaman, you can see how tauren rituals use beasts’ physical parts, such as talons, to make purifying totems and draw power from the land’s native spirits.",["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren, you honor the sacrifice of Mulgore’s wildlife through the hunt. Every beast taken helps heal the Earth Mother when goblin exploitation strikes."},kw={"bloodhoof village","cleansing totem","mulgore","mull thunderhorn","plainstrider","plainstrider talon","poison water","winterhoof water well"},rel={"quest:748","npc:mull-thunderhorn","item:prairie-wolf-paw","subzone:winterhoof-water-well","quest:766","zone:mulgore","topic:goblin"},src={"Plainstrider Talon","Poison Water (Classic)"}}
E["item:prairie-alpha-tooth"] = {t="item",n="Prairie Alpha Tooth",z="mulgore",s="Around Thunder Bluff, prairie wolf alphas roam Mulgore’s open plains. Mull Thunderhorn needs their teeth to cleanse the Wildmane Well.",h="Around Thunder Bluff, prairie wolf alphas roam Mulgore’s open plains.",sec={{t="A Cleansing Offering",b="Mulgore’s wells sustain the tauren, and keeping them pure calls for rites of respect and sacrifice. Mull Thunderhorn of Bloodhoof Village needs teeth from the prairie wolf alphas that prowl beneath Thunder Bluff to cleanse the Wildmane Well. These fierce pack leaders embody the strength of the wild. Their teeth honor the balance between hunter, prey, and the spirits of the earth.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is a Prairie Alpha Tooth used for?",a="Mull Thunderhorn uses eight prairie alpha teeth in a ritual to cleanse the Wildmane Well, the final contaminated well near Bloodhoof Village.",al={"alpha tooth purpose","what are wolf teeth for mulgore","wildmane totem item"},it="why_wanted"},{q="Where do Prairie Wolf Alphas roam?",a="Prairie wolf alphas roam the open grassy plains of central and northern Mulgore, especially around the bluffs at the base of Thunder Bluff.",al={"where are prairie wolf alphas","alpha wolf locations","prairie wolf alpha spots"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why does a water cleansing ritual require wolf teeth?",a="Tauren rites honor nature’s balance. The cleansing calls for sacred parts taken from Mulgore’s fierce native beasts, symbols of sacrifice and the vitality of its wildlife.",al={"why wolf teeth for water","mull thunderhorn tooth ritual lore"},it="purpose"},{q="What is a Prairie Alpha Tooth?",a="A Prairie Alpha Tooth is a tooth taken from a prairie wolf alpha, one of the fiercest pack leaders prowling the plains of Mulgore. Among the tauren, these fangs represent the strength and spirit of the land's apex predators, carrying spiritual weight when offered in ritual.",al={"prairie alpha tooth","what is prairie alpha tooth","prairie wolf alpha tooth"},it="identity",add="f1f3b80c"},{q="Where does a Prairie Alpha Tooth come from?",a="These teeth are harvested from prairie wolf alphas that roam the grasslands of Mulgore, particularly near the base of Thunder Bluff to the north. Being natural fangs of living predators, they are taken directly from the beasts during the hunt rather than crafted by mortal hands.",al={"where do prairie alpha teeth come from","prairie alpha tooth origin","who drops prairie alpha tooth"},it="origin",add="0674aadd"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren, taking a prairie alpha’s fang honors the hunter’s sacred duty to gather what is needed with respect and return that primal essence to heal the poisoned earth.",["class:Shaman"]="As a shaman, you appease the spirits of the wells through earthly balance. Offering an apex predator’s fangs anchors that natural harmony."},kw={"bloodhoof village","mulgore","mull thunderhorn","prairie alpha tooth","prairie wolf alpha","water well","wildmane totem"},rel={"quest:759","npc:mull-thunderhorn","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:thunderbluff","zone:mulgore"},src={"Prairie Alpha Tooth","Wildmane Totem (Classic)"}}
E["item:prairie-wolf-paw"] = {t="item",n="Prairie Wolf Paw",z="mulgore",s="Across Mulgore, Prairie Wolf Paws are gathered from the wolves that hunt the plains. Mull Thunderhorn in Bloodhoof Village uses them with plainstrider talons to make a cleansing totem.",h="Across Mulgore, Prairie Wolf Paws are gathered from the wolves that hunt the plains.",sec={{t="Natural Reagents of the Plains",b="Prairie wolves hunt in packs across Mulgore’s rolling grasses, alongside prairie stalkers and their pack alphas. They threaten young hunters and local livestock, and their place in the spiritual balance of the plains matters to tauren shamans. The shamans see the paws as more than trophies: they carry the land’s innate vitality.",sp=0},{t="Cleansing the Wells",b="When goblin ventures tainted Mulgore’s sacred water reserves, shaman Mull Thunderhorn needed the land’s physical aid against the pollution. Joined with plainstrider talons, wolf paws provide the spiritual resonance for cleansing totems. With one made, the work of purifying the Winterhoof Water Well can begin.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What creatures drop a Prairie Wolf Paw?",a="Prairie wolves, prairie stalkers, and prairie wolf alphas hunt across Mulgore’s plains, primarily southwest of Bloodhoof Village, and provide Prairie Wolf Paws.",al={"who drops prairie wolf paw","where to get prairie wolf paws","prairie wolf paw source"},gp=true},{q="Who wants Prairie Wolf Paws?",a="Mull Thunderhorn, a tauren shaman in Bloodhoof Village, wants them to help make a cleansing totem for the tainted water wells.",al={"who needs prairie wolf paws","mull thunderhorn wolf paws","prairie wolf paw turn in"},gp=true},{q="Why are wolf paws needed to cleanse water?",a="Tauren shamans channel the land’s natural vitality against corruption. Joined with bird talons, wolf paws help bind Mulgore’s living spirit into an elemental totem that can purge goblin poisons.",al={"why wolf paws","prairie wolf paw lore","why does mull want paws"},it="why_wanted"},{q="Which water well do these paws help cleanse?",a="The paws help make a cleansing totem for the Winterhoof Water Well, poisoned by goblin interlopers and their agents.",al={"which well","winterhoof well cleansing"},gp=true},{q="What is a Prairie Wolf Paw?",a="A Prairie Wolf Paw is a natural ritual component harvested from the wild canines roaming the plains of Mulgore. Rather than serving as mere trophies, these paws carry the living essence of the land, making them valuable to tauren shamans who craft totems to heal and preserve the earth.",al={"prairie wolf paw","what are prairie wolf paws","wolf paw mulgore"},it="identity",add="94c15e4c"},{q="Where do Prairie Wolf Paws come from?",a="These paws are harvested directly from prairie wolves, prairie stalkers, and wolf alphas native to the grassy plateaus of Mulgore. They are natural parts of the wilderness, taken by hunters to offer the land's strength in times of spiritual need.",al={"where do prairie wolf paws come from","prairie wolf paw source","who drops wolf paws"},it="origin",add="7a637be5"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For tauren, harvesting wolf paws is a solemn duty of stewardship: the land’s native beasts help heal ancestral water wells tainted by goblin greed.",["class:Shaman"]="Cleansing totems made from local animal parts draw on the land’s living essence to purge unnatural corruption."},kw={"bloodhoof village","mulgore","mull thunderhorn","plainstrider talon","poison water","prairie wolf","prairie wolf paw","winterhoof water well"},rel={"quest:748","npc:mull-thunderhorn","subzone:winterhoof-water-well","item:plainstrider-talon","zone:mulgore","topic:goblin"},src={"Prairie Wolf Paw","Poison Water (Classic)"}}
E["item:stalker-claws"] = {t="item",n="Stalker Claws",z="mulgore",s="Across Mulgore, Stalker Claws are taken from prairie stalkers for a cleansing rite. Tauren shamans bind them into totems used to purify tainted wells.",h="Across Mulgore, Stalker Claws are taken from prairie stalkers for a cleansing rite.",sec={{t="Predators of the Plains",b="Prairie stalkers hunt across Mulgore’s grassy steppes. Their stealth and lethal precision make them deadly predators, yet they keep an essential balance within the Earth Mother's design. Mull Thunderhorn needs their claws to help heal the blighted land. He seeks no harm to the beasts; the natural force of their hunt holds the spiritual strength needed to counter artificial corruption.",sp=0},{t="Cleansing the Wells",b="Goblin drilling and mining across central Kalimdor have sent chemical poisons into the underground waters that feed ancestral tauren wells. The Thunderhorn Water Well has been tainted by vile muck. Tribal shamans bind Stalker Claws and Cougar Claws into a Thunderhorn Cleansing Totem, a spiritual conduit that scours the unnatural sludge from the earth's springs.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where do Stalker Claws come from?",a="Prairie stalkers roam the grassy hills of Mulgore, especially east and west of Bloodhoof Village. Their claws are gathered by hunting them.",al={"where to find stalker claws","who drops stalker claws","stalker claws source"},it="origin",gp=true},{q="Why does Mull Thunderhorn need these claws?",a="Mull Thunderhorn needs the claws, along with flatland cougar claws, to make a Thunderhorn Cleansing Totem. The native hunters' purity and strength help purify the poisoned wells.",al={"why gather stalker claws","mull thunderhorn purpose","use of stalker claws"},it="why_wanted"},{q="Why is the Thunderhorn Water Well tainted?",a="Goblin operations in Mulgore cause unnatural industrial pollution. Their work disrupts the natural balance and poisons the local water table.",al={"who poisoned the well","why is thunderhorn well sick","thunderhorn pollution"},it="threat"},{q="What happens after the claws are gathered?",a="After the claws are gathered, Mull carves them into a cleansing totem. The totem must then be carried directly to the Thunderhorn Water Well to purge its lingering corruption.",al={"what comes next","next step after claws","cleansing totem ritual"},it="next_step"},{q="What are Stalker Claws?",a="Stalker Claws are the natural talons harvested from prairie stalkers, fierce feline predators roaming the plains of Mulgore. Though plain hunting trophies to an outsider, tauren shamans view them as embodiments of the creature's natural predatory focus and harmony with the land.",al={"what are stalker claws","stalker claws item","stalker claw"},it="identity",add="a496bd75"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren, gathering these claws helps your tribe restore its sacred waters, poisoned by careless industrial trespassers.",["class:Shaman"]="As a shaman, you bind the fierce purity of wild plains predators into cleansing totems. Their claws are essential to empower the totems."},kw={"cleansing totem","mulgore","mull thunderhorn","prairie stalker","stalker claws","thunderhorn totem","water well"},rel={"quest:756","item:thunderhorn-cleansing-totem","npc:mull-thunderhorn","subzone:thunderhorn-water-well","item:cougar-claws","quest:758","zone:mulgore","topic:goblin"},src={"Stalker Claws","Thunderhorn Totem (Classic)"}}
E["item:thunderhorn-cleansing-totem"] = {t="item",n="Thunderhorn Cleansing Totem",z="mulgore",s="Mull Thunderhorn prepares the Thunderhorn Cleansing Totem in Bloodhoof Village. Ancestral magic in the totem is meant to purify the Thunderhorn Water Well.",h="Mull Thunderhorn prepares the Thunderhorn Cleansing Totem in Bloodhoof Village.",sec={{t="Purifying the Thunderhorn Well",b="Mull Thunderhorn entrusts a prepared totem to travelers in Bloodhoof Village. They carry it north to the Thunderhorn clan's sacred spring, where foul agents have tainted the water. The ancestral wells dug by the tauren tribes nourish their herds and hunting grounds. Through ritual invocation, the totem calms the water's disturbed spirits and cleanses the site.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of the Thunderhorn Cleansing Totem?",a="The totem is prepared to cleanse the corrupted Thunderhorn Water Well north of Bloodhoof Village. Its use drives away the foul influence poisoning the well and pleases the ancestral spirits guarding it.",al={"what does the totem do","why use thunderhorn totem","cleansing totem purpose"},it="purpose"},{q="Who crafted the totem?",a="Mull Thunderhorn crafts and bestows the totem. The elder lives in Bloodhoof Village, watches over the health of the land, and communicates with the ancestors.",al={"who made this totem","who gives the thunderhorn totem","mull thunderhorn totem"},it="origin"},{q="Where must the totem be used?",a="Use the totem at the Thunderhorn Water Well, north of Bloodhoof Village in central Mulgore.",al={"where do i use the totem","totem location","where is thunderhorn well"},it="place"},{q="What happens after the ritual is performed?",a="Cleansing the well brings peace to the Thunderhorn clan's ancestral spirits. Mull Thunderhorn dreams of their gratitude, praise, and ancestral gifts for the one who purified their grounds.",al={"after cleansing the well","ritual outcome","ancestor reaction"},sp=1},{q="What is the Thunderhorn Cleansing Totem?",a="The Thunderhorn Cleansing Totem is a consecrated ritual implement crafted specifically to purge the taint corrupting the Thunderhorn Water Well in Mulgore. Infused with ancestral and elemental magic, it serves as a sacred conduit that channels spiritual energies directly into the fouled spring to restore the pure, life-giving waters upon which the local tauren depend.",al={"what is thunderhorn cleansing totem","thunderhorn totem what is it","thunderhorn cleansing totem lore"},it="identity",add="0770e0ea"},{q="Why is the Thunderhorn Cleansing Totem needed?",a="The Thunderhorn clan's ancestral water well has fallen victim to unnatural corruption, threatening the land and the beasts that drink from it. Ordinary remedies cannot undo such a blight; Mull Thunderhorn prepared this enchanted totem so that an emissary can conduct the solemn ritual required to banish the taint and appease the ancestral spirits watching over the well.",al={"why need thunderhorn totem","why do i need the cleansing totem","thunderhorn cleansing totem purpose"},it="why_wanted",add="d3c4386d"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren, you have a sacred duty to purify ancestral wells for the Earth Mother and the clan ancestors who watch over Mulgore.",["class:Shaman"]="As a shaman, you know cleansing totems can pacify disturbed nature spirits and restore balance where elemental energies have turned bitter."},kw={"bloodhoof","cleansing","mulgore","mull","ritual","thunderhorn","totem","water well"},rel={"quest:758","subzone:thunderhorn-water-well","zone:mulgore"},src={"Thunderhorn Cleansing Totem","Thunderhorn Cleansing (Classic)"}}
E["item:totem-of-hawkwind"] = {t="item",n="Totem of Hawkwind",z="mulgore",s="At Camp Narache, young tauren receive the Totem of Hawkwind before departing for Bloodhoof Village. They present it to Baine Bloodhoof as proof that they have completed their first trials and are ready to continue the Rites of the Earthmother.",h="At Camp Narache, young tauren receive the Totem of Hawkwind before departing for Bloodhoof Village.",sec={{t="A Chieftain's Endorsement",b="Chief Hawkwind carves the Totem of Hawkwind atop Red Cloud Mesa for youth coming of age. The token introduces its bearer and proves their passage. Tauren rarely use formal writ; meaningful symbols and familiar tribal craft carry a leader's intent. After a youth completes the first trials at Camp Narache, Hawkwind sends them onward with the totem, along the road into the wider lands of Mulgore. Its distinct carvings identify the bearer to Baine Bloodhoof and show they have the spirit and strength for the next trials, on the way to seeking the blessing of Thunder Bluff.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Totem of Hawkwind used for?",a="Young tauren carry the Totem of Hawkwind from Camp Narache to Bloodhoof Village as a token of passage. They show it to Baine Bloodhoof as proof Chief Hawkwind has judged them ready to continue the Rites of the Earthmother.",al={"what does totem of hawkwind do","totem of hawkwind purpose","why do i have this totem"},it="purpose"},{q="Who made the totem?",a="Chief Hawkwind carves the totem at Red Cloud Mesa. Its distinct carvings are recognized by allied chieftains across Mulgore.",al={"who carved the totem","totem maker","hawkwind totem origins"},it="origin"},{q="Who receives the Totem of Hawkwind?",a="The totem is brought to Baine Bloodhoof, son of Cairne Bloodhoof. He oversees Bloodhoof Village, east of Red Cloud Mesa.",al={"who do i give the totem to","totem recipient","where does the totem go"},it="target"},{q="Why use a totem instead of a letter?",a="Tauren rely on oral tradition, physical artistry, and ancestral symbols. A carved totem carries the personal touch and spirit of its maker, and tribal leaders give it more weight than ink on parchment.",al={"why not a letter","tauren token traditions"}},{q="What is the Totem of Hawkwind?",a="The Totem of Hawkwind is a carved token of introduction entrusted to promising young tauren who have completed their initial trials in the Red Cloud Mesa. Bearing the distinct markings of Chief Hawkwind, it serves as proof that you are ready to venture beyond Camp Narache and undertake the further Rites of the Earthmother.",al={"totem of hawkwind identity","what is this totem","hawkwind totem"},it="identity",add="0f3f23e4"},{q="Why does Baine Bloodhoof want the Totem of Hawkwind?",a="Baine Bloodhoof looks for the totem as an unmistakable sign of your readiness and character. Carved by Chief Hawkwind, its patterns show Baine that an aspiring youth has earned Hawkwind's personal recommendation to continue their spiritual trials and seek the acceptance of Thunder Bluff's elders.",al={"why does baine want the totem","why give totem to baine","baine bloodhoof totem"},it="why_wanted",add="bb2bdc29"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren, carrying this totem marks your passage from childhood on Red Cloud Mesa into Mulgore's greater tribal lands."},kw={"baine bloodhoof","bloodhoof village","camp narache","chief hawkwind","hawkwind","rites of the earthmother","totem"},rel={"quest:763","npc:chief-hawkwind","npc:baine-bloodhoof","subzone:camp-narache","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore","zone:thunderbluff"},src={"Totem of Hawkwind","Rites of the Earthmother (2)"}}
E["item:trophy-swoop-quill"] = {t="item",n="Trophy Swoop Quill",z="mulgore",s="Across Mulgore, swoops circle above the plains, and their quills mark a hunter’s skill. Tauren bring these feathers to Harken Windtotem in Bloodhoof Village.",h="Across Mulgore, swoops circle above the plains, and their quills mark a hunter’s skill.",sec={{t="Token of the Hunt",b="Swoops ride high above Mulgore’s grasslands, where they are hard to track. Young tauren hunters and travelers in Bloodhoof Village seek a trophy quill to prove their cleverness and resolve. Bringing one to elders such as Harken Windtotem honors the hunter’s clan and shows the sharp senses needed to thrive on the plains.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is a Trophy Swoop Quill used for?",a="Trophy Swoop Quills prove hunting merit when you bring them to Harken Windtotem in Bloodhoof Village. The feathers show determination and sharp observation, and honor your standing among the local tauren.",al={"what are swoop quills for","who wants swoop quills","swoop quill use"},it="why_wanted",gp=true},{q="Where do Trophy Swoop Quills come from?",a="Trophy Swoop Quills come from common swoops, wiry swoops, and taloned swoops soaring across Mulgore’s rolling plains.",al={"where to get swoop quills","who drops trophy swoop quill","swoop locations"},it="origin",gp=true},{q="Why are swoops considered difficult to hunt?",a="Harken Windtotem describes swoops as cunning birds that circle high above the grass and move quickly across the land. Sharp eyes help you spot them, and fleet footwork helps you run them down.",al={"why hunt swoops","are swoops hard to kill"}},{q="What is a Trophy Swoop Quill?",a="A Trophy Swoop Quill is a prized feather taken from the predatory swoops that soar across Mulgore. Because swoops are keen-eyed and elusive creatures, gathering their feathers requires sharp senses and swift feet. Among the tauren, carrying these quills serves as a badge of cleverness and determination, demonstrating that a hunter possesses the patience and skill needed to stalk and overcome cunning prey.",al={"trophy swoop quill","what is swoop quill","swoop quills lore"},it="identity",add="91a49961"},{q="Why does Harken Windtotem want Trophy Swoop Quills?",a="Harken Windtotem in Bloodhoof Village asks for these quills as a trial of skill and character. Hunting swoops is no easy feat, and returning with a collection of their finest feathers proves your merit to the village. Delivering them shows that you possess the patience and determination expected of a true hunter, bringing honor to both yourself and your clan.",al={"why collect swoop quills","why harken wants swoop quills","swoop hunting purpose"},it="why_wanted",add="62601276"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren, hunting swoops and bringing home their quills tests patience and cleverness across Mulgore, honoring you and your clan.",["class:Hunter"]="As a hunter, tracking airborne prey across open plains tests your tracking, patience, and marksmanship."},kw={"bloodhoof village","harken windtotem","mulgore","swoop hunting","swoop quill","taloned swoop","trophy swoop quill","wiryswoop"},rel={"quest:761","npc:harken-windtotem","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore","topic:hunter"},src={"Trophy Swoop Quill","Swoop Hunting"}}
E["item:venture-co-documents"] = {t="item",n="Venture Co. Documents",z="mulgore",s="In eastern Mulgore, Venture Co. Documents confirm illegal strip-mining in tauren lands. The papers were recovered from a crate amid a ruined caravan and detail the company's operations.",h="In eastern Mulgore, Venture Co.",sec={{t="Evidence of Exploitation",b="Venture Co. Documents were found atop mined ore in a ransacked wagon. The ledgers show the goblin-led cartel has quietly established drilling and mining outposts in Mulgore's eastern foothills. Across Kalimdor, the Venture Company is known for ruthless deforestation and uncontrolled excavation. Morin Cloudstalker and the tauren Outrunners use the seized records to judge the threat to their sacred ancestral plains and rally hunters to drive out the trespassers.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What do the Venture Co. Documents contain?",a="The papers detail the Venture Company's mining and extraction in eastern Mulgore. They reveal the goblin enterprise is harvesting and carrying away the region's mineral wealth.",al={"venture co papers contents","what are venture documents","what is written in documents"},it="identity"},{q="Where were these documents discovered?",a="The papers were found in a sealed Venture Company crate amid a ransacked wagon's wreckage at the Ravaged Caravan site, east of Bloodhoof Village.",al={"where do i find the documents","venture documents location","ravaged caravan crate"},it="place"},{q="Who needs to see these papers?",a="Bring the papers to Morin Cloudstalker, a tauren hunter stationed along the main trail east of Bloodhoof Village. He watches over Mulgore's security.",al={"who do i bring documents to","turn in venture documents","morin cloudstalker documents"},it="target"},{q="Why are the Venture Company mining in Mulgore?",a="The Venture Company mines for profit, seeking rich mineral veins and untamed territories to strip of valuable ores. It has zero regard for native treaties, spiritual customs, or the environment.",al={"why are goblins in mulgore","venture co mulgore goal","why venture co digging"},it="motive"},{q="Where do the Venture Co. Documents come from, and who wrote them?",a="These papers concern the Venture Company's clandestine mining operations in the eastern foothills of Mulgore. Addressed to Supervisor Fizsprocket on behalf of the company's advisory board and President Razdunk, the documents were packed atop a crate of raw ore and minerals found near a ransacked caravan, ready to be shipped out alongside the stolen wealth of the plains.",al={"venture co documents origin","who made venture co documents","where did venture co papers come from"},it="origin",add="33362eae"},{q="Why does Morin Cloudstalker want the Venture Co. Documents?",a="The tauren Outrunners protecting Mulgore require undeniable proof of the Venture Company's encroaching presence. Morin Cloudstalker needs these records to confirm the exact scope of the goblins' mining schemes, giving the defenders of Bloodhoof Village the vital intelligence they need to halt the exploitation and pillaging of their ancestral lands.",al={"why does morin want the venture co documents","why keep venture co documents","who wants venture co papers"},it="why_wanted",add="f91b68fc"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren, you know goblin machinery defiles the Earth Mother's sacred earth. These papers help the elders confirm the desecration's extent and launch retaliatory strikes.",["profession:Mining"]="As a miner, you can see why the raw mineral samples packed beside the papers matter: they show that the foothills hold rich, untapped veins ripe for excavation."},kw={"morin cloudstalker","mulgore mining","ravaged caravan","venture co documents","venture company"},rel={"quest:749","npc:morin-cloudstalker","quest:764","subzone:ravaged-caravan","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore","topic:horde"},src={"Venture Co. Documents","The Ravaged Caravan (Horde) (2)"}}
E["item:verdant-note"] = {t="item",n="Verdant Note",z="mulgore",s="In Camp Narache, Verdant Note calls young tauren druids to begin their training. Sent for Gart Mistrunner, it guides them toward the rites of the Earthmother.",h="In Camp Narache, Verdant Note calls young tauren druids to begin their training.",sec={{t="A Call to the Path",b="Young tauren with a gift for nature receive Verdant Note soon after their trials begin in the Red Cloud Mesa. A messenger carries Gart Mistrunner's brief summons to Camp Narache, where the initiate must report to the mentor. Restless spirits and shifting elements trouble Mulgore, and the elders need new druids trained quickly.",sp=0},{t="Teachings of the Earthmother",b="Gart Mistrunner teaches initiates to commune with nature. He warns that night elves share a bond with the wild but keep their distance from tauren, guarded by ancient pride. Tauren druidism centers on reverence for the Earthmother. Initiates learn to hear the land's restless ancestral voices and serve as her living instruments.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Verdant Note?",a="Verdant Note summons fledgling tauren druids to Camp Narache. It tells them to seek Gart Mistrunner and begin their spiritual training.",al={"verdant note item","what is this note"},it="identity"},{q="Who sends the Verdant Note?",a="A messenger carries Verdant Note on Gart Mistrunner's behalf. Gart is the druid trainer at Camp Narache on the Red Cloud Mesa.",al={"who sent verdant note","who wrote verdant note"},it="origin"},{q="Why is Gart Mistrunner calling for druid initiates?",a="Restless ancestral spirits trouble Mulgore and nearby regions. Gart needs new druids trained quickly to commune with the wild spirits and heal the disturbed land.",al={"why does gart want me","gart mistrunner reason"},it="motive"},{q="What does Gart tell druids regarding the night elves?",a="Gart warns that night elves share a sacred bond with nature but are proud and unlikely to welcome tauren into their circles. He urges initiates to focus on the teachings of the Earthmother.",al={"night elves and tauren druids","what about night elves"}},{q="Why does Gart Mistrunner want you to have the Verdant Note?",a="Gart Mistrunner sends you the Verdant Note to summon you for your druidic training at Camp Narache. Restless spirits have stirred throughout Mulgore and lands beyond, and Gart requires new initiates to learn to listen to the Earthmother and the spirits of nature. The note serves as an urgent summons to step forward and embrace your duties as an avatar of the natural world.",al={"why verdant note","why does gart send the note","verdant note purpose"},it="why_wanted",add="59de7025"}},ang={["class:Druid"]="As a druid, Verdant Note marks your acceptance into tauren druidic study and begins your lifelong communion with the Earthmother.",["race:Tauren"]="As Shu'halo, your druidism is an ancestral calling tied to healing the balance of your sacred hunting grounds in Mulgore."},kw={"camp narache","druid","earthmother","gart mistrunner","mulgore","red cloud mesa","verdant note"},rel={"quest:3094","npc:gart-mistrunner","topic:druid","subzone:camp-narache","zone:mulgore"},src={"Verdant Note","Verdant Note (quest)"}}
E["item:water-of-the-seers"] = {t="item",n="Water of the Seers",z="mulgore",s="In Bloodhoof Village, young tauren drink the sacred Water of the Seers before their tribal trials. The draught opens the mind to the spirit world and summons a Plains Vision to guide them.",h="In Bloodhoof Village, young tauren drink the sacred Water of the Seers before their tribal trials.",sec={{t="A Vessel for the Spirit",b="Young tauren drink the Water of the Seers before the tribal fire in Bloodhoof Village. Tribal mystics prepare the draught for the ancient Rite of Vision. It opens the drinker’s spiritual sight, and the Plains Vision appears from the embers to guide the way to Seer Wiserunner.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What happens after drinking the Water of the Seers?",a="Drinking the Water of the Seers beside the tribal fire opens your spiritual senses. The Plains Vision appears from the flames to guide you across Mulgore.",al={"what does water of the seers do","drinking water of the seers","plains vision water"},it="power"},{q="Where should the water be consumed?",a="Drink the Water of the Seers before the large tribal fire in Bloodhoof Village, just north of the shamanic elders.",al={"where to drink water of the seers","water of the seers location","tribal fire location"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Who does the vision guide you to?",a="The Plains Vision guides you northwest into Mulgore’s foothills, to the cave where Seer Wiserunner awaits to interpret your journey.",al={"where does the wolf go","plains vision destination","who is the seer"},it="target"},{q="What is Water of the Seers?",a="Water of the Seers is a sacred, holy draught prepared by tauren mystics for initiates undertaking the traditional rites of passage. Ingesting these blessed waters opens your spiritual senses, allowing you to perceive omens and guide spirits that remain hidden to ordinary eyes.",al={"what is water of the seers","water of the seers lore","what kind of item is water of the seers"},it="identity",add="6b2d8d4a"},{q="Where does Water of the Seers come from, and who made it?",a="The water is ritually prepared and bestowed by Zarlman Two-Moons in Bloodhoof Village. He brews it from well stones and Ambercorn gathered across Mulgore, imbuing it with spiritual potency for young tauren undergoing the Rites of the Earthmother.",al={"who made water of the seers","origin of water of the seers","where does water of the seers come from"},it="origin",add="6ddc8851"},{q="Why does anyone want Water of the Seers?",a="You need the Water of the Seers to complete the Rite of Vision, an essential spiritual trial for the tauren. Drinking the draught by the tribal fire summons the Plains Vision—a spirit guide meant to lead you across Mulgore toward your destined path and elder advisors.",al={"why drink water of the seers","purpose of water of the seers","why do i need water of the seers"},it="why_wanted",add="bc18f3dc"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren, you drink the Water of the Seers as part of your passage into adulthood and your people’s sacred traditions.",["class:Shaman"]="As a shaman, the Water of the Seers gives you an early glimpse of communion with the spirit world."},kw={"bloodhoof village","plains vision","rite of vision","seer wiserunner","tribal fire","water of the seers"},rel={"quest:767","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore"},src={"Water of the Seers","Rite of Vision (3)"}}
E["item:water-pitcher"] = {t="item",n="Water Pitcher",z="mulgore",s="At Red Cloud Mesa, Water Pitcher carries fresh spring water from the local well to Camp Narache. Greatmother Hawkwind asks young tauren to bring it back as a lesson in humility, service, and respect for tribal elders.",h="At Red Cloud Mesa, Water Pitcher carries fresh spring water from the local well to Camp Narache.",sec={{t="A Lesson in Humility",b="Water Pitcher rests beside the well southeast of Camp Narache. Greatmother Hawkwind asks a novice to fill it and carry it to Chief Hawkwind. The chore tests their character and teaches young braves that no duty is beneath a tribal protector. Caring for the everyday needs of their kin matters as much as wielding a weapon on the hunt.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the water pitcher used for?",a="Water Pitcher carries fresh well water back to Camp Narache. Hauling it across the plains tests a young adventurer’s willingness to do everyday labor for the good of the tribe.",al={"pitcher purpose","why carry water pitcher","humble task pitcher"},it="purpose",gp=true},{q="Where do you find the water pitcher?",a="You find Water Pitcher beside the well southeast of Camp Narache on Red Cloud Mesa, near where Greatmother Hawkwind tends to her tasks.",al={"pitcher location","where is water pitcher","find pitcher mulgore"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Who asks you to fetch the water pitcher?",a="Greatmother Hawkwind asks young travelers to collect Water Pitcher. Her aging legs make the trek difficult, so she asks eager youths to test their vitality and humility.",al={"who gives pitcher quest","greatmother pitcher"},it="motive",gp=true},{q="Who receives the water pitcher once it is filled?",a="You bring the filled pitcher to Chief Hawkwind at Camp Narache. He sees the deed as proof that its bearer has the heart and dedication to serve the tribe.",al={"who gets pitcher","deliver water pitcher","bring pitcher to"},it="target",gp=true},{q="What is the Water Pitcher?",a="The Water Pitcher is a simple clay vessel kept at the freshwater well on the plains southeast of Camp Narache. Filled with fresh well water, it serves as vital refreshment for the young hunters, braves, and elders living in the camp atop Red Cloud Mesa.",al={"what is water pitcher","water pitcher info","water pitcher mulgore"},it="identity",add="d76860be"},{q="Why does Chief Hawkwind need the Water Pitcher?",a="Gathering fresh water from the plains well is essential daily work for the survival of Camp Narache. His mother, Greatmother Hawkwind, tasks young adventurers with hauling the pitcher up to the chief to assist the tribe and prove they are willing to perform even the most humble chores for their people.",al={"why want water pitcher","why bring water pitcher","chief hawkwind water pitcher"},it="why_wanted",add="a1f72ee9"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a young tauren coming of age on Red Cloud Mesa, carrying Water Pitcher is an early lesson: honoring elders and helping the community are foundations of strength."},kw={"a humble task","camp narache","chief hawkwind","greatmother hawkwind","mulgore well","red cloud mesa","water pitcher"},rel={"quest:752","npc:greatmother-hawkwind","npc:chief-hawkwind","subzone:camp-narache","zone:mulgore","zone:thunderbluff"},src={"Water Pitcher","A Humble Task (2)"}}
E["item:well-stone"] = {t="item",n="Well Stone",z="mulgore",s="Across Mulgore, Well Stones rest beside the water wells. Their earth and water magic helps prepare the Water of the Seers for tauren rites of passage.",h="Across Mulgore, Well Stones rest beside the water wells.",sec={{t="Vessel of Earthen Energies",b="Well Stones rest in the soft earth beside Mulgore’s wells, where they absorb the spiritual resonance of the plains and the waters beneath them. Shaman and seers such as Zarlman Two-Moons know these stones as vessels of vital magic, not ordinary river pebbles. Combined with grounded Ambercorn beneath the plains’ mighty trees, their mineral properties temper the brew that awakens visionary trances in young tauren during the sacred Rite of Vision.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What are Well Stones used for?",a="Well Stones are combined with Ambercorn to brew the Water of the Seers. Initiates drink it before the tribal fire, and it grants a guiding vision to those seeking to prove their spiritual maturity.",al={"well stone purpose","what is well stone for","why gather well stones"},it="purpose"},{q="Where are Well Stones found?",a="Well Stones lie on the ground around the communal water wells scattered across Mulgore, where the plains’ life-giving moisture meets the soil.",al={"where to find well stone","well stone locations","well stone mulgore"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Who asks for these stones?",a="Zarlman Two-Moons, a respected mystic in Bloodhoof Village, directs initiates to gather Well Stones as part of the Rite of Vision.",al={"who needs well stones","zarlman well stones","rite of vision stones"},gp=true},{q="What is a Well Stone?",a="A Well Stone is a naturally occurring stone infused with magical elements, gathered from the earth surrounding the sacred water wells across Mulgore. Tauren mystics prize these stones for ritual alchemy, as their connection to the land's pure waters makes them potent catalytic reagents.",al={"what are well stones","well stone mulgore","well stone lore"},it="identity",add="0148a630"},{q="Where do Well Stones come from?",a="Well Stones are not crafted by mortal hands; they are drawn straight from the soil and sediment surrounding Mulgore's water wells. Shaped and touched over generations by the sacred springs that feed the plains, these minerals naturally absorb the ambient spiritual and elemental energy of the land.",al={"where do well stones come from","who made well stones","well stone origin"},it="origin",add="a4e2d25c"},{q="Why does Zarlman Two-Moons want Well Stones?",a="Zarlman Two-Moons requires Well Stones to prepare the Water of the Seers for young tauren undertaking the Rite of Vision. When crushed and steeped alongside Ambercorn, the magical elements within the stones craft a potent sacred brew that awakens your spiritual sight, revealing ancestral guides to light your path.",al={"why gather well stones","why does zarlman want well stones","well stone rite of vision"},it="why_wanted",add="461b83d8"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For young tauren, gathering Well Stones is an essential step in showing communion with the land. It teaches how the earth’s physical elements contribute to spiritual enlightenment.",["class:Shaman"]="Shaman know Well Stones as natural anchors of earth and water magic. Their latent spirits come together in a transformative ceremonial mixture."},kw={"ambercorn","bloodhoof village","mulgore","rite of vision","water of the seers","well stone","zarlman two-moons"},rel={"quest:767","item:water-of-the-seers","item:ambercorn","npc:zarlman-two-moons","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore"},src={"Well Stone","Rite of Vision (2)"}}
E["item:wildmane-cleansing-totem"] = {t="item",n="Wildmane Cleansing Totem",z="mulgore",s="At Mulgore’s Wildmane Water Well, one last cleansing remains. Mull Thunderhorn prepares a totem for the northern well after the Winterhoof and Thunderhorn wells are cleansed.",h="At Mulgore’s Wildmane Water Well, one last cleansing remains.",sec={{t="Purifying the Sacred Wells",b="The Wildmane Water Well still needs cleansing. Goblin ventures have poisoned the land and fouled Mulgore’s scarce water, so the tauren turn to sacred ritual against the blight. After the Winterhoof and Thunderhorn wells are cleansed, Mull Thunderhorn crafts a final totem for the well north of Thunder Bluff. Infused with natural spirits, it purifies the fetid water so life can nourish the plains again.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of the Wildmane Cleansing Totem?",a="The totem is used in a tauren purification ritual to cleanse the poisons and foul runoff in the Wildmane Water Well north of Thunder Bluff.",al={"what does the totem do","wildmane totem use","why cleanse the well"},it="purpose"},{q="Who creates the Wildmane Cleansing Totem?",a="Mull Thunderhorn creates the totem after you gather the needed components and cleanse the other tainted wells in Mulgore.",al={"who gave me this totem","mull thunderhorn totem","where do i get the cleansing totem"},it="origin"},{q="Why are the water wells in Mulgore poisoned?",a="Goblin ventures have brought poisons and industrial runoff into Mulgore’s water, threatening the tauren tribes and the natural balance of the plains.",al={"who poisoned the wells","why is the well poisonous","goblin poisons mulgore"},it="motive"},{q="Where does this totem need to be used?",a="Use the totem at the Wildmane Water Well, north of Thunder Bluff.",al={"wildmane well location","where is wildmane water well","where do i take the totem"},it="place",gp=true},{q="What is the Wildmane Cleansing Totem?",a="It is a sacred ritual implement prepared by the shaman Mull Thunderhorn in Bloodhoof Village. Imbued with the favor of the spirits after your purification of the Winterhoof and Thunderhorn wells, this totem carries the power needed to perform the cleansing ritual at the final corrupted water well north of Thunder Bluff.",al={"what is wildmane cleansing totem","wildmane totem","cleansing totem wildmane"},it="identity",add="6c54f1c7"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren, restoring the Wildmane well is an obligation to the Earth Mother and a way to protect your ancestral homeland from foreign industrial toxins.",["class:Shaman"]="As a shaman, you channel spiritual purification through carved totems to mend water spirits wounded by reckless industry."},kw={"bloodhoof village","goblin poisons","mulgore","mull thunderhorn","tauren","wildmane cleansing totem","wildmane water well"},rel={"quest:760","quest:759","subzone:wildmane-water-well","npc:mull-thunderhorn","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore","zone:thunderbluff","topic:goblin"},src={"Wildmane Cleansing Totem","Wildmane Cleansing (Classic)"}}
E["item:winterhoof-cleansing-totem"] = {t="item",n="Winterhoof Cleansing Totem",z="mulgore",s="At Winterhoof Water Well, Venture Company poisons the water beneath Mulgore's plains. Mull Thunderhorn crafts a totem to cleanse it.",h="At Winterhoof Water Well, Venture Company poisons the water beneath Mulgore's plains.",sec={{t="Purifying the Spring",b="Mull Thunderhorn crafts the Winterhoof Cleansing Totem in Bloodhoof Village. Venture Company goblins have tainted Mulgore's underground water with their operations. Mull imbues the carved wood with protective magic meant to wash away the foreign poisons. Carried past hostile goblin guards to Winterhoof Water Well, the totem channels a focused ritual that disperses the contamination. Pure drinking water returns for the plains' wandering herds and native tribes.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Winterhoof Cleansing Totem used for?",a="You bring the totem to Winterhoof Water Well, southeast of Bloodhoof Village, and perform a ritual that scours contamination from the aquifer.",al={"cleansing totem purpose","winterhoof totem use","what does totem do"},it="purpose"},{q="Who crafted this totem?",a="Mull Thunderhorn, a respected tauren in Bloodhoof Village, carves and prepares the totem to undo the damage to Winterhoof Water Well.",al={"who made winterhoof totem","creator of cleansing totem","mull totem"},it="origin"},{q="Why was the Winterhoof Water Well polluted?",a="The Venture Company establishes an encampment around the spring. Runoff, waste, and chemical sludge from its logging and mining machinery pollute the natural watering hole.",al={"who poisoned winterhoof well","well pollution cause","why is well dirty"},it="conflict"},{q="What happens once the cleansing ritual is complete?",a="The murky water becomes clean and wholesome again, and local wildlife and migrating tauren herds can safely drink from the well.",al={"after using totem","well cleansed result","does well get fixed"},it="next_step"},{q="What is the Winterhoof Cleansing Totem?",a="It is a sacred ritual totem carved by Mull Thunderhorn in Mulgore. Infused with natural spirits, the totem is specifically designed to perform a purification rite over the poisoned Winterhoof Water Well, purging the industrial pollutants introduced by the Venture Company.",al={"winterhoof cleansing totem","what is winterhoof totem","about winterhoof cleansing totem"},it="identity",add="25396a0d"},{q="Why does Mull Thunderhorn need the Winterhoof Cleansing Totem used?",a="The tauren rely on the water wells of Mulgore to sustain themselves and the wild beasts of the plains. Because goblins from the Venture Company fouled the Winterhoof Water Well, Mull Thunderhorn needs an adventurer to carry this totem into hostile territory and cleanse the water before the taint permanently ruins the land.",al={"why use winterhoof cleansing totem","why cleanse winterhoof well","winterhoof totem purpose"},it="why_wanted",add="b2bc5f58"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For tauren, the desecration of fresh water strikes at the heart of their bond with the Earth Mother. The totem's rite helps defend their homeland.",["class:Shaman"]="As a shaman, you wield the harmony of earth and water to cast out artificial corruption, in keeping with shamanic principles."},kw={"bloodhoof village","cleansing","mulgore","mull thunderhorn","totem","venture company","water well","winterhoof"},rel={"quest:754","subzone:winterhoof-water-well","npc:mull-thunderhorn","subzone:bloodhoof-village","topic:venture-company","zone:mulgore","topic:goblin"},src={"Winterhoof Cleansing Totem","Winterhoof Cleansing (Classic)"}}
E["npc:baine-bloodhoof"] = {t="npc",n="Baine Bloodhoof",z="mulgore",s="In Bloodhoof Village, Baine Bloodhoof guards his people and Mulgore. While Cairne leads from Thunder Bluff, Baine directs local defense against gnolls and outside threats.",h="In Bloodhoof Village, Baine Bloodhoof guards his people and Mulgore.",sec={{t="Son of the High Chieftain",b="Baine Bloodhoof grew up traveling with his nomadic people, the son of Cairne. They settled in Mulgore’s grasslands, where centaur clans remained a threat. After the Third War, Khiragg’s warband captured Baine, and grief left Cairne nearly unable to lead the new settlements. Rexxar freed Baine from captivity. The rescue restored Cairne’s spirit, and the tauren stood with Thrall’s Horde against the invading Kul Tirans, securing their foothold on the continent.",sp=0},{t="Guardian of Bloodhoof Village",b="Baine lives among his kin in Bloodhoof Village rather than at the capital mesas. As Cairne grows older, Baine bears more responsibility for the people and represents the next generation of tauren leadership. He directs braves and visiting allies against Palemane gnolls that trouble the borders, and watches for outside influences that threaten Mulgore’s natural balance.",sp=0},{t="Defending the Earth Mother",b="Baine faces encroachers who give little thought to spiritual balance. East of Mulgore, the goblin Venture Company cuts into the sacred landscape. Dwarven prospectors from Bael Modan dig ancestral soil at the Bael'dun Digsite. Baine urges a firm but measured response: warn the miners and destroy their equipment instead of rushing into needless bloodshed. He says their violent digging hurts the spirits of the earth, but the miners refuse to listen.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Baine Bloodhoof?",a="Baine Bloodhoof is Cairne Bloodhoof’s only son. He oversees defense and tribal affairs in Bloodhoof Village, directing local braves and protecting Mulgore while his father rules from Thunder Bluff.",al={"baine lore","who is baine","cairnes son"},it="identity"},{q="Where can Baine be found right now?",a="Baine is in Bloodhoof Village, in central Mulgore just south of Thunder Bluff. Near the town center, he organizes tasks for newly arrived adventurers.",al={"baine location","where is baine","baine bloodhoof spot"},it="why_here"},{q="How was Baine rescued from the centaur?",a="After the Third War, Khiragg captured Baine, leaving Cairne in debilitating despair. Rexxar sought help from Bovan Windtotem’s outcasts, tracked the centaur to their encampment, slew Baine’s captors, and returned him safely to his father.",al={"baine kidnapped","rexxar saves baine","baine captured"},it="history"},{q="What problems does Baine manage in Bloodhoof Village?",a="Baine coordinates efforts against Palemane gnolls harassing local herds. He also watches foreign intruders, including the goblin Venture Company and Alliance dwarven excavators disrupting the earth.",al={"baine quests","what does baine do","bloodhoof village troubles"},it="purpose"},{q="What is Baine's stance on the Bael'dun dwarven digsite?",a="Baine believes the Bael'dun miners’ violent digging hurts the spirits of the earth and defiles the land. He sends travelers to seize and smash their tools, hoping to scare the miners away and show them that hollowing the land is no path to knowledge.",al={"baine dwarves","bael dun mining","dwarf digsite mulgore"},it="conflict"},{q="Will Baine succeed Cairne as High Chieftain?",a="As Cairne’s son, Baine takes a greater role in leading the tauren as his father grows older. Many see him as the natural heir to the united tribes.",al={"does baine become leader","future high chieftain","cairne heir"}},{q="Whose side is Baine Bloodhoof on?",a="Baine is completely devoted to the tauren people and the Horde. As the son of High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof, his loyalties lie firmly with his tribe in Mulgore and the larger coalition forged with Thrall's orcs and the Darkspear trolls.",al={"baine bloodhoof faction","is baine horde","baine allegiance"},it="side",add="59aa4b20"},{q="What does Baine Bloodhoof want?",a="Baine seeks to safeguard Mulgore and protect the peaceful homeland his father secured for their people. Right now, he aims to repel the aggressive Palemane gnolls, investigate the Venture Company's operations to the east, and drive off the dwarven miners scarring the sacred earth at the Bael'dun Digsite.",al={"baine bloodhoof goals","baine goal","what does baine want"},it="wants",add="9cbf1856"},{q="Who is Baine Bloodhoof tied to?",a="Baine is the son of High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof. Beyond his family, he shares a lasting debt of gratitude with Rexxar, the Champion of the Horde who rescued him from captivity when centaur raiders threatened his life.",al={"baine family","who is baine related to","baine connections"},it="ties",add="1590aad3"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Baine carries the honorable lineage of High Chieftain Cairne and the hope of the young tribes gathered across Mulgore’s plains.",["faction:Horde"]="Baine brings calm wisdom and quiet martial strength to Thrall’s Horde, guiding those who pass through central Mulgore.",["class:Shaman"]="Baine puts the spirits first. He sends travelers to silence dwarven machines and reckless miners before their digging further wounds the spirits of the earth."},kw={"baine","bloodhoof","bloodhoof village","cairne","centaur","khiragg","mulgore","palemane","rexxar","thunder bluff"},rel={"subzone:bloodhoof-village","npc:cairne-bloodhoof","zone:mulgore","zone:thunderbluff","subzone:bael-dun-digsite","topic:centaur","topic:tauren","topic:horde"},src={"Baine Bloodhoof"}}
E["npc:cairne-bloodhoof"] = {t="npc",n="Cairne Bloodhoof",z="mulgore",s="In Thunder Bluff, Cairne Bloodhoof rules with hard-won wisdom. The venerable High Chieftain leads the united tauren tribes, and bound their fate to Thrall’s Horde after the orcs helped them escape extinction at centaur hands.",h="In Thunder Bluff, Cairne Bloodhoof rules with hard-won wisdom.",sec={{t="A Nation in Mulgore",b="Cairne Bloodhoof led the tauren from a life of flight to a home in Mulgore. For generations, centaur marauders hunted the wandering tribes across the Barrens and drove them toward extinction. When Thrall and his orcs reached Kalimdor, Cairne saw a rare nobility in them. Thrall offered military protection and an escort for Cairne’s desperate caravan across the plains, in return for guidance to the Oracle at Stonetalon Peak. The tauren settled on Thunder Bluff’s towering mesas, where Cairne united the tribes and gave them a permanent home.",sp=0},{t="Pledge to the Horde",b="Cairne Bloodhoof fought beside Thrall’s Horde and Jaina Proudmoore’s humans against the Burning Legion during the Third War. Grateful to Thrall, he had followed the orcs to Stonetalon Peak. When Admiral Daelin Proudmoore invaded Durotar, Cairne took up arms again and fought alongside Rexxar after his captured son, Baine, was safely recovered. Now one of the Horde’s most respected elders, Cairne remains Thrall’s closest confidant and offers calm counsel against reckless warmongering.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Cairne Bloodhoof?",a="Cairne Bloodhoof is the High Chieftain of the united tauren tribes, chieftain of the Bloodhoof tribe, and leader of Thunder Bluff. An ancient warrior, he led the tauren out of nomadic exile and into a secure homeland.",al={"who is cairne","tauren leader","high chieftain"},it="identity"},{q="Why are the tauren allied with the orcs?",a="During the Third War, marauding centaur tribes hunted the tauren toward extinction. Thrall and his newly arrived orcs fought off the marauders and escorted Cairne’s great caravan to Mulgore. In gratitude, Cairne pledged his people’s loyalty and strength to Thrall’s Horde.",al={"why tauren join horde","cairne thrall friendship","tauren orc alliance"},it="side"},{q="Where does Cairne reside?",a="Cairne resides in Thunder Bluff, the city built on Mulgore’s high mesas. He rules alongside the leaders of the reunited tauren tribes.",al={"where is cairne","cairne location","find cairne bloodhoof"},it="place"},{q="Who is Baine Bloodhoof to Cairne?",a="Baine is Cairne’s beloved son and heir. When centaur raiders abducted Baine, Cairne fell into deep grief and refused to fight until Rexxar and his companions found Baine and brought him safely home.",al={"cairne son","is baine cairne's son","baine bloodhoof relation"},it="ties"},{q="What does Cairne Bloodhoof want?",a="Cairne desires lasting peace and prosperity for his people. For years, the nomadic tauren faced near extinction at the hands of marauding centaur tribes. Having united the tribes atop the high mesas of Mulgore, his focus is securing their ancestral home, safeguarding their peaceful spiritual traditions, and standing faithfully alongside his Horde allies against any who threaten Kalimdor.",al={"cairne goals","what does cairne want","cairne ambitions"},it="wants",add="d2aba303"},{q="Why is Cairne Bloodhoof in Thunder Bluff?",a="As High Chieftain of the united tauren tribes, Cairne rules his people from their capital of Thunder Bluff. He founded the settlement atop the towering mesas of Mulgore to protect the tauren from marauding centaur, and he remains here to govern the tribes, counsel shamans, and coordinate defense alongside his Horde allies.",al={"why is cairne in thunder bluff","why is cairne here","cairne bloodhoof location reason"},it="why_here",add="665f60ad"},{q="Why is Cairne Bloodhoof famous?",a="Cairne is venerated as a peerless warrior and the visionary leader who united the scattered tauren tribes. Renowned across Kalimdor for his immense strength and seasoned wisdom, he played a crucial role in defeating the Burning Legion atop Mount Hyjal and establishing the modern Horde alongside Thrall.",al={"cairne bloodhoof reputation","is cairne bloodhoof famous","why is cairne respected"},it="fame",add="a87d4b70"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren, Cairne is the savior of your people. He united the scattered tribes, repelled the centaur, and led the building of Thunder Bluff.",["faction:Horde"]="As a member of the Horde, you share its cause with Cairne, a founding father on Kalimdor and a pillar of honor, wisdom, and military power."},kw={"baine","bloodhoof","cairne","centaur","high chieftain","mulgore","thrall","thunder bluff"},rel={"npc:thrall","topic:tauren","zone:thunderbluff","zone:mulgore","npc:baine-bloodhoof","topic:centaur","topic:horde","subzone:stonetalon-peak"},src={"Cairne Bloodhoof"}}
E["npc:chief-hawkwind"] = {t="npc",n="Chief Hawkwind",z="mulgore",s="At Camp Narache, Chief Hawkwind prepares young Shu'halo for the wider world. He guides their traditional rites of passage on Red Cloud Mesa in Mulgore, teaching humility, respect for the land, and the strength to protect their people.",h="At Camp Narache, Chief Hawkwind prepares young Shu'halo for the wider world.",sec={{t="Chieftain of the Mesa",b="Chief Hawkwind prepares young tauren at Camp Narache for life beyond Red Cloud Mesa. On the mesa, fledgling hunters, shamans, druids, and warriors undertake the ancient Rites of the Earthmother. Hawkwind oversees the customs, teaching each youth duty and endurance before they leave the mesa's safety.",sp=0},{t="Guiding the Rites",b="Chief Hawkwind begins his lessons with humility, not martial glory. He sends initiates to fetch clean spring water and do other simple chores, teaching reverence for nature and their elders. Once they show humility, braves face tests of martial prowess. Their trials test their resolve against aggressive Bristleback quilboar encroaching on the ancestral plains.",sp=0},{t="Defending Camp Narache",b="Chief Hawkwind must also face dangers at Camp Narache's doorstep. The Bristleback tribe presses against Red Cloud Mesa's perimeter with growing boldness. Hawkwind coordinates the settlement's defense, sending newly proven braves to blunt quilboar assaults and keep the sacred training grounds secure.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Chief Hawkwind?",a="Chief Hawkwind leads Camp Narache on Red Cloud Mesa. He oversees tribal matters there and mentors young tauren preparing to enter the wider world of Mulgore and Kalimdor.",al={"hawkwind","chief hawkwind lore","who is the chief at camp narache"},it="identity"},{q="What are the Rites of the Earthmother?",a="The Rites of the Earthmother are sacred trials that young tauren undertake as they come of age. Elders such as Chief Hawkwind guide the rites, which test humility, strength, courage, and spiritual ties to the land and ancestors.",al={"tauren rites","rites of earthmother","what are the rites"}},{q="Why does Chief Hawkwind have you fetch water first?",a="Chief Hawkwind gives you the task of fetching water to teach that service and respect for the community matter as much as martial strength. Gathering fresh water for tribal elders teaches patience and humility before initiates face battle.",al={"a humble task","why fetch water","hawkwind water quest"},it="motive"},{q="What threat is Camp Narache facing?",a="Hostile Bristleback quilboar from the hills around Red Cloud Mesa keep pressure on Camp Narache. Chief Hawkwind organizes the mesa's defense and sends initiates to thin their ranks.",al={"narache threats","who is attacking camp narache","quilboar camp narache"},it="threat"},{q="Where does Chief Hawkwind send adventurers next?",a="After a young brave completes the preliminary rites on the mesa, Chief Hawkwind sends them down into Mulgore's valleys. They go to Bloodhoof Village to find Chief Baine Bloodhoof, son of Cairne, and continue the Rites of the Earthmother.",al={"where do i go after hawkwind","after camp narache","leaving red cloud mesa"},it="next_step"},{q="Is Chief Hawkwind related to Greatmother Hawkwind?",a="Yes. Greatmother Hawkwind is Chief Hawkwind's mother. Their family has deep roots and a long history of leadership among the tauren of Red Cloud Mesa.",al={"greatmother hawkwind relation","hawkwind family","who is greatmother hawkwind"},it="ties"},{q="Whose side is Chief Hawkwind on?",a="Chief Hawkwind stands loyally with his fellow tauren and the Horde. As a tribal leader stationed in the Red Cloud Mesa, he works directly to guide and strengthen the newest generation of tauren defenders, preparing them to serve both their people and the wider Horde.",al={"chief hawkwind faction","hawkwind horde or alliance","who does chief hawkwind serve"},it="side",add="c01d188d"},{q="What does Chief Hawkwind want?",a="Hawkwind seeks to protect Camp Narache and ensure young tauren are thoroughly tested before venturing out into Kalimdor. He oversees the sacred Rites of the Earthmother, testing both your humility and strength against the encroaching Bristleback quilboar so you may honorably serve your people.",al={"what is chief hawkwind goal","chief hawkwind purpose","what does hawkwind want"},it="wants",add="e0733ded"},{q="Why is Chief Hawkwind at Camp Narache?",a="Chief Hawkwind resides at Camp Narache to tend to local tribal matters and guide young adventurers through their first trials. From this elevated mesa, he watches over the training grounds and organizes defense efforts against the aggressive Bristleback quilboar who constantly harass the camp.",al={"why is chief hawkwind here","hawkwind camp narache why","chief hawkwind location why"},it="why_here",add="d395b627"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren, you learn the Shu'halo's first values from Chief Hawkwind: reverence for the Earthmother, loyalty to your tribe, and humility in strength.",["faction:Horde"]="As a member of the Horde, you join newly raised tauren braves shaped by Hawkwind's teachings into capable warriors and spellcasters, and steadfast, honorable allies."},kw={"a humble task","bristleback","camp narache","chief hawkwind","mulgore","red cloud mesa","rite of strength","rites of the earthmother","tauren chieftain"},rel={"subzone:camp-narache","zone:mulgore","topic:tauren","quest:763","quest:752","quest:757","quest:781","npc:greatmother-hawkwind"},src={"Chief Hawkwind"}}
E["npc:doomsayer-wiserunner"] = {t="npc",n="Seer Wiserunner",z="mulgore",s="At his cave northwest of Bloodhoof Village, Seer Wiserunner guides young tauren through sacred rites. The respected elder helps them seek spiritual wisdom in Mulgore.",h="At his cave northwest of Bloodhoof Village, Seer Wiserunner guides young tauren through sacred rites.",sec={{t="A Guide on the Sacred Path",b="Seer Wiserunner guides young tauren through their ancestral rites. After their first trials of strength and vision, they seek his counsel. He interprets visions of the Earth Mother and teaches them how to honor their forebears. His wisdom helps bind Mulgore’s scattered nomadic tribes into one community.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Seer Wiserunner?",a="Seer Wiserunner is a respected tauren elder who guides young initiates through the sacred Rites of the Earthmother.",al={"who is wiserunner","seer wiserunner","wiserunner tauren elder"},it="identity"},{q="What role does he play in Mulgore?",a="Seer Wiserunner mentors young tauren. After their early rites of vision, he receives the braves for the Rite of Wisdom and tests their readiness to serve their tribe and the Earth Mother.",al={"what does wiserunner do","seer wiserunner purpose","wiserunner role"},it="purpose"},{q="Where can Seer Wiserunner be found?",a="Seer Wiserunner can be found in a cave in the hills northwest of Bloodhoof Village. The vision from the Rite of Vision leads tauren youth there, and he sends them east of Thunder Bluff to the ancestral spirits of Red Rocks.",al={"where is wiserunner","seer wiserunner location","wiserunner coords"},gp=true},{q="Whose side is Seer Wiserunner on?",a="He is loyal to the tauren tribes and the Horde. Stationed in Mulgore, Seer Wiserunner guides young tauren through their sacred ancestral trials, upholding the venerable spiritual traditions of his people and honoring the wisdom of the Earth Mother.",al={"seer wiserunner faction","wiserunner side","is seer wiserunner horde"},it="side",add="f44673b7"},{q="What does Seer Wiserunner want?",a="He seeks to shepherd young tauren through their sacred spiritual milestones. By presiding over trials such as the Rite of Wisdom, he ensures that the next generation honors ancestral guidance and proves their worthiness before the spirits.",al={"seer wiserunner goal","what does wiserunner want","seer wiserunner motive"},it="wants",add="fbd19081"},{q="Why is Seer Wiserunner here?",a="He resides in the rolling hills of Mulgore to serve as a spiritual mentor. As tauren youths undertake their sacred rites of passage, he awaits them to test their insight and bestow ancestral wisdom upon those who complete their pilgrimage.",al={"why is seer wiserunner in mulgore","seer wiserunner location why","why is wiserunner here"},it="why_here",add="bf3ce735"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a young shu'halo venturing beyond Red Cloud Mesa, you can look to Seer Wiserunner for guidance on the ancestral journey toward spiritual maturity.",["class:Shaman"]="As a shaman, you can recognize Wiserunner as a keeper of ancient traditions who understands messages woven through the earth and sky."},kw={"camp narache","doomsayer wiserunner","mulgore","rite of vision","rite of wisdom","seer wiserunner","tauren elder"},rel={"quest:773","quest:767","zone:mulgore","topic:tauren"},src={"Doomsayer Wiserunner"}}
E["npc:elder-bloodhoof"] = {t="npc",n="Elder Bloodhoof",z="mulgore",s="During the Lunar Festival, Elder Bloodhoof rests just north of Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore. The Bloodhoof tribe honors him as a guardian spirit of its ancestors.",h="During the Lunar Festival, Elder Bloodhoof rests just north of Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore.",sec={{t="Spirit of the Ancestors",b="Near Bloodhoof Village, Elder Bloodhoof watches over his people. The tauren honor him as a spirit bound to their lineage and to the plains of Mulgore. During the Lunar Festival, respectful travelers seek his wisdom and receive his blessings. His presence keeps the guidance of dead chieftains and tribal forebears close to the Bloodhoof tribe.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Elder Bloodhoof?",a="Elder Bloodhoof is a ghostly tauren spirit who rests just north of Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore. The Bloodhoof tribe honors him as one of its ancestors and seeks his wisdom during the Lunar Festival.",al={"elder bloodhoof lore","who is the ghost outside bloodhoof village","elder bloodhoof identity"},it="identity"},{q="When can you see Elder Bloodhoof?",a="You can see Elder Bloodhoof during the annual Lunar Festival, when he appears to offer blessings.",al={"where is elder bloodhoof","elder bloodhoof missing","how to find elder bloodhoof"},gp=true},{q="What is his connection to Bloodhoof Village?",a="Elder Bloodhoof rests just north of Bloodhoof Village and watches over the Bloodhoof tribe. Tauren tradition holds ancestral spirits close to the places where their descendants live and gather.",al={"why is he near bloodhoof village","elder bloodhoof relation to cairne"},it="ties"},{q="Whose side is Elder Bloodhoof on?",a="Elder Bloodhoof is a revered ancestor of the Bloodhoof tauren and allied with the Horde. As one of the ancestral spirits honored during the Lunar Festival, he offers blessings and wisdom to travelers honoring his memory and his people's sacred traditions.",al={"elder bloodhoof faction","is elder bloodhoof horde","who does elder bloodhoof serve"},it="side",add="376494a8"},{q="What does Elder Bloodhoof want?",a="Elder Bloodhoof wishes to be remembered and honored by the living. When travelers seek out the ancient spirits of Kalimdor during the Lunar Festival, he grants his blessings, ancestral coins, and words of wisdom in exchange for your respect and recognition of the past.",al={"elder bloodhoof goal","what does bloodhoof the elder want","why talk to elder bloodhoof"},it="wants",add="f5643df0"},{q="Why is Elder Bloodhoof here in Mulgore?",a="His spirit lingers just north of Bloodhoof Village because these grassy plains are the ancestral home of his tribe. He watches over his descendants from beyond the veil of death, returning each year to the land where the Bloodhoof people live and herd.",al={"why is elder bloodhoof here","why is elder bloodhoof outside bloodhoof village","elder bloodhoof location reason"},it="why_here",add="2e395d43"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren, you can honor your tribal lineage by paying respects to Elder Bloodhoof, an ancestor revered by the Bloodhoof tribe.",["class:Shaman"]="As a shaman, you can look to Elder Bloodhoof as an ancestral spirit who guides and protects his descendants."},kw={"ancestor","bloodhoof village","elder bloodhoof","lunar festival","mulgore","red rocks","tauren spirit"},rel={"subzone:bloodhoof-village","quest:8673","zone:mulgore","subzone:red-rocks","topic:tauren"},src={"Elder Bloodhoof"}}
E["npc:gart-mistrunner"] = {t="npc",n="Gart Mistrunner",z="mulgore",s="At Camp Narache, Gart Mistrunner guides novice tauren called by the Earth Mother. He teaches their first rites of nature magic on the Red Cloud Mesa.",h="At Camp Narache, Gart Mistrunner guides novice tauren called by the Earth Mother.",sec={{t="Mentor of the Red Cloud Mesa",b="Gart Mistrunner watches over Camp Narache as the Shu'halo rekindle their ancient druidic path. New tauren druids are sent to him when they take their first steps on the Red Cloud Mesa, often with a note commending them to his care. Gart teaches them to commune with the natural world. They draw on nature's restorative power and Mu'sha's lunar radiance to heal allies and strike down threats to the land.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Gart Mistrunner?",a="Gart Mistrunner trains druids at Camp Narache in the Red Cloud Mesa of Mulgore. He teaches novice tauren their first spells and rites.",al={"who is gart","gart mistrunner lore","gart mistrunner"},it="identity"},{q="What spells does he teach newly awakened druids?",a="Gart teaches beginners essential nature abilities, including the healing spell Healing Touch and the offensive nature magic Moonfire.",al={"what does gart teach","gart mistrunner training","druid spells camp narache"},gp=true},{q="What is his connection to the Mistrunner clan?",a="Mistrunner is Gart's family name. No lore describes a Mistrunner tribe or says more about his kin; he is known as the druid trainer of Camp Narache.",al={"mistrunner clan","who are mistrunners"},it="ties"},{q="Whose side is Gart Mistrunner on?",a="Gart Mistrunner is devoted to the tauren tribes and stands with the Horde. Stationed in the heart of Red Cloud Mesa at Camp Narache, he works within his ancestral homeland to safeguard tauren traditions, nurturing young practitioners who use nature's gifts to protect the land and serve their people.",al={"gart mistrunner faction","who does gart serve","gart mistrunner horde"},it="side",add="fd7992e7"},{q="Why is Gart Mistrunner here?",a="Gart resides at Camp Narache to instruct novice tauren who hear the call of the wild. As a druid trainer on the Red Cloud Mesa, he introduces newly awakened initiates to the teachings of nature, guiding their early spiritual and martial growth before they venture out into the wider plains of Mulgore.",al={"why is gart in camp narache","gart mistrunner purpose","what is gart mistrunner doing here"},it="why_here",add="4b2be6b9"},{q="What does Gart Mistrunner want?",a="Gart seeks to foster harmony between the tauren and the Earth Mother by training new druids in natural balance. He desires to see young initiates master early rites such as rejuvenating healing and balance incantations, preparing them to heal the land and defend their tribe against encroaching threats.",al={"gart mistrunner goals","what does gart want","gart mistrunner motives"},it="wants",add="8def0ffd"}},ang={["class:Druid"]="As a tauren druid, Gart Mistrunner is the first master to shape your communion with the wilds and the gifts of the Earth Mother.",["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren, you see Gart Mistrunner pass the Shu'halo's ancient druidic traditions to the youth of Camp Narache."},kw={"camp narache","druid trainer","gart mistrunner","mistrunner","mulgore","red cloud mesa","tauren druid"},rel={"subzone:camp-narache","zone:mulgore","topic:druid","topic:tauren","quest:3094"},src={"Gart Mistrunner"}}
E["npc:greatmother-hawkwind"] = {t="npc",n="Greatmother Hawkwind",z="mulgore",s="Beside the well on Red Cloud Mesa, Greatmother Hawkwind guides young tauren. As mother of Chief Hawkwind, Camp Narache's leader, she holds to the tribe's shared duty despite her age.",h="Beside the well on Red Cloud Mesa, Greatmother Hawkwind guides young tauren.",sec={{t="Elder of the Mesa",b="Greatmother Hawkwind spends her days beside Camp Narache's fresh water well, guiding young tauren as they begin their rites of passage. Age has slowed her stride, but she still takes part in village life. She believes every member of the tribe, young or old, must help sustain the community. She often asks young tauren to fetch water and help with simple chores.",sp=0},{t="A Mother's Guidance",b="Greatmother Hawkwind is mother to Chief Hawkwind, who leads Camp Narache. She offers counsel as threats face Red Cloud Mesa. Her son organizes hunters and warriors against Bristleback quilboar and local predators. She tends to the settlement's moral heart, reminding the newly awakened that strength begins with humility and service to kin.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Greatmother Hawkwind?",a="Greatmother Hawkwind is an elderly tauren matriarch at Camp Narache on Red Cloud Mesa. She is the mother of Chief Hawkwind and guides young tauren.",al={"greatmother hawkwind lore","who is greatmother hawkwind","camp narache elder"},it="identity"},{q="Where can she be found?",a="She sits beside the water well southeast of Camp Narache, on Red Cloud Mesa in southwestern Mulgore.",al={"greatmother hawkwind location","where is greatmother hawkwind"},it="place",gp=true},{q="What tasks does she ask of young tauren?",a="Greatmother Hawkwind asks young tauren to gather water from the village well and deliver it to others in the camp. The chore teaches that no task is beneath a true servant of the tribe.",al={"greatmother hawkwind quest","a humble task"},it="wants",gp=true},{q="What is her relation to Chief Hawkwind?",a="Greatmother Hawkwind is the mother of Chief Hawkwind, who oversees the defense and training of young braves at Camp Narache.",al={"chief hawkwind mother","is she related to chief hawkwind"},it="ties"},{q="Whose side is Greatmother Hawkwind on?",a="Greatmother Hawkwind is devoted to her people, the tauren of the Horde. As the elder matriarch of Camp Narache, her loyalty lies entirely with her tribe, her family, and the young braves setting out to serve the ancestors and the Earth Mother.",al={"greatmother hawkwind faction","who does greatmother hawkwind serve","greatmother hawkwind allegiance"},it="side",add="f701fe56"},{q="What does Greatmother Hawkwind want?",a="Despite her advanced age, Greatmother Hawkwind insists on doing her part to care for Camp Narache. She tends to the settlement's needs, seeing to simple domestic duties such as hauling fresh water from the village well, and she asks young tauren to lend a helping hand with these humble everyday chores.",al={"what does greatmother hawkwind need","greatmother hawkwind goal","why talk to greatmother hawkwind"},it="wants",add="8d52ad78"},{q="Why is Greatmother Hawkwind here?",a="Greatmother Hawkwind resides in Camp Narache as the village elder and the mother of Chief Hawkwind. You will find her by the settlement's central water well, diligently working to support her tribe and watching over the young hunters and shamans beginning their journeys on Red Cloud Mesa.",al={"why is greatmother hawkwind in camp narache","greatmother hawkwind location why","why is she at the well"},it="why_here",add="2db356dc"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a young tauren beginning your journey on Red Cloud Mesa, you can see the Earth Mother's values in Greatmother Hawkwind: respect for elders, solidarity with your community, and humble service."},kw={"a humble task","camp narache","chief hawkwind","greatmother hawkwind","mulgore","red cloud mesa","tauren elder"},rel={"npc:chief-hawkwind","subzone:camp-narache","quest:752","topic:tauren","zone:mulgore"},src={"Greatmother Hawkwind"}}
E["npc:grull-hawkwind"] = {t="npc",n="Grull Hawkwind",z="mulgore",s="At Camp Narache on Red Cloud Mesa, Grull Hawkwind guides young tauren through their first trials. As Chief Hawkwind’s son, he prepares hunters and warriors to test their resolve and honor their ancestors.",h="At Camp Narache on Red Cloud Mesa, Grull Hawkwind guides young tauren through their first trials.",sec={{t="A Chieftain's Son",b="At Camp Narache, Grull Hawkwind helps prepare the tribe’s youth. The son of Chief Hawkwind, he guides new arrivals on Red Cloud Mesa and judges whether they are ready to serve the Shu'halo. He sets trials of stamina, tracking, and survival against plainstriders and mountain cougars. Through these trials, young tauren learn that balance and respect for nature are foundations of life in Mulgore.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Grull Hawkwind?",a="Grull Hawkwind guides young tauren at Camp Narache on Red Cloud Mesa. He is the son of Chief Hawkwind and mentors those beginning their journey.",al={"grull hawkwind role","who is grull","grull hawkwind lore"},it="identity"},{q="What role does Grull Hawkwind play for young adventurers?",a="Grull Hawkwind assesses novice tauren’s basic training and gives them early trials in hunting and survival across the surrounding plains.",al={"grull quests","what does grull do","grull hawkwind purpose"},it="purpose"},{q="Is Grull related to the camp's leadership?",a="Yes. Grull Hawkwind is the son of Chief Hawkwind, the chieftain of Camp Narache. He upholds his father’s traditions and helps prepare the camp’s defenders.",al={"chief hawkwind son","grull hawkwind family","is grull related to the chief"},it="ties"},{q="Whose side is Grull Hawkwind on?",a="Grull Hawkwind stands proudly with the tauren tribes and the Horde. Residing in Camp Narache, he serves his people under the leadership of his father and High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof, dedicated to helping young tauren grow into capable defenders of their ancestral homeland in Mulgore.",al={"grull hawkwind faction","who does grull serve","grull hawkwind allegiance"},it="side",add="36d17655"},{q="What does Grull Hawkwind want?",a="Grull wants to see young tauren prove their strength and devotion to the tribe. He tests incoming youths to ensure they possess the fortitude, discipline, and hunting skills necessary to survive the wilderness and protect Camp Narache from nearby threats.",al={"what does grull want","grull hawkwind goals","why does grull give quests"},it="wants",add="56962b2d"},{q="Why is Grull Hawkwind here in Camp Narache?",a="Camp Narache is the ancestral testing ground for youth coming of age on the Red Cloud Mesa, and it is governed by Grull's father, Chief Hawkwind. Grull remains here to help guide new initiates through their opening rites and support his family's leadership over the camp.",al={"why is grull here","grull camp narache role","why is grull in the red cloud mesa"},it="why_here",add="379dd378"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a young tauren on Red Cloud Mesa, you may meet Grull Hawkwind, one of the first braves to test you and introduce you to Shu'halo traditions.",["class:Hunter"]="As a hunter in Mulgore, you face Grull Hawkwind’s early trials, which test your patience and skill against the local wildlife."},kw={"camp narache","chief hawkwind","grull hawkwind","mulgore","red cloud mesa","rite of strength","tauren"},rel={"npc:chief-hawkwind","subzone:camp-narache","zone:mulgore","topic:tauren","quest:757","quest:747","quest:780"},src={"Grull Hawkwind"}}
E["npc:harb-clawhoof"] = {t="npc",n="Harb Clawhoof",z="mulgore",s="Near Bloodhoof Village, Harb Clawhoof tends the tauren’s kodos. He offers them to worthy tauren riders and trusted champions of Thunder Bluff.",h="Near Bloodhoof Village, Harb Clawhoof tends the tauren’s kodos.",sec={{t="Breeder of the Great Kodo",b="Harb Clawhoof tends the kodos near Bloodhoof Village. These great beasts carry heavy loads across long distances and are vital to tauren travel and work on the plains. Harb cares for each one, trains it, and pairs it with riders respected by the tauren people.",sp=0},{t="Steeds of the Plains",b="Tauren earn the trust needed to ride Harb’s kodos, while outsiders face closer scrutiny. Their size and temperament make kodos difficult for those unfamiliar with them to guide. Non-tauren allies must prove their loyalty and earn exalted standing with Thunder Bluff before riding one of Harb’s mounts.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where can I find Harb Clawhoof?",a="Harb Clawhoof tends the kodo corral on the outskirts of Bloodhoof Village in central Mulgore, just south of Thunder Bluff.",al={"harb clawhoof location","where is harb clawhoof","kodo vendor location"},it="place",gp=true},{q="What does Harb Clawhoof sell?",a="Harb sells several kinds of kodo mounts, including brown and gray kodos and heavily armored great kodos for seasoned riders.",al={"what does harb sell","harb mounts","kodo vendor wares"},it="purpose",gp=true},{q="Can non-tauren buy kodos from Harb?",a="Yes. Tauren can buy kodos directly, while other Horde races must earn exalted standing with Thunder Bluff and prove themselves to the tribe before Harb entrusts them with one.",al={"can orcs buy kodos","can trolls ride kodos","kodo reputation requirement"},gp=true},{q="Why are kodos so important to the tauren?",a="Kodos suit the tauren’s nomadic life and the open plains of central Kalimdor. They carry burdens, serve as war mounts, and transport trade across the rugged land.",al={"tauren kodo lore","why kodos"}},{q="Who is Harb Clawhoof?",a="Harb Clawhoof is a tauren kodo breeder and mount merchant stationed in Bloodhoof Village. Respected among his people for raising and tending to the mighty beasts of Mulgore, he ensures that capable champions have steadfast mounts suited for the rigors of travel and battle across Kalimdor.",al={"who is harb","harb clawhoof lore","who is harb clawhoof"},it="identity",add="2e2e2604"},{q="Whose side is Harb Clawhoof on?",a="Harb is fiercely loyal to Thunder Bluff and the Horde. While he gladly provides mounts to his fellow tauren, outsiders from other Horde races must prove their dedication and earn great standing with Thunder Bluff before he will trust them with one of his prized kodos.",al={"harb clawhoof faction","is harb clawhoof horde","harb clawhoof allegiance"},it="side",add="ceacb5fa"},{q="What does Harb Clawhoof want?",a="Harb wants to raise strong, reliable kodos and see them entrusted only to riders who truly respect them. To the tauren, the kodo is not merely a beast of burden but the backbone of their civilization, and Harb works to preserve that sacred partnership.",al={"what does harb want","harb clawhoof goal","harb clawhoof purpose"},it="wants",add="0020906d"},{q="Why is Harb Clawhoof in Bloodhoof Village?",a="Bloodhoof Village is a central gathering place for young tauren and travelers journeying outward from Camp Narache into the wider plains of Mulgore. Harb maintains his stables here where there is ample open pasture to graze and rear strong kodo herds for the defense and prosperity of the tribes.",al={"why is harb in bloodhoof","why is harb here","harb bloodhoof village"},it="why_here",add="efcee9d4"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren, Harb Clawhoof offers you a mount tied to your heritage, useful for travel across Mulgore and bound to your nomadic traditions.",["faction:Horde"]="As an orc, troll, or Forsaken, earning a kodo from Harb marks honor and friendship recognized by the elders of Thunder Bluff."},kw={"bloodhoof village","harb clawhoof","kodo trainer","kodo vendor","mount vendor","mulgore kodo","thunder bluff mounts"},rel={"subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore","zone:thunderbluff","topic:tauren","topic:horde"},src={"Harb Clawhoof"}}
E["npc:harken-windtotem"] = {t="npc",n="Harken Windtotem",z="mulgore",s="In Bloodhoof Village, Harken Windtotem guides young hunters across Mulgore’s plains. He teaches the traditions of his people and asks hunters and travelers to prove their tracking and foraging skills.",h="In Bloodhoof Village, Harken Windtotem guides young hunters across Mulgore’s plains.",sec={{t="Huntsman of Bloodhoof",b="Near the heart of Bloodhoof Village, Harken Windtotem guides those learning the tauren hunting ways. Life on Mulgore’s plains calls for respect for the natural world and the skills needed to sustain a tribe. Harken tests young hunters by sending them after the swoops that circle the mesas. They gather the birds’ quills, showing patience, aim, and care in taking only what they need.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Harken Windtotem?",a="Harken Windtotem is a tauren huntsman in Bloodhoof Village, where he guides travelers and young hunters in the tracking and hunting traditions of the tauren.",al={"harken windtotem lore","who is harken"},it="identity"},{q="Where can Harken Windtotem be found?",a="Harken Windtotem can be found in Bloodhoof Village, south of Thunder Bluff in central Mulgore.",al={"harken location","where is harken windtotem"},it="place",gp=true},{q="What tasks does Harken give to adventurers?",a="Harken Windtotem sends hunters into Mulgore’s grasslands to hunt wild swoops and gather their sturdy flight quills.",al={"harken windtotem quests","swoop hunting quest"},it="purpose"},{q="Is Harken related to Bovan Windtotem?",a="Harken Windtotem and Bovan Windtotem share a clan name, suggesting a tribal kinship or family connection. Harken focuses on local hunts in Mulgore.",al={"is harken related to bovan","bovan windtotem relation"},it="ties"},{q="Whose side is Harken Windtotem on?",a="Harken Windtotem stands loyally with the tauren tribes and the Horde. Based in Bloodhoof Village, his focus lies entirely on supporting the local settlement and guiding young hunters as they work to sustain their people in Mulgore.",al={"harken windtotem faction","who does harken serve","is harken horde"},it="side",add="b6c1a716"},{q="What does Harken Windtotem want?",a="Harken seeks capable hunters to thin the local swoop population and gather their quills. The plains of Mulgore demand self-reliance, and by putting fledglings to the test against swoops, he helps provide useful materials while honing the skills of young travelers.",al={"what does harken want","harken windtotem goal","why does harken give quests"},it="wants",add="fffafcb2"},{q="Why is Harken Windtotem in Bloodhoof Village?",a="As a member of the Windtotem tribe residing in Bloodhoof Village, Harken oversees hunting efforts across the central plains. He remains in the village to direct passing hunters, ensuring that the wildlife remains balanced and the settlement has the resources it needs.",al={"why is harken here","harken windtotem purpose","why is harken in bloodhoof"},it="why_here",add="4b75134c"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren, you can see Harken Windtotem’s respect for the ancestral ways of hunting: discipline and self-sufficiency on the plains, with honor for the beasts of the Earth Mother.",["class:Hunter"]="As a hunter passing through Bloodhoof Village, you can test your marksmanship and gathering skills by bringing down airborne swoops."},kw={"bloodhoof village","harken windtotem","mulgore","swoop hunting","tauren","windtotem"},rel={"subzone:bloodhoof-village","quest:761","zone:mulgore","topic:tauren"},src={"Harken Windtotem"}}
E["npc:lanka-farshot"] = {t="npc",n="Lanka Farshot",z="mulgore",s="At Camp Narache, Lanka Farshot guides young tauren onto the hunter's path. He is stationed within the Red Cloud Mesa and teaches tracking, marksmanship, and survival on Mulgore's plains.",h="At Camp Narache, Lanka Farshot guides young tauren onto the hunter's path.",sec={{t="Mentor of the Red Cloud Mesa",b="At Camp Narache, Lanka Farshot welcomes new tauren called to the hunter's path. Fresh recruits arrive with an etched note that sends them to seek his counsel. Under his guidance, they learn ranged combat, how to track prey, and respect for the wild beasts roaming Mulgore's grassy plains. He prepares each young hunter to scout and provide for the tribe.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Lanka Farshot?",a="Lanka Farshot trains tauren hunters at Camp Narache on the Red Cloud Mesa in Mulgore. He guides young hunters as they begin their training.",al={"who is lanka","lanka farshot npc","camp narache hunter"},it="identity"},{q="What quests does Lanka Farshot offer?",a="An etched note directs young tauren hunters to Lanka Farshot when they arrive at Camp Narache. He guides them through the first steps of the hunter's craft.",al={"lanka farshot quests","hunter quests camp narache"},gp=true},{q="Is Lanka Farshot related to Mixie Farshot?",a="The shared surname gives no evidence that Lanka Farshot is related to the goblin Mixie Farshot.",al={"is lanka related to mixie","mixie farshot relation"},it="ties"},{q="Whose side is Lanka Farshot on?",a="Lanka Farshot is loyal to the tauren tribes and the Horde. Stationed within the safe haven of Camp Narache, he serves his people by guiding young tauren hunters as they learn to survive and contribute to the united tribes under High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof.",al={"lanka farshot faction","who does lanka farshot serve","is lanka horde"},it="side",add="3509322d"},{q="Why is Lanka Farshot here at Camp Narache?",a="Lanka resides in Camp Narache to instruct novice hunters beginning their trials on the Red Cloud Mesa. The plains of Mulgore demand keen senses and steady aim, and Lanka stands ready to pass down the essential arts of tracking and marksmanship to the next generation.",al={"why is lanka here","lanka farshot location purpose","what brings lanka to camp narache"},it="why_here",add="fd40a21a"},{q="What does Lanka Farshot want?",a="Lanka seeks to ensure that every aspiring tauren hunter masters the ways of the wild. He wants you to respect your quarry, hone your marksmanship, and carry on the proud traditions necessary to feed and defend your people across the grasslands.",al={"lanka farshot goal","what is lanka trying to do","lanka farshot purpose"},it="wants",add="2df75887"}},ang={["class:Hunter"]="As a tauren hunter beginning training in Mulgore, you meet Lanka Farshot as your first mentor. He teaches marksmanship and survival in the wild.",["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren, you see Lanka Farshot teach hunting as a duty to protect the tribe and live in balance with the plains."},kw={"camp narache","hunter trainer","lanka farshot","mulgore","red cloud mesa","tauren hunter"},rel={"subzone:camp-narache","zone:mulgore","quest:3092","quest:6065","topic:tauren","topic:horde"},src={"Lanka Farshot"}}
E["npc:lorekeeper-raintotem"] = {t="npc",n="Lorekeeper Raintotem",z="mulgore",s="South of Red Rocks, Lorekeeper Raintotem tends Mulgore’s sacred burial grounds. He asks travelers to drive off Bristleback quilboar disturbing them.",h="South of Red Rocks, Lorekeeper Raintotem tends Mulgore’s sacred burial grounds.",sec={{t="Watcher of Red Rocks",b="Lorekeeper Raintotem tends his people’s sacred burial grounds near Red Rocks in Mulgore. Bristleback Interlopers disturb the resting place, and he asks travelers to drive them away so the ancestors may rest in peace.",sp=0},{t="Defender of Ancestral Grounds",b="Lorekeeper Raintotem watches over the burial sites at Red Rocks. Bristleback quilboar disturb the resting places of tauren chieftains and shamans. Raintotem calls on travelers to restore quiet and dignity to the grounds and their dead.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where can I find Lorekeeper Raintotem?",a="Find Lorekeeper Raintotem in northern Mulgore, just south of Red Rocks along the path toward the ancestral cliffs.",al={"raintotem location","where is lorekeeper raintotem","find raintotem mulgore"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why does Raintotem seek help with the burial grounds?",a="Raintotem asks travelers to drive Bristleback Interlopers from Red Rocks, where they ravage the sacred tauren burial ground.",al={"raintotem sacred burial","why help raintotem","red rocks burial grounds"},it="purpose"},{q="Why does Raintotem need help at Red Rocks?",a="Red Rocks is a sacred tauren burial ground, and Bristleback quilboar are ravaging it. Raintotem asks capable travelers to restore the dishonored ancestral grounds.",al={"red rocks problem","why sacred burial quest"},it="threat"},{q="Who is Lorekeeper Raintotem?",a="Lorekeeper Raintotem serves his tauren community, preserving ancestral traditions and ensuring the fallen receive sacred burial.",al={"who is raintotem","lorekeeper raintotem info","about raintotem"},it="identity",add="4ada6d87"},{q="Whose side is Lorekeeper Raintotem on?",a="Lorekeeper Raintotem is wholly loyal to the tauren tribes of Mulgore and the Horde under High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof. He serves as an honored cultural elder, assisting any Horde adventurer who shows respect to the sacred rites and ancestors of his people.",al={"raintotem faction","is raintotem horde","raintotem allegiance"},it="side",add="29f679b7"},{q="Why is Lorekeeper Raintotem here?",a="Raintotem keeps watch south of Red Rocks because it is the sacred ancestral burial grounds of the tauren tribes. He remains stationed nearby to protect the hallowed soil, guide pilgrims seeking ancestral blessing, and confront threats that disturb the spirits resting within the red stone cliffs.",al={"why is raintotem at red rocks","raintotem location reason","why is lorekeeper raintotem in mulgore"},it="why_here",add="6a12fd3c"},{q="What does Lorekeeper Raintotem want?",a="He tends to the ancestral burial grounds at Red Rocks and asks adventurers to drive away the Bristleback Interlopers disturbing them.",al={"what does raintotem want","raintotem goals","what is raintotem asking for"},it="wants",add="88640b34"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren, helping Raintotem defend Red Rocks honors your ancestors and preserves your people’s burial traditions.",["class:Shaman"]="As a shaman, helping Raintotem guard Red Rocks serves your duty to the restless dead, ensuring ancestral spirits receive peace and reverence."},kw={"lorekeeper raintotem","mulgore","raintotem","red rocks","sacred burial"},rel={"subzone:red-rocks","quest:833","zone:mulgore","topic:tauren"},src={"Lorekeeper Raintotem"}}
E["npc:minor-manifestation-of-earth"] = {t="npc",n="Minor Manifestation of Earth",z="mulgore",s="At sacred stones in Mulgore and Durotar, Minor Manifestation of Earth waits unseen. Fledgling shamans who drink Earth Sapta can meet the spirit and receive rough quartz for carving their first earth totem.",h="At sacred stones in Mulgore and Durotar, Minor Manifestation of Earth waits unseen.",sec={{t="Voice of the Stone",b="Earth spirits dwell beneath dirt and stone, but reaching them takes a deliberate rite. At Kodo Rock in Mulgore and Spirit Rock in Durotar, Minor Manifestations of Earth linger beyond ordinary sight, surrounded by lesser elemental presences. Only initiates who drink Earth Sapta, a draught brewed by shamanic mentors, can cross the veil between the physical world and the elements. When an initiate makes contact, the spirit grants the earth's blessings and a piece of rough quartz. The novice can use it as the spiritual focus for making an Earth Totem.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why can't I see the Minor Manifestation of Earth?",a="Minor Manifestation of Earth remains unseen by ordinary sight on the elemental plane. Drink Earth Sapta from your shaman trainer to perceive and speak with elemental spirits.",al={"cannot see manifestation","where is the earth spirit","earth manifestation invisible"},gp=true},{q="Where can these earth spirits be found?",a="These earth spirits appear at Kodo Rock in Mulgore and Spirit Rock in Durotar. Both places are traditional proving grounds for young tauren, orc, and troll shamans.",al={"minor manifestation location","where to find manifestation of earth"},it="place",gp=true},{q="What is the spirit's purpose during the rite?",a="The manifestation tests and acknowledges your bond with the earth element. After you speak with it, the spirit gives you rough quartz to take back to your shaman trainer for making an Earth Totem.",al={"what does manifestation do","manifestation quest purpose"},it="purpose"},{q="What are the smaller creatures surrounding it?",a="Lesser manifestations of earth gather around the central spirit. They are minor echoes of the element drawn to the sacred grounds, part of the natural communion, and cannot be directly engaged.",al={"lesser manifestations","small earth elementals around it"}},{q="Who is the Minor Manifestation of Earth?",a="The Minor Manifestation of Earth is an elemental earth spirit summoned and revered at sacred grounds like Kodo Rock in Mulgore and Spirit Rock in Durotar. Invisible to ordinary sight, it reveals itself only to shamans who have expanded their spiritual awareness through ceremonial draughts such as Earth Sapta.",al={"what is the minor manifestation of earth","who is manifestation of earth","minor manifestation earth lore"},it="identity",add="40387732"},{q="Whose side is the Minor Manifestation of Earth on?",a="It serves neither the Horde nor the Alliance. As an embodiment of the elements, it aligns with the balance of the natural world and answers the petitions of initiates who demonstrate the respect and spiritual clarity required to commune with the earth.",al={"minor manifestation earth faction","is minor manifestation of earth friendly","which side is the earth manifestation"},it="side",add="f34b34da"},{q="What does the Minor Manifestation of Earth want?",a="It desires communion with those walking the path of the shaman. When approached by an initiate who has consumed the Earth Sapta, it seeks to test their resolve and bestow a piece of Rough Quartz to form the bedrock of their first totem.",al={"what does manifestation of earth want","earth manifestation desire","what does the minor manifestation seek"},it="wants",add="95a7d1ef"},{q="Why is the Minor Manifestation of Earth here?",a="It resides at sacred places of elemental power, such as Kodo Rock in Mulgore and Spirit Rock in Durotar, to serve as a bridge between the living land and prospective shamans undergoing the Call of Earth.",al={"why is the minor manifestation of earth at kodo rock","why is manifestation of earth here","why is the earth spirit here"},it="why_here",add="17161102"}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="As a young Horde shaman, you commune with Minor Manifestation of Earth under the influence of Earth Sapta to earn your first totem.",["race:Tauren"]="As a young tauren, you travel east across Mulgore to Kodo Rock to commune with this manifestation as part of your ancestral spiritual trials."},kw={"call of earth","earth sapta","earth totem","kodo rock","minor manifestation of earth","rough quartz","spirit rock"},rel={"quest:1520","subzone:kodo-rock","subzone:spirit-rock","zone:mulgore","zone:durotar"},src={"Minor Manifestation of Earth"}}
E["npc:morin-cloudstalker"] = {t="npc",n="Morin Cloudstalker",z="mulgore",s="Along the eastern road from Bloodhoof Village, Morin Cloudstalker watches over Mulgore. The tauren scout keeps an eye on trade routes and investigates threats to travelers.",h="Along the eastern road from Bloodhoof Village, Morin Cloudstalker watches over Mulgore.",sec={{t="Watcher on the Southern Trail",b="Morin Cloudstalker watches the trail east toward the Barrens. He scouts the road and guards those traveling through Mulgore. The plains are usually peaceful, but trade routes and shifting territories have brought opportunists and bandits close to the tauren homeland.",sp=0},{t="Investigating the Caravan",b="Morin's scouts attacked and burned a Venture Co. caravan on Stonebull Lake's northern shore. Goblin salvagers appeared near the burned wagons, so he sent adventurers to inspect the surviving supply crates and learn what the Venture Co. had been hauling.",sp=0},{t="Defending Against Venture Co.",b="The Venture Company has established operations in Mulgore, troubling Morin beyond the threat of common brigands. He sends Horde adventurers against its logging and mining outposts, including Supervisor Fizsprocket, to curb the damage to the sacred plains.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Where can I find Morin Cloudstalker?",a="Morin Cloudstalker stands beside the dirt trail east of Bloodhoof Village, on the road toward the Barrens.",al={"morin location","where is morin","morin cloudstalker map"},it="place",gp=true},{q="What is Morin's role in Mulgore?",a="Morin watches travel through southern Mulgore and organizes defenses against bandits and corporate intruders.",al={"who is morin cloudstalker","morin role"},it="purpose"},{q="Why is Morin investigating the ruined caravan?",a="Morin's scouts attacked and burned a Venture Co. caravan near Stonebull Lake. After goblin salvagers appeared at the wreckage, he asked adventurers to inspect the surviving crates and find out what the Venture Co. was moving.",al={"ravaged caravan quest","caravan attack mulgore"},it="motive"},{q="Why does Morin want the Venture Company stopped?",a="The Venture Company is stripping Mulgore's natural resources with machinery and deforestation. Morin wants its overseers, including Supervisor Fizsprocket, stopped.",al={"venture co mulgore","why kill fizsprocket"},it="threat"},{q="Who does Morin report to?",a="Morin acts on behalf of Bloodhoof Village's elders and chieftains, and the wider tauren leadership across Mulgore.",al={"morin allegiance","who sent morin"},it="ties"},{q="Does Morin offer quests to Alliance players?",a="No. Morin is loyal to the Horde and interacts only with Horde adventurers.",al={"can alliance talk to morin","morin faction"},gp=true},{q="Who is Morin Cloudstalker?",a="Morin Cloudstalker is a tauren scout and protector watching over Mulgore. Positioned along the road leading east from Bloodhoof Village toward the Barrens, he keeps a sharp eye on threats creeping into ancestral tauren lands, dispatching capable Horde adventurers to safeguard the plains from raiders and exploiters.",al={"morin cloudstalker who is he","who is morin","morin lore"},it="identity",add="2ee6eb23"},{q="Whose side is Morin Cloudstalker on?",a="Morin is fiercely loyal to Thunder Bluff and the Horde. He guards the heartland of the tauren people and only offers his guidance and tasks to fellow Horde travelers willing to defend Mulgore's fragile peace.",al={"morin cloudstalker faction","morin alliance or horde","is morin horde"},it="side",add="b362fb80"},{q="What does Morin Cloudstalker want?",a="Morin seeks to protect the roads and natural resources of Mulgore. He sends adventurers to search the Venture Co. caravan his scouts burned and to stop the destructive operations of the Venture Company, particularly demanding an end to Supervisor Fizsprocket's exploitative mining in the hills.",al={"what does morin want","morin goals","morin tasks"},it="wants",add="7047ff15"},{q="Why is Morin Cloudstalker stationed here?",a="Morin is stationed along the road east of Bloodhoof Village to watch over travel through Mulgore. This critical junction allows him to monitor traveling caravans and intercept encroaching dangers—such as Venture Company defilers—before they reach Bloodhoof Village.",al={"why is morin here","why is morin on the road","morin location reason"},it="why_here",add="9ad7af70"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren traveling out from Bloodhoof Village, you can see Morin guarding your homeland against bandits and corporate defilers.",["faction:Horde"]="Morin gives Horde champions an early chance to protect the supplies and sacred lands of their new tauren allies."},kw={"bloodhoof village","great gate","morin cloudstalker","mulgore","ravaged caravan","supervisor fizsprocket","venture co"},rel={"quest:749","quest:764","quest:765","subzone:bloodhoof-village","subzone:ravaged-caravan","zone:mulgore","topic:tauren"},src={"Morin Cloudstalker"}}
E["npc:mull-thunderhorn"] = {t="npc",n="Mull Thunderhorn",z="mulgore",s="In Bloodhoof Village, Mull Thunderhorn watches over Mulgore’s sacred wells. He serves the Earth Mother and guards the balance of the land. Outside exploitation threatens the waters, and he guides young tauren in cleansing the ancestral springs.",h="In Bloodhoof Village, Mull Thunderhorn watches over Mulgore’s sacred wells.",sec={{t="Guardian of the Sacred Waters",b="Mull Thunderhorn watches over Mulgore’s spiritual well-being from Bloodhoof Village. The nomadic tribes have gathered under High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof to make a permanent home, but greed and reckless expansion already threaten their lands. Mull guides young braves setting out from Camp Narache and calls on them to protect their ancestors’ vital resources.",sp=0},{t="Tainted Wells and the Venture Company",b="Mull Thunderhorn faces sickness in the Winterhoof, Thunderhorn, and Wildmane water wells. The corruption comes from goblins of the Venture Company, a commercial outfit cutting into the surrounding hills and pouring poisons into the local water table. Mull sends young tauren to prepare purification totems and cleanse each ancestral well before the corruption spreads through the land and its wildlife.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is Mull Thunderhorn?",a="Mull Thunderhorn watches over the sacred water wells in Bloodhoof Village, Mulgore. He guides rites to keep the earth and its waters pure.",al={"mull thunderhorn lore","who is mull","bloodhoof village mull"},it="identity"},{q="Why is Mull Thunderhorn worried about the water wells?",a="The ancestral wells, including Winterhoof, Thunderhorn, and Wildmane, provide life-giving water for Mulgore’s tribes and wildlife. Nearby operations have tainted the springs with runoff and pollution, threatening the balance of the plains.",al={"why are wells poisoned","water wells mulgore","winterhoof well poisoned"},it="why_here"},{q="Who is polluting the water wells in Mulgore?",a="Goblins of the Venture Company are polluting Mulgore’s water wells. The commercial outfit is establishing mining and logging operations around the edges of Mulgore, and its work sends toxins into the local water table.",al={"who poisoned the water","venture co in mulgore","who tainted the wells"},it="threat"},{q="Why do Mull's purification quests only involve tauren?",a="Mulgore’s wells hold ancestral and spiritual meaning for the tauren tribes, including the Thunderhorn, Winterhoof, and Wildmane clans. Their cleansing is a sacred rite of passage and a duty for maturing tauren.",al={"tauren only quests","why only tauren","mull thunderhorn requirements"},it="why_me"},{q="How are the water wells purified?",a="Mull asks travelers to gather totems consecrated with offerings from the land, such as paws, talons, claws, and teeth from Mulgore’s beasts. The totems are brought to the fouled wells to channel spiritual cleansing and wash away unnatural toxins.",al={"how to cleanse wells","cleansing totems","cleaning the water"},it="purpose"},{q="What is the Rite of Vision?",a="The Rite of Vision is a spiritual journey for young tauren to commune with their ancestors and animal spirits. Initiates go to Zarlman Two-Moons in Bloodhoof Village for this visionary trial, one of the Rites of the Earthmother.",al={"rite of vision quest","what is rite of vision"}},{q="Whose side is Mull Thunderhorn on?",a="Mull stands loyally with his people and the Horde, devoted to the tribes dwelling in Mulgore. As a respected member of Bloodhoof Village, he watches over the ancestral plains and answers the spiritual call to protect his kin and their sacred lands from outside corruption.",al={"mull thunderhorn faction","is mull horde","who does mull serve"},it="side",add="7b9ffdeb"},{q="What does Mull Thunderhorn want?",a="Mull wants to restore and cleanse Mulgore's sacred water wells, including the Winterhoof, Thunderhorn, and Wildmane sources. He seeks trustworthy tauren to craft traditional totems and perform the vital purification rites needed to undo the foul contamination threatening the land's lifeblood.",al={"what does mull want","mull thunderhorn goal","why does mull need help"},it="wants",add="be00c091"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For tauren, Mull Thunderhorn carries tribal memory and ancestral duty. He guides young braves in protecting the sacred plains from greedy outsiders.",["class:Shaman"]="As a shaman, you recognize Mull’s reliance on elemental harmony. His cleansing totems call on ancestral spirits to mend waters harmed by mechanical greed.",["class:Druid"]="As a druid, you share Mull’s concern as corrupted wells poison the balance of Mulgore’s wildlife.",["faction:Horde"]="As a member of the Horde, you can see the strain between its people and unaligned organizations like the Venture Company, which takes Kalimdor’s riches without consent."},kw={"bloodhoof village","mulgore","mull thunderhorn","poison water","thunderhorn totem","venture company","water wells","wildmane cleansing","winterhoof cleansing"},rel={"zone:mulgore","subzone:bloodhoof-village","quest:748","quest:754","quest:756","quest:758","topic:venture-company","topic:tauren"},src={"Mull Thunderhorn"}}
E["npc:ruul-eagletalon"] = {t="npc",n="Ruul Eagletalon",z="mulgore",s="At Bloodhoof Village, Ruul Eagletalon watches the settlement’s borders. He keeps an eye on Mulgore’s wildlife, especially the aggressive Windfury harpies pressing into tauren lands.",h="At Bloodhoof Village, Ruul Eagletalon watches the settlement’s borders.",sec={{t="Watcher of Bloodhoof",b="Ruul Eagletalon tends to Bloodhoof Village’s security as young braves arrive from Camp Narache. Mulgore is a peaceful homeland beside the arid wastes of the Barrens, yet territorial predators and hostile creatures still press against its pastures. Ruul watches for dangers on the nearby plains and directs capable travelers against the savage Windfury harpies, which prey on unwary nomads and game.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Ruul Eagletalon?",a="Ruul Eagletalon watches over Bloodhoof Village and directs travelers against threats across Mulgore.",al={"who is ruul","ruul eagletalon npc","bloodhoof ruul"},it="identity"},{q="What threat does Ruul Eagletalon ask travelers to deal with?",a="Ruul warns travelers about aggressive Windfury harpies in Mulgore and asks capable braves to reduce their numbers before they harm the village.",al={"ruul quest","dangers of the windfury","harpies mulgore quest"},it="wants"},{q="Where can Ruul Eagletalon be found?",a="Ruul Eagletalon can be found on the grounds of Bloodhoof Village, by the shores of Stonebull Lake in central Mulgore.",al={"ruul eagletalon location","find ruul","where is ruul"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Whose side is Ruul Eagletalon on?",a="Ruul Eagletalon stands firmly with the Bloodhoof tauren and the Horde. Stationed in Bloodhoof Village, his loyalty is to his people and the protection of their ancestral pastures in Mulgore.",al={"ruul eagletalon faction","is ruul horde","ruul eagletalon allegiance"},it="side",add="17869be4"},{q="Why is Ruul Eagletalon in Bloodhoof Village?",a="Ruul stays in Bloodhoof Village to safeguard the settlement and nearby plains from surrounding threats, enlisting capable travelers to cull the aggressive Windfury harpy presence that encroaches upon the tauren lands.",al={"why is ruul here","ruul eagletalon purpose","what is ruul doing in bloodhoof"},it="why_here",add="5abfc287"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren, you know the vigilance needed to keep Mulgore safe from territorial predators and winged raiders."},kw={"bloodhoof","eagletalon","harpies","mulgore","ruul","windfury"},rel={"subzone:bloodhoof-village","quest:743","zone:mulgore","topic:tauren"},src={"Ruul Eagletalon"}}
E["npc:seer-graytongue"] = {t="npc",n="Seer Graytongue",z="mulgore",s="On Red Cloud Mesa, Seer Graytongue guides young tauren through their first tribal trials. Near Camp Narache in Mulgore, he teaches them to honor the Earthmother before they enter the wider world.",h="On Red Cloud Mesa, Seer Graytongue guides young tauren through their first tribal trials.",sec={{t="A Guide on the Mesa",b="Young tauren braves and spellweavers find Seer Graytongue atop Red Cloud Mesa. From there, he guides those setting out from Camp Narache through sacred steps, including the Rites of the Earthmother. Their trials test resolve against Mulgore’s harsh plains and dangerous beasts. Through them, Graytongue teaches honor, patience, and reverence, virtues every tauren carries into adulthood.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Seer Graytongue?",a="Seer Graytongue is an elder tauren seer who lives in a lodge atop Red Cloud Mesa in Mulgore. He guides young heroes through traditional ceremonies that prove their worth to the tribe.",al={"graytongue","seer graytongue role","who is the seer on red cloud mesa"},it="identity"},{q="What trials does Seer Graytongue oversee?",a="Seer Graytongue guides young tauren through parts of the Rites of the Earthmother, including the Rite of Strength. These rituals test a brave’s fortitude and respect for the plains’ natural balance.",al={"graytongue quests","what does graytongue want","rites of the earthmother graytongue"},it="purpose"},{q="Where can I find Seer Graytongue?",a="Seer Graytongue lives in a leather lodge along the edge of Red Cloud Mesa, just outside Camp Narache in Mulgore.",al={"seer graytongue location","where is graytongue","graytongue hut"},gp=true},{q="Whose side is Seer Graytongue on?",a="Seer Graytongue is loyal to the tauren tribes and the Horde. As a spiritual elder residing within the heart of Camp Narache, he tends to the sacred customs of his people and works alongside chieftain leadership to nurture the next generation of tauren champions.",al={"seer graytongue faction","is seer graytongue horde","seer graytongue loyalty"},it="side",add="ef1aa2b1"},{q="What does Seer Graytongue want?",a="Seer Graytongue seeks to guide fledgling tauren along the sacred path of the Earthmother. By directing young braves through spiritual and physical rites, he ensures they prove their resolve, honor the spirits, and become worthy defenders of the tribe.",al={"what does graytongue want","graytongue goal","seer graytongue purpose"},it="wants",add="9354d517"},{q="Why is Seer Graytongue here?",a="Seer Graytongue makes his home on Red Cloud Mesa to serve as a vital guide for youth coming of age. Camp Narache is where young tauren take their first steps into the wider world, and Graytongue remains stationed here to oversee their traditional rites of passage.",al={"why is seer graytongue in red cloud mesa","seer graytongue location reason","why is graytongue here"},it="why_here",add="ec4e7fed"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a young tauren, visiting Seer Graytongue marks a sacred rite of passage, joining personal strength with the Earthmother’s eternal blessings.",["class:Shaman"]="As a shaman, you can look to elders like Graytongue to learn how sacred rituals and trials bind a warrior’s physical might to ancestral guidance."},kw={"camp narache","graytongue","mulgore","red cloud mesa","rite of strength","rites of the earthmother","seer graytongue"},rel={"quest:763","quest:757","zone:mulgore","topic:tauren","topic:quilboar"},src={"Seer Graytongue"}}
E["npc:seer-ravenfeather"] = {t="npc",n="Seer Ravenfeather",z="mulgore",s="At Camp Narache on the Red Cloud Mesa, Seer Ravenfeather guides young tauren in the ways of the spirits and Mulgore's land.",h="At Camp Narache on the Red Cloud Mesa, Seer Ravenfeather guides young tauren in the ways of the spirits and...",sec={{t="A Guide on the Mesa",b="At Camp Narache, Seer Ravenfeather teaches young tauren who have chosen the shamanic path. Red Cloud Mesa is a sacred proving ground, and she prepares new practitioners for their duties before they venture farther into Mulgore. She teaches the rites that let them commune with elemental forces and honor the Earth Mother.",sp=0},{t="The Call of the Elements",b="Seer Ravenfeather guides new shamans through their first spiritual steps and the traditional Call of Earth rite. Under her guidance, initiates prepare ceremonial saptas and seek communion with the earthen spirits among the mesa's stony outcrops. The bond they forge anchors their elemental magic.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Seer Ravenfeather?",a="Seer Ravenfeather guides young tauren shamans at Camp Narache on the Red Cloud Mesa.",al={"seer ravenfeather lore","who is ravenfeather","camp narache seer"},it="identity"},{q="What role does she play in shaman training?",a="She leads novice shamans through their earliest rites, including the Call of Earth and communion with the spirits of the land.",al={"shaman quests ravenfeather","call of earth trainer","what does ravenfeather teach"},it="purpose"},{q="Where can I find Seer Ravenfeather?",a="Seer Ravenfeather is at Camp Narache on the Red Cloud Mesa in southwestern Mulgore, among its tents and lodges.",al={"ravenfeather location","where is ravenfeather","camp narache location"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Whose side is Seer Ravenfeather on?",a="Seer Ravenfeather is loyal to the tauren tribes and the Horde. Stationed in Camp Narache, she serves her people by guiding young tauren who are called to walk the spiritual paths of shamanism and commune with the spirits of the land.",al={"seer ravenfeather faction","is seer ravenfeather horde","ravenfeather allegiance"},it="side",add="56dac3cc"},{q="What does Seer Ravenfeather want?",a="Her goal is to instruct and prepare novice spiritual seekers in Camp Narache. She guides young initiates through sacred rites—most notably communion with the earth spirits—helping them earn their totems and maintain harmony between the tribe and the land.",al={"seer ravenfeather goals","what does ravenfeather want","seer ravenfeather purpose"},it="wants",add="d16cefae"},{q="Why is Seer Ravenfeather here in Camp Narache?",a="Camp Narache is the gathering ground where young tauren of the Red Cloud Mesa begin their journeys. Seer Ravenfeather remains here to watch over emerging mystics, ensuring every initiate learns the ancient traditions, rites, and reverent connection to the elements from their very first steps.",al={"why is seer ravenfeather in camp narache","seer ravenfeather red cloud mesa","why is ravenfeather here"},it="why_here",add="f326607e"}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="As a young tauren shaman, you can look to Seer Ravenfeather as your first guide in learning to commune with the elements and bind the powers of earth.",["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren, you can find in Seer Ravenfeather of Camp Narache a guide to the spiritual tradition that anchors your people as they settle into their ancestral home."},kw={"call of earth","camp narache","earth sapta","mulgore","ravenfeather","red cloud mesa","seer ravenfeather","shaman"},rel={"subzone:camp-narache","zone:mulgore","quest:1520","topic:tauren"},src={"Seer Ravenfeather"}}
E["npc:skorn-whitecloud"] = {t="npc",n="Skorn Whitecloud",z="mulgore",s="At Bloodhoof Village, aging tauren hunter Skorn Whitecloud guides young hunters and aids travelers facing Mulgore’s dangers.",h="At Bloodhoof Village, aging tauren hunter Skorn Whitecloud guides young hunters and aids travelers facing...",sec={{t="A Guide on the Golden Plains",b="Skorn Whitecloud lives at Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore, where he guides young hunters in the ways of the hunt. He teaches respect for the plains’ wild creatures. Aspiring hunters prove themselves against cunning flatland prowlers, then take the claws to Melor Stonehoof in Thunder Bluff. Skorn wants those who take up bow or spear to understand the balance between predator and prey.",sp=0},{t="Vigilance Against the Unnatural",b="Skorn Whitecloud watches for strange disturbances across Mulgore’s savannah. Signs of demonic corruption can appear on the wildlife and beasts of the plains. When they do, Skorn helps piece together what happened, keeping the tribes alert to malevolent forces entering their ancestral home.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Where can Skorn Whitecloud be found?",a="Skorn Whitecloud can be found at Bloodhoof Village, the tauren settlement in Mulgore’s southern grasslands.",al={"skorn whitecloud location","where is skorn","skorn whitecloud map"},it="place",gp=true},{q="What role does Skorn play for young hunters?",a="Skorn Whitecloud teaches young hunters the principles of the hunt through The Hunter's Way. He sends them to prove their tracking and combat skills against cunning flatland prowlers, then directs them to Melor Stonehoof in Thunder Bluff.",al={"what does skorn do","skorn hunter quest","the hunters way"},it="purpose"},{q="What goods does Skorn sell?",a="Skorn Whitecloud offers Skorn's Hammer or Skorn's Rifle to a hunter who brings proof that the great wolf Ghost Howl has fallen. His own hunting days are over.",al={"skorn vendor","what does skorn sell","skorn weapons"},it="item",gp=true},{q="Is Skorn Whitecloud related to any other tauren in Mulgore?",a="No relatives of Skorn Whitecloud are named in Mulgore. He is known as an old hunter of Bloodhoof Village whose hunting days are behind him.",al={"whitecloud family","skorn relatives"},it="ties"},{q="Who is Skorn Whitecloud?",a="Skorn Whitecloud is an aging tauren hunter residing at Bloodhoof Village in the rolling plains of Mulgore. A respected elder huntsman of his tribe, he mentors rising braves in tracking, hunting, and honoring the traditions of the hunt across tauren lands.",al={"who is skorn","skorn whitecloud lore","who is skorn whitecloud"},it="identity",add="c8bc2edc"},{q="Whose side is Skorn Whitecloud on?",a="Skorn is loyal to the Thunder Bluff tauren and stands firmly with the Horde. He welcomes travelers of the Horde in Bloodhoof Village, offering wisdom to those who walk the hunter's path in Mulgore.",al={"skorn whitecloud faction","skorn whitecloud alliance or horde","what side is skorn on"},it="side",add="3da50b72"},{q="What does Skorn Whitecloud want?",a="Skorn seeks to guide young tauren along the hunter's way, testing their prowess against the predators of Mulgore. He tasks promising hunters with tracking dangerous quarry—including the cunning flatland prowlers—to ensure they learn self-reliance and reverence for the beasts they hunt.",al={"what does skorn want","skorn whitecloud quest motive","skorn whitecloud purpose"},it="wants",add="c55728a0"},{q="Why is Skorn Whitecloud here?",a="Skorn makes his home at Bloodhoof Village in the heart of Mulgore. His hunting days are behind him, but he stays close to the open prairie where game is plentiful and young trackers come to earn their place among their people.",al={"why is skorn at camp sungraze","why is skorn whitecloud here","skorn whitecloud location reason"},it="why_here",add="5990a9e1"}},ang={["class:Hunter"]="As a young tauren hunter, you can learn from Skorn Whitecloud how to stalk swift predators like the flatland prowler and honor the spiritual harmony of the hunt.",["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren, you can see in Skorn the elders’ duty to pass on the honor of the hunt and the practical wisdom of a long life on the plains."},kw={"bloodhoof village","flatland prowler","mulgore","skorn","tauren","the hunter's way","whitecloud"},rel={"zone:mulgore","topic:tauren","quest:861","quest:770"},src={"Skorn Whitecloud"}}
E["npc:vorn-skyseer"] = {t="npc",n="Vorn Skyseer",z="mulgore",s="At Camp Narache, Vorn Skyseer welcomes young tauren beginning their journeys. He lives among the tents on Red Cloud Mesa in Mulgore and introduces newcomers to camp life.",h="At Camp Narache, Vorn Skyseer welcomes young tauren beginning their journeys.",sec={{t="A Friendly Face at Camp Narache",b="At Camp Narache, Vorn Skyseer greets young tauren beginning their rites of passage. They prepare to leave Red Cloud Mesa for the wider valleys of Mulgore, and his kind words and welcome help them settle into camp.",sp=0},{t="A Detached Observer",b="Vorn Skyseer leaves the camp's defense to its braves. He lives quietly and without hurry, watching over the plains of his homeland.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Vorn Skyseer?",a="Vorn Skyseer is an elder resident of Camp Narache in Mulgore. He welcomes young tauren braves beginning their introductory rites at Red Cloud Mesa.",al={"vorn skyseer lore","who is vorn","vorn mulgore"},it="identity"},{q="Where can I find Vorn Skyseer?",a="You can find Vorn Skyseer resting outdoors among the tents of Camp Narache, atop Red Cloud Mesa in southwestern Mulgore.",al={"vorn skyseer location","where is vorn","vorn camp narache"},it="place",gp=true},{q="What quests does Vorn offer?",a="Vorn receives the Bloodhoof Village Gift Voucher in Welcome! He greets its bearer and offers a small companion to join them on their adventures.",al={"vorn skyseer quests","vorn welcome quest"},it="purpose",gp=true},{q="Whose side is Vorn Skyseer on?",a="Vorn Skyseer is a tauren residing at Camp Narache on Red Cloud Mesa, standing firmly with the tribes of Mulgore and the Horde. He is no warrior; his home and loyalties belong to his people.",al={"vorn skyseer faction","who does vorn serve","vorn allegiance"},it="side",add="786f5f1f"},{q="Why is Vorn Skyseer in Camp Narache?",a="Vorn Skyseer lives at Camp Narache to greet the young braves beginning their journeys on Red Cloud Mesa. He offers kind words and hospitality, helping new arrivals settle into the camp.",al={"why is vorn here","vorn skyseer role bloodhoof","what does vorn do"},it="why_here",add="28d2e499"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a young tauren on Red Cloud Mesa, you may find Vorn Skyseer among the first familiar, reassuring faces to greet you as you prepare for the wider life of Mulgore.",["faction:Horde"]="As a member of the Horde, you can see in Vorn the calm, steady nature of the plains tribes gathered under Cairne Bloodhoof's leadership to build a permanent home."},kw={"camp narache","mulgore","red cloud mesa","tauren","vorn skyseer","welcome"},rel={"subzone:camp-narache","zone:mulgore","topic:tauren"},src={"Vorn Skyseer"}}
E["npc:yaw-sharpmane"] = {t="npc",n="Yaw Sharpmane",z="mulgore",s="In Bloodhoof Village, Yaw Sharpmane guides young tauren hunters. He teaches them to track, survive, and tame Mulgore's wild beasts.",h="In Bloodhoof Village, Yaw Sharpmane guides young tauren hunters.",sec={{t="A Guide on the Plains",b="Yaw Sharpmane teaches marksmanship, tracking, and survival in Bloodhoof Village. Young hunters who have proven themselves in Red Cloud Mesa come to him to hone their skills before venturing onto the wider plains of Mulgore.",sp=0},{t="Mastering the Bond",b="Yaw Sharpmane guides new hunters through the ancient rite of taming wild beasts. He asks them to bond with Mulgore's native animals, testing their patience and respect for nature's balance. Then he teaches them to care for and train a lasting companion.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Yaw Sharpmane?",a="Yaw Sharpmane trains hunters in Bloodhoof Village. He teaches young tauren marksmanship, wilderness survival, and how to tame animal companions.",al={"yaw sharpmane","who is yaw","bloodhoof hunter trainer"},it="identity"},{q="Where can I find Yaw Sharpmane?",a="You can find Yaw Sharpmane in Bloodhoof Village, in central Mulgore along the shores of Stonebull Lake.",al={"yaw sharpmane location","where is yaw","yaw coords"},gp=true},{q="What does Yaw Sharpmane teach hunters?",a="Yaw Sharpmane teaches hunters basic abilities and guides promising hunters through the rites of taming and commanding beasts.",al={"what does yaw teach","yaw hunter skills","yaw abilities"},gp=true},{q="What quests does Yaw offer?",a="Yaw Sharpmane guides tauren hunters through the beast-taming trials, from 'The Hunter's Path' to 'Taming the Beast' and 'Training the Beast.'",al={"yaw quests","yaw sharpmane quests"},gp=true},{q="Why do hunters need to speak to him around level 10?",a="When you reach your tenth season, you go to Yaw Sharpmane to learn how to capture, feed, and command a lasting animal companion from Mulgore's wilds.",al={"yaw level 10","tame beast bloodhoof"},gp=true},{q="Does Yaw Sharpmane interact with non-hunters?",a="Yaw Sharpmane speaks politely with travelers of other callings, though his knowledge and trials are for hunters.",al={"yaw non hunters","can other classes talk to yaw"},gp=true},{q="Whose side is Yaw Sharpmane on?",a="Yaw Sharpmane stands firmly with the Bloodhoof tauren and the Horde. Stationed in Bloodhoof Village, he helps ensure that the young defenders of Mulgore are well-versed in the ancestral ways of tracking, marksmanship, and taming the wild beasts of their homelands.",al={"yaw sharpmane faction","is yaw sharpmane horde","yaw sharpmane allegiance"},it="side",add="ce9d051b"},{q="What does Yaw Sharpmane want?",a="Yaw seeks to instruct novice hunters in the core traditions of the hunt. He guides young tauren to commune with nature, understand the creatures of Mulgore, and master the ancient rite of taming beasts so that they may survive the wild and serve their tribe.",al={"yaw sharpmane goal","yaw sharpmane motives","what does yaw sharpmane do"},it="wants",add="e7f68d71"},{q="Why is Yaw Sharpmane in Bloodhoof Village?",a="Bloodhoof Village serves as the primary gathering hub for young tauren leaving the training grounds of Camp Narache. Yaw Sharpmane resides here to guide hunters as they take their first major steps across Mulgore, teaching them to bond with animal companions and master their craft.",al={"why is yaw sharpmane here","yaw sharpmane bloodhoof village purpose"},it="why_here",add="cc781595"}},ang={["class:Hunter"]="As a tauren hunter leaving Red Cloud Mesa, Yaw Sharpmane can guide you as you bond with your first wild companion.",["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren, you follow the Shu'halo tradition of living in harmony with nature and honoring Mulgore's wild beasts."},kw={"beasts","bloodhoof","hunter","mulgore","sharpmane","taming","tauren","trainer","yaw"},rel={"subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore","quest:6061","quest:6081","quest:6065","topic:tauren"},src={"Yaw Sharpmane"}}
E["npc:zarlman-two-moons"] = {t="npc",n="Zarlman Two-Moons",z="mulgore",s="In central Mulgore, Zarlman Two-Moons guides Bloodhoof Village's spiritual life. While Baine Bloodhoof tends its daily affairs, Zarlman communes with the spirits and guides young tauren through the Rite of Vision.",h="In central Mulgore, Zarlman Two-Moons guides Bloodhoof Village's spiritual life.",sec={{t="Keeper of the Unseen",b="Zarlman Two-Moons tends the spirits while Baine Bloodhoof commands Bloodhoof Village's defenses and temporal needs. The village looks to Zarlman for harmony with the spirit world. He joins the plains' everyday struggles with the voices of ancestors and honors the Earth Mother and ancestral wisdom.",sp=0},{t="The Rite of Vision",b="Zarlman guides maturing tauren through the Rite of Vision, a traditional trial each must undertake. He teaches initiates to prepare sacred draughts and heed ancestral portents. Young hunters, shamans, and warriors seek their destined path and spirit guides through the rite. Zarlman teaches them that strength on the plains means little without spiritual clarity.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Zarlman Two-Moons?",a="Zarlman Two-Moons guides the spiritual life of Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore. While Baine Bloodhoof tends the village's practical defense and daily affairs, Zarlman watches over its spiritual well-being and guides young tauren through tribal traditions.",al={"who is zarlman","zarlman two moons lore","who is the shaman in bloodhoof"},it="identity"},{q="What is Zarlman's role in Bloodhoof Village?",a="Zarlman oversees spiritual affairs, communion with ancestors, and cultural rites in Bloodhoof Village. He complements Baine Bloodhoof's physical leadership and keeps the tribe in balance with nature spirits and ancestors.",al={"zarlman job","zarlman purpose","bloodhoof village mystic"},it="purpose"},{q="What is the Rite of Vision?",a="The Rite of Vision is a traditional passage for young tauren coming of age in Mulgore. Guided by Zarlman, initiates drink a ritual concoction, see visions, and receive guidance from nature spirits.",al={"rite of vision quest","what is the vision rite","tauren vision quest"}},{q="Where can Zarlman Two-Moons be found?",a="Zarlman Two-Moons can be found in Bloodhoof Village, in central Mulgore within the curve of Stonebull Lake.",al={"where is zarlman","zarlman location","how to find zarlman"},it="place",gp=true},{q="How does Zarlman relate to Baine Bloodhoof?",a="Zarlman is Baine Bloodhoof's spiritual counterpart in Bloodhoof Village. Baine leads in physical and martial matters, while Zarlman offers mystical wisdom and watches over the tribe's spiritual balance.",al={"baine and zarlman","zarlman relation to baine"},it="ties"},{q="Why is the Rite of Vision important to young tauren?",a="The Rite of Vision marks a tauren's passage into adulthood. It acknowledges the youth as a member of the tribe, reveals their inner spirit guide, and cements their bond with the Earth Mother.",al={"why do rite of vision","rite of vision importance"}},{q="Whose side is Zarlman Two-Moons on?",a="Zarlman Two-Moons is devoted entirely to the Bloodhoof tribe and the Horde. Stationed in Bloodhoof Village, he works closely with Baine Bloodhoof to ensure their people remain spiritually resilient against any threat facing Mulgore.",al={"who does zarlman serve","zarlman two moons faction","is zarlman horde"},it="side",add="caff3cf9"},{q="What does Zarlman Two-Moons want?",a="Zarlman seeks to safeguard the spiritual well-being of Bloodhoof Village. While physical leadership falls to Baine Bloodhoof, Zarlman guides young adventurers through ancient traditions like the Rite of Vision to commune with the spirits and uncover their true path.",al={"what does zarlman want","zarlman goal","what is zarlman doing"},it="wants",add="45ee9eb7"},{q="Why is Zarlman Two-Moons here?",a="Zarlman remains in Bloodhoof Village to serve as its primary spiritual guardian. As tribal members grow into adulthood, he oversees the sacred rites necessary to commune with the ancestral spirits and protect their ancestral lands from unseen spiritual perils.",al={"why is zarlman in bloodhoof village","why is zarlman here"},it="why_here",add="1734c724"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren adventurer, you can look to Zarlman for guidance through the sacred rites of your youth and the awakening of your spiritual sight.",["class:Shaman"]="As a shaman, you follow the tauren tradition Zarlman embodies: communion with the spirits alongside practical tribal leadership.",["faction:Horde"]="As a Horde ally arriving in Mulgore's central hub, you can learn from Zarlman about the spiritual customs that shape tauren culture."},kw={"bloodhoof","mulgore","rite","shaman","spirits","tauren","two-moons","village","vision","zarlman"},rel={"subzone:bloodhoof-village","npc:baine-bloodhoof","quest:767","zone:mulgore","topic:tauren"},src={"Zarlman Two-Moons"}}
E["quest:1462"] = {t="quest",n="Earth Sapta",z="mulgore",s="At Camp Narache, Seer Ravenfeather prepares another Earth Sapta when a young shaman loses the brew before completing the rite. The draught opens a novice’s sight to the spirits of stone and lets the trial continue.",h="At Camp Narache, Seer Ravenfeather prepares another Earth Sapta when a young shaman loses the brew before...",sec={{t="A Visionary Draught",b="Young shaman begin by learning to sense forces beyond ordinary sight. To meet Mulgore’s earth elementals, an initiate drinks a sapta, a ritual brew made with natural essences of stone and soil. The Earth Sapta lifts the veil between the physical and spiritual realms, letting a novice see and commune with the spirits of the land.",sp=0},{t="A Second Chance at Sight",b="A novice may lose or spill the brew before reaching the stone monument for communion. Seer Ravenfeather waits at Camp Narache and prepares another vessel when a pupil needs one. With the draught restored, the novice can continue the rite and face the spirits.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is an Earth Sapta?",a="An Earth Sapta is a herbal preparation brewed by shamanic elders. When an initiate drinks it, the draught awakens inner senses and grants the spiritual sight needed to perceive and commune with earth elementals hidden from ordinary eyes.",al={"what is sapta","earth sapta lore","what does the brew do"},it="item"},{q="Why would an initiate need another Earth Sapta?",a="An initiate may lose or spill the draught in the wilds before reaching the sacred site. Seer Ravenfeather provides another dose so a mistake does not halt the training.",al={"lost my sapta","why get another sapta","lost earth sapta"},it="purpose"},{q="Who is Seer Ravenfeather?",a="Seer Ravenfeather is a tauren spiritual guide at Camp Narache in Mulgore. She guides young tauren shaman through their earliest trials and first communion with the spirits of the earth.",al={"who is ravenfeather","seer ravenfeather lore","camp narache seer"},it="identity"},{q="Why must shaman drink a sapta to see elementals?",a="Novice shaman have not yet learned to perceive the elements at will. A sapta bridges their senses until they learn to listen to and commune with the elements through their own power.",al={"why need a potion to see spirits","why drink sapta"},it="power"},{q="Where does the trial of the Earth Sapta take place?",a="The trial takes place in the hills of Red Cloud Mesa in Mulgore, near Camp Narache. Novices carry the draught to a sacred stone monument where local earth spirits dwell.",al={"where do i use sapta","call of earth location"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Do all shaman use saptas?",a="Tauren and orcish shamanic traditions both use saptas for initial elemental initiations. Earth, fire, and water each have their own sapta to attune a shaman’s senses to that elemental plane.",al={"do orcs use saptas","are saptas only tauren"}},{q="Why does Seer Ravenfeather give you another Earth Sapta?",a="Seer Ravenfeather is tasked with guiding young tauren initiates through their elemental trials. Without drinking the Earth Sapta, an initiate cannot commune with the spirit of the earth or complete their communion. Should you misplace or spill your draught, Ravenfeather provides another so that your sacred journey is not halted before you ever reach the elemental.",al={"why ravenfeather earth sapta","seer ravenfeather motive","why give sapta again"},it="motive",add="5f71bc62"}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="As a shaman, you begin with a sapta that opens your sight to the elemental forces guiding the world.",["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren, you begin honoring the Earth Mother by learning to hear the land. The elders of Camp Narache make sure no youth is turned away from this sacred path.",["faction:Horde"]="As a member of the Horde, you see shamanic traditions nurtured in Mulgore, where tribal seers guide the next generation of spiritual leaders."},kw={"call of earth","camp narache","draught","earth sapta","elemental","mulgore","seer ravenfeather","shaman"},rel={"npc:seer-ravenfeather","quest:1516","zone:mulgore"},m={start="Seer Ravenfeather",level="4",faction="Horde",id=1462},src={"Earth Sapta (Mulgore)"}}
E["quest:1520"] = {t="quest",n="Call of Earth",z="mulgore",s="At Kodo Rock, you seek communion with earth through a sacred tauren rite. Drinking an Earth Sapta lets you see a Minor Manifestation of Earth.",h="At Kodo Rock, you seek communion with earth through a sacred tauren rite.",sec={{t="The Purpose of the Sapta",b="Shamanic elders brew a sapta to bind an initiate’s mortal spirit to a primal element. Ancestral magic guards the brewing secrets, known to only a select few among the tauren. Drink the draught at a sacred site, and your mind opens beyond the physical world. The veil parts, and the elements can be witnessed in their raw form.",sp=0},{t="Communion at Kodo Rock",b="East of Camp Narache, Kodo Rock rises as a stone pillar and holy sanctuary. Drink the sapta there, and a Minor Manifestation of Earth appears before you. Along the shaman’s path, other aspects of nature are volatile, fiery, or raging. Earth asks for quiet humility instead. Honor the land, and its spirit grants you the mountain’s enduring strength and the plains’ calm patience.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is an Earth Sapta?",a="An Earth Sapta is a ceremonial draught brewed by shamanic elders. Its magic opens your perception so you can see and speak with elemental earth spirits.",al={"what is sapta","earth sapta meaning","what does the drink do"},it="item"},{q="Where is Kodo Rock located?",a="Kodo Rock is a natural stone monolith east of Camp Narache, across the Red Cloud Mesa.",al={"find kodo rock","kodo rock location","where is the shrine"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Who crafted the Earth Sapta for this rite?",a="Seer Ravenfeather, an elder shaman at Camp Narache, prepared and granted the potion. Seer Ravenfeather oversees the training of young spiritual guides.",al={"who gives the sapta","seer ravenfeather sapta"},it="origin"},{q="Why must the sapta be consumed at a holy site?",a="Tauren tradition calls for a sapta to be drunk only in a consecrated shrine, where elemental spirits dwell and can receive the petitioner safely. The draught is sacred and dangerous if misused.",al={"why drink at kodo rock","can i drink sapta anywhere"},it="why_here"},{q="What happens after drinking the potion at Kodo Rock?",a="Drinking the potion at Kodo Rock lifts the mundane veil and reveals a Minor Manifestation of Earth. Speaking with the spirit completes the shaman’s bond with earth.",al={"what appears","minor manifestation of earth spawn"},it="next_step"},{q="What lesson does the earth spirit impart to initiates?",a="The earth spirit teaches humble patience, not combat. Fire and air can be chaotic and violent, while earth offers strength, endurance, and a quiet foundation for the shaman’s soul.",al={"earth spirit lesson","meaning of earth rite"},it="purpose"},{q="Why does Seer Ravenfeather send you to Kodo Rock?",a="Seer Ravenfeather is guiding your path as an initiate shaman. The Earth Sapta she prepared is infused with magic designed to open your perception to the spirits of the world. By directing you to drink the brew at the sacred shrine of Kodo Rock, she aims to permanently bind your spirit to the element of earth, allowing you to commune directly with its manifestations and learn its core wisdom.",al={"seer ravenfeather motive","why drink earth sapta","why ravenfeather call of earth"},it="motive",add="52e0a178"}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="As a shaman, this rite forms the spiritual core of your bond with the land. It is the bridge to communion with earth and to receiving an Earth Totem.",["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren, you can take part in the sapta ritual, one of the most revered coming-of-age tests for spiritualists on the Red Cloud Mesa."},kw={"call of earth","camp narache","earth sapta","kodo rock","manifestation","mulgore","sapta","seer ravenfeather"},rel={"npc:seer-ravenfeather","npc:minor-manifestation-of-earth","subzone:kodo-rock","subzone:camp-narache","zone:mulgore"},m={start="Seer Ravenfeather",["end"]="Minor Manifestation of Earth",level="4",faction="Horde",id=1520},src={"Call of Earth (Mulgore 2)"}}
E["quest:1521"] = {t="quest",n="Call of Earth",z="mulgore",s="After the first communion with the land spirits in Mulgore, a young tauren shaman returns to Camp Narache with a gift from the earth. Drinking the Earth Sapta lets the initiate speak directly with a spirit manifestation and receive raw stone, binding them to the first of the four elements.",h="After the first communion with the land spirits in Mulgore, a young tauren shaman returns to Camp Narache...",sec={{t="A Gift from the Mountain",b="After drinking the Earth Sapta, you meet the Minor Manifestation of Earth atop the crags near Red Cloud Mesa. The spirit gives you a plain Rough Quartz. It represents your pact with the element and the endurance of stone. The spirit reminds you that patience and strength are the virtues the earth demands, and promises harmony with the land through mutual respect.",sp=0},{t="The First Totem",b="At Camp Narache, Seer Ravenfeather shapes the gifted quartz into your first Earth Totem. The totem is more than a mark of status: it is a lasting physical and spiritual anchor. Through this carved focus, you channel the earth’s grounding, protective magic and take your first true step along the fourfold elemental path.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of the Rough Quartz?",a="The Rough Quartz is a token the Minor Manifestation of Earth gives you as proof of communion. Seer Ravenfeather uses it as the spiritual heart of your new Earth Totem.",al={"why do i need rough quartz","what is the quartz for","rough quartz use"},it="item"},{q="Why is an Earth Totem so important to a shaman?",a="Totems let a shaman channel elemental power. Without an Earth Totem as a focus, a shaman cannot petition the land spirits to manifest spells and protective wards.",al={"why do i need earth totem","what does earth totem do","earth totem lore"},it="power"},{q="Who is Seer Ravenfeather?",a="Seer Ravenfeather is the spiritual guide and shaman trainer at Camp Narache on Red Cloud Mesa. She oversees young tauren’s elemental trials, guides them through their first visions, and crafts their sacred implements.",al={"who is ravenfeather","seer ravenfeather lore","camp narache seer"},it="identity"},{q="What are the four paths Seer Ravenfeather mentions?",a="Seer Ravenfeather means the four primal elements a shaman must master: Earth, Fire, Water, and Air. Earth is the foundation, representing endurance and steadfastness, and it is the initiate’s first trial.",al={"four paths","four elements shaman","what are the paths"},it="purpose"},{q="Where does this communion take place?",a="The communion takes place among the rugged cliffs bordering Red Cloud Mesa in Mulgore. You can reach the Minor Manifestation of Earth only after drinking the Earth Sapta.",al={"where do i find the earth spirit","manifestation location","where is the spirit"},it="place"},{q="Do other Horde shamans undertake a similar rite?",a="Yes. Orcs and Darkspear trolls in Durotar undertake their own Call of Earth in the Valley of Trials. They seek communion with local elemental manifestations and carve their first totems.",al={"do orcs do call of earth","troll call of earth","do all shamans get earth totem"},it="people"},{q="Why does the Minor Manifestation of Earth send you back to Seer Ravenfeather?",a="The elemental spirit has tested your resolve and deemed you worthy to walk the path of earth. By bestowing a piece of itself—the rough quartz—it provides tangible proof of your communion and mutual respect. It bids you return to Seer Ravenfeather so the stone can form the heart of your first totem, binding the spirits of earth to your service as you uphold patience, strength, and wisdom.",al={"minor manifestation of earth motive","why send rough quartz","manifestation of earth purpose"},it="motive",add="43ccddec"}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="As a shaman, this rite gives you your first totem. Carrying it grounds your spiritual identity and lets you cast essential earth-aligned wards.",["race:Tauren"]="Tauren culture holds the Earth Mother in profound reverence. This trial binds an initiate to the living rock and soil of their ancestral homeland.",["faction:Horde"]="As a Horde shaman, you serve as a spiritual anchor and advisor, bridging the mortal races with Kalimdor’s volatile elemental forces."},kw={"call of earth","camp narache","earth totem","minor manifestation of earth","mulgore","red cloud mesa","rough quartz","seer ravenfeather","tauren shaman"},rel={"npc:seer-ravenfeather","npc:minor-manifestation-of-earth","quest:1520","subzone:camp-narache","zone:mulgore"},m={start="Minor Manifestation of Earth",["end"]="Seer Ravenfeather",level="4",faction="Horde",id=1521},src={"Call of Earth (Mulgore 3)"}}
E["quest:3092"] = {t="quest",n="Etched Note",z="mulgore",s="At Camp Narache, a summons calls a young tauren hunter to train with Lanka Farshot. The message marks the start of study in the ways of the wild.",h="At Camp Narache, a summons calls a young tauren hunter to train with Lanka Farshot.",sec={{t="A Summons to the Hunt",b="Grull Hawkwind passes the etched message to a young tauren with a gift for marksmanship and tracking. The novice has been training among the bluffs of Red Cloud Mesa. Lanka Farshot has taken notice of the youth's bloodline and raw ability, and calls them to study under his watch.",sp=0},{t="Tradition of the Plains",b="Among the Shu'halo, hunters do more than scout or fight. They feed the tribes with their quarry, guard nomadic camps, and keep close to the beasts of Kalimdor. Lanka's training begins with weapons and the creatures of Mulgore. In time, the novice may learn the ancient art of taming wild companions.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of the Etched Note?",a="The Etched Note introduces a novice tauren hunter to Lanka Farshot, the trainer in Camp Narache.",al={"what does etched note do","etched note purpose","why did i get etched note"},it="purpose"},{q="Who delivers the note?",a="Grull Hawkwind passes the note along and says a messenger from the hunter trainer sought out the young hunter moments earlier.",al={"who gives etched note","etched note quest giver","rohaku note"}},{q="Where is Lanka Farshot located?",a="Lanka Farshot is in Camp Narache on Red Cloud Mesa, near the hunter training grounds, ready to instruct new hunters.",al={"where is lanka farshot","find lanka farshot","lanka location"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why are hunters so revered among the tauren?",a="Hunters feed the tauren, read the wilderness, and keep harmony with the spirits of the plains.",al={"tauren hunter lore","why are tauren hunters important"},it="people"},{q="What skills does this training introduce?",a="Lanka teaches basic hunting, ranged weapons, knowledge of wild beasts, and, in time, the rites for taming animal companions.",al={"what does lanka teach","hunter basics tauren"},it="for_you",gp=true},{q="What is the next step after reading the note?",a="You can report to Lanka Farshot in Camp Narache to begin training. He teaches you to fire your rifle, tame a pet, and more as you grow.",al={"next quest after etched note","etched note follow up"},it="next_step",gp=true},{q="Why does Grull Hawkwind give you the etched note?",a="Grull Hawkwind is merely passing along word on behalf of a messenger sent across Camp Narache. Lanka Farshot, the camp's hunter trainer, sent the messenger specifically to seek you out as you come of age. Grull simply ensures the summons finds your hands without delay so you can begin proper training among your people.",al={"why rohaku gives note","rohaku stonehoof motive","why did rohaku give me this"},it="motive",add="69749d8d"}},ang={["class:Hunter"]="As a hunter, you begin learning marksmanship, survival in the wild, and how to live with beast companions.",["race:Tauren"]="As Shu'halo, you honor the Earth Mother by respecting the beasts that sustain your tribe across Mulgore's plains."},kw={"camp narache","etched note","lanka farshot","mulgore","red cloud mesa","tauren hunter"},rel={"npc:lanka-farshot","subzone:camp-narache","zone:mulgore"},m={start="Grull Hawkwind",["end"]="Lanka Farshot",level="3",faction="Horde",id=3092},src={"Etched Note (quest)"}}
E["quest:3094"] = {t="quest",n="Verdant Note",z="mulgore",s="At Camp Narache, a summons calls promising young tauren to seek Gart Mistrunner. Grull Hawkwind delivers the message on Gart's behalf.",h="At Camp Narache, a summons calls promising young tauren to seek Gart Mistrunner.",sec={{t="A Summons to the Path",b="On the Red Cloud Mesa, Grull Hawkwind gives young tauren a message from a messenger sent by Gart Mistrunner. The summons calls those showing natural gifts to take up their responsibilities. It invites them to enter the ancient practice of tauren druidism and turn from simple village errands toward the balance of the plains.",sp=0},{t="Voices of the Earthmother",b="Gart Mistrunner warns that ancestral and natural spirits have grown restless across Mulgore. Tauren druids act as living avatars of nature, keeping harmony with the Earthmother's will despite distant rivalries. Night elves practice druidic arts too, but Gart says their pride blinds them to the tauren's deep bond with the natural realm.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Verdant Note?",a="The Verdant Note summons young tauren druids to seek Gart Mistrunner at Camp Narache and begin training in the druidic arts.",al={"verdant note","druid letter","mulgore druid quest note"},it="item"},{q="Who delivers the note to the player?",a="Grull Hawkwind hands the note to the young druid and says a messenger sent by Gart Mistrunner was searching for them.",al={"who gives verdant note","verdant note quest starter"}},{q="Where can Gart Mistrunner be found?",a="Gart Mistrunner can be found at Camp Narache, beneath the shelter of its natural bluffs, where he trains novice tauren druids.",al={"gart mistrunner location","where is gart"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why does Gart Mistrunner warn against the night elves?",a="Gart Mistrunner says night elf pride blinds them to the tauren's deep bond with nature. He urges his student to focus on the Earthmother's wisdom.",al={"tauren and night elf druids","night elf pride druid"}},{q="Why are the spirits in Mulgore restless?",a="Gart Mistrunner senses ancestral and natural spirits stirring across Mulgore. Their unrest marks the new druid's lifelong responsibility to balance the wild.",al={"mulgore spirits restless","why are nature spirits angry"},it="threat"},{q="What ability does Gart teach next after completing this quest?",a="After the young druid reports to Gart Mistrunner, he becomes their trainer and promises to teach the Earthmother's gifts as they grow in wisdom.",al={"what comes after verdant note","druid quest follow up"},it="next_step",gp=true},{q="Why does Grull Hawkwind give you the Verdant Note?",a="A messenger specifically sought you out on behalf of Gart Mistrunner, the camp's druid mentor. Grull Hawkwind is simply passing along the urgent message so that you do not delay in beginning your formal spiritual training under Gart's guidance.",al={"why rohaku gives note","rohaku stonehoof motive","why does rohaku want this done"},it="motive",add="92e5d696"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren, you inherit druidism as a sacred covenant rooted in devotion to the Earthmother.",["class:Druid"]="As a druid, you begin learning to channel the wild energies and restless spirits of Azeroth.",["faction:Horde"]="As a member of the Horde, you join the tauren druids, who bridge the faction and Kalimdor's natural mysteries while balancing its martial strength."},kw={"camp narache","druid","earthmother","gart mistrunner","grull hawkwind","mulgore","red cloud mesa","verdant note"},rel={"npc:gart-mistrunner","npc:grull-hawkwind","subzone:camp-narache","zone:mulgore","topic:druid"},m={start="Grull Hawkwind",["end"]="Gart Mistrunner",level="3",faction="Horde",id=3094},src={"Verdant Note (quest)"}}
E["quest:3376"] = {t="quest",n="Break Sharptusk!",z="mulgore",s="Brave Windfeather sends you east from Red Cloud Mesa to face the raids on Mulgore. Sharptusk Thornmantle leads the Bristleback quilboar in Brambleblade Ravine, and his death could strike a hard blow against their attacks.",h="Brave Windfeather sends you east from Red Cloud Mesa to face the raids on Mulgore.",sec={{t="A Home Won in Blood",b="Red Cloud Mesa is home to young tauren learning the hunt and the ways of the Earth Mother. East of the mesa, Brambleblade Ravine lies in the hands of the Bristleback quilboar. They see the tauren as invaders taking sacred ground, and their skirmishes threaten the tribes trying to make a stable home in Mulgore.",sp=0},{t="The Threat of Thornmantle",b="The Bristleback attacks on Red Cloud Mesa follow the command of Chief Sharptusk Thornmantle. Deep in Brambleblade Ravine, he directs his warriors and geomancers against the Shu'halo from a fortified makeshift village. Brave Windfeather knows the raids will continue while Sharptusk lives. She sends you into the ravine to kill him and bring back his severed head as proof.",sp=0},{t="The Lessons of the Hunt",b="Brave Windfeather teaches that hunting an enemy who can hunt back tests more than skill. For young tauren and their allies, the hunt for Sharptusk demands courage, awareness, and discipline. His death brings justice for fallen kin and marks a hunter's readiness to travel across Mulgore's wider plains.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Chief Sharptusk Thornmantle?",a="Chief Sharptusk Thornmantle leads the Bristleback quilboar in Brambleblade Ravine. His tribe stages raids and skirmishes against the tauren settlement on Red Cloud Mesa.",al={"who is sharptusk","sharptusk thornmantle lore","who is the quilboar chief"},it="target"},{q="Where can Chief Sharptusk Thornmantle be found?",a="Sharptusk Thornmantle lives deep in Brambleblade Ravine, east of Red Cloud Mesa. He directs his tribe from a makeshift village inside the thorn-covered canyon.",al={"where is sharptusk","sharptusk location","how to find sharptusk"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Who gives the quest Break Sharptusk!?",a="Brave Windfeather offers the hunt for Sharptusk. The seasoned tauren warrior guards Camp Narache on Red Cloud Mesa and charges newcomers with proving their mettle against the Bristleback threat.",al={"quest giver break sharptusk","who starts break sharptusk","brave windfeather location"},it="identity",gp=true},{q="Why do the Bristleback quilboar hate the tauren?",a="The Bristleback quilboar revere their demigod Agamaggan and defend the lands they claim. They see tauren settlements in Mulgore as trespass on their ancestral hunting grounds.",al={"why do quilboar attack","tauren quilboar conflict","why are quilboar hostile"},it="conflict"},{q="What proof does Brave Windfeather demand?",a="Brave Windfeather demands Sharptusk Thornmantle's severed head. It proves the chief has fallen and warns the remaining Bristlebacks.",al={"what do i bring windfeather","sharptusk proof","quest objective sharptusk"},it="item"},{q="What is the cultural significance of this hunt for young tauren?",a="Tauren maturity means understanding the balance between hunter and hunted. Windfeather teaches that facing a cunning enemy like Sharptusk shows young warriors the cost of mortal combat and the duty of defending their tribe.",al={"why kill sharptusk lore","tauren rite of passage sharptusk","meaning of the hunt"},it="purpose"},{q="Why does Brave Windfeather want Chief Sharptusk killed?",a="The Bristleback quilboar wage a relentless campaign against the tauren of Red Cloud Mesa, threatening the safety of their newly carved home. Chief Sharptusk Thornmantle leads these raids from nearby Brambleblade Ravine. Brave Windfeather seeks to strike a decisive blow against their leadership to halt the bloodshed and deliver a firm warning to any who would contest the tauren's presence on the mesa.",al={"windfeather motive","why kill sharptusk","why break sharptusk"},it="motive",add="d0ec7fec"},{q="Why does Brave Windfeather task you instead of dealing with Sharptusk herself?",a="Other seasoned defenders have already attempted to dislodge the quilboar chief and failed, while the remaining braves must stay behind to guard Camp Narache and protect the young from roving threats. Having heard whispers of your growing promise as a rising hunter, Windfeather sees you as an unproven yet formidable talent capable of besting a cunning adversary and learning the true weight of the hunt.",al={"why send me","why not the braves","why not brave windfeather"},it="why_me",add="8bbc47fc"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For a young tauren, killing Sharptusk Thornmantle is one of the final trials before leaving Camp Narache. It proves you are ready to defend the tribes from hostile neighbors.",["faction:Horde"]="As an ally of the Horde, you help steady Mulgore and secure peaceful lands for your new tauren brothers by killing Chief Sharptusk.",["class:Hunter"]="As a hunter, you face an early test: tracking prey with its own cunning, weapons, and intent to hunt you in return."},kw={"brambleblade","bristleback","mulgore","narache","quilboar","red cloud mesa","sharptusk","thornmantle","windfeather"},rel={"subzone:brambleblade-ravine","topic:quilboar","topic:tauren","zone:mulgore"},m={start="Brave Windfeather",level="5",faction="Horde",id=3376,ri={"Chief Sharptusk Thornmantle's Head"}},src={"Break Sharptusk!"}}
E["quest:6061"] = {t="quest",n="Taming the Beast",z="mulgore",s="At Bloodhoof Village, Taming the Beast begins a young tauren hunter’s training with Yaw Sharpmane. The first trial takes the hunter into Mulgore to tame an adult plainstrider and begin a bond with the creatures of the Earthmother.",h="At Bloodhoof Village, Taming the Beast begins a young tauren hunter’s training with Yaw Sharpmane.",sec={{t="A Sacred Bond with the Wild",b="Tauren hunters seek more than meat and hides when they hunt. Their ancestral traditions teach that meeting Mulgore’s wild creatures can bring a hunter closer to the Earthmother. Taming a companion turns a beast that might have been an adversary into a partner, joined with the hunter in the natural balance of the plains.",sp=0},{t="The First Test",b="Yaw Sharpmane starts a hunter’s training with plainstriders that roam Mulgore’s grasslands. The hunter carries an enchanted taming rod and faces an adult plainstrider, calming its wild instincts and earning its trust. This first trial tests the hunter’s resolve and prepares them to face more demanding beasts.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of this trial?",a="This is the first practical test for a young tauren hunter learning to form a bond with an animal. Using a taming rod to calm an adult plainstrider teaches the basics of a spiritual and working bond with beasts.",al={"why do i tame a plainstrider","purpose of quest","tauren pet quest purpose"},it="purpose"},{q="Who gives this quest and where are they located?",a="Yaw Sharpmane, the hunter trainer, gives the trial in Bloodhoof Village, in central Mulgore.",al={"who gives quest","quest giver location","yaw sharpmane location"},it="identity",gp=true},{q="Do I keep the adult plainstrider permanently?",a="No. The beasts in Yaw Sharpmane’s first three trials are temporary practice partners. After completing the full training and mastering beast taming, you can tame a compatible companion permanently.",al={"is this pet permanent","do i keep the plainstrider","keep beast after quest"},gp=true},{q="Why do tauren view taming beasts as a spiritual act?",a="Tauren tradition teaches that meeting and overcoming wild creatures lets hunters take in their spirits and join with the natural world. Taming a beast shows respect and unity with the Earthmother’s creations.",al={"tauren pet lore","earthmother taming lore","why tame beasts tauren"},it="history"},{q="Where can adult plainstriders be found?",a="Adult plainstriders roam the valleys and savannahs around Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore.",al={"where are plainstriders","plainstrider location","find adult plainstrider"},it="place",gp=true},{q="What comes after this quest?",a="When you return the taming rod to Yaw Sharpmane, the next trial sends you to tame a stalker, then an aggressive swoop. After those trials, you learn to train beasts permanently.",al={"what is next","quest chain after this","next step taming"},it="next_step",gp=true},{q="Why does Yaw Sharpmane want me to tame a plainstrider?",a="Yaw Sharpmane is guiding you through the sacred traditions of the tauren hunter. To tauren, besting and taming wild beasts is a spiritual discipline that draws the hunter closer to the Earthmother and harmonizes them with the natural world. He starts your training with the swift adult plainstriders of Mulgore so you can master the patience and spiritual focus needed before you bond with a permanent companion.",al={"why tame plainstrider","yaw sharpmane motive","why adult plainstrider"},it="motive",rc="Tauren",add="fd2667bc"},{q="Why does Yaw Sharpmane have me do this trial instead of doing it himself?",a="This is a personal rite of passage that every aspiring hunter must undergo for themselves. Yaw Sharpmane is an experienced mentor entrusted with teaching ancestral customs, not doing the work for you. Taming a wild creature requires forging your own spiritual link with nature and opening yourself to the Earthmother's presence. No elder or guard can build that bond on your behalf.",al={"why send me","why yaw sharpmane needs me","why must i tame the beast"},it="why_me",rc="Tauren",add="fe0439d7"}},ang={["class:Hunter"]="As a hunter, you begin learning to bond with animals through the Earthmother’s guidance.",["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren, you inherit Mulgore’s ancestral hunting tradition, where taming strengthens the bond between hunter and nature."},kw={"bloodhoof village","earthmother","hunter quest","mulgore","plainstrider","taming","taming rod","yaw sharpmane"},rel={"npc:yaw-sharpmane","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore","quest:6065","quest:6081","topic:tauren"},m={start="Yaw Sharpmane",level="10",faction="Horde",id=6061},src={"Taming the Beast (Tauren 1)"}}
E["quest:6065"] = {t="quest",n="The Hunter's Path",z="mulgore",s="In Mulgore, young tauren hunters are sent to Bloodhoof Village for advanced training. There, they seek Yaw Sharpmane and begin the trials of taming and bonding with wild beasts.",h="In Mulgore, young tauren hunters are sent to Bloodhoof Village for advanced training.",sec={{t="A Rite of Passage",b="Among the Shu'halo, hunting is sacred. A hunter learns the balance of nature, honors the spirits of the plains, and respects the animals that sustain the tribes. When young hunters prove they can survive in the wild and use a bow or gun, their elders send them to seasoned mentors. That journey marks their step beyond solitary tracking and toward adulthood in tauren culture.",sp=0},{t="The Road to Bloodhoof Village",b="The road crosses Mulgore's grasslands to Bloodhoof Village, beside Stonebull Lake. There, Yaw Sharpmane teaches hunters about the local beasts. Meeting him marks a hunter's readiness for the trials of beast taming, where they learn to commune with predators as well as hunt them.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of The Hunter's Path?",a="The Hunter's Path sends novice tauren hunters to Bloodhoof Village to begin their beast-taming trials. There, they speak with Yaw Sharpmane, who oversees the rituals for bonding with an animal companion.",al={"why get this quest","hunters path purpose","hunter pet quest start"},it="purpose"},{q="Who sends you on this quest?",a="Tauren hunter trainers send aspiring hunters once they have mastered the basics of survival and marksmanship. Kary Thunderhorn in Thunder Bluff is one such trainer.",al={"who gives the hunters path","hunter trainer quest"}},{q="Who is Yaw Sharpmane?",a="Yaw Sharpmane is a respected tauren hunter trainer in Bloodhoof Village. He teaches young hunters to approach, calm, and tame Mulgore's wild beasts.",al={"who is sharpmane","yaw sharpmane role"},it="identity"},{q="What happens after completing this quest?",a="After speaking with Yaw Sharpmane, you begin the 'Taming the Beast' trials. You venture into the wild with a taming rod and pacify several native creatures before earning the right to train a permanent companion.",al={"what comes next","next quest after hunters path"},it="next_step"},{q="Why do tauren hunters bond with beasts instead of just hunting them?",a="Tauren tradition holds that beasts are kindred spirits of the Earth Mother. A bonded animal is a companion and partner on the hunt, sharing in mutual respect and survival on the plains.",al={"tauren pet lore","why tame pets tauren"},it="history"},{q="Can hunters of other races take this specific quest?",a="This path is for tauren hunters training in Mulgore. Other Horde races, such as orcs and trolls, receive similar training directions in their home territories in Durotar.",al={"is this tauren only","can orcs do hunters path tauren"},gp=true},{q="Why does your trainer send you to Yaw Sharpmane?",a="Your trainer recognizes that you have grown experienced enough in the wild to take the next crucial step in a hunter's life: bonding with and training an animal companion. They send you to Yaw Sharpmane in Bloodhoof Village because he possesses the specialized knowledge and ancient traditions required to guide you through the trials of taming beasts.",al={"why see yaw sharpmane","trainer motive hunters path","why send me to bloodhoof village"},it="motive",rc="Tauren",add="a380c02c"}},ang={["class:Hunter"]="As a hunter, you take your first step from archer or rifleman toward becoming a beastmaster with a loyal companion at your side.",["race:Tauren"]="As one of the Shu'halo, you learn to tame wild beasts as an expression of reverence for nature and the Earth Mother's creation.",["faction:Horde"]="As a Horde hunter, you rely on fierce frontier beasts to defend the front line and track through Kalimdor's harsh wilderness."},kw={"beast","bloodhoof village","camp narache","hunter's path","mulgore","pet quest","taming","yaw sharpmane"},rel={"npc:yaw-sharpmane","quest:6061","quest:6081","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore","topic:tauren"},m={["end"]="Yaw Sharpmane",level="10",faction="Horde",id=6065},src={"The Hunter's Path (Tauren)"}}
E["quest:6087"] = {t="quest",n="Taming the Beast",z="mulgore",s="In Bloodhoof Village, Yaw Sharpmane sets a young tauren hunter a new trial. The hunter must master the swift prairie stalker.",h="In Bloodhoof Village, Yaw Sharpmane sets a young tauren hunter a new trial.",sec={{t="The Stalker's Lessons",b="After mastering the adult plainstrider’s endurance, you face a different kind of hunter. Prairie stalkers rely on speed, move in packs, and attack without fear. To tame one, you must approach this cunning predator without breaking stride, matching the wolf’s decisive nature.",sp=0},{t="Muscle to Muscle",b="Yaw Sharpmane teaches you that hunting beside a beast means neither servitude nor brute force. You and the predator must fight as one, joined by respect and shared rhythm. Bonding with a prairie stalker under Mulgore’s open skies teaches you to complement your companion’s attacks and move with it in battle.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What animal does Yaw Sharpmane ask me to tame in this trial?",a="Yaw Sharpmane asks you to tame a prairie stalker, a swift wolf that prowls Mulgore’s plains.",al={"what beast do i tame","which pet is next","tame prairie stalker"},it="target"},{q="Why does a tauren hunter learn to tame wolves?",a="Yaw Sharpmane teaches that prairie stalkers embody speed and fearlessness. Bonding with one helps you learn those traits and coordinate attacks with a swift hunting partner.",al={"why wolves","prairie stalker lore","why tame a wolf"},it="motive"},{q="Where can prairie stalkers usually be found?",a="Prairie stalkers roam the grassy plains of central and northern Mulgore, including the prairies around Bloodhoof Village.",al={"prairie stalker location","where are prairie stalkers","find prairie stalker"},gp=true},{q="Do I get to keep the prairie stalker after the quest?",a="No. The taming rod forms only a temporary bond for training. After completing all the trials, you gain the lasting ability to tame any wild companion you choose.",al={"can i keep the wolf","keep prairie stalker","is this my permanent pet"},gp=true},{q="Who is Yaw Sharpmane?",a="Yaw Sharpmane is the hunter trainer in Bloodhoof Village. He guides young tauren through the spiritual and practical steps of mastering Mulgore’s wilds.",al={"who is yaw","sharpmane lore","yaw bloodhoof"},it="identity"},{q="What comes after taming the prairie stalker?",a="After Yaw Sharpmane confirms you have mastered the wolf, he sends you to the third and final trial, where you face the stealthy swoop.",al={"next quest","what is after prairie stalker","third taming quest"},it="next_step"},{q="Why does Yaw Sharpmane send me to tame the beast instead of handling it himself?",a="This is not a task or chore to be delegated; it is a sacred rite of passage in your training as a hunter. Yaw Sharpmane already knows the ways of the wild and commands his own bond with nature. To become a true hunter of the Earthmother, you must walk the path yourself, learning through firsthand trial how to face a swift, fearless predator and bind your spirits together into a unified team.",al={"why me yaw sharpmane","why must i tame the prairie stalker","why doesn't yaw tame it"},it="why_me",add="f904daad"}},ang={["class:Hunter"]="As a hunter, you learn to join survival skills with coordinated attacks, fighting beside a swift companion.",["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren, you follow the Shu'halo way of beast taming: forging a sacred kinship under the Earthmother’s watchful grace."},kw={"bloodhoof village","hunter trial","mulgore","prairie stalker","taming rod","taming the beast","tauren hunter","yaw sharpmane"},rel={"npc:yaw-sharpmane","subzone:bloodhoof-village","quest:6061","quest:6081","quest:6065","zone:mulgore","topic:tauren"},m={start="Yaw Sharpmane",level="10",faction="Horde",id=6087},src={"Taming the Beast (Tauren 2)"}}
E["quest:6088"] = {t="quest",n="Taming the Beast",z="mulgore",s="In Mulgore, Taming the Beast brings a young tauren hunter to the last lesson under Yaw Sharpmane in Bloodhoof Village. A taming rod lets the hunter bond with a wild swoop and prove mastery of the air. Completing the trial grants lasting knowledge to tame, call, and dismiss a companion, with the Earthmother's trust.",h="In Mulgore, Taming the Beast brings a young tauren hunter to the last lesson under Yaw Sharpmane in Bloodhoof...",sec={{t="Mastery of the Sky",b="After you subdue predators on Mulgore's plains, Yaw Sharpmane turns your attention to the open sky. A swoop is a fierce bird of prey with a sharp beak, sweeping dives, and an intimidating air. Its talons test your reflexes and calm as you use the enchanted rod to bring its instincts into accord with your own.",sp=0},{t="The Earthmother's Gift",b="For the tauren, hunting and companionship with beasts are spiritual pursuits, not mere subjugation. Mastering creatures of earth and sky shows the balance and respect needed to walk the plains without disturbing nature. Yaw Sharpmane takes back his temporary taming rod and teaches you lasting techniques to call, dismiss, and permanently tame a companion of your choosing.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why do I have to tame a swoop for the final trial?",a="Yaw Sharpmane tests you against creatures of the sky in this final trial. Swoops strike swiftly and sharply, and their imposing presence tests whether a hunter can handle both air and ground predators before earning independence.",al={"why tame swoop","purpose of swoop taming","why a swoop"},it="target"},{q="Do I keep the swoop I tame during this quest?",a="No. You tame the swoop only temporarily with Yaw Sharpmane's rod during your training. Afterward, you can tame any wild beast you wish to keep permanently.",al={"do i keep the swoop","is the swoop my pet","can i keep this bird"},gp=true},{q="Who gives this quest and where are they located?",a="Yaw Sharpmane, the hunter trainer in Bloodhoof Village in central Mulgore, gives you the third and final step of your initial beast-taming trials.",al={"quest giver","who starts this","where is yaw sharpmane"},it="place",gp=true},{q="What happens after I return the taming rod?",a="Yaw Sharpmane teaches you the inherent abilities to tame, call, and dismiss beasts on your own. He also directs you to Thunder Bluff to learn how to train and care for your chosen companion.",al={"what do i learn next","rewards for finishing","what is after this quest"},it="next_step",gp=true},{q="Where can I find wild swoops in Mulgore?",a="Wild swoops roam Mulgore's open grasslands and mesa edges, often circling the plains just outside Bloodhoof Village.",al={"swoop locations","where are swoops","find a swoop"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why is pet taming considered a rite of passage for tauren?",a="Tauren see beasts as fellow children of the Earthmother, not tools. Bonding with a companion takes patience, mutual respect, and spiritual balance, not force alone.",al={"tauren hunter lore","hunter spiritual path","earthmother connection"},it="history"},{q="Why does Yaw Sharpmane want me to tame a swoop?",a="Yaw Sharpmane is guiding you through the final trial of your hunter training. By learning to charm a creature of the skies, you prove your mastery over beasts of all domains. Once you master this last challenge under the grace of the Earthmother, Yaw can finally bestow upon you the permanent power to tame, summon, and dismiss a companion beast of your own choosing.",al={"yaw sharpmane motive","why tame a swoop","why does yaw want this done"},it="motive",add="8301e163"},{q="Why does Yaw Sharpmane have me do this trial instead of doing it himself?",a="Yaw Sharpmane has already mastered the hunter's way; his duty now is that of a trainer and elder guide in Bloodhoof Village. Taming is not an errand to be handled by village guards or mentors, but an essential personal rite of passage. You must prove your own spirit and bond with nature before you can be entrusted with a lifelong animal companion.",al={"why me taming the beast","why send me yaw sharpmane","why must i tame the swoop"},it="why_me",add="fc772ddc"}},ang={["class:Hunter"]="As a hunter, you complete the foundational training chain and gain the ability to tame beasts permanently, marking your passage from apprentice to self-reliant scout of the wilds.",["race:Tauren"]="As a Shu'halo, bonding with a wild beast is spiritual communion blessed by the Earthmother, with deep cultural meaning across Mulgore's plains."},kw={"bloodhoof village","earthmother","hunter pet","mulgore","swoop","taming rod","taming the beast","yaw sharpmane"},rel={"quest:6081","quest:6065","quest:6061","npc:yaw-sharpmane","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore","topic:tauren"},m={start="Yaw Sharpmane",level="10",faction="Horde",id=6088},src={"Taming the Beast (Tauren 3)"}}
E["quest:6089"] = {t="quest",n="Training the Beast",z="mulgore",s="In Mulgore, young tauren hunters learn to live and hunt beside a beast. After proving their patience and resolve by soothing the plains’ wild creatures, they travel to Thunder Bluff to learn the lasting bond of care and command.",h="In Mulgore, young tauren hunters learn to live and hunt beside a beast.",sec={{t="The Path to the Great Bluff",b="After facing plainstriders, prairie stalkers, and swoops in Mulgore, tauren hunters are ready to take up the full mantle of the hunter. Yaw Sharpmane, their mentor in Bloodhoof Village, sends each novice to Hunter Rise in Thunder Bluff. There, Holt Thunderhorn teaches them. The shu'halo honor him for communing with the wilds, and his reverence for the Earthmother is greater still.",sp=0},{t="A Sacred Bond",b="To the tauren, a beast is a partner joined through harmony with the natural world, never a weapon or thrall. Holt Thunderhorn teaches hunters how to sustain that bond. They learn to guide a creature's combat instincts, feed it regularly, mend its injuries, and call its spirit back if it falls defending its companion on the plains.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who do I speak to for this quest?",a="Speak with Holt Thunderhorn atop Hunter Rise in Thunder Bluff. After your first trials taming wild beasts in Mulgore, Yaw Sharpmane sends you to him.",al={"quest turn in","who is holt thunderhorn","where is holt"},it="next_step",gp=true},{q="What skills do tauren hunters learn upon completing this quest?",a="Holt Thunderhorn teaches you to train your companion in new abilities, feed it to keep it loyal and content, and revive it if it perishes in combat.",al={"what abilities do i get","quest reward skills","what does holt teach"},it="purpose",gp=true},{q="Where does this quest fit in the tauren hunter chain?",a="This is the final step in the sequence. It follows The Hunter's Path and the three trials of Taming the Beast, where you temporarily pacify an adult plainstrider, a prairie stalker, and a swoop across the plains.",al={"hunter quest chain","pet quest mulgore","training the beast chain"},it="history",gp=true},{q="Why does Holt Thunderhorn hold such high standing among tauren hunters?",a="Holt has spent a lifetime testing himself against Azeroth's fiercest beasts and commands several powerful companions. He also keeps a deep spiritual bond with the Earthmother, and his wisdom makes him the chief teacher of young shu'halo hunters in Thunder Bluff.",al={"holt thunderhorn lore","who is holt"},it="identity"},{q="Can hunters tame their permanent pet before turning in this quest?",a="You can already tame wild beasts from the earlier trial, but until you speak with Holt Thunderhorn, you lack the knowledge to feed your companion, revive it, or teach it skills.",al={"can i tame a pet now","when do i get my pet","do i need training the beast"},gp=true},{q="Why is pet care tied to the Earthmother in tauren culture?",a="Tauren see every living creature as a child of the Earthmother. A hunter does not dominate a beast by sheer force of will, but enters a spiritual covenant, offering food and care in return for loyalty and protection.",al={"tauren pet lore","earthmother beasts"},it="ties"},{q="Why does Yaw Sharpmane send you to Holt Thunderhorn?",a="Yaw Sharpmane has taught you how to tame beasts, but commanding and nurturing them requires deeper wisdom. Rather than leave your education half-finished, he directs you to Holt Thunderhorn on Hunter Rise in Thunder Bluff. Holt is among the most revered hunters of the tauren, having bested fierce creatures across Azeroth, and only someone with his profound bond with the Earthmother can properly bestow the sacred responsibilities of raising and training an animal companion.",al={"why does yaw send you away","yaw sharpmane motive","why go to thunder bluff"},it="motive",add="44ef2a73"}},ang={["class:Hunter"]="As a hunter, you leave behind the fight alone and stand beside a loyal companion. This rite completes your first training as a warden of the wilderness.",["race:Tauren"]="As shu'halo, you take an animal companion into battle with a sacred duty to care for it and respect the Earthmother's creations."},kw={"beast training","holt thunderhorn","hunter rise","mulgore","tauren hunter","thunder bluff","training the beast","yaw sharpmane"},rel={"npc:holt-thunderhorn","npc:yaw-sharpmane","quest:6061","quest:6065","subzone:hunter-rise","zone:thunderbluff","zone:mulgore","topic:tauren"},m={start="Yaw Sharpmane",["end"]="Holt Thunderhorn",level="10",faction="Horde",id=6089},src={"Training the Beast (Tauren)"}}
E["quest:743"] = {t="quest",n="Dangers of the Windfury",z="mulgore",s="Ruul Eagletalon asks you to test your readiness against the Windfury harpies in Mulgore. The hunt takes you to the southeastern cliffs, where their speed and claws put your skill to the test before you leave the tauren homeland.",h="Ruul Eagletalon asks you to test your readiness against the Windfury harpies in Mulgore.",sec={{t="A Teacher's Assessment",b="In Bloodhoof Village, Ruul Eagletalon watches travelers pass through Mulgore. He asks those coming of age or preparing for wider travels to prove their combat instincts. Beyond Mulgore's sheltered valleys, survival demands poise against cunning foes, and Ruul measures your mettle against the land's fiercest native predators.",sp=0},{t="The Windfury Threat",b="The Windfury harpies roost along Mulgore's southeastern mountain rim, away from the beaten trails. They threaten wandering wildlife and isolated travelers, diving swiftly and striking with razor-sharp claws as they hunt for fresh flesh. Defeating them and bringing back their talons proves you can overcome a settled threat and react quickly to aerial attackers.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Ruul Eagletalon?",a="Ruul Eagletalon is a veteran tauren warrior and teacher in Bloodhoof Village. He tests young adventurers before they travel into the more perilous reaches of Kalimdor.",al={"ruul eagletalon","who gives dangers of the windfury","ruul"},it="identity"},{q="Where are the Windfury harpies found in Mulgore?",a="The Windfury harpies roost along Mulgore's southeastern mountains, in crude nests high on the crags and foothill slopes. Their nests lie away from the main road through the valley.",al={"windfury harpy location","where are windfury harpies","windfury nest location"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why are the Windfury harpies considered natural enemies of the tauren?",a="The Windfury harpies are aggressive and territorial scavengers that attack those who come near their roosts. Their hunger for fresh meat and attacks on travelers and grazers make them a constant threat to the plains.",al={"why do tauren fight harpies","harpy threat mulgore","windfury lore"},it="hostile"},{q="What proof does Ruul Eagletalon demand from the hunt?",a="Ruul asks you to bring back eight Windfury Talons from slain harpies. The talons are their main natural weapons.",al={"dangers of the windfury proof","what to collect for ruul","windfury quest objective"},it="item",gp=true},{q="Why must adventurers undergo this trial before leaving Mulgore?",a="Ruul sees the Windfury harpies as a test of martial readiness. Their speed, ferocity, and claws challenge those preparing to face dangers beyond Mulgore's defensive bluffs.",al={"purpose of the trial","why do dangers of the windfury","ruul's test"},it="purpose"},{q="Where is Bloodhoof Village located?",a="Bloodhoof Village lies in central Mulgore near Stonebull Lake, south of the Great Gate and Thunder Bluff. Tauren travelers find culture, trade, and training there.",al={"bloodhoof village location","where is bloodhoof","ruul location"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why does Ruul Eagletalon want the Windfury harpies hunted?",a="Ruul Eagletalon is a warrior and mentor charged with preparing young adventurers before they wander beyond the safe boundaries of Mulgore. He sees the ferocious Windfury harpies as a natural trial—a true measure of your martial prowess and readiness to face the harsher threats of the wider world.",al={"why hunt harpies","ruul motive","ruul eagletalon reason"},it="motive",add="d8522cbd"},{q="Who are the Windfury harpies?",a="The Windfury harpies are savage, avian scavengers nesting along the mountain ledges southeast of Bloodhoof Village. Vicious and cunning, they have long preyed upon travelers and wildlife across Mulgore, driven by a relentless hunger for fresh meat and a natural enmity toward the tauren.",al={"windfury harpies","who are windfury","windfury harpies mulgore"},it="target",add="1789ad72"},{q="Why doesn't Ruul Eagletalon hunt the harpies himself?",a="Ruul is a teacher whose duty in Bloodhoof Village is to foster the next generation of defenders, not fight their battles for them. This task is explicitly a rite of passage: you must prove your own strength against Mulgore's predators before you are considered fit to journey beyond the plains.",al={"why me","why send me","why doesnt ruul do it"},it="why_me",add="29c303b8"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a young tauren, clearing Windfury nests helps defend the plains and marks a passage into acknowledged adulthood under the village elders' watch.",["class:Warrior"]="As a warrior, Ruul Eagletalon's challenge teaches you to parry swift diving strikes and face vicious aerial attackers with grounded discipline.",["faction:Horde"]="As an ally of the tauren, taking Ruul's trial shows your dedication to protecting the Horde's heartland and can earn the respect of Bloodhoof Village."},kw={"bloodhoof village","dangers of the windfury","harpies","mulgore","ruul eagletalon","tauren trial","windfury","windfury talons"},rel={"npc:ruul-eagletalon","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore"},m={start="Ruul Eagletalon",faction="Horde",id=743},src={"Dangers of the Windfury"}}
E["quest:745"] = {t="quest",n="Sharing the Land",z="mulgore",s="In Mulgore, Baine Bloodhoof calls you to stop Palemane gnolls from killing the plains' wildlife. By striking their hunters, you help protect the herds and the tauren way of living with the Earth Mother.",h="In Mulgore, Baine Bloodhoof calls you to stop Palemane gnolls from killing the plains' wildlife.",sec={{t="A Land Out of Balance",b="The tauren take from the land only what they need and honor the Earth Mother in all things. Palemane gnolls have settled south of Bloodhoof Village and in a cavern to the west, where they slaughter wildlife across the plains. Their hunting leaves carcasses behind and harms the balance of the grasslands. The tauren are building a peaceful home in Mulgore, but the gnolls' wasteful killing threatens it.",sp=0},{t="When Words Fail",b="Baine Bloodhoof tried to speak with the Palemane gnolls and settle the matter peacefully. They rejected every attempt and kept killing wildlife. Now Baine sends young braves to their hunting camps to put down the poachers, tanners, and skinners before the grasslands suffer lasting harm.",sp=0},{t="The Circle of Life and Death",b="When you return, Baine teaches that taking life is never for malice, but may be needed to keep harmony. Life and death form a circle, and every guardian of Mulgore must carry that truth.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who gives this quest?",a="Baine Bloodhoof, son of High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof, gives you this work in Bloodhoof Village, in central Mulgore.",al={"quest giver","who starts sharing the land","baine quest"},it="identity"},{q="Why are the tauren targeting the Palemane gnolls?",a="The Palemane gnolls hunt Mulgore's wildlife recklessly and deplete the herds. They also rejected the tauren's attempts to speak peacefully.",al={"why kill gnolls","palemane problem","what did gnolls do"},it="motive"},{q="Where are these gnolls located?",a="The Palemanes camp south of Bloodhoof Village and live in the Palemane Rock cave system to the west.",al={"gnoll locations","palemane camp","where to find palemanes"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Did the tauren try to make peace first?",a="Yes. Baine says the tauren tried several times to speak with the gnolls and share the land, but the Palemanes rejected their peaceful offers.",al={"did tauren talk to gnolls","palemane diplomacy"},it="history"},{q="What lesson is Baine trying to teach here?",a="Baine wants young defenders to understand that life and death form an unbreakable circle. Taking life may be needed to protect the balance, but it must be done with respect for the Earth Mother.",al={"baine lesson","meaning of sharing the land"},it="purpose"},{q="Who are the Palemane gnolls?",a="The Palemane gnolls are a primitive, hyena-like tribe native to Mulgore. They have pale fur, defend their territory aggressively, and disregard the harmony of the plains.",al={"who are palemanes","palemane tribe"},it="people"},{q="Who are you sent against in Sharing the Land?",a="Baine sends you against the Palemane gnolls who have encroached upon the plains south of Bloodhoof Village and taken shelter in a western cave. These poachers, skinners, and tanners have been recklessly slaughtering Mulgore's wildlife without reverence for balance or the Earth Mother. Because they violently spurned every peaceful tauren overture, force is the only measure left to halt their poaching.",al={"sharing the land targets","who do i kill sharing the land","palemane gnolls sharing the land"},it="target",add="901e0c5d"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a young tauren, answering Baine's call teaches you to defend Mulgore and honor the Earth Mother's balance.",["class:Hunter"]="The Palemane skinners and poachers hunt with cruelty and waste, without a true hunter's respect for wild creatures.",["class:Druid"]="As a druid, you know unchecked poaching harms the spirit of the plains, and the land's harmony needs your help.",["faction:Horde"]="As a member of the Horde, helping Baine Bloodhoof strengthens your bond with the leaders of the young tauren nation."},kw={"baine bloodhoof","bloodhoof village","gnolls","mulgore","palemane","poachers","sharing the land","skinners","tanners"},rel={"npc:baine-bloodhoof","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore","topic:gnoll"},m={start="Baine Bloodhoof",level="6",faction="Horde",id=745},src={"Sharing the Land (Classic)"}}
E["quest:746"] = {t="quest",n="Dwarven Digging",z="mulgore",s="At Bloodhoof Village, Baine Bloodhoof fears the dwarves' digging will anger Mulgore's beasts and scar its sacred earth. He asks young Horde defenders to break the prospectors' picks and halt their work.",h="At Bloodhoof Village, Baine Bloodhoof fears the dwarves' digging will anger Mulgore's beasts and scar its...",sec={{t="Trespassers in the Hills",b="Northwest of Bloodhoof Village, dwarven prospectors have marked out a dig site in Mulgore's hills. The Explorer's League searches Kalimdor for titan relics and secrets of the earthen, often without regard for the lands or customs of those who live there. To the tauren, the earth is a living mother, worthy of reverence rather than excavation for stone and metal.",sp=0},{t="Defending the Sacred Earth",b="Baine Bloodhoof sees the dwarves' machines and blasting as a threat to the plains' fragile balance. Their noise agitates the local wildlife and stirs the kodos into frenzy. The tauren mean to smash the prospectors' picks at the forges, sending a blunt warning and stopping the digging before it causes lasting harm.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why are dwarves digging in Mulgore?",a="Dwarven prospectors search Mulgore for hidden secrets and artifacts beneath the earth. Their mining disregards tauren territory and customs, bringing them into conflict with the tauren.",al={"dwarves in mulgore","why are prospectors here","dwarven excavation mulgore"},it="why_here"},{q="Who gives the quest Dwarven Digging?",a="Baine Bloodhoof gives the request at Bloodhoof Village in central Mulgore. He is the young son of High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof.",al={"quest giver dwarven digging","who starts dwarven digging"},gp=true},{q="Why does Baine want their tools smashed instead of just stolen?",a="Baine wants the picks broken so the excavation stops at once. Destroying them at the forges warns the dwarves that brute force will not win the land's secrets.",al={"why break the picks","smashing dwarven tools"},it="motive"},{q="How does the digging affect the wildlife of Mulgore?",a="The digging and scars in the earth agitate Mulgore's native creatures, especially the kodos roaming the hills. Peaceful herds grow irritable and aggressive.",al={"angry kodos","why are beasts mad"},it="danger"},{q="Where can the picks be smashed?",a="You can smash the picks at a working forge. The dwarven excavation camp has one, and the blacksmiths in Thunder Bluff have another.",al={"where is the forge","smash prospector picks forge"},gp=true},{q="Does this quest tie into the larger Explorer's League presence in Kalimdor?",a="Yes. Dwarven expeditions from Ironforge have outposts across Kalimdor, including Mulgore, the Barrens, and Southern Kalimdor. Their claims to land and sacred ground often bring them into skirmishes with Horde peoples.",al={"explorers league mulgore","ironforge in kalimdor"},it="ties"},{q="What are the Broken Tools for?",a="The Broken Tools serve as tangible proof that you have put a stop to the dwarves' operations. Rather than simply driving off the prospectors or taking their equipment, Baine Bloodhoof wants their picks smashed at a forge and returned to him to ensure their work is halted and a clear message is sent against defiling the sacred earth.",al={"broken tools","why broken tools","purpose of broken tools"},it="item",add="755e231a"},{q="Who are the dwarves digging in Mulgore?",a="They are dwarven prospectors who have set up an excavation camp in the northwestern hills of Mulgore. Driven by rumors that ancient secrets lie buried beneath the plains, they seek historical relics and titan lore, heedless of the spiritual defilement their quarries inflict upon the land and its wildlife.",al={"dwarves in mulgore","who are the dwarven diggers","prospectors in mulgore"},it="target",add="95107d94"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren, you know the Earth Mother's body is sacred. Foreigners boring into Mulgore's steppes demand a firm response.",["faction:Horde"]="As a member of the Horde, you see Alliance dwarves crossing into Horde lands as a threat to the fragile peace since the Third War and a disregard for tribal borders.",["class:Shaman"]="As a shaman, you know careless digging disturbs the bedrock, unsettles the elements, and stirs unrest among the land's spirits and creatures.",["profession:Mining"]="As a miner, you may value what lies in stone, but the dwarves' work in Mulgore ignores respect and harmony for reckless destruction."},kw={"baine bloodhoof","bloodhoof village","dwarf","forge","kodo","mulgore","picks","prospector"},rel={"subzone:bloodhoof-village","npc:baine-bloodhoof","zone:mulgore","zone:thunderbluff"},m={start="Baine Bloodhoof",level="8",faction="Horde",id=746,ri={"Broken Tools"}},src={"Dwarven Digging"}}
E["quest:747"] = {t="quest",n="The Hunt Begins",z="mulgore",s="Young hunters at Camp Narache gather food and feathers for their people. Grull Hawkwind asks you to hunt plainstriders on Red Cloud Mesa, where the village depends on what the young can provide.",h="Young hunters at Camp Narache gather food and feathers for their people.",sec={{t="A Tribe's Sustenance",b="On Red Cloud Mesa, the tauren hunt to feed and clothe their people. They also value the stalk and chase as a test of discipline and a sport. When Camp Narache's stores run low, the young hunters gather plainstrider meat for cooking and feathers for tribal garments.",sp=0},{t="Earning Recognition",b="Grull Hawkwind judges the newcomers who hope to leave Red Cloud Mesa for the wider plains of Mulgore. Before they can meet his father, Chief Hawkwind, he asks them to prove their ability. A successful hunt shows self-reliance and helps support the tribe.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who gives this quest?",a="Grull Hawkwind gives you this work in Camp Narache. He is Chief Hawkwind's son and guides young hunters on Red Cloud Mesa.",al={"quest giver","who starts the hunt begins","grull hawkwind"},it="identity"},{q="Why are plainstriders hunted instead of other beasts?",a="Plainstriders are plentiful on the mesa. Their meat helps replenish low food stores, and their feathers are used in traditional tauren clothing and adornments.",al={"why plainstriders","plainstrider meat and feathers","hunting plainstriders"},it="target"},{q="Why do I have to prove myself before seeing Chief Hawkwind?",a="Grull Hawkwind asks you to show basic survival skills and loyalty to the village before you meet Chief Hawkwind and take on deeper responsibilities.",al={"why prove to chief hawkwind","chief hawkwind approval","why hunt first"},it="why_me"},{q="What is the broader goal of these trials on Red Cloud Mesa?",a="Red Cloud Mesa tests young tauren before they descend to Mulgore's wider plains and enter Thunder Bluff, the capital city.",al={"mesa trials purpose","why am i on red cloud mesa","leaving red cloud mesa"},it="purpose"},{q="What comes after this hunt?",a="After you bring back meat and feathers, Grull approves your hunt and sends you to track larger, harder-to-find game across the mesa.",al={"next quest","followup","what is after the hunt begins"},it="next_step"},{q="Do tauren only hunt for survival?",a="Tauren hunt for food and resources such as leather, bone, and feathers, and also for sport and spiritual communion. Hunting sharpens the instincts that keep their tribes alert and strong.",al={"tauren hunting philosophy","hunt for sport","why do tauren hunt"}},{q="Why does Grull Hawkwind want this done?",a="Grull Hawkwind sees promise in you and wants you to prove your worth to the tribe and his father, Chief Hawkwind. Camp Narache's stores of meat have run low, and the camp needs fresh feathers. By sending you to harvest them, he tests your hunting instincts while restocking the village's essential provisions.",al={"why does grull want plainstriders hunted","grull hawkwind motive","why hunt plainstriders for grull"},it="motive",add="395288c5"},{q="What is the Plainstrider Meat for?",a="The meat is gathered to replenish Camp Narache's dwindling food stores. Survival on Red Cloud Mesa demands reliable sustenance, and hunting plainstriders provides the fresh meat needed to feed the young hunters and elders dwelling in the camp.",al={"what is plainstrider meat used for","why collect plainstrider meat","plainstrider meat use"},it="item",add="f26a5c4f"},{q="What is the Plainstrider Feather for?",a="Plainstrider feathers are used by the tauren of Camp Narache to craft clothing and adornments. The tribe relies on every part of the beasts they hunt, turning sturdy plumage into practical attire and regalia suited for life on the mesa.",al={"what are plainstrider feathers used for","why collect plainstrider feathers","plainstrider feather use"},it="item",add="fb1052a7"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a young shu'halo, your first hunt for plainstriders on the mesa marks your bond with the land and your duty to feed Camp Narache.",["class:Hunter"]="As a hunter, you practice marksmanship and tracking while hunting the swift plainstriders of Red Cloud Mesa, and learn to honor the beasts that sustain you."},kw={"camp narache","chief hawkwind","feathers","grull hawkwind","hunt begins","mulgore","plainstrider","red cloud mesa"},rel={"npc:grull-hawkwind","npc:chief-hawkwind","subzone:camp-narache","zone:mulgore","zone:thunderbluff","topic:tauren"},m={start="Grull Hawkwind",level="2",faction="Horde",id=747,ri={"Plainstrider Feather","Plainstrider Meat"}},src={"The Hunt Begins"}}
E["quest:748"] = {t="quest",n="Poison Water",z="mulgore",s="In Mulgore, tainted wells threaten the water that sustains the tauren and local wildlife. Mull Thunderhorn in Bloodhoof Village asks young Horde defenders to gather parts from the plains for a cleansing totem.",h="In Mulgore, tainted wells threaten the water that sustains the tauren and local wildlife.",sec={{t="A Land Desecrated",b="Goblin interlopers and their lackeys have tainted Mulgore's wells, threatening the water that sustains the tauren tribes and local wildlife. Mull Thunderhorn, an elder in Bloodhoof Village, has discovered the pollution. For the tauren, who recently settled here, the wells are vital to life on these sacred plains.",sp=0},{t="Gathering Nature's Essence",b="Mull turns to tauren shamanism to cleanse the Winterhoof Water Well. He needs a ritual totem made with the strength of Mulgore's native beasts. You gather paws from prairie wolves and talons from mature plainstriders on the southwestern plains, bringing the land's own strength to bear against the pollution.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who poisoned the water wells in Mulgore?",a="Goblin interlopers and their lackeys have tainted Mulgore's sacred wells, threatening the water that sustains the tauren and local wildlife.",al={"who tainted the wells","who poisoned mulgore","who polluted the water"},it="threat"},{q="Why does Mull Thunderhorn need wolf paws and plainstrider talons?",a="Mull uses prairie wolf paws and plainstrider talons to make a cleansing totem rooted in the strength of Mulgore's native beasts.",al={"why wolf paws","why plainstrider talons","totem materials"},it="item"},{q="Which well does this quest prepare to cleanse?",a="The gathered parts will make a cleansing totem for the Winterhoof Water Well.",al={"what well is this for","which water well"},it="target"},{q="Where is Mull Thunderhorn located?",a="Mull Thunderhorn is in Bloodhoof Village, the main tauren settlement in central Mulgore, near Stonebull Lake.",al={"mull thunderhorn location","where is mull","where to turn in poison water"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Where can the necessary wolves and plainstriders be found?",a="Prairie wolves and adult plainstriders roam the plains southwest of Bloodhoof Village.",al={"where to find prairie wolves","plainstrider location","hunting grounds"},it="target",gp=true},{q="What happens after Mull receives the paws and talons?",a="Mull binds the paws and talons into a Winterhoof Cleansing Totem, then sends you to the Winterhoof Water Well to perform the purification rite.",al={"next quest after poison water","what do i do next"},it="next_step"},{q="Why does Mull Thunderhorn want this done?",a="Goblins and their hirelings have corrupted the sacred water wells across Mulgore, striking at the very lifeblood of the tauren homeland. For Mull Thunderhorn and the people of Bloodhoof Village, leaving the wells tainted invites sickness and defiles the sacred balance of the earth. Purifying them is an urgent spiritual and practical duty.",al={"mull thunderhorn motive","why cleanse the water wells","why poison water quest"},it="motive",add="50414ce1"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For a tauren, poisoned fresh water violates the Earth Mother's gifts. Cleansing the wells is a duty as you come of age in Mulgore.",["class:Shaman"]="For a shaman, restoring polluted water with a totem made from beast parts reflects tauren shamanism and its bond with local wildlife.",["class:Druid"]="For a druid, goblins poisoning fresh springs threatens Mulgore's natural balance. You must protect its fragile flora and fauna."},kw={"bloodhoof village","cleansing totem","mulgore","mull thunderhorn","plainstrider","poison water","prairie wolf","winterhoof water well"},rel={"npc:mull-thunderhorn","subzone:winterhoof-water-well","zone:mulgore","topic:goblin"},m={start="Mull Thunderhorn",level="5",faction="Horde",id=748,ri={"Plainstrider Talon","Prairie Wolf Paw"}},src={"Poison Water (Classic)"}}
E["quest:749"] = {t="quest",n="The Ravaged Caravan",z="mulgore",s="Near Stonebull Lake, The Ravaged Caravan still draws Venture Company salvagers. Morin Cloudstalker sends travelers to inspect the wreckage after tauren warriors burned the convoy in retribution for lost companions. Intact crates reveal the industrial scale of Venture Company operations in Mulgore.",h="Near Stonebull Lake, The Ravaged Caravan still draws Venture Company salvagers.",sec={{t="Retribution by the Lake",b="Tauren warriors burned a Venture Company convoy after an earlier skirmish cost them two companions. Morin Cloudstalker and his warriors struck swiftly, then torched the wagons to drive out the armed intruders. They left the smoking wreckage, believing the fire would consume whatever the goblins had come to haul.",sp=0},{t="Unfinished Business",b="Venture Company salvagers returned to the blackened wreckage, suggesting the fire had failed to destroy the most crucial cargo. Morin now sends scouts to inspect the remains rather than risk more lives blindly. Intact crates among the soot-stained debris bear the Venture Company Mining Division insignia. They are packed for shipment to central processing facilities.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why was the caravan destroyed?",a="Morin Cloudstalker and his tauren warriors ambushed and burned the caravan in retribution after two of their scouts were killed in an earlier skirmish with the Venture Company.",al={"who destroyed the caravan","why is the caravan burned","caravan attack"},it="history"},{q="Where is the Ravaged Caravan located?",a="The burned wagons lie along Stonebull Lake’s northern shore, east of Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore.",al={"caravan location","where is the caravan","ravaged caravan spot"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why are the goblins returning to the wreckage?",a="The Venture Company sent salvagers because the fire failed to destroy all its cargo. They seek to recover durable supply crates marked for shipment to its central processing operations.",al={"why are salvagers here","venture co salvagers","what do the goblins want"},it="why_here"},{q="What is inside the supply crates?",a="The crates bear the Venture Company Mining Division insignia and hold mineral resources labeled for refinement and processing at the company’s primary industrial plants.",al={"crate contents","what is in the crate","sealed supply crate"},it="item"},{q="Who is Morin Cloudstalker?",a="Morin Cloudstalker is a tauren scout and tracker stationed east of Bloodhoof Village. He monitors hostile incursions across central Mulgore, particularly those from the Venture Company.",al={"who is morin","morin cloudstalker role"},it="identity"},{q="What does this caravan reveal about the Venture Company?",a="The caravan shows the Venture Company’s presence in Mulgore is more than a mere exploratory team. It is an organized industrial extraction effort with armed supply lines and centralized refining networks.",al={"venture co mulgore presence","why are goblins in mulgore"},it="threat"},{q="Why does Morin Cloudstalker want the caravan crates examined?",a="Morin and his braves ambushed the Venture Company caravan by Stonebull Lake to avenge fallen comrades, burning the wagons and assuming the fire would wipe out anything useful. Seeing goblin salvagers picking through the wreckage alerted him that something valuable or dangerous survived the flames. He needs you to inspect the crates to learn what the Venture Company was moving through Mulgore and why they are so eager to retrieve it.",al={"why examine caravan","morin cloudstalker motive","why search supply crates"},it="motive",add="89fc7cfd"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Venture Company machines desecrate the plains, and its timber-clearing strikes directly at your ancestral homeland. Recovering and destroying its supplies helps defend Mulgore.",["faction:Horde"]="Unchecked corporate expansion into Mulgore threatens Thunder Bluff’s stability, so Horde scouts must disrupt Venture Company logistics.",["profession:Mining"]="The sturdy crates and logistics notes show the Venture Company’s aggressive mineral surveying and extraction operations across Mulgore."},kw={"morin cloudstalker","mulgore","ravaged caravan","stonebull lake","supply crate","venture co","venture company"},rel={"npc:morin-cloudstalker","subzone:ravaged-caravan","subzone:stonebull-lake","quest:764","zone:mulgore"},m={start="Morin Cloudstalker",["end"]="Sealed Supply Crate",faction="Horde",id=749},src={"The Ravaged Caravan (Horde) (1)"}}
E["quest:751"] = {t="quest",n="The Ravaged Caravan",z="mulgore",s="Investigating a broken transport in Mulgore reveals that goblin industrialists have established an illegal foothold in tauren territory. By recovering documents from a sealed crate, scouts confirm the Venture Company is systematically stripping the land's mineral wealth. This discovery rallies the Bloodhoof Outrunners to mount an active defense of their ancestral plains.",h="Investigating a broken transport in Mulgore reveals that goblin industrialists have established an illegal...",sec={{t="Evidence of Exploitation",b="The wreckage of a ravaged caravan east of Bloodhoof Village tells an unsettling story. Within a sealed supply crate lies a dense cache of mined ore and minerals alongside a bound sheaf of Venture Company documents. This paperwork provides irrefutable proof that the greedy Venture Company has begun an unpermitted mining operation deep in the tranquil valleys of Mulgore.",sp=0},{t="The Outrunners' Vigil",b="Morin Cloudstalker and his fellow Outrunners have maintained a cautious watch over Mulgore's borders, mindful of outside factions looking to exploit their peaceful homeland. Delivering the retrieved documents confirms Morin's worst fears: smoke rising over the eastern foothills signals an escalating commercial invasion. The tauren are left with no choice but to push back against the defilers before their hills are gutted entirely.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is inside the sealed supply crate?",a="Prying open the crate reveals a packed assortment of mined ore, raw minerals, and a sheaf of Venture Company documents outlining their operations.",al={"supply crate loot","what is in the crate","crate contents"},it="item"},{q="Who is Morin Cloudstalker?",a="Morin Cloudstalker is a tauren Outrunner stationed on the road east of Bloodhoof Village toward the Barrens border, tasked with patrolling Mulgore and tracking foreign threats.",al={"who is morin","morin cloudstalker role","outrunner morin"},it="identity"},{q="Why is the Venture Company in Mulgore?",a="The Venture Company seeks to ruthlessly exploit Mulgore's rich natural resources and veins of mineral ore, carving into the hills without regard for the earth or its native people.",al={"why are goblins here","venture co goal mulgore","what do goblins want"},it="why_here"},{q="Where did the caravan come from?",a="The caravan belonged to the Venture Company, transporting mined ore and operational paperwork out from their foothill operations before it was destroyed along the trail.",al={"whose caravan was it","caravan origin","who attacked the caravan"},it="origin"},{q="Where does this investigation lead next?",a="Armed with the documents, Morin Cloudstalker directs warriors to directly confront the Venture Company's leadership and dismantle their mining operation in the eastern foothills.",al={"what comes next","next quest after ravaged caravan","following up on the caravan"},sp=1,it="next_step"},{q="Why does this threaten the tauren?",a="The tauren view the land as sacred and revere the Earth Mother. The Venture Company's reckless strip mining and industrial pollution actively defile Mulgore's pristine landscape.",al={"why do tauren care","venture co threat","threat to mulgore"},it="threat"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For the tauren, seeing crude industrial machinery tear into the ancestral plains of Mulgore is an affront to the Earth Mother and an act of war against their newfound peace.",["faction:Horde"]="The Horde relies on the loyalty and stability of Mulgore; leaving foreign merchant cartels to despoil its hills would threaten the safety of Thunder Bluff itself.",["class:Druid"]="Druids hold the balance of the land sacred, making the destructive, smoke-choked strip mining of the Venture Company a desecration that must be cleansed.",["profession:Mining"]="The high-grade ores packed within the crate prove the eastern hills of Mulgore conceal rich subterranean veins that outsiders are racing to exploit."},kw={"bloodhoof village","morin cloudstalker","mulgore","outrunners","ravaged caravan","supply crate","venture co","venture co documents"},rel={"npc:morin-cloudstalker","quest:764","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore"},m={start="Sealed Supply Crate",["end"]="Morin Cloudstalker",faction="Horde",id=751},src={"The Ravaged Caravan (Horde) (2)"}}
E["quest:752"] = {t="quest",n="A Humble Task",z="mulgore",s="A Humble Task is an early rite of duty given by Chief Hawkwind in Camp Narache. As the Chieftain attends to pressing tribal concerns on the Red Cloud Mesa, he asks a young adventurer to venture southeast and check on his elderly mother at the local water well. The request emphasizes communal responsibility, elder reverence, and the daily harmony essential to the tauren way of life.",h="A Humble Task is an early rite of duty given by Chief Hawkwind in Camp Narache.",sec={{t="Communal Harmony and Duty",b="In the shadow of Camp Narache, every member of the tribe is expected to pull their weight and share in the harmony of life. For young tauren coming of age, tribal duty is not measured solely in battle prowess against quilboars or predators; it begins with basic social responsibility. Chief Hawkwind demonstrates this ethos directly: bound to his post by administrative and defensive leadership, he places his trust in novice adventurers to watch over the community's vulnerable elders.",sp=0},{t="The Well Southeast of Narache",b="Mulgore's Red Cloud Mesa relies on vital natural water wells scattered beyond the perimeter of the encampment. Early in the morning, Greatmother Hawkwind left the village unattended to retrieve fresh water from the southeastern spring. In a land stalked by territorial wildlife and encroaching Bristleback quilboars, an elder remaining absent for hours raises quiet concern, prompting Hawkwind to send aid to ensure she is unharmed.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who offers this quest and where do I find them?",a="Chief Hawkwind, the leader of Camp Narache on the Red Cloud Mesa in Mulgore, offers the quest. He stands in the central pavilion of the camp overseeing local affairs.",al={"who gives a humble task","where to pick up quest","chief hawkwind location"},gp=true},{q="Where is Greatmother Hawkwind located?",a="Greatmother Hawkwind is located just southeast of Camp Narache, tending to the water well on the lower elevation of the mesa.",al={"greatmother hawkwind location","where is the mother","water well location"},gp=true},{q="Why doesn't Chief Hawkwind check on his mother himself?",a="Chief Hawkwind must remain at Camp Narache to oversee pressing tribal matters and guide young braves. As a chieftain, he relies on the tribe's collective responsibility and trusts rising adventurers to look after village affairs in his stead.",al={"why cant chief go","why doesn't hawkwind check","chief hawkwind busy"},it="why_me"},{q="Is Greatmother Hawkwind in danger at the well?",a="While the surrounding plains host aggressive mountain cougars and striders, Greatmother Hawkwind is found safe and sound beside the well, pleasantly surprised that someone went out of their way to check on an elder.",al={"is she safe","is greatmother attacked","danger at water well"},it="danger"},{q="What cultural significance does this quest carry for the tauren?",a="Tauren culture places immense value on family, community cohesion, and reverence for elders. The quest teaches young heroes that true strength is rooted in humble service and protecting one's kin rather than chasing personal glory.",al={"tauren culture humble task","why is this a humble task","tauren elders"}},{q="What happens after finding Greatmother Hawkwind?",a="Once you speak with Greatmother Hawkwind, she completes the task and asks for further assistance in carrying the filled water pitcher back to the camp.",al={"next quest","a humble task follow up","what to do after finding mother"},it="next_step",gp=true},{q="Why does Chief Hawkwind want you to check on his mother?",a="His elderly mother left Camp Narache early in the morning to draw water from the well to the southeast, and she has been gone for quite a long time. Tauren life is rooted in communal responsibility and mutual care, so while Hawkwind worries for her well-being, his duties as chieftain keep him anchored at the camp.",al={"why does hawkwind want this done","chief hawkwind motive","why check on greatmother hawkwind"},it="motive",add="b1d313d3"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For young tauren braves, answering the Chieftain's call demonstrates the core tenets of the Earth Mother's teachings: respect for elders and unhesitating commitment to the tribe's shared well-being.",["faction:Horde"]="The quiet, pastoral nature of Narache's tasks highlights how the tauren's tranquil communal philosophy contrasts with the harsher, survivalist grit found across the rest of the fledgling Horde."},kw={"a humble task","camp narache","chief hawkwind","greatmother hawkwind","mulgore","red cloud mesa","water well"},rel={"npc:chief-hawkwind","npc:greatmother-hawkwind","subzone:camp-narache","zone:mulgore"},m={start="Chief Hawkwind",["end"]="Greatmother Hawkwind",level="2",faction="Horde",id=752},src={"A Humble Task"}}
E["quest:753"] = {t="quest",n="A Humble Task",z="mulgore",s="In Red Cloud Mesa, A Humble Task sends young tauren to carry well water to Camp Narache. Greatmother Hawkwind asks for this service, a first lesson in humility and care for the tribe.",h="In Red Cloud Mesa, A Humble Task sends young tauren to carry well water to Camp Narache.",sec={{t="Vitality of the Youth",b="Greatmother Hawkwind has walked many trails, though age has slowed her across the plains. She tends to her duties beside the well on Red Cloud Mesa's southern edge. When young tauren with hopes of defending their people meet her, she turns their energy toward the needs of the community. Service gives them a chance to prove themselves through care for others.",sp=0},{t="Service to the Tribe",b="You carry a filled pitcher northwest to Camp Narache and give it to Chief Hawkwind, the settlement's leader and Greatmother Hawkwind's son. Life across Mulgore's dry plains depends on shared labor and steady supplies of fresh water. By carrying a burden for an elder, you show respect for the people whose care holds the tribe together.",sp=0},{t="A Path Toward Thunder Bluff",b="When you bring his mother's water to Camp Narache, Chief Hawkwind sees more than a chore completed. Tauren custom asks those who would champion their people across distant lands to begin with humility and a ready hand. Your quiet discipline catches the chief's eye, and he recommends you for the Rites of the Earthmother, spiritual tests that open the gates to Thunder Bluff.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Greatmother Hawkwind?",a="Greatmother Hawkwind is an elder of the Hawkwind family who tends the well on Red Cloud Mesa. She is Chief Hawkwind's mother and teaches young tauren humility and care for their tribe.",al={"greatmother hawkwind","who gives a humble task","hawkwind elder"},it="identity"},{q="Where is the water well located?",a="The water well is southeast of Camp Narache, on the grassy plains of Red Cloud Mesa. Pitchers of fresh water rest beside it for travelers and camp caretakers to carry back.",al={"water well location","where is the well","find water pitcher"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Who is Chief Hawkwind?",a="Chief Hawkwind leads Camp Narache on Red Cloud Mesa. He directs the training and spiritual rites of young tauren as they prepare to serve the wider tribe in Mulgore.",al={"chief hawkwind","who is the chief","camp narache leader"},it="leader"},{q="Why is this chore treated as an important test?",a="Tauren value humility, care for the community, and respect for elders. Everyday duties show that you care for your people as well as your own glory in battle.",al={"why carry water","why humble task","is this quest important"},it="purpose"},{q="What comes after completing A Humble Task?",a="After carrying the water, Chief Hawkwind directs you to the Rites of the Earthmother, traditional challenges for tauren coming of age.",al={"what comes next","next quest","after humble task"},it="next_step"},{q="Where does this path eventually lead?",a="The elders' respect prepares you to leave Red Cloud Mesa and travel across Mulgore toward Thunder Bluff, the capital's high mesas.",al={"future destination","where are we going","thunder bluff goal"}},{q="Why does Greatmother Hawkwind want this done?",a="Greatmother Hawkwind admits that her aging legs lack their former vigor, making simple daily chores around the Red Cloud Mesa slow and tiring. More importantly, she views this routine errand as a vital moral lesson for you. Delivering essential drinking water to her son, Chief Hawkwind, serves as an early trial to see if you possess the humility, patience, and community spirit expected of a true servant of the tribe.",al={"greatmother hawkwind motive","why bring water chief hawkwind","why humble task"},it="motive",add="57bce500"},{q="What is the Water Pitcher for?",a="The Water Pitcher is a simple clay vessel drawn fresh from the well on Red Cloud Mesa to provide clean water for Camp Narache. Transporting it tests your dedication to communal chores, ensuring that even fledgling adventurers learn the value of sustaining their kin before pursuing grander martial glory.",al={"water pitcher purpose","why water pitcher","what is the pitcher"},it="item",add="b511fd77"},{q="Why does Greatmother Hawkwind ask you instead of doing it herself?",a="Greatmother Hawkwind is elderly and admits her legs no longer have their former strength, meaning carrying heavy pitchers takes her far longer than it used to. When an able-bodied novice like you approaches, she seizes the chance to both ease her own burden and test your willingness to perform unglamorous, everyday labor for the camp.",al={"why not greatmother","why ask me for water","why cant hawkwind get water"},it="why_me",add="19ad5d96"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren, carrying water for your elders shows communal care and readiness to walk the path of the Earthmother.",["faction:Horde"]="As a member of the Horde, you see how the Hawkwind family teaches tauren solidarity and survival in Mulgore, a peaceful yet steadfast part of Thrall's Horde."},kw={"camp narache","chief hawkwind","greatmother hawkwind","humble task","mulgore","red cloud mesa","rites of the earthmother","water pitcher"},rel={"quest:752","npc:greatmother-hawkwind","npc:chief-hawkwind","quest:763","subzone:camp-narache","zone:mulgore","zone:thunderbluff"},m={start="Greatmother Hawkwind",["end"]="Chief Hawkwind",level="3",faction="Horde",id=753,ri={"Water Pitcher"}},src={"A Humble Task (2)"}}
E["quest:754"] = {t="quest",n="Winterhoof Cleansing",z="mulgore",s="Winterhoof Cleansing is a rite entrusted to Horde adventurers by Mull Thunderhorn in Bloodhoof Village. After goblin operations fouled the local springs, a consecrated totem must be carried into the Venture Company's territory to purify the Winterhoof Water Well. Restoring this spring is a vital first step in healing the poisoned waters of central Mulgore.",h="Winterhoof Cleansing is a rite entrusted to Horde adventurers by Mull Thunderhorn in Bloodhoof Village.",sec={{t="A Land Despoiled",b="The grassy plains of Mulgore rely on a delicate network of ancestral water wells to sustain both wild game and the scattered tauren tribes. Recently, ruthless industrial mercenaries of the Venture Company moved into the rolling hills southeast of Bloodhoof Village. Their operations have contaminated the local aquifers, leaving the Winterhoof Water Well rank and toxic. Wild beasts shun the tainted water, and the nearby camps face an impending crisis if the corruption spreads further through the earth.",sp=0},{t="The Ritual of Purifying Waters",b="To counter the industrial filth, Mull Thunderhorn carves and enchants a ritual cleansing totem infused with the blessings of the Earth Mother. However, delivering this remedy requires braving armed Venture Company goons who have staked their claim over the spring. By placing the totem directly at the wellhead, an adventurer dispels the foul runoff, allowing clean, life-giving water to rise once more from the depths of the earth.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where is the Winterhoof Water Well located?",a="The Winterhoof Water Well is located in the hills to the southeast of Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore. It is heavily patrolled by goblin laborers and mercenaries working for the Venture Company.",al={"winterhoof well location","where is winterhoof well","how to find winterhoof well"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why was the water well poisoned?",a="The Venture Company established mining and excavation operations nearby, allowing toxic industrial runoff and refuse to seep directly into the aquifer, rendering the natural spring undrinkable for local wildlife and the tauren.",al={"who poisoned the water","why is the well dirty","venture company well"},it="threat"},{q="Who is Mull Thunderhorn?",a="Mull Thunderhorn is a spiritual elder stationed in Bloodhoof Village. Deeply tuned to the health of the earth, he crafts ceremonial totems to undo the environmental devastation wrought by outside invaders.",al={"who is mull","mull thunderhorn role"},it="identity"},{q="What happens after the well is cleansed?",a="Once the Winterhoof Water Well runs clear and wildlife begins drinking from it safely again, Mull Thunderhorn directs your attention toward other compromised wells in Mulgore, starting with the Thunderhorn Totem.",al={"what comes next","after winterhoof cleansing","what does mull do next"},sp=1,it="next_step"},{q="Who are the guards at the well?",a="The well is occupied by laborers and hirelings employed by the Venture Company, an unscrupulous goblin-led syndicate that strips lands of natural resources with no regard for the local ecosystem.",al={"who guards winterhoof well","what enemies are at the well"},it="target"},{q="How does the cleansing totem work?",a="Carved with sacred tauren rites and warding rituals, the totem draws upon natural spirits and the blessings of the Earth Mother to purge unnatural chemical pollutants from the spring's source.",al={"cleansing totem lore","how does mull totem work"},it="power"},{q="Why does Mull Thunderhorn want the Winterhoof Water Well cleansed?",a="The Winterhoof Water Well is vital to the balance and survival of Mulgore. Its water has been fouled, threatening both the local wildlife and the tribes who depend on it. Mull Thunderhorn has prepared a consecrated cleansing totem, but its power must be brought directly to the site to heal the waters and restore harmony to the land.",al={"why cleanse the winterhoof well","mull thunderhorn motive","why cleanse winterhoof water"},it="motive",add="16ee77f0"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="The wells of Mulgore are sacred life-givers gifted by the Earth Mother; defending them against goblin greed is both a civic duty and a holy obligation for every tauren.",["class:Shaman"]="Channeling spirits through totems to reverse unnatural industrial defilement represents the core purpose of a shaman's bond with the elemental waters.",["class:Druid"]="Seeing the immediate return of thirsty wildlife to the purified spring proves the vital necessity of healing nature wherever civilization threatens to despoil it.",["faction:Horde"]="Securing Mulgore's fresh water supply stabilizes the heartlands of the newly settled tauren, strengthening the southern pillar of the young Horde."},kw={"bloodhoof village","cleansing","mulgore","mull thunderhorn","poison","totem","venture company","water well","winterhoof"},rel={"npc:mull-thunderhorn","subzone:winterhoof-water-well","subzone:bloodhoof-village","topic:venture-company","zone:mulgore","topic:goblin"},m={start="Mull Thunderhorn",level="6",faction="Horde",id=754},src={"Winterhoof Cleansing (Classic)"}}
E["quest:756"] = {t="quest",n="Thunderhorn Totem",z="mulgore",s="In Bloodhoof Village, Mull Thunderhorn asks young Horde champions to help make a sacred cleansing totem. Goblin pollution is spreading through Mulgore's water wells, and natural predator claws will bind the land's primal strength into ritual wood.",h="In Bloodhoof Village, Mull Thunderhorn asks young Horde champions to help make a sacred cleansing totem.",sec={{t="Hunting the Plains Predators",b="Mull Thunderhorn needs natural claws to make a cleansing totem. The Venture Company is poisoning Mulgore's water sources through its industrial exploitation, and he works to craft a remedy. Prairie stalkers and flatland cougars roam east and west of Bloodhoof Village. Their claws carry the plains predators' natural essences, channeling their raw, uncorrupted predatory vigor directly into the totem.",sp=0},{t="The Nature of the Hunt",b="Tauren tradition distinguishes hunting for sustenance or spiritual balance from the goblins' wholesale plunder. Mull says the native predators' pure way of hunting can help purify corrupted earth. Goblin machinery fouls the springs with oil and industrial waste, while the beasts' natural strength provides the spiritual catalyst to restore the Thunderhorn Water Well.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why does Mull Thunderhorn need predator claws?",a="Mull uses prairie stalker and flatland cougar claws to channel the plains' pure, primal predatory spirit into a cleansing totem. He believes the spirit of natural hunters can counter artificial, industrial corruption.",al={"why claws","purpose of stalker claws","cougar claws purpose"},it="item"},{q="Who is corrupting the water in Mulgore?",a="Venture Company goblins operating in Mulgore pollute the land and water. Their reckless resource extraction and mining rigs send toxic sludge into the aquifers that feed the tribal wells.",al={"who tainted the well","why is the well poisoned","what poisoned mulgore"},it="threat"},{q="Where are the prairie stalkers and flatland cougars found?",a="Prairie stalkers and flatland cougars roam the open plains of central Mulgore, mainly across the rolling savannas east and west of Bloodhoof Village.",al={"stalker location","where to find cougars","cougar claw locations"},gp=true},{q="What happens after crafting the Thunderhorn Totem?",a="After carving and enchanting the totem with the claws, Mull sends you to the tainted Thunderhorn Water Well to perform the cleansing ritual and drive out the poison.",al={"what next","after thunderhorn totem","totem follow up"},sp=1,it="next_step"},{q="How does this relate to the previous cleansing at Winterhoof Well?",a="The tauren are working to preserve Mulgore's sacred waters. The Winterhoof Water Well was purified first, and now contamination threatens the Thunderhorn well further north.",al={"winterhoof connection","previous totem quest"},it="history"},{q="Why don't the tauren simply use herbal remedies instead of animal claws?",a="Tauren ritual craft draws on elemental and physical aspects of nature. The sharp purity of apex predators provides the active, contending spiritual force needed to cut through aggressive goblin industrial poisons.",al={"why not herbs","shamanic totem materials"}},{q="Why does Mull Thunderhorn want this done?",a="The Thunderhorn Water Well is growing more tainted by the hour, poisoned by reckless goblin operations nearby. Mull Thunderhorn needs to craft a sacred cleansing totem to purify the well before the corruption spreads further through the earth and harms his people.",al={"why mull wants this","mull thunderhorn motive","why craft thunderhorn totem"},it="motive",add="cb047e40"},{q="What are you sent against for the claws?",a="You are sent to hunt the wild predators prowling Mulgore's plains: the prairie stalkers and flatland cougars. These beasts roam naturally to the east and west of Bloodhoof Village, living and hunting as part of the land's natural balance.",al={"what do i hunt","who are the targets","thunderhorn totem prey"},it="target",add="43953f1d"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren, defending Mulgore's aquifers from foreign exploitation is a sacred tribal duty. The Earth Mother's mandate calls for life in balance rather than plunder.",["class:Shaman"]="As a shaman, you invoke the living spirits of the land to cleanse water corrupted by unnatural, careless enterprise.",["class:Hunter"]="As a hunter, you learn the plains' native stalking beasts and honor the kill as you gather their claws to preserve the ecosystem."},kw={"bloodhoof village","cleansing","cougar claws","flatland cougar","mulgore","mull thunderhorn","prairie stalker","stalker claws","thunderhorn totem","water well"},rel={"quest:758","npc:mull-thunderhorn","subzone:thunderhorn-water-well","zone:mulgore","topic:goblin"},m={start="Mull Thunderhorn",level="7",faction="Horde",id=756,ri={"Cougar Claws","Stalker Claws"}},src={"Thunderhorn Totem (Classic)"}}
E["quest:757"] = {t="quest",n="Rite of Strength",z="mulgore",s="At Camp Narache, young tauren face the Rite of Strength against Bristleback quilboar. They bring their belts to Chief Hawkwind to prove their courage and defend the tribe.",h="At Camp Narache, young tauren face the Rite of Strength against Bristleback quilboar.",sec={{t="The First Trial",b="Seer Graytongue calls young tauren to begin the Rites of the Earthmother with the Rite of Strength. Those who seek recognition from the tribal elders and acceptance in Thunder Bluff must face this duty. The trial tests whether an initiate can meet the tribe's mortal foes with courage and act to defend Red Cloud Mesa.",sp=0},{t="The Bristleback Threat",b="East of Camp Narache, Brambleblade Ravine is home to Bristleback quilboar. The quilboar trespass on ancestral lands, ambush hunting parties, and raid camp supplies under cover of darkness. You are asked to slay them and bring back their belts. The proof shows your valor and helps keep the settlement secure.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of the Rite of Strength?",a="The Rite of Strength tests a young tauren's courage in combat as the first step in the Rites of the Earthmother. Defeating threats to the camp earns an initiate the right to continue toward respect in Thunder Bluff.",al={"why do rite of strength","rite of strength lore","purpose of rite of strength"},it="purpose"},{q="Who assigns and judges this trial?",a="Seer Graytongue gives the charge and explains its spiritual and practical demands. Bring twelve Bristleback belts to Chief Hawkwind at Camp Narache as proof of valor.",al={"who gives rite of strength","who turns in rite of strength"},it="identity"},{q="Where is Brambleblade Ravine located?",a="Brambleblade Ravine lies east of Camp Narache on Red Cloud Mesa. Its thorny canyon is home to hostile Bristleback quilboar.",al={"where is brambleblade ravine","brambleblade location"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why are the Bristlebacks being targeted?",a="The Bristlebacks trespass on the tribe's ancestral hunting grounds. They ambush tauren hunters and steal provisions from Camp Narache in the dark, making them enemies of the tribe.",al={"why kill bristlebacks","what did the quilboar do"},it="threat"},{q="Why collect belts instead of other trophies?",a="The belts are proof that an initiate faced the quilboar and defeated their adult warriors in close combat, rather than scavenging from a distance.",al={"why bristleback belts","why belts"},it="item"},{q="What comes after completing the Rite of Strength?",a="Passing the Rite of Strength proves an initiate's valor and qualifies them for the next trials of the Earthmother. These trials continue the formal path to adulthood and spiritual standing within the tribe.",al={"what is after rite of strength","next earthmother rite"},it="next_step"},{q="Why does Seer Graytongue send you to the Bristlebacks?",a="Seer Graytongue guides the youth of Camp Narache through the sacred Rites of the Earthmother. For your first trial, he must test your martial resolve against a real and pressing danger to the tribe. The Bristleback quilboar have been encroaching on tauren lands, ambushing hunters and stealing under cover of darkness. By sending you to confront them, Graytongue ensures you protect your people while proving your strength and earning the tribe's respect.",al={"why does graytongue want this","seer graytongue motive","why rite of strength"},it="motive",add="f38315d5"},{q="Why are you sent to do this trial alone?",a="The Rite of Strength is a personal rite of passage, not a simple garrison chore. While camp braves guard the perimeter, every young tauren must independently demonstrate valor and martial capability before they are acknowledged as an adult in Thunder Bluff. The trial demands that you face the enemies of your people yourself so the elders know you can defend the tribe when called upon.",al={"why me rite of strength","why not send guards bristlebacks","why do i have to fight bristlebacks"},it="why_me",add="e0dd8e68"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a young tauren, you begin the ancient rites by showing you can defend your people from the quilboar before leaving Red Cloud Mesa.",["faction:Horde"]="As a member of the Horde, you help defend Red Cloud Mesa, one of its core recruiting grounds, so young warriors and shamans can mature safely and aid the new union."},kw={"brambleblade ravine","bristleback","camp narache","chief hawkwind","earthmother","mulgore","quilboar","rite of strength","seer graytongue"},rel={"quest:763","npc:seer-graytongue","npc:chief-hawkwind","subzone:brambleblade-ravine","subzone:camp-narache","topic:tauren","zone:mulgore","zone:thunderbluff"},m={start="Seer Graytongue",["end"]="Chief Hawkwind",level="4",faction="Horde",id=757,ri={"Bristleback Belt"}},src={"Rite of Strength (Classic)"}}
E["quest:758"] = {t="quest",n="Thunderhorn Cleansing",z="mulgore",s="Thunderhorn Cleansing is an essential spiritual rite in Mulgore, charging travelers with purifying the corrupted Thunderhorn Water Well. Armed with a ritual totem prepared by Mull Thunderhorn, adventurers restore the contaminated spring vital to the local tribe. Successful purification earns the heartfelt approval of the Thunderhorn ancestors.",h="Thunderhorn Cleansing is an essential spiritual rite in Mulgore, charging travelers with purifying the...",sec={{t="A Tainted Lifeline",b="Water wells across the sweeping plains of Mulgore are vital lifelines for the tauren nomadic tribes who recently settled the grasslands. When foul poisons taint these underground springs, both wildlife and nearby settlements face devastating consequences. The Thunderhorn Water Well, situated north of Bloodhoof Village, fallen victim to contamination, requires deliberate spiritual intervention to heal the troubled land.",sp=0},{t="Ancestral Cleansing",b="To counteract the corruption, Mull Thunderhorn crafts a consecrated Cleansing Totem imbued with spiritual potency. Carrying this relic directly to the poisoned waters allows adventurers to channel ancestral power into the spring, dispersing the toxins and restoring pristine natural balance. Mull receives direct confirmation of the deed's success when the spirits of Thunderhorn ancestors visit him in a dream to express their enduring gratitude.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where is the Thunderhorn Water Well located?",a="The Thunderhorn Water Well lies in the plains north of Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore. It serves as one of several vital watering holes supplying the local tauren clans.",al={"thunderhorn well location","where is well","where do i use totem"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Who initiates this cleansing ritual?",a="Mull Thunderhorn initiates the rite. He can be found in Bloodhoof Village, where he works to fashion specialized totems to heal Mulgore's distressed environment.",al={"who gives quest","mull thunderhorn location","quest giver"},it="identity"},{q="Why must the water wells in Mulgore be cleansed?",a="The wells across Mulgore have fallen prey to unnatural poisoning and corruption, which threatens the delicate balance of the plains and the survival of the tauren tribes who depend on clean water.",al={"why cleanse wells","what happened to the water","why is well poisoned"},it="motive"},{q="How does Mull know the ritual was successful?",a="Upon completing the rite at the well, the spirits of the Thunderhorn clan's ancestors appear directly to Mull in a dream, praising the adventurer's deeds and offering ancestral thanks.",al={"ancestor dream","how mull knows","well cleansed result"}},{q="What follows the cleansing of the Thunderhorn well?",a="Purifying the Thunderhorn well is part of a broader effort to safeguard Mulgore's springs. Next, Mull sends travelers onward to aid another troubled region by constructing the Wildmane Totem.",al={"what next","next quest after thunderhorn","wildmane totem next"},it="next_step"},{q="What is the Thunderhorn Cleansing Totem?",a="The Thunderhorn Cleansing Totem is a ritually carved spiritual focus prepared by Mull Thunderhorn specifically designed to purify the corrupted waters of his clan's ancestral well.",al={"cleansing totem","what is the totem item","mull totem"},it="item"},{q="Why does Mull Thunderhorn need you to cleanse the well?",a="Mull Thunderhorn has crafted the ritual focus, but cleansing sacred ground is a rite of service and honor for rising champions among the tauren and their allies. By carrying the Thunderhorn Cleansing Totem to the tainted well yourself, you prove your dedication to the Earthmother and the ancestral spirits of his clan, earning the favor and guidance of the ancestors who watch over the waters.",al={"why me mull thunderhorn","why does mull send you","why perform cleansing ritual"},it="why_me",add="da3030fe"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Restoring the sacred springs honors the Earth Mother and protects your kin as they carve out a permanent, peaceful home in Mulgore.",["class:Shaman"]="Channeling ancestral energy through totems to heal corrupted natural springs is the very foundation of the shamanic calling.",["class:Druid"]="Cleansing poisoned waters halts the spread of corruption across Mulgore's ecosystem, preserving the natural harmony of the plains.",["faction:Horde"]="Assisting the tauren with their sacred springs strengthens the bond between the young Horde and its proud Shu'halo allies."},kw={"ancestral spirits","bloodhoof village","cleansing totem","earth mother","mulgore","mull thunderhorn","thunderhorn cleansing","thunderhorn water well"},rel={"npc:mull-thunderhorn","subzone:thunderhorn-water-well","zone:mulgore"},m={start="Mull Thunderhorn",level="8",faction="Horde",id=758},src={"Thunderhorn Cleansing (Classic)"}}
E["quest:759"] = {t="quest",n="Wildmane Totem",z="mulgore",s="Wildmane Totem represents the third and final stage in Mull Thunderhorn's efforts to craft cleansing totems for Mulgore's poisoned wells. To restore the Wildmane Well, the ritual demands offerings taken from the territory's fiercest natural predators: prairie wolf alphas.",h="Wildmane Totem represents the third and final stage in Mull Thunderhorn's efforts to craft cleansing totems...",sec={{t="The Final Well",b="After securing the materials required to consecrate totems for the Winterhoof and Thunderhorn wells, only one afflicted source remains in central Mulgore: the Wildmane Well. The poisoned water across the plains threatens both migrating wildlife and settlements, demanding powerful primal ingredients to restore the earth's natural balance.",sp=0},{t="The Predator's Strength",b="To counter the potent corruption lingering in the northern waters, Mull Thunderhorn requires a sacrifice of ferocity. Prairie wolf alphas prowl the grasslands, particularly around the towering bluffs beneath Thunder Bluff. Harvesting their teeth channels the relentless drive and resilience of Mulgore's apex hunters directly into the crafting of the cleansing totem.",sp=0},{t="Sacrifice and Balance",b="In tauren spiritual tradition, drawing upon the land's creatures carries emotional and elemental weight. Mull views the gathering of alpha teeth not as conquest, but as a somber necessity where the sacrifice of Mulgore's predators helps heal the Earth Mother's stricken waters.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Wildmane Totem needed for?",a="The totem is crafted by Mull Thunderhorn to cleanse the Wildmane Well, the final contaminated water source endangering the tauren of Mulgore.",al={"why make wildmane totem","wildmane totem purpose"},it="purpose"},{q="Why are prairie wolf alpha teeth specifically required?",a="Purifying the deep contamination at the Wildmane Well requires channeling the fierce, unyielding strength of an apex predator. The teeth of the pack leaders provide the necessary primal power.",al={"why wolf teeth","prairie alpha teeth purpose"},it="why_wanted"},{q="Where are prairie wolf alphas found in Mulgore?",a="Prairie wolf alphas wander the northern plains of Mulgore, commonly prowling the grasslands surrounding the base of Thunder Bluff.",al={"prairie wolf alpha location","where to find prairie alphas"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Who crafts the totem?",a="Mull Thunderhorn, a spiritual elder stationed in Bloodhoof Village, crafts each cleansing totem after an initiate gathers the necessary offerings.",al={"who makes the totem","totem quest giver"},it="identity"},{q="What follows the crafting of the Wildmane Totem?",a="Once Mull Thunderhorn assembles the totem from the alpha teeth, you are sent north with it to perform the actual purification at the Wildmane Well.",al={"what comes next","wildmane totem next quest"},it="next_step"},{q="How does this ritual reflect tauren beliefs?",a="The tauren view taking an animal's life as a solemn exchange with nature. Mull acknowledges the sacrifice of the wolves with both sadness for their death and pride in their role in healing the earth.",al={"tauren philosophy totem","mull thunderhorn beliefs"}},{q="Why does Mull Thunderhorn want the Wildmane Totem made?",a="Only one corrupted water well remains to be purified in Mulgore—the one belonging to the Wildmane clan. Mull Thunderhorn has dedicated himself to cleansing the sacred wells of the earthmother, and crafting this final cleansing totem using the predatory spirit of the land is essential to completing the sacred rite and restoring pure water to the tribe.",al={"why mull wants wildmane totem","mull thunderhorn motive","why make wildmane totem"},it="motive",add="054e923c"},{q="What is the Prairie Alpha Tooth used for?",a="The teeth of the prairie wolf alpha represent the sacrifice and ferocious vitality of Mulgore's apex predators. Mull Thunderhorn binds eight of these fangs into the Wildmane Totem so that its spirit can appease the natural order and empower the ritual needed to purge the taint from the Wildmane water well.",al={"prairie alpha tooth use","what are prairie alpha teeth for","teeth for wildmane totem"},it="item",add="2aa23a29"},{q="Why are you hunting prairie wolf alphas?",a="You are sent to hunt prairie wolf alphas, the dominant pack leaders that roam the plains around the base of Thunder Bluff. Their strength and ferocity embody the natural balance of the plains, providing the potent essence Mull Thunderhorn requires for the final water-cleansing ritual.",al={"why kill prairie wolf alphas","prairie wolf alpha target","who are you sent after"},it="target",add="eb5f4bd0"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="The cleansing of ancestral wells underscores the tauren obligation to mend environmental imbalance and honor the sacrifices demanded by the Earth Mother.",["class:Shaman"]="Shaman appreciate the delicate rites involved in harmonizing predator totems with elemental water to purge toxic blights.",["class:Hunter"]="Tracking down the cunning alpha wolves around the high bluffs tests a hunter's respect for the wilderness and its sharpest predators."},kw={"bloodhoof village","mulgore","mull thunderhorn","prairie wolf alpha","thunder bluff","totem","water well","wildmane"},rel={"npc:mull-thunderhorn","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore","zone:thunderbluff"},m={start="Mull Thunderhorn",level="10",faction="Horde",id=759,ri={"Prairie Alpha Tooth"}},src={"Wildmane Totem (Classic)"}}
E["quest:760"] = {t="quest",n="Wildmane Cleansing",z="mulgore",s="Wildmane Cleansing marks the culmination of the tauren ritual efforts to purify the vital watering holes of Mulgore. Entrusted by Mull Thunderhorn in Bloodhoof Village, heroes take the completed Wildmane Totem north to cleanse the final poisoned well. Driving back goblin pollutants restores balance to the plains and secures the pastoral livelihood of the tauren tribes.",h="Wildmane Cleansing marks the culmination of the tauren ritual efforts to purify the vital watering holes of...",sec={{t="Purifying the Earth Mother's Gift",b="Water is sacred lifeblood across the sun-drenched plains of Mulgore, sustaining the herds, the soil, and the nomadic tribes who have recently gathered around Thunder Bluff. When the Venture Company and encroaching goblins began contaminating these aquifers with toxic industrial runoff, the tauren elders recognized an existential threat. Following the purification of the Winterhoof and Thunderhorn wells, the Wildmane Water Well stands as the third and final corrupted site requiring spiritual intervention.",sp=0},{t="The Wildmane Totem",b="Crafted from specific elements gathered across the region, the Wildmane Cleansing Totem channels the restorative power of ancestral spirits and elemental earth. Planting the totem beside the foul waters north of Thunder Bluff disperses the foul chemicals, drawing out the toxins and reviving the natural flow. By completing the ritual, the tauren safeguard their people from disease and send an unmistakable message that the despoiling of their sacred ground will not be tolerated.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why was the Wildmane Water Well poisoned?",a="Goblin operations, driven by industrial greed and logging enterprises, introduced foul poisons and chemical waste into Mulgore's subterranean waters, threatening local wildlife and tribal settlements alike.",al={"who poisoned the well","why is the well dirty","goblin poison"},it="conflict"},{q="Where is the Wildmane Water Well located?",a="The Wildmane Water Well is situated on the plains directly north of the towering mesas of Thunder Bluff.",al={"wildmane well location","where is wildmane well","finding wildmane well"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Who initiates this cleansing ritual?",a="Mull Thunderhorn, an elder stationed in Bloodhoof Village, guides you through the creation of the totems and sends you to purify the wells.",al={"who gives wildmane cleansing","quest giver","mull thunderhorn location"},it="identity"},{q="What other wells are part of this purification effort?",a="The Wildmane Well is the third in a trio of vital water sources, preceded by the Winterhoof Water Well and the Thunderhorn Water Well, both to the south.",al={"other wells","mulgore wells","winterhoof thunderhorn wells"},it="history"},{q="How does the cleansing totem work?",a="The totem is infused with ancestral magic and purified natural elements, channeling restorative spiritual energy into the water table to neutralize goblin pollutants.",al={"cleansing totem mechanic","how does totem clean water"},it="power"},{q="What does completing this ritual mean for Bloodhoof Village?",a="Cleansing the three wells guarantees safe drinking water for all tribes in central Mulgore and honors the tauren sacred duty to heal the Earth Mother from industrial exploitation.",al={"outcome of quest","why cleanse the wells","tauren lore importance"},it="purpose"},{q="Why does Mull Thunderhorn want the Wildmane Well cleansed?",a="Mull Thunderhorn watches over the spiritual balance and vital resources of Mulgore. The Venture Company's foul operations poisoned the land's precious water sources, threatening the survival of the tribes and their herds. The Wildmane Well is the last of the three ancient wells still tainted, and purifying it is essential to heal the Earth Mother and secure safe drinking water for Bloodhoof Village and Thunder Bluff.",al={"mull thunderhorn motive","why cleanse wildmane well","why does mull care about the wells"},it="motive",add="119d5a48"},{q="What is the Wildmane Cleansing Totem?",a="The Wildmane Cleansing Totem is a ritually prepared implement crafted by Mull Thunderhorn to channel ancestral and earthen spirits. When activated at the tainted Wildmane Water Well north of Thunder Bluff, the totem expels the toxic sludge left behind by goblin polluters, purifying the aquifer and returning the spring to its natural, pristine state.",al={"wildmane cleansing totem use","what is the wildmane totem","cleansing totem purpose"},it="item",add="b4cee6a1"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Restoring Mulgore's three ancestral wells is a foundational rite of passage that proves a young brave's devotion to preserving the Earth Mother's gifts.",["class:Shaman"]="Employing ritual totems to undo mechanical pollution exemplifies the shamanic duty to maintain harmony between natural elements and living communities.",["class:Druid"]="Reversing goblin environmental destruction directly reflects the Cenarion mandate to heal blighted ecosystems and protect verdant pastures."},kw={"bloodhoof village","cleansing","earth mother","mulgore","mull thunderhorn","totem","venture company","water well","wildmane"},rel={"npc:mull-thunderhorn","quest:759","subzone:wildmane-water-well","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore","zone:thunderbluff","topic:goblin"},m={start="Mull Thunderhorn",level="10",faction="Horde",id=760,ri={"Wildmane Cleansing Totem"}},src={"Wildmane Cleansing (Classic)"}}
E["quest:761"] = {t="quest",n="Swoop Hunting",z="mulgore",s="Swoop Hunting is a rite of tracking and marksmanship offered by Harken Windtotem in Bloodhoof Village. To prove their cleverness and patience, hunters and travelers are challenged to stalk the elusive birds across the rolling plains of Mulgore. Returning with their pristine trophy quills demonstrates both dedication to the hunt and respect for the natural trials of the Earth Mother.",h="Swoop Hunting is a rite of tracking and marksmanship offered by Harken Windtotem in Bloodhoof Village.",sec={{t="A Test of Keen Eyes",b="The grassy steppes of Mulgore are teeming with beasts, but few test a pursuer's instincts like the native swoop. These large predatory birds blend seamlessly into the skyline and plains, wheeling overhead or skimming low across the tall grass. Unlike lumbering plainstriders or grazing kodo, swoops demand acute focus and swift feet to run down, making their pursuit a traditional proving exercise for younger hunters seeking to earn their standing among the tribes.",sp=0},{t="Badges of the Hunt",b="For Harken Windtotem, gathering trophy quills is less about culling wildlife and more about cultivating discipline. In tauren culture, carrying tokens from a hard-won stalk signifies cleverness and perseverance. Presenting these unblemished quills to elders in Bloodhoof Village marks an adventurer as capable of sustaining and defending the community, demonstrating that their hooves are swift and their senses sharp enough to master the wider world.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of hunting swoops for Harken Windtotem?",a="Harken Windtotem tasks hunters with tracking swoops across Mulgore to test their cleverness, speed, and dedication. Bringing back intact trophy quills serves as recognized proof of a hunter's skill and earns honor among the tauren of Bloodhoof Village.",al={"why hunt swoops","harken windtotem quest purpose","swoop hunting purpose"},it="motive"},{q="Where are swoops typically encountered?",a="Swoops can be found scattered throughout the open plains of Mulgore, soaring above the prairies or scanning the grasses for prey outside settlements like Bloodhoof Village.",al={"where are swoops","swoop locations mulgore","find swoops"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why are swoops considered tricky to hunt?",a="Unlike herd animals that move together across the flats, swoops are swift, alert raptors that remain widespread and observant. Stalking them requires sharp vision to spot their flight paths and quick strides to bring them down before they escape.",al={"why are swoops hard to catch","swoop behavior"},it="threat"},{q="What do the trophy quills signify to the tauren?",a="Within tauren traditions, trophy swoop quills represent patience, sharp vision, and successful pursuit. Displaying them signifies that a hunter possesses the instincts necessary to provide for and protect the tribe.",al={"trophy swoop quill meaning","significance of quills"},it="item"},{q="Who is Harken Windtotem?",a="Harken Windtotem is a respected tauren resident of Bloodhoof Village who observes newcomers and aspiring hunters, evaluating their resourcefulness through traditional trials across the plains.",al={"who gives swoop hunting","harken windtotem lore"},it="identity"},{q="Does this hunt impact the balance of nature in Mulgore?",a="The tauren approach hunting as a sacred dialogue with the Earth Mother rather than wanton slaughter. Gathering quills is a measured test of discipline that honors the cleverness of the prey while sustaining tribal balance.",al={"is swoop hunting overhunting","swoop balance mulgore"}},{q="Why does Harken Windtotem send an outsider instead of hunting swoops himself?",a="Harken Windtotem does not send you because he is unable to hunt, but to test your worth. For tauren, tracking the cunning swoop across the plains requires keen eyesight, patience, and swift hooves. By tasking you with bringing back their quills, Harken gives you an opportunity to prove your skill and determination to Bloodhoof Village, demonstrating that you are a hunter of genuine merit.",al={"why send me swoop hunting","why harken need me","why can't harken hunt swoops"},it="why_me",add="0d785fcd"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For tauren youths and wanderers, hunting the swift swoops of Mulgore is a venerable rite of passage that sharpens tracking instincts and upholds ancestral hunting customs.",["class:Hunter"]="The hunt offers an early masterclass in leading aerial quarry, assessing behavioral patterns, and executing precise strikes against vigilant predators.",["class:Druid"]="Tracking swoops across the windswept mesas brings druids into closer communion with the predatory birds that maintain the balance of Mulgore's skies."},kw={"bloodhoof village","harken windtotem","mulgore","swoop","swoop hunting","tauren trial","trophy swoop quill"},rel={"npc:harken-windtotem","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore"},m={start="Harken Windtotem",faction="Horde",id=761,ri={"Trophy Swoop Quill"}},src={"Swoop Hunting"}}
E["quest:763"] = {t="quest",n="Rites of the Earthmother",z="mulgore",s="Rites of the Earthmother marks the transition of a young tauren out of the secluded Red Cloud Mesa and into the wider plains of Mulgore. Having proven your mettle in Camp Narache, Chief Hawkwind sends you north to Bloodhoof Village with a carved totem to continue the ancient trials under Baine Bloodhoof.",h="Rites of the Earthmother marks the transition of a young tauren out of the secluded Red Cloud Mesa and into...",sec={{t="Leaving the Mesa",b="For generations, young tauren have tested their strength within the safe cradle of Camp Narache on Red Cloud Mesa. Completing the initial trials proves one has the discipline and resolve to survive, but the sacred Rites of the Earthmother require far more than surviving local wildlife. Chief Hawkwind recognizes when an initiate has outgrown the mesa's sheltered hunting grounds and must step into the broader community.",sp=0},{t="A Token for Baine",b="To bridge the journey between Narache and the central settlements, Chief Hawkwind provides a hand-carved totem as a mark of introduction. Bearing this emblem down the winding path to Bloodhoof Village, you present yourself to Baine Bloodhoof, the young chieftain of the village and son of High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof. The totem serves as testament to your readiness for the rites of spirit and wisdom that lie ahead.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of this quest?",a="It serves as the rite of passage that guides you out of the starting area of Camp Narache and directs you toward Bloodhoof Village, connecting you to the next chapter of the tauren ceremonial trials.",al={"why do rites of the earthmother","what does this quest do","purpose of quest"},it="purpose"},{q="Who is Baine Bloodhoof?",a="Baine Bloodhoof is the son of High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof. He leads Bloodhoof Village, serving as a respected local chieftain who oversees the ongoing spiritual and martial testing of young tauren.",al={"who is baine","baine lore","son of cairne"},it="identity"},{q="What is the Totem of Hawkwind?",a="It is a wooden totem hand-carved by Chief Hawkwind of Camp Narache. It acts as an official credential and seal of approval, proving to Baine Bloodhoof that you have earned Hawkwind's blessing to undertake further rites.",al={"totem of hawkwind","hawkwinds totem","carved totem"},it="item"},{q="What rites come after this?",a="Upon arriving in Bloodhoof Village and meeting with Baine, you are tasked with the Rite of Vision, which tests your spiritual attunement through sacred ancestral visions.",al={"next rites","what comes after","next quest"},it="next_step"},{q="Where is Bloodhoof Village located?",a="Bloodhoof Village lies north of Camp Narache, situated along the shores of Stonebull Lake in central Mulgore. Travelers need only follow the main road down from the mesa and head north.",al={"where is bloodhoof village","how to find bloodhoof village","path to bloodhoof"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why must the Earthmother rites be completed?",a="The Rites of the Earthmother are deeply sacred traditions among the tauren. Passing all stages of the rites demonstrates physical fortitude, spiritual connection, and wisdom, earning the initiate true recognition from the elders of Thunder Bluff.",al={"why earthmother rites","tauren trials purpose","rites of earthmother lore"},it="origin"},{q="Why does Chief Hawkwind send you to Bloodhoof Village?",a="Chief Hawkwind has guided you through the introductory trials of the Red Cloud Mesa, but Camp Narache can teach you no more. To complete the sacred Rites of the Earthmother and gain the acceptance of Thunder Bluff's elders, you must broaden your experience in the wider plains of Mulgore. By sending you to Baine Bloodhoof with his carved totem, Hawkwind passes responsibility for your spiritual and physical growth to a leader who can test your readiness for true adulthood.",al={"why send to bloodhoof","chief hawkwind motive","why rites of the earthmother"},it="motive",add="4a33bb81",nx={8}},{q="Why must you deliver the totem yourself?",a="The journey is not an errand of courier duty, but an integral part of your rite of passage. Stepping beyond the sheltered confines of the Red Cloud Mesa and following the open road into Mulgore proves your independence and endurance. Delivering the Totem of Hawkwind with your own hands allows Baine Bloodhoof to look upon you, recognize Hawkwind's personal commendation carved into the wood, and judge your character firsthand.",al={"why do I take the totem","why can't hawkwind send a messenger","why me rites of the earthmother"},it="why_me",add="9c980781"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Every young tauren must walk the path of the Earthmother to prove their worth to the elders and find their place among the united tribes.",["faction:Horde"]="The traditional rites reinforce tribal unity across Mulgore, ensuring that new defenders of the Horde possess both warrior prowess and deep spiritual grounding."},kw={"baine bloodhoof","bloodhoof village","cairne bloodhoof","camp narache","chief hawkwind","mulgore","rites of the earthmother","totem of hawkwind"},rel={"subzone:camp-narache","subzone:bloodhoof-village","npc:chief-hawkwind","npc:baine-bloodhoof","quest:767","zone:mulgore","zone:thunderbluff"},m={start="Chief Hawkwind",["end"]="Baine Bloodhoof",level="5",faction="Horde",id=763},src={"Rites of the Earthmother (2)"}}
E["quest:764"] = {t="quest",n="The Venture Co.",z="mulgore",s="Investigating a ravaged Venture Company caravan in Mulgore uncovers an aggressive buildup of Venture Company goblins digging into the eastern hills. Having had their formal requests for mining permits flatly rejected, the company chose to quarry the Earth Mother's soil anyway, prompting Morin Cloudstalker to commission a lethal response.",h="Investigating a ravaged Venture Company caravan in Mulgore uncovers an aggressive buildup of Venture Company...",sec={{t="Industrial Intrusion",b="The tauren of Mulgore revere the land as an extension of the Earth Mother, cherishing its pristine plains and balanced wildlife. When the Venture Company approached the tribes seeking rights to blast and excavate the rolling hills for raw ore, their petitions were categorically denied. Undeterred by tribal decrees or territorial sovereignty, the relentless cartel sneaked heavy machinery, laborers, and overseers directly into Mulgore's eastern ridges to mine illicitly.",sp=0},{t="An Unmistakable Message",b="Tauren scouts like Morin Cloudstalker recognize that corporate greed cannot be reasoned with through diplomatic appeals once picks hit the stone. With documents recovered from a ravaged Venture Co. caravan confirming the company's mining operation, Morin dispatches Horde champions to cut down both the taskmasters cracking the whips and the laborers driving the drills. The bloody reprisal is intended to ensure the Venture Company calculates the cost of trespassing as far too steep.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why is the Venture Company in Mulgore?",a="The Venture Company established an illegal outpost and mine along the eastern hills of Mulgore to extract minerals and natural resources. After the tauren formally denied them official mining rights, the cartel ignored tribal authority and set up operations regardless.",al={"why are goblins in mulgore","venture co mulgore motive","what does venture co want"},it="why_here"},{q="Who gave the order to attack the miners?",a="Morin Cloudstalker, a tauren keeping watch near Bloodhoof Village, issues the order. He views the cartel's refusal to accept tribal refusal as a direct violation that demands physical force rather than further talk.",al={"who gives this quest","morin cloudstalker role","quest giver"},it="conflict"},{q="Where is the Venture Company mine located?",a="The main Venture Company Mine sits in the foothills east of the Ravaged Caravan and Bloodhoof Village. A smaller gathering of company personnel can also be encountered at an outpost tucked into the bluffs northeast of Thunder Bluff's northern elevators.",al={"venture co mine location","where are the supervisors","where to find venture co"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Did the tauren ever try to negotiate with the goblins?",a="Yes. The Venture Company petitioned the tauren for formal mining rights in Mulgore. The tribal leaders refused to let them strip-mine their ancestral lands, but the goblins simply chose to dig without permission anyway.",al={"did tauren permit mining","mining rights mulgore","did goblins ask permission"},it="history"},{q="Are these goblins part of the Steamwheedle Cartel?",a="No. The Venture Company is an independent, notoriously predatory industrial cartel distinct from neutral trading groups like the Steamwheedle Cartel. They maintain hostile relations with local populations wherever they exploit resources.",al={"are these ratchet goblins","is venture co friendly","venture company faction"},it="identity"},{q="How does this connect to the ravaged caravans?",a="The ravaged caravan was a Venture Company convoy destroyed by the tauren. The mining-division crates and documents recovered from its wreckage revealed the company's operation in Mulgore, prompting this strike on their mine.",al={"caravan connection","did venture co attack caravans","the ravaged caravan connection"},it="ties"},{q="Why does Morin Cloudstalker want the Venture Co. attacked?",a="The Venture Company was officially denied mining rights in Mulgore, yet they ignored the refusal and began building up equipment and workers regardless. Morin Cloudstalker cannot permit them to despoil the land and plunder its natural resources unchecked. Since diplomacy and flat rejections failed to sway their greed, he believes the tauren must deliver an unmistakable, forceful message that trespass and theft will not be tolerated.",al={"morin cloudstalker motive","why attack venture co","why does morin want venture co killed"},it="motive",add="f6df914e"},{q="Who are you being sent to fight at the mine?",a="You are sent against the Venture Co. Supervisors and Workers stationed at their mining operation in eastern Mulgore. These goblins and hired laborers are carving deep into the sacred hills to strip the earth of minerals without tribal permission, led by profit-driven foremen who enforce the company's aggressive, illicit expansion.",al={"venture co quest targets","who are the venture co workers","who do i fight in the mine"},it="target",add="eda5096c"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For the tauren, the Earth Mother is sacred. The Venture Company's strip-mining is not just trespassing, but a violent desecration of their ancestral home that warrants an immediate, forceful eviction.",["faction:Horde"]="Defending Mulgore against commercial exploitation cements your dedication to protecting Cairne Bloodhoof's people as they work to secure their new homeland.",["profession:Mining"]="The Venture Company operates purely through brute-force extraction, cutting corners and destabilizing the land with no regard for the natural balance or safe mining craft."},kw={"goblin","mining rights","morin cloudstalker","mulgore","ravaged caravan","venture co","venture co mine","venture company"},rel={"subzone:venture-co-mine","quest:765","quest:749","npc:morin-cloudstalker","subzone:ravaged-caravan","zone:mulgore","topic:tauren","topic:goblin"},m={start="Morin Cloudstalker",level="10",faction="Horde",id=764},src={"The Venture Co. (Classic)"}}
E["quest:765"] = {t="quest",n="Supervisor Fizsprocket",z="mulgore",s="Supervisor Fizsprocket is a high-ranking Venture Company overseer stationed within the mining tunnels of Mulgore. Charged by Morin Cloudstalker with putting an end to the goblin enterprise's calculated pillaging, Horde adventurers enter the mine to eliminate him and recover his detailed clipboard.",h="Supervisor Fizsprocket is a high-ranking Venture Company overseer stationed within the mining tunnels of...",sec={{t="A Plot Against the Plains",b="Documents recovered from a destroyed Venture Company caravan revealed that the Venture Company was not merely digging for ore—they were deliberately formulating schemes to drive the tauren from Mulgore entirely. By uprooting the locals, the rapacious corporation sought unchecked freedom to strip the rolling hills and deep subterranean veins of every natural resource.",sp=0},{t="Strike at the Foreman",b="Morin Cloudstalker, enraged by the corporate atrocities plotted against his people, directs champions into the Venture Company mine located just east of the ravaged trade wagons. Tucked deep within the right-hand tunnels surrounded by laborers and sub-bosses, Supervisor Fizsprocket manages operations. Striking down Fizsprocket and claiming his clipboard shatters local coordination and yields critical intelligence regarding the company's future designs.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is Supervisor Fizsprocket?",a="Supervisor Fizsprocket is a Venture Company boss running operations inside the mining tunnels in eastern Mulgore. He oversees the workforce tearing into the earth and executes corporate strategies aimed at expanding their operations across the region.",al={"fizsprocket","who is fizsprocket","fizsprocket lore"},it="target"},{q="Why does Morin Cloudstalker want Fizsprocket dead?",a="Morin discovered documents on a raided caravan proving the Venture Company plotted to drive the tauren off their ancestral homeland for profit. Fizsprocket is the direct supervisor carrying out these calculated exploitation schemes.",al={"why kill fizsprocket","morin cloudstalker motivation"},it="motive"},{q="Where is Supervisor Fizsprocket located?",a="He is located inside the Venture Company mine directly east of the Ravaged Caravan in Mulgore. Inside the mine, follow the far right tunnel to a chamber where he stands surrounded by workers and supervisors.",al={"where is fizsprocket","fizsprocket mine location","fizsprocket map"},it="place",gp=true},{q="What proof does Morin require of Fizsprocket's defeat?",a="Morin Cloudstalker requires Fizsprocket's personal clipboard, which contains ledgers and records that might reveal further Venture Company ambitions in Mulgore.",al={"proof of death","fizsprocket clipboard"},it="item",gp=true},{q="Why is the Venture Company in Mulgore?",a="The Venture Company is an unscrupulous commercial cartel bent on stripping virgin territory of timber, minerals, and valuable subterranean wealth, completely disregarding indigenous inhabitants or environmental balance.",al={"venture co mulgore","why are goblins in mulgore"},it="why_here"},{q="Does killing Fizsprocket drive the Venture Company away completely?",a="No. While Fizsprocket's demise disrupts their local leadership and provides Morin Cloudstalker with vital notes on their plans, the Venture Company remains a persistent threat embedded in Mulgore's hills.",al={"is venture co gone","after fizsprocket dies"},it="conflict"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="The Venture Company's open scheme to displace your tribe and plunder the sacred earth makes slaying Fizsprocket an act of vital territorial defense.",["faction:Horde"]="Assisting the tauren against encroaching corporate pillagers solidifies the Horde's protection over newly claimed heartlands in Kalimdor."},kw={"clipboard","fizsprocket","mine","morin cloudstalker","mulgore","supervisor","venture co"},rel={"npc:morin-cloudstalker","quest:764","quest:749","subzone:ravaged-caravan","zone:mulgore"},m={start="Morin Cloudstalker",level="12",faction="Horde",id=765},src={"Supervisor Fizsprocket (Classic)"}}
E["quest:767"] = {t="quest",n="Rite of Vision",z="mulgore",s="The Rite of Vision is a pivotal coming-of-age trial within the Rites of the Earthmother, testing a young adventurer's spiritual maturity. Initiated by Baine Bloodhoof, the quest directs aspirants to seek counsel from the village seer, Zarlman Two-Moons. Undertaking this journey is essential for anyone seeking the blessings and respect of the elders on the rises of Thunder Bluff.",h="The Rite of Vision is a pivotal coming-of-age trial within the Rites of the Earthmother, testing a young...",sec={{t="A Sacred Stepping Stone",b="For generations, the nomadic tauren have turned to the Earthmother to guide their people across the plains of Kalimdor. Now gathered within the safe borders of Mulgore, their spiritual traditions remain the cornerstone of their society. The Rite of Vision is one of the ancient trials known as the Rites of the Earthmother. While earlier trials test an initiate's physical strength and connection to the hunt, the Rite of Vision demands an inward journey, directing young tauren and honored allies to commune directly with the spirits.",sp=0},{t="The Seer's Guidance",b="Before any spiritual vision can be sought, an initiate must be prepared by someone steeped in ancient wisdom. In Bloodhoof Village, that responsibility falls to the revered shaman Zarlman Two-Moons. Entrusted by Baine Bloodhoof to prepare travelers, Zarlman possesses the keen sight to recognize those walking the path of the ancestors, offering the preliminary rites and sacred preparations necessary to pierce the veil of the physical world.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Rite of Vision?",a="The Rite of Vision is a sacred ritual within the Rites of the Earthmother. It is designed to broaden an adventurer's spiritual sight, allowing them to commune with ancestral spirits and demonstrate their spiritual readiness to the elders of Thunder Bluff.",al={"rite of vision quest","what is rite of vision","rite of vision lore"},it="purpose"},{q="Who starts this quest?",a="The quest is given by Baine Bloodhoof, the son of High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof. Baine watches over the settlement of Bloodhoof Village and directs worthy travelers toward the village's spiritual elders.",al={"who gives rite of vision","rite of vision quest giver"},gp=true},{q="Who is Zarlman Two-Moons?",a="Zarlman Two-Moons is a seasoned tauren shaman and the spiritual leader of Bloodhoof Village. He prepares travelers and initiates who seek to undergo sacred trials such as the Rite of Vision.",al={"who is zarlman","zarlman two moons lore"},it="identity"},{q="Why are the Rites of the Earthmother important to the tauren?",a="The Rites of the Earthmother represent a traditional rite of passage for tauren coming of age. Successfully completing them demonstrates physical capability, spiritual attunement, and an enduring respect for the land, earning the honor of the high elders.",al={"earthmother rites meaning","why do rites of earthmother"},it="history"},{q="Can non-tauren undertake the Rite of Vision?",a="Yes. Horde allies who travel through Mulgore and earn the trust of the Bloodhoof tribe are permitted to walk the path of the Earthmother, showing respect to tauren tradition in exchange for the elders' favor.",al={"can orcs do rite of vision","non tauren earthmother"}},{q="Where does this trial take place?",a="The trial begins in Bloodhoof Village, a prominent tauren settlement situated near the banks of Stonebull Lake in central Mulgore, south of Thunder Bluff.",al={"where is rite of vision","rite of vision location"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why does Baine Bloodhoof want you to undertake the Rite of Vision?",a="Baine Bloodhoof is preparing you to walk the path of your ancestors and prove your worth to the tauren people. As you come of age in Mulgore, you must undertake the sacred Rites of the Earthmother to commune with the spirits, learn reverence for the land, and ultimately earn the respect of the elders in Thunder Bluff. Baine directs you to village mystic Zarlman Two-Moons so you may properly begin the spiritual trial.",al={"why does baine send you on rite of vision","baine bloodhoof motive rite of vision","why do the rite of vision"},it="motive",add="1fa313da"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="The Rite of Vision is an essential cultural milestone for every young tauren, marking your transition into a trusted defender and spirit-walker of the tribes.",["class:Shaman"]="Seeking Zarlman Two-Moons and walking the path of visions resonates deeply with the shamanic duty to balance worldly affairs with the will of the ancestors.",["class:Druid"]="Communing with the Earthmother's visions directly reinforces the druidic calling to listen closely to the wisdom and whispers of the living world.",["faction:Horde"]="Undertaking these traditional sacred rites proves your loyalty and cultural reverence to the tauren elders who help guide the Horde."},kw={"baine bloodhoof","bloodhoof village","mulgore","rite of vision","rites of the earthmother","tauren trial","thunder bluff","zarlman two-moons"},rel={"quest:763","npc:baine-bloodhoof","npc:zarlman-two-moons","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:thunderbluff","zone:mulgore"},m={start="Baine Bloodhoof",["end"]="Zarlman Two-Moons",level="6",faction="Horde",id=767},src={"Rite of Vision"}}
E["quest:770"] = {t="quest",n="The Demon Scarred Cloak",z="mulgore",s="The Demon Scarred Cloak is a ragged pelt recovered from the legendary white wolf Ghost Howl, who roams the grassy plains of Mulgore. Marked by an enduring wound inflicted during past clashes with the Burning Legion, the pelt serves as proof of the beast's release from torment. Bringing it to the elders of Bloodhoof Village brings closure to the story of a tragic local legend.",h="The Demon Scarred Cloak is a ragged pelt recovered from the legendary white wolf Ghost Howl, who roams the...",sec={{t="A Relic of Fel Wounds",b="Ghost Howl is an elusive white wolf known across Mulgore for both his ferocity and his sorrowful howl. When slain, the beast leaves behind a pelt that is visibly scarred by foul demonic corruption. These lingering marks date back to the wolf's desperate battles against the invading demons of the Burning Legion, a confrontation that left Ghost Howl wounded in both flesh and spirit long after the Third War came to a close.",sp=0},{t="Peace for a Troubled Spirit",b="The tauren of Mulgore maintain a deep respect for the wild beasts of their homeland, revering them as children of the Earth Mother. Presenting the scarred cloak to Skorn Whitecloud in Bloodhoof Village confirms the demise of the tormented predator. Skorn recognizes the immense skill required to track and defeat Ghost Howl, expressing the somber hope that in death, the wolf's tortured spirit has finally attained peace.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Demon Scarred Cloak?",a="It is the ragged hide harvested from Ghost Howl, a legendary white wolf in Mulgore. The pelt is visibly scarred by fel energies from an old battle against the Burning Legion.",al={"what is demon scarred cloak","demon scarred cloak item","ghost howl pelt"},it="item"},{q="Where did the demon scars on the cloak come from?",a="Ghost Howl earned his wounds during clashes against the Burning Legion when the demonic invaders pushed across Kalimdor during the Third War.",al={"why is ghost howl scarred","how did ghost howl get scarred","ghost howl legion"},it="origin"},{q="Who do I bring the cloak to?",a="Bring the cloak to Skorn Whitecloud, an elder tauren hunter who resides in Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore.",al={"where do i turn in demon scarred cloak","who wants demon scarred cloak","skorn whitecloud turn in"},it="next_step",gp=true},{q="Why does Skorn Whitecloud care about Ghost Howl?",a="Skorn Whitecloud is a retired hunter who recognizes Ghost Howl as a formidable beast that suffered deeply from demonic corruption. He respects the skill required to fell such a creature and hopes its spirit can finally rest.",al={"skorn whitecloud lore","why does skorn want ghost howl"},it="ties"},{q="Where is Ghost Howl found?",a="Ghost Howl is a rare white wolf that roams the rolling plains of Mulgore, typically sighted in the northern or central open fields between Red Cloud Mesa and Thunder Bluff.",al={"where to find ghost howl","ghost howl location","ghost howl spawn"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why do the tauren hope the wolf's spirit finds peace?",a="Tauren culture emphasizes harmony with nature and reverence for wild spirits. Because demonic corruption brought unending torment to Ghost Howl's life, his defeat is viewed as a merciful release back to the Earth Mother.",al={"tauren belief ghost howl","ghost howl spirit"},it="history"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For the Shu'halo, hunting Ghost Howl is not an act of cruelty or conquest, but a somber ritual to grant an agonized creature peace from the lingering taint of the Burning Legion.",["class:Hunter"]="Tracking down a lone, scarred alpha like Ghost Howl is an essential rite of passage, earning high praise and a master hunter's gift from the elder Skorn Whitecloud.",["class:Druid"]="Druids view the demon-scarred pelt as a grim reminder that the land and its wild inhabitants still bear unhealed wounds from the demonic invasion of Kalimdor."},kw={"burning legion","demon scarred cloak","ghost howl","mulgore","skorn whitecloud","white wolf"},rel={"npc:skorn-whitecloud","zone:mulgore","topic:burning-legion"},m={start="Demon Scarred Cloak",["end"]="Skorn Whitecloud",faction="Horde",id=770,ri={"Demon Scarred Cloak"}},src={"The Demon Scarred Cloak"}}
E["quest:771"] = {t="quest",n="Rite of Vision",z="mulgore",s="The Rite of Vision is a foundational spiritual trial undergone by young tauren stepping beyond the Red Cloud Mesa into greater Mulgore. To pierce the veil and receive guidance from ancestral spirits, initiates must gather natural reagents to brew the Water of the Seers under the watchful eye of Zarlman Two-Moons in Bloodhoof Village.",h="The Rite of Vision is a foundational spiritual trial undergone by young tauren stepping beyond the Red Cloud...",sec={{t="The Reagents of the Land",b="Before any vision can be granted, the initiate must demonstrate an intimate understanding of the natural gifts found across Mulgore. Zarlman Two-Moons instructs the seeker to collect well stones found near the sacred communal water wells and ambercorn fallen beneath the roots of Mulgore's ancient trees. These natural elements are not merely physical objects; to the tauren, they hold the latent magic and lifeblood of the Earth Mother, ready to be channeled into ritual use.",sp=0},{t="Brewing the Water of the Seers",b="Once gathered, Zarlman combines the stones and ambercorn into the potent Water of the Seers. When consumed beside the village's tribal flame, this ceremonial draft clears the mind and summons an ethereal guide. Following this manifestation across the plains forms the true heart of the rite, testing the initiate's ability to walk between the physical world and the spiritual whispers of the land.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of the Rite of Vision?",a="The Rite of Vision is a spiritual test for tauren coming of age and seeking their path. By preparing and drinking the Water of the Seers, the initiate learns to commune with spirits and follow ancestral omens across the plains of Mulgore.",al={"what is rite of vision","why do rite of vision","purpose of rite of vision"},it="purpose"},{q="Who is Zarlman Two-Moons?",a="Zarlman Two-Moons is a tauren shaman and seer stationed in Bloodhoof Village. He oversees spiritual trials for young adventurers, preparing ritual draughts such as the Water of the Seers and interpreting the signs of the Earth Mother.",al={"who is zarlman","zarlman two moons role","questgiver zarlman"},it="identity"},{q="Where are well stones and ambercorn found?",a="Well stones are gathered from the ground near the freshwater wells scattered across Mulgore. Ambercorn can be found scattered at the bases of the large, sprawling trees dotting the surrounding grassy landscape.",al={"where to find ambercorn","well stones location","reagents for rite of vision"},gp=true},{q="What happens after drinking the Water of the Seers?",a="Drinking the draft near the tribal fire opens the imbiber's spiritual sight, revealing a ghostly animal guide. The seeker must follow this spirit across the plains to uncover the next stage of their sacred journey.",al={"what happens next rite of vision","water of the seers effect","vision quest follow-up"},sp=1,it="next_step"},{q="Why do the tauren revere water wells so deeply?",a="Water is the lifeblood of Mulgore and a precious gift of the Earth Mother. The wells serve as sacred communal centers that nourish both the land and its herds, making the stones gathered beside them spiritually charged.",al={"tauren water wells","why are wells sacred","mulgore water"},it="history"},{q="Does this quest lead to further rites?",a="Yes. This task is part of a multi-stage ceremony. Once Zarlman prepares the Water of the Seers, initiates must drink it, track the vision that appears, and present themselves to elder leaders across Mulgore.",al={"next quest in rite of vision","is rite of vision a chain","tauren rites chain"},sp=1,it="next_step",gp=true},{q="Why does Zarlman Two-Moons want this done?",a="As a tribal shaman, Zarlman Two-Moons is responsible for guiding promising young adventurers through the sacred traditions of the tauren. He cannot simply grant you spiritual wisdom; you must earn the favor of the Earth Mother and your ancestors by participating directly in the ritual, starting with gathering the sacred elements of the land yourself.",al={"zarlman two moons motive","why does zarlman send you","zarlman rite of vision reason"},it="motive",add="6b13f1a7"},{q="What are the Well Stones for?",a="Well stones are gathered from the sacred water wells throughout Mulgore. Imbued with the natural magic of the Earth Mother's purest springs, they serve as one of the key reagents Zarlman Two-Moons needs to brew the mystical Water of the Seers.",al={"well stones purpose","why need well stone","well stone use"},it="item",add="cc7474fb"},{q="What is the Ambercorn for?",a="Fallen from the mighty trees across Mulgore, Ambercorn carries the natural essence of the land's flora. Combined with well stones and steeped by Zarlman Two-Moons, it provides the magical properties required to concoct the Water of the Seers, granting you ancestral visions.",al={"ambercorn purpose","what is ambercorn used for","why get ambercorn"},it="item",add="4efb8f2d"},{q="Why must you gather the reagents instead of Zarlman Two-Moons?",a="The Rite of Vision is an individual trial of spiritual growth, not a routine chore. To commune with the spirits, you must demonstrate reverence and harmony with the land by seeking out its natural blessings with your own hands. Having someone else collect the reagents would defeat the trial's sacred purpose.",al={"why do i have to get ambercorn","why doesn't zarlman gather well stones","why me rite of vision"},it="why_me",add="761f0189"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For tauren, the Rite of Vision is an essential cultural milestone, binding an emerging warrior, hunter, or mystic to the spiritual heritage of the Earth Mother and the ancestors.",["class:Shaman"]="Shaman will recognize the preparation of the Water of the Seers as a traditional grounding ritual, attuning the spirit to read unseen elements before conversing with spirits directly.",["class:Druid"]="Druids hold deep reverence for the ambercorn and well stones, seeing them as direct manifestations of the flora and life-giving waterways that sustain Mulgore's balance."},kw={"ambercorn","bloodhoof village","earth mother","mulgore","rite of vision","water of the seers","well stones","zarlman two-moons"},rel={"quest:767","npc:zarlman-two-moons","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore","quest:92472"},m={start="Zarlman Two-Moons",level="7",faction="Horde",id=771,ri={"Ambercorn","Well Stone"}},src={"Rite of Vision (2)"}}
E["quest:772"] = {t="quest",n="Rite of Vision",z="mulgore",s="The Rite of Vision is the spiritual climax of an ancient trial undertaken by young tauren in Bloodhoof Village. By drinking the sacred Water of the Seers beside the tribal bonfire, initiates summon a spectral guide to lead them toward their destiny.",h="The Rite of Vision is the spiritual climax of an ancient trial undertaken by young tauren in Bloodhoof...",sec={{t="The Water of the Seers",b="After gathering the necessary ritual herbs and passing the initial trials of faith, an initiate is granted the Water of the Seers by the village shaman, Zarlman Two-Moons. This potent draught must be ingested before the roaring tribal fire at the heart of Bloodhoof Village. When consumed with a focused and respectful mind, the sacred waters expand the drinker's perception beyond physical sight, bridging the veil between the material plane and the spiritual realm.",sp=0},{t="Following the Ghost Wolf",b="Upon drinking the brew, the flames yield an ethereal apparition: an ancestral ghost wolf. The spirit does not speak or fight, but silently lopes northwest across the rolling plains of Mulgore. A true initiate must heed its quiet path, tracking its journey away from the village and deep into the rocky hills where the hermit Seer Wiserunner awaits in secluded meditation.",sp=0},{t="A Walk to Wisdom",b="Reaching Seer Wiserunner at the mouth of his mountain cavern signifies the successful completion of the vision. This journey across the grassy hills tests an initiate's patience, trust in ancestral guidance, and attunement to the Earth Mother. Passing this rite grants the young adventurer the right to undergo the ultimate test of adulthood: the Rite of Wisdom.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What happens when I drink the Water of the Seers?",a="When ingested near the tribal fire in Bloodhoof Village, the water opens your spiritual senses. Shortly after drinking, a ghostly spirit wolf appears in front of the flames to guide your path.",al={"how does water of seers work","drinking water of the seers","what does the water do"},it="item"},{q="Where does the spirit guide lead me?",a="The spectral wolf trots northwest out of Bloodhoof Village across the hills of Mulgore, leading straight to a rocky cave where Seer Wiserunner dwells.",al={"where does ghost wolf go","who does the wolf lead to","wolf destination"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Who is Zarlman Two-Moons?",a="Zarlman Two-Moons is a prominent tauren shaman in Bloodhoof Village tasked with preparing initiates for spiritual adulthood through ancestral rites and sacred concoctions.",al={"who gives the rite of vision","zarlman two moons role"},it="identity"},{q="Who is Seer Wiserunner?",a="Seer Wiserunner is an elder tauren mystic who lives in contemplation within the cliffs northwest of Bloodhoof Village. Initiates who follow their spirit vision find him to finish the trial.",al={"who is wiserunner","seer in cave mulgore"},it="target"},{q="Why must the water be drunk near the tribal fire?",a="The tribal bonfire acts as a sacred anchor for Bloodhoof Village's ancestral spirits. The combination of its consecrated flames and the holy water allows the vision to bridge into the waking world.",al={"why tribal fire","bonfire rite of vision"},it="origin"},{q="What rite comes after the Rite of Vision?",a="Upon completing the Rite of Vision with Seer Wiserunner, initiates are directed to undertake the Rite of Wisdom, the final spiritual trial of their early journey in Mulgore.",al={"next quest after rite of vision","what comes next"},it="next_step"},{q="Why does Zarlman Two-Moons want me to undergo the Rite of Vision?",a="As a shaman of Bloodhoof Village, Zarlman Two-Moons guides young tauren along the sacred paths of their ancestors. Having gathered the components for the Water of the Seers, he now presents the brew so you can commune directly with the spirits, witness your vision, and take the next step toward discovering your destiny within the tribe.",al={"why rite of vision","zarlman two-moons motive","purpose of rite of vision"},it="motive",add="659b3713"},{q="Why must I undertake this rite myself?",a="The rites of the Earthmother are deeply personal trials of maturity and spiritual awakening. No guard or elder can walk the path of the spirits for you; only your own spirit can receive the guidance meant to reveal your place and purpose among the tauren people.",al={"why must I do the rite","why me rite of vision","why can't someone else do the rite"},it="why_me",add="b8c8221c"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For young tauren, the Rite of Vision is a deeply sacred passage into adulthood, demonstrating communion with the ancestors and obedience to spiritual guidance.",["class:Shaman"]="Following an ancestral spirit wolf is an early lesson in walking between worlds, grounding a shaman's bond with the spirits that guide the Horde.",["class:Druid"]="The rite reinforces reverence for the natural world and spiritual humility before the guidance of animal forms."},kw={"bloodhoof village","ghost wolf","mulgore","rite of vision","seer wiserunner","tribal fire","water of the seers","zarlman two-moons"},rel={"quest:767","npc:zarlman-two-moons","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore"},m={start="Zarlman Two-Moons",["end"]="Seer Wiserunner",level="7",faction="Horde",id=772},src={"Rite of Vision (3)"}}
E["quest:773"] = {t="quest",n="Rite of Wisdom",z="mulgore",s="The Rite of Wisdom is the concluding trial in the traditional Rites of the Earthmother undertaken by young adventurers in Mulgore. After purifying their sight through the Rite of Vision, petitioners travel to the sacred burial grounds of Red Rocks to commune with an Ancestral Spirit and receive the blessing of the dead.",h="The Rite of Wisdom is the concluding trial in the traditional Rites of the Earthmother undertaken by young...",sec={{t="The Trial of Red Rocks",b="Having cleared their perception through the Rite of Vision, young protectors are directed east of Thunder Bluff toward Red Rocks. This ancient burial ground holds the physical remains and lingering spirits of those who fought and died to carve out a peaceful existence in Mulgore. Passing the trial requires approaching the resting grounds with proper reverence and communion, facing whatever territorial threats roam the hills to seek the counsel of the honored dead.",sp=0},{t="Passing the Mantle",b="At the summit of Red Rocks, the communion with the Ancestral Spirit is not a test of martial strength, but of spiritual readiness and continuity. The spirits resting there represent the generation that fought alongside Cairne Bloodhoof to establish Thunder Bluff and safeguard the tribes. By receiving their blessing, the supplicant officially inherits the solemn duty of protecting the city, earning their place as a recognized adult and defender worthy of entering Thunder Bluff with pride.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of the Rite of Wisdom?",a="The Rite of Wisdom completes the Rites of the Earthmother, bridging the visionary communion of youth with the solemn responsibilities of adulthood. By gaining the blessing of the ancestral spirits, petitioners prove they respect the sacrifices of those who came before and are prepared to defend Thunder Bluff.",al={"why do rite of wisdom","what does rite of wisdom do","rite of wisdom purpose"},it="purpose"},{q="Who sends you on the Rite of Wisdom?",a="Seer Wiserunner, in his cave northwest of Bloodhoof Village, sends initiates on this trial once they have successfully completed the Rite of Vision and drunk from the Water of the Seers.",al={"who gives rite of wisdom","where do i start rite of wisdom"},it="origin"},{q="Where do you find the Ancestral Spirit?",a="The Ancestral Spirit is located at Red Rocks, an ancient tauren burial ground nestled in the rocky bluffs directly east of Thunder Bluff.",al={"ancestral spirit location","where is the spirit","where to find ancestral spirit"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why is the Rite of Vision required first?",a="Only those who have drunk from the Water of the Seers and had their spiritual sight cleansed are able to perceive and receive the blessing of the ancient spirits at Red Rocks.",al={"why do vision first","rite of vision prerequisite","water of seers requirement"},it="history"},{q="What happens after completing the Rite of Wisdom?",a="Having earned the approval of the ancestors, initiates are instructed to make their official journey into the high rises of Thunder Bluff.",al={"what next after rite of wisdom","next quest after rite of wisdom"},it="next_step"},{q="What creatures threaten the grounds of Red Rocks?",a="The sacred graveyard at Red Rocks has been encroached upon by aggressive Palemane gnolls, who lurk along the rocky trails leading up to the ancient burial site.",al={"enemies at red rocks","what guards red rocks","gnolls at red rocks"},it="danger"},{q="Why does Seer Wiserunner send you on the Rite of Wisdom?",a="Seer Wiserunner guides promising young tauren through the traditional Rites of the Earthmother. Having seen you endure the vision granted by the Water of the Seers, he directs you to Red Rocks so you can receive the blessing of the ancestors. For Wiserunner and the elders, this rite ensures that only those spiritually prepared and acknowledged by the ancestors are welcomed into Thunder Bluff as protectors of the tribe.",al={"seer wiserunner motive","why rite of wisdom","why send me rite of wisdom"},it="motive",add="740e2883"},{q="Why does Seer Wiserunner need you to go instead of going himself?",a="The Rite of Wisdom is a personal trial of passage, not an errand that someone else can run on your behalf. To gain acceptance among the elders and earn the right to enter Thunder Bluff, you must personally seek out the Ancestral Spirit at Red Rocks and demonstrate the spiritual clarity gained from the Water of the Seers. The duty of protecting the city can only be bestowed directly upon you.",al={"why me rite of wisdom","why doesn't seer wiserunner go","why do i have to seek ancestral spirit"},it="why_me",add="8728f84f"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For the shu'halo, receiving the mantle from the ancestors at Red Rocks is the culminating moment of passage into full adulthood and acceptance among the tribal elders.",["class:Shaman"]="Communing directly with an Ancestral Spirit reinforces the core shamanic duty of honoring the dead and keeping faith with the spirits that watch over Mulgore.",["class:Druid"]="The rite demonstrates the balance between the living world and the eternal cycle overseen by the Earthmother, grounding young druids in their duty to the land."},kw={"ancestral spirit","mulgore","red rocks","rite of wisdom","rites of the earthmother","seer wiserunner","thunder bluff","water of the seers"},rel={"quest:767","quest:763","subzone:red-rocks","zone:thunderbluff","zone:mulgore","topic:tauren","topic:gnoll"},m={start="Seer Wiserunner",["end"]="Ancestral Spirit",level="10",faction="Horde",id=773},src={"Rite of Wisdom (Classic)"}}
E["quest:775"] = {t="quest",n="Journey into Thunder Bluff",z="mulgore",s="Journey into Thunder Bluff is the culminating pilgrimage marking the end of a young hero's trials across Mulgore. Having completed the sacred Rites of the Earthmother under the guidance of the spirits, the adventurer is granted an audience with the High Chieftain of the tauren, Cairne Bloodhoof.",h="Journey into Thunder Bluff is the culminating pilgrimage marking the end of a young hero's trials across...",sec={{t="A Rite of Passage Concluded",b="Before any traveler or fledgling warrior can truly claim to have proven themselves in Mulgore, they must undergo the ancient Rites of the Earthmother. These trials test strength, vision, and wisdom across the rolling plains and ancestral sites of the land. Once the Ancestral Spirit acknowledges the final rite, the initiate is deemed worthy of entering the city of Thunder Bluff not as a mere visitor, but as an honored defender of the tauren people.",sp=0},{t="Ascending to the High Rise",b="The quest directs the traveler from the sacred ground where the spirits wander straight to the towering heights of Thunder Bluff. Perched atop the mesas, the city is a beacon of peace carved from red stone and supported by sweeping rope bridges and massive wooden elevators. There, on the High Rise, High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof awaits to personally greet those who have walked the path of the ancestors.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who sends you on this journey?",a="The quest is bestowed by an Ancestral Spirit after you finish the Rite of Wisdom, the final step in the traditional Rites of the Earthmother.",al={"who gives quest","ancestral spirit quest","quest giver"},it="identity"},{q="Where can I find Cairne Bloodhoof?",a="Cairne Bloodhoof resides on the High Rise, the central elevated mesa in Thunder Bluff overlooking the surrounding red plateaus and plains.",al={"cairne location","where is cairne","thunder bluff leader"},it="leader",gp=true},{q="What is the significance of completing this quest?",a="It serves as the formal graduation from the starting adventures of Mulgore. Presenting yourself to Cairne marks you as fully recognized by the tauren leadership and ready to take on duties throughout the wider Horde.",al={"why visit cairne","rites meaning","significance of thunder bluff journey"},it="purpose"},{q="Do only tauren complete this journey?",a="While it is a deeply traditional tauren rite of passage, any member of the Horde who proves their dedication through the Rites of the Earthmother is granted the same honor and sent to meet the High Chieftain.",al={"can orcs do this","who can do journey to thunder bluff","races"},it="people"},{q="What happens after speaking to Cairne?",a="Cairne welcomes you to his home, acknowledges your deeds, and points you forward into the broader conflicts facing the Horde across Kalimdor.",al={"next quest","what after cairne","after journey into thunder bluff"},it="next_step"},{q="How do you reach the High Rise in Thunder Bluff?",a="Take the great mechanical elevators from the plains below up to the mesas, then cross the rope and timber bridges that lead toward the central High Rise platform where the Chieftain's lodge stands.",al={"getting to high rise","how to get to cairne","thunder bluff elevators"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why does the Ancestral Spirit send you to Thunder Bluff?",a="By surviving the trials across Mulgore and communing with the spirits, you have proven your wisdom, strength, and devotion through the Rites of the Earthmother. The Ancestral Spirit directs you to Thunder Bluff so that your deeds may be recognized by the tauren people and their highest leader, Cairne Bloodhoof. Standing before the High Chieftain marks your formal acceptance into their society as an honored champion.",al={"why ancestral spirit sends you","ancestral spirit motive","why go to cairne"},it="motive",add="74afb15b"},{q="Why must you deliver the news to Cairne Bloodhoof yourself?",a="The Rites of the Earthmother are a sacred personal trial. The Ancestral Spirit is an otherworldly guide bound to Mulgore's holy grounds, unable and unwilling to carry earthly reports. Because it is your spirit and resolve that were tested, the honor of presenting yourself to High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof belongs solely to you.",al={"why me cairne","why must i visit cairne","why do i have to see cairne bloodhoof"},it="why_me",add="c86a41a9"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For a young tauren, standing before Cairne Bloodhoof after completing the Earthmother's rites is the definitive threshold of adulthood and tribal honor.",["faction:Horde"]="The willingness of the tauren to welcome any allied race who completes their sacred traditions speaks to the mutual respect holding the young Horde together."},kw={"ancestral spirit","cairne bloodhoof","high rise","journey into thunder bluff","mulgore","rites of the earthmother","thunder bluff"},rel={"quest:763","npc:cairne-bloodhoof","zone:thunderbluff","subzone:high-rise","zone:mulgore"},m={start="Ancestral Spirit",["end"]="Cairne Bloodhoof",level="10",faction="Horde",id=775},src={"Journey into Thunder Bluff (Classic)"}}
E["quest:780"] = {t="quest",n="The Battleboars",z="mulgore",s="The Battleboars is a rite-of-passage hunting quest given by Grull Hawkwind in Camp Narache. Young tauren are tasked with culling the vicious war-trained boars of Brambleblade Ravine to defend tribal lands and feed the settlement's youth.",h="The Battleboars is a rite-of-passage hunting quest given by Grull Hawkwind in Camp Narache.",sec={{t="A Threat on the Border",b="To the east of Red Cloud Mesa lies Brambleblade Ravine, a thorn-choked scar held by the Bristleback quilboar. As tensions between the Shu'halo and the quilboar escalate into open war, the Bristlebacks have trained vicious battleboars to probe tauren boundaries and terrorize their traditional hunting grounds. Left unchecked, these aggressive beasts threaten not only game trails but the safety of Camp Narache itself.",sp=0},{t="Feeding the Tribe",b="For the tauren, hunting serves both defensive necessity and communal sustenance. Grull Hawkwind instructs initiates to bring back the snouts and flanks of the slain battleboars. Even beasts trained by quilboar hands are not wasted; their meat and gristle are harvested to prepare hearty stew for the tribe's youth, turning a quilboar weapon into nourishment for the next generation of tauren defenders.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why are the battleboars attacking the mesa?",a="The battleboars have been deliberately bred and trained by the Bristleback quilboar inhabiting Brambleblade Ravine. The quilboar use them as shock animals to harass the tauren and encroach upon Red Cloud Mesa's tribal hunting grounds.",al={"battleboar aggression","why battleboars hostile","battleboars attacking"},it="threat"},{q="Who sends players on this hunt?",a="Grull Hawkwind, a prominent hunter and elder stationed at Camp Narache, directs young tauren through the concluding trials of their early training.",al={"quest giver battleboars","who gives the battleboars quest","grull hawkwind role"},it="people",gp=true},{q="Where are the battleboars found?",a="They wander near and within Brambleblade Ravine, a rugged canyon located just east of Camp Narache on the eastern edge of Red Cloud Mesa.",al={"battleboar location","where are battleboars","brambleblade ravine boars"},it="place",gp=true},{q="What happens to the meat collected from the boars?",a="The battleboar flanks and snouts are brought back to Camp Narache to be simmered into stew for the settlement's children and elders, honoring the tauren tradition of utilizing what they hunt.",al={"battleboar flanks use","what is boar meat for","boar stew"},it="item"},{q="How does this quest fit into the local quilboar conflict?",a="It serves as an early introduction to the open war between the tauren and the Bristleback tribe. Culling their war animals thins the beasts harassing tauren hunting grounds and sends a clear message to the Bristlebacks of Brambleblade Ravine.",al={"tauren quilboar war","bristleback conflict","quilboar lore"},it="conflict"},{q="What quest precedes The Battleboars?",a="It is preceded by The Hunt Continues, an earlier task from Grull Hawkwind focused on learning the hunting grounds of Red Cloud Mesa.",al={"battleboars prerequisite","previous quest battleboars","hunt continues chain"},gp=true},{q="Why does Grull Hawkwind want the battleboars killed?",a="The battleboars have pushed out from Brambleblade Ravine and encroached straight into the Red Cloud tribe's ancestral hunting grounds. Bred and trained to be vicious by the Bristleback quilboar, their mounting aggression directly threatens Camp Narache. Grull Hawkwind intends to send the quilboar a firm message by thinning their war-beasts while turning the hunt into food for the tribe's young.",al={"why grull hawkwind want battleboars dead","grull hawkwind battleboars motive","why kill battleboars mulgore"},it="motive",add="b70ec92c"},{q="What are battleboars?",a="Battleboars are ferocious beasts raised and trained by the Bristleback quilboar of Brambleblade Ravine. Unlike the wild mountain cougars and plainstriders native to the Red Cloud Mesa, these boars are purposefully conditioned for war and malice, serving as living weapons in the Bristlebacks' ongoing struggle against the tauren.",al={"who are the battleboars","what are the battleboars","battleboars lore"},it="target",add="58a19c3f"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="This task embodies the tauren balance of defending the Earth Mother's territories while ensuring nothing taken in battle is wasted, turning enemies' war beasts into sustenance for the camp's calves.",["class:Hunter"]="Facing beasts trained for malice by the Bristlebacks teaches young hunters the sharp contrast between natural wildlife and animals twisted into weapons of war.",["profession:Cooking"]="The request for flanks and snouts highlights the fundamental tauren culinary ethos: coarse, tough meats can always be rendered down into hearty, sustaining stews."},kw={"battleboar","brambleblade ravine","bristleback","camp narache","grull hawkwind","mulgore","quilboar","red cloud mesa"},rel={"npc:grull-hawkwind","subzone:brambleblade-ravine","subzone:camp-narache","topic:quilboar","zone:mulgore"},m={start="Grull Hawkwind",level="4",faction="Horde",id=780,ri={"Battleboar Flank","Battleboar Snout"}},src={"The Battleboars"}}
E["quest:781"] = {t="quest",n="Attack on Camp Narache",z="mulgore",s="Attack on Camp Narache is a vital discovery made within the quilboar den of Brambleblade Ravine. A dirt-stained map reveals that the aggressive Bristleback tribe is actively plotting an organized siege against Camp Narache. Delivering these plans to Chief Hawkwind alerts Camp Narache in time to summon aid from Bloodhoof Village.",h="Attack on Camp Narache is a vital discovery made within the quilboar den of Brambleblade Ravine.",sec={{t="A Plot in the Brambles",b="While scouting deep inside the caverns of Brambleblade Ravine, young tauren adventurers uncover a crude, dirt-stained map laying on the rock floor. Upon closer inspection, the parchment reveals deliberate tactical markings made by the Bristleback quilboar. Rather than engaging in mere opportunistic skirmishes, the quilboar are coordinating a full-scale, prolonged siege against Camp Narache.",sp=0},{t="Warning the Chieftain",b="Carrying the attack plans back to Camp Narache provides Chief Hawkwind with crucial intelligence. Recognizing the dire threat posed to the relatively undefended camp and its young initiates, Hawkwind prepares to send word to Bloodhoof Village for reinforcements. The intercepted map transforms what could have been a catastrophic ambush into a prepared defense.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where are the Bristleback attack plans found?",a="The plans originate from a dirt-stained map located inside a cave in Brambleblade Ravine, just up a path to the right of Chief Sharptusk's hut.",al={"map location","where is dirt-stained map","find the plans"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Who receives the Bristleback attack plans?",a="The plans must be delivered directly to Chief Hawkwind at the center of Camp Narache.",al={"who to turn in to","who gets the map","quest ender"},it="next_step",gp=true},{q="Why are the Bristleback quilboar targeting Camp Narache?",a="The Bristleback tribe seeks to drive the tauren from the Red Cloud Mesa and reclaim the hunting grounds and sacred plains of Mulgore for their own kind.",al={"why attack camp narache","quilboar motives mulgore","bristleback siege"},it="conflict"},{q="How does Chief Hawkwind plan to counter the siege?",a="With early warning provided by the intercepted map, Chief Hawkwind intends to call upon their brethren in Bloodhoof Village to reinforce the mesa and repel the assault.",al={"hawkwind plan","reinforcements camp narache"},it="next_step"},{q="Does this quest require speaking to anyone before picking up the map?",a="No. The quest begins directly from the Dirt-stained Map lying on the cave floor in Brambleblade Ravine, turning into the battle plans once retrieved.",al={"quest starter","dirt stained map starter","how to start attack on camp narache"},gp=true},{q="What happens if Camp Narache falls?",a="Camp Narache is the primary haven for young tauren braves and rites of passage; losing it would compromise the security of Red Cloud Mesa and sever Mulgore's spiritual training grounds.",al={"stakes of camp narache","why defend narache"},it="danger"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For young tauren braves undertaking their earliest rites on the mesa, intercepting this map proves your loyalty to your tribe and protects your kin from a sudden massacre.",["faction:Horde"]="Preserving the tauren's foothold in Mulgore ensures the Horde retains its vital southern anchor in central Kalimdor."},kw={"attack plans","bloodhoof village","brambleblade ravine","bristleback","camp narache","chief hawkwind","dirt-stained map","quilboar","red cloud mesa"},rel={"subzone:camp-narache","subzone:brambleblade-ravine","npc:chief-hawkwind","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore"},m={start="Dirt-stained Map",["end"]="Chief Hawkwind",level="4",faction="Horde",id=781},src={"Attack on Camp Narache"}}
E["quest:833"] = {t="quest",n="A Sacred Burial",z="mulgore",s="A Sacred Burial is a quest entrusted by Lorekeeper Raintotem, tasking Horde adventurers with protecting Red Rocks in eastern Mulgore. The sacred ancestral burial grounds have been overrun by marauding Bristleback quilboar who are defiling the graves of esteemed tauren warriors. Driving off these interlopers restores honor and peace to those who gave their lives founding and defending Thunder Bluff.",h="A Sacred Burial is a quest entrusted by Lorekeeper Raintotem, tasking Horde adventurers with protecting Red...",sec={{t="Hallowed Ground of the Shu'halo",b="Red Rocks serves as one of the most revered sanctuaries in all of Mulgore. Tucked into the crags beneath the eastern mountains, it is the final resting place reserved exclusively for the most valiant tauren. The heroes interred here are legendary warriors who shed blood to protect their tribes, uphold their chieftains, and carve out a permanent homeland atop the mesas of Thunder Bluff. To the Shu'halo, disturbing their rest is an affront not just to tradition, but to the Earth Mother herself.",sp=0},{t="The Bristleback Desecration",b="The nomadic quilboar of the Bristleback tribe have persistently contested tauren control over central Kalimdor, but their recent push into Red Rocks represents a grave escalation. Bands of Bristleback Interlopers have crept onto the holy grounds, ravaging the earth and disturbing the resting sites. With the guardian Lorekeeper Raintotem too aged to single-handedly drive the invaders away, young champions of the Horde must take up weapons to purge the site of the intruders and defend the honored dead.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Red Rocks?",a="Red Rocks is a sacred ancestral cemetery located along the eastern mountains of Mulgore. It is reserved specifically for the most valiant tauren warriors, particularly those who died defending their tribes and founding Thunder Bluff.",al={"where is red rocks","red rocks mulgore","tauren burial ground"},it="place"},{q="Who is Lorekeeper Raintotem?",a="Lorekeeper Raintotem is an elder tauren tasked with tending and safeguarding the sacred grounds of Red Rocks. Recognizing that he is past his prime and cannot physically repel the invading Bristlebacks alone, he calls upon capable adventurers to defend the dead.",al={"raintotem","lorekeeper raintotem","who gives a sacred burial"},it="identity"},{q="Why are the Bristleback quilboar at Red Rocks?",a="The Bristleback tribe continuously presses against tauren territory across Mulgore and the Barrens. Desperate for territory and resources, bands of interlopers have pushed into the crags of Red Rocks, desecrating the gravesites in the process.",al={"why are quilboar at red rocks","bristleback interlopers","quilboar invasion"},it="target"},{q="Why is this desecration so offensive to the tauren?",a="Tauren culture places immense reverence upon honoring their ancestors and living in balance with the spirits of the fallen. Allowing savage quilboar to ravage the earth and disturb the bones of heroes who died for Thunder Bluff is a deeply spiritual insult.",al={"tauren funerary customs","why tauren care about burial"},it="motive"},{q="Where does this quest lead next?",a="A Sacred Burial is a standalone defensive task offered by Lorekeeper Raintotem. Once the Bristleback Interlopers are dealt with, the immediate desecration is halted.",al={"sacred burial follow up","what comes after sacred burial"},it="next_step"},{q="Who is buried at Red Rocks?",a="The site is reserved for champion tauren who fought for the survival of their tribes and chieftains, especially those whose sacrifices made the establishment and defense of Thunder Bluff possible.",al={"who is buried here","red rocks graves"},it="history"},{q="Why does Lorekeeper Raintotem ask you instead of driving them off himself?",a="Lorekeeper Raintotem is an elder who is simply too old and past his prime to take up arms against a violent warband. Tasked with tending and honoring the spirits of Red Rocks rather than fighting on the front lines, he relies on capable travelers like you to handle the physical danger of driving off the Bristleback invaders.",al={"why can't raintotem do it","why raintotem needs help","why doesn't raintotem fight"},it="why_me",add="e11b84bd"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Defending Red Rocks is a matter of profound ancestral honor, ensuring that the warriors who made your people's unified homeland possible rest undisturbed.",["class:Shaman"]="The desecration of sacred grounds agitates the spirits of the honored ancestors, making your intervention necessary to restore balance to the earth.",["faction:Horde"]="Assisting the tauren elders in securing their sacred monuments solidifies the Horde's pledge to defend Mulgore alongside their allies."},kw={"a sacred burial","ancestors","bristleback interloper","lorekeeper raintotem","mulgore","quilboar","red rocks","thunder bluff"},rel={"subzone:red-rocks","npc:lorekeeper-raintotem","zone:mulgore","topic:tauren","zone:thunderbluff"},m={start="Lorekeeper Raintotem",faction="Horde",id=833},src={"A Sacred Burial"}}
E["quest:861"] = {t="quest",n="The Hunter's Way",z="mulgore",s="In Mulgore, Skorn Whitecloud guides aspiring trackers through the Hunter's Way. To prove their skill and respect for the wild, you hunt flatland prowlers and bring their claws to Melor Stonehoof on Thunder Bluff's Hunter Rise.",h="In Mulgore, Skorn Whitecloud guides aspiring trackers through the Hunter's Way.",sec={{t="A Tradition of the Plains",b="For the tauren, hunting is among the highest honors. It calls for patience, attention to the Earth Mother's balance, and respect for the beasts across the plains. Elder Skorn Whitecloud has spent decades tracking across Kalimdor and now rests in Mulgore. He sees young adventurers eager to roam and gives them a traditional trial to shape their ambition into discipline.",sp=0},{t="The Stalkers of the Grasslands",b="Flatland prowlers test a candidate's tracking skill. These great cats move swiftly and quietly, hiding in Mulgore's high grass before ambushing their prey. Bring four sets of claws to show you can spot a predator before it catches you, then turn its ferocity against it.",sp=0},{t="Reaching Hunter Rise",b="After gathering the claws, you climb the wooden towers and bridges to Thunder Bluff's Hunter Rise. Melor Stonehoof receives the trophies there. A master tracker who values Skorn Whitecloud's ancient wisdom, Melor accepts them as proof that you have the spirit to learn more of Kalimdor's hunting ways.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who offers the task to walk the Hunter's Way?",a="Skorn Whitecloud, an elder resting in Mulgore, offers the trial to young adventurers eager to master the wilderness.",al={"quest giver","who gives the quest","skorn whitecloud"},it="identity"},{q="Where do I take the flatland prowler claws?",a="Bring the flatland prowler claws to Melor Stonehoof on the Hunter Rise in Thunder Bluff.",al={"where is melor stonehoof","turn in location","hunter rise"},it="next_step",gp=true},{q="Why are flatland prowler claws chosen as the tribute?",a="Flatland prowlers are stealthy, dangerous hunters of Mulgore's grasslands. Tracking and defeating them tests the sharp eyes, patience, and discipline needed for advanced hunting.",al={"why flatland prowlers","prowler claws lore","what are prowlers"},it="why_wanted"},{q="Is this quest restricted only to the Hunter class?",a="No. Skorn Whitecloud offers this test to any young Horde adventurer eager to roam and learn the traditions of the plains.",al={"hunters only?","can other classes do this","is it for hunters"},gp=true},{q="Where does the hunter's journey lead after speaking to Melor?",a="Melor Stonehoof recognizes your growing talent and directs you toward the harsher Barrens, where experienced trackers such as Sergra Darkthorn can guide you.",al={"what comes next","next quest","where to go after melor"},sp=1,it="next_step"},{q="What significance does hunting hold in tauren culture?",a="For the tauren, hunting is among the highest honors. It means living in harmony with the Earth Mother's creation, taking only what is needed, and respecting the quarry that sustains the tribes.",al={"tauren hunting lore","hunting honor tauren","why is hunting important to tauren"},it="fame"},{q="Why does Skorn Whitecloud want this done?",a="Skorn Whitecloud is an elder hunter whose own roaming days are behind him, but his spirit still burns fiercely. Seeing your restlessness and desire to explore, he wants to guide your ambition toward the honorable traditions of the hunt. By testing your capability and directing you to Melor Stonehoof on Hunter's Rise, Skorn ensures that the tauren's sacred hunting heritage is passed on to a promising new generation.",al={"why does skorn give this quest","skorn whitecloud motive","why send me to melor"},it="motive",add="1c4e016d"},{q="What is the Flatland Prowler Claw for?",a="The claws serve as undeniable proof of your tracking skill, cunning, and martial resolve. Flatland prowlers are fierce and elusive predators of the Mulgore plains. Presenting four of their claws to Melor Stonehoof demonstrates that you possess both the patience to hunt dangerous game and the strength to overcome it, showing the masters on Hunter's Rise that you are worthy of further training.",al={"flatland prowler claws purpose","why collect claws","prowler claw tribute"},it="item",add="837cf763"},{q="What are flatland prowlers and where are they found?",a="Flatland prowlers are predatory big cats native to the open grasslands of Mulgore. They roam the plains stalking game, relying on their stealth, speed, and razor claws to bring down prey. Because of their natural cunning and strength, they are considered an ideal quarry for testing whether an aspiring hunter has mastered the basic skills needed to track and fell wild beasts.",al={"flatland prowlers","mulgore prowlers","what are the prowlers"},it="target",add="d784e285"},{q="Why does Skorn Whitecloud send you instead of hunting the prowlers himself?",a="Skorn has hunted across Mulgore for many years, but his body has grown old even while his spirit remains sharp. More importantly, the task is not a simple gathering chore for camp provisions—it is a rite of passage for you. Skorn recognizes your youthful wanderlust and tests your resolve directly so that you may prove yourself worthy of guidance under Melor Stonehoof.",al={"why doesnt skorn hunt","why me skorn whitecloud","why must i get the claws"},it="why_me",add="3cad1e98"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a young tauren, bringing prowler claws to Hunter Rise marks a rite of passage tied to generations of ancestors who hunted these same plains.",["class:Hunter"]="As a Hunter, you are tested on the wild's core lessons: stalk an ambush predator before it strikes, and read the grasslands' subtle signs.",["faction:Horde"]="As a Horde wanderer, you can learn tauren reverence for nature and earn their trust on Thunder Bluff's heights."},kw={"flatland prowler","flatland prowler claws","hunter rise","melor stonehoof","mulgore","skorn whitecloud","the hunter's way","thunder bluff"},rel={"npc:skorn-whitecloud","npc:melor-stonehoof","zone:mulgore","zone:thunderbluff","topic:tauren","quest:6065"},m={start="Skorn Whitecloud",["end"]="Melor Stonehoof",level="10",faction="Horde",id=861,ri={"Flatland Prowler Claw"}},src={"The Hunter's Way (Classic)"}}
E["quest:8673"] = {t="quest",n="Bloodhoof the Elder",z="mulgore",s="During the Lunar Festival, travelers honor Bloodhoof the Elder just north of Bloodhoof Village. The ancestral spirit appears near the bridge and offers a Coin of Ancestry in return.",h="During the Lunar Festival, travelers honor Bloodhoof the Elder just north of Bloodhoof Village.",sec={{t="A Visit from the Ancestors",b="During the Lunar Festival, spirits of heroes and elders from ages past return to Azeroth. Elder Bloodhoof appears just north of Bloodhoof Village, near the bridge. Travelers honor the sacrifices of the past by approaching the elder, who offers words of encouragement and a Coin of Ancestry.",sp=0},{t="The Bloodhoof Legacy",b="On Mulgore's ancestral lands, Elder Bloodhoof links the living tauren tribes with the memory of their forebears. Honoring the dead is central to tauren spiritual life. It keeps the Earth Mother, the ancestors, and those walking the plains in harmony.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where can I find Elder Bloodhoof?",a="Elder Bloodhoof appears just outside Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore, near the bridge, beneath a beam of celestial light.",al={"elder bloodhoof location","where is elder bloodhoof","bloodhoof elder spot"}},{q="What is the purpose of seeking out Elder Bloodhoof?",a="Travelers seek Elder Bloodhoof during the Lunar Festival to honor the ancestors. In return, the spirit gives them a Coin of Ancestry and wisdom about neighboring spirits such as Elder Proudhorn.",al={"why talk to elder bloodhoof","bloodhoof elder purpose","what does elder bloodhoof give"}},{q="Why is there a beam of light shining down on him?",a="The beam of light marks Elder Bloodhoof as an ancestral spirit manifested during the Lunar Festival. It symbolizes a sacred link to the skies and the spirits of the past.",al={"why is elder bloodhoof glowing","light on elder bloodhoof"}},{q="Can Alliance characters pay homage to Elder Bloodhoof?",a="The Lunar Festival ancestors honor all mortals who show reverence, whatever their faction. Alliance travelers must tread carefully because Elder Bloodhoof is at the edge of Horde territory.",al={"can alliance do this quest","is elder bloodhoof horde only"}},{q="Who is Elder Proudhorn that this elder mentions?",a="Elder Proudhorn is another ancestral spirit honored during the festival, in Thunder Bluff. Elder Bloodhoof can guide travelers to him.",al={"elder wheathoof","who is wheathoof"}},{q="What do the Coins of Ancestry represent?",a="Coins of Ancestry are tokens of favor from Azeroth's honored spirits. They show that a traveler has honored the past, and festival celebrants trade them for festive attire, schematics, and celebratory wares.",al={"what are coins of ancestry","lunar festival tokens"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren, greeting Elder Bloodhoof on your home soil honors the tribe's legacy and its part in leading your people to a peaceful haven in Mulgore.",["class:Druid"]="As a druid, you honor the Lunar Festival spirits and commune with the elders, strengthening your bond with the earth and stars.",["class:Shaman"]="As a shaman, you commune with manifest ancestors like Elder Bloodhoof, joining the living with ancestral spirits.",["faction:Alliance"]="As an Alliance traveler, reaching Elder Bloodhoof means crossing hostile Horde plains into the heart of Mulgore."},kw={"ancestor","bloodhoof village","coin of ancestry","elder bloodhoof","lunar festival","mulgore"},rel={"npc:elder-bloodhoof","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore","topic:light"},m={start="Elder Bloodhoof",id=8673},src={"Bloodhoof the Elder"}}
E["quest:96659"] = {t="quest",n="The Adventurer",z="mulgore",s="Chief Hawkwind sends young travelers from Red Cloud Mesa with supplies for Kaga Wildhoof near Bloodhoof Village. She once aided him, and he means to repay that favor.",h="Chief Hawkwind sends young travelers from Red Cloud Mesa with supplies for Kaga Wildhoof near Bloodhoof...",sec={{t="A Chieftain's Repayment",b="Chief Hawkwind asks you to carry provisions to Kaga Wildhoof near Bloodhoof Village. Long ago, Wildhoof aided him when he needed help, and he remembers that kindness. Now she has made camp outside the village. As young travelers leave the mesa, Hawkwind entrusts them with supplies for his old benefactor.",sp=0},{t="Leaving the Mesa",b="The trail leads from Red Cloud Mesa toward Bloodhoof Village, across the plains of Mulgore. Carrying Hawkwind's bundle takes you beyond Camp Narache, where young tauren have trained, and into the wider lands of the United Tauren Tribes.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Kaga Wildhoof?",a="Kaga Wildhoof is a seasoned tauren adventurer camped just outside Bloodhoof Village. She aided Chief Hawkwind in the past.",al={"kaga wildhoof","who is wildhoof","where is kaga"},it="target"},{q="Why does Chief Hawkwind send these supplies?",a="Chief Hawkwind sends the supplies to repay Kaga Wildhoof for the help she gave him long ago.",al={"hawkwind debt","why send supplies","purpose of supply bundle"},it="motive"},{q="Where is Kaga Wildhoof's camp located?",a="Kaga Wildhoof's camp is just outside Bloodhoof Village, down the path from Red Cloud Mesa into central Mulgore.",al={"kaga camp location","find kaga","bloodhoof village camp"},it="place",gp=true},{q="When does this quest become available?",a="Chief Hawkwind offers this work when you have proven yourself on Red Cloud Mesa and are ready to travel into Mulgore.",al={"when do i get the adventurer","hawkwind quest timing","mesa exit quest"},gp=true},{q="Does this quest take you away from Camp Narache?",a="Yes. You carry the supplies from Camp Narache down the trail toward Bloodhoof Village.",al={"leaving red cloud mesa","journey to bloodhoof"},gp=true},{q="What is contained within the Supply Bundle?",a="The Supply Bundle holds essential supplies for an adventurer traveling beyond the settlements.",al={"what are the supplies","supply bundle contents"},it="item"},{q="Why does Chief Hawkwind ask you to deliver the supplies?",a="You have completed your initial rites in the Red Cloud Mesa and are preparing to leave Camp Narache to venture into the broader plains of Mulgore. With your departure already taking you past Bloodhoof Village, Chief Hawkwind entrusts you with this errand rather than dispatching braves who are needed to guard the mesa and protect the young tauren training there.",al={"why me hawkwind","why send me with supplies","why does hawkwind choose me"},it="why_me",add="2c6e5885"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a young tauren, carrying Hawkwind's supplies to Kaga Wildhoof takes you beyond Camp Narache and into the plains of Mulgore.",["faction:Horde"]="As a Horde traveler leaving Red Cloud Mesa, you carry supplies to Kaga Wildhoof, an experienced wanderer near Bloodhoof Village."},kw={"bloodhoof village","camp narache","chief hawkwind","kaga wildhoof","mulgore","red cloud mesa","supply bundle","the adventurer"},rel={"npc:chief-hawkwind","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore"},m={start="Chief Hawkwind",["end"]="Kaga Wildhoof",level="6",faction="Horde",id=96659},src={"The Adventurer (Mulgore)"},fv=true}
E["subzone:bael-dun-digsite"] = {t="subzone",n="Bael'dun Digsite",z="mulgore",s="Dwarven prospectors cut into the western hills of Mulgore at Bael'dun Digsite. Their search for ancient Titan relics has brought them into conflict with the tauren, who see the digging as an insult to the Earth Mother.",h="Dwarven prospectors cut into the western hills of Mulgore at Bael'dun Digsite.",sec={{t="Intrusion in the Hills",b="Dwarven prospectors dig into the mountain slopes of western Mulgore. Their camp marks the southernmost reach of the Explorers' League expedition based at Bael Modan in the southern Barrens. The dwarves came with picks, shovels, and blasting powder, seeking ancient Titan relics buried beneath the plains. They paid no heed to borders or to the spiritual traditions of the newly settled tribes.",sp=0},{t="Defending the Earth Mother",b="The Bloodhoof tribe sees the digging as an assault on the Earth Mother. Baine Bloodhoof first urged caution and warned the dwarves to leave, but they refused to abandon their trenches. With diplomacy failed, the tauren resolved to break the expedition's tools and drive out the miners before their digging cuts deeper into Mulgore's sacred earth.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why are dwarves digging in Mulgore?",a="The prospectors belong to the dwarven expedition based at Bael Modan in the southern Barrens. The Explorers' League seeks ancient artifacts and Titan heritage, and the dwarves chose this outpost where their instruments pointed to relics, disregarding tauren sovereignty.",al={"why are dwarves here","dwarves in mulgore","baeldun dwarves"},it="motive"},{q="How does the Bloodhoof tribe respond to the digsite?",a="The tauren see the digsite as a wound against the Earth Mother. After the dwarves refused early warnings to leave peacefully, Baine Bloodhoof and the village leaders sent warriors to destroy the miners' tools and drive them from Mulgore.",al={"what do tauren think","baine reaction","tauren response to digsite"},it="conflict"},{q="What is the connection between Bael'dun and Bael Modan?",a="Bael'dun is an outpost of the fortress and main excavation at Bael Modan, just across the mountain ridge in the southern Barrens.",al={"bael modan link","where do these dwarves come from"},it="ties"},{q="Why didn't the tauren simply negotiate with the expedition?",a="Baine Bloodhoof first tried diplomacy and warned the expedition leaders. The dwarves treated Mulgore as unclaimed wilderness and ignored tauren spiritual concerns, leaving the tribe to use force.",al={"did tauren try peace","diplomacy with dwarves"}},{q="What is Bael'dun Digsite?",a="Bael'dun Digsite is an active dwarven excavation carved directly into the western mountains of Mulgore. Established by Prospector Khazgorm and members of the Dwarven Archaeological League out of Bael Modan, it represents an unwelcome Alliance intrusion deep inside tauren territory.",al={"what is baeldun digsite","baeldun quarry mulgore","dwarf digsite mulgore"},it="place",add="02606c94"},{q="What happened at Bael'dun Digsite?",a="Dwarven excavators arrived from the Barrens to unearth titan relics and ancient secrets buried beneath the soil. Ignoring the furious objections and warnings of Baine Bloodhoof, they began tearing into the earth. The Bloodhoof tribe responded by sabotaging their mining efforts and breaking their pickaxes to force them out of sacred lands.",al={"baeldun history","what happened at the digsite"},it="history",add="cc534035"},{q="Who runs Bael'dun Digsite?",a="The site is operated by dwarven miners and excavators dispatched from Bael Modan in the southern Barrens. They labor under the orders of the Explorer's League and dwarven expedition leaders who prioritize uncovering ancient artifacts over maintaining peace with the native tauren.",al={"who controls baeldun","baeldun digsite leader","who runs the digsite"},it="residents",add="fc08309c"},{q="Is Bael'dun Digsite dangerous?",a="Yes. The dwarven excavators and foremen stationed here are hostile to Horde travelers and defend their claim with picks, rifles, and bludgeons. Furthermore, their relentless dynamiting and gouging of the mountainside threatens to disturb the natural spirits of Mulgore.",al={"how dangerous is baeldun digsite","is baeldun safe"},it="danger",add="30c4a8af"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren, you see Bael'dun's scars on Mulgore's western hills as an insult to the Earth Mother. The ancestral soil must be defended from foreign desecration.",["faction:Alliance"]="As an Alliance member, you may understand the miners' thirst for historical discovery. Their digging has reached deep into Horde territory and brought them into violent conflict with the locals."},kw={"bael modan","bael'dun","baeldun","baine bloodhoof","digsite","dwarves","earth mother","explorers league","mulgore"},rel={"zone:mulgore","subzone:bael-modan","npc:baine-bloodhoof","topic:earth-mother","topic:tauren"},src={"Bael'dun Digsite"}}
E["subzone:bloodhoof-village"] = {t="subzone",n="Bloodhoof Village",z="mulgore",s="Young tauren gather at Bloodhoof Village beside Stonebull Lake in central Mulgore. Led by Baine Bloodhoof, the village guides them from Camp Narache toward Thunder Bluff and tends the plains’ defense, trade, and rites.",h="Young tauren gather at Bloodhoof Village beside Stonebull Lake in central Mulgore.",sec={{t="Heart of the Plains",b="Bloodhoof Village gathers tauren around Stonebull Lake in central Mulgore. Young tauren arrive from their trials at Camp Narache to hone their skills before traveling onward to the great mesas of Thunder Bluff. Patient breeders here tame wild kodo beasts for travel, while the village is also a center of craft and ceremony.",sp=0},{t="Leadership and Rites",b="Baine Bloodhoof, son of High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof, oversees daily governance and the region’s defense. Braves patrol the grasslands against aggressive wildlife, quilboar encroachers, and the Venture Company. Spiritual guides such as Zarlman Two-Moons tend to the tribe’s inner life and lead novices through sacred journeys like the Rite of Vision.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who leads Bloodhoof Village?",a="Baine Bloodhoof, son of High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof, leads Bloodhoof Village and directs its defenses against threats to the central plains.",al={"village leader","baine bloodhoof location","who is in charge of bloodhoof village"},it="in_charge"},{q="What major rites are conducted here?",a="Spiritual guides such as Zarlman Two-Moons lead young tauren through the traditional Rite of Vision, an essential spiritual journey on the path to adulthood.",al={"rite of vision","spiritual trials","zarlman two-moons quest"},it="purpose"},{q="What is the settlement known for producing?",a="Bloodhoof Village is the main place where wild plains kodos are tamed and trained as mounts.",al={"tauren mounts","kodo taming","bloodhoof drums"}},{q="Where do travelers go after finishing their tasks here?",a="After their deeds in central Mulgore, travelers are generally directed north to Thunder Bluff, the tauren capital.",al={"after bloodhoof","where next after bloodhoof","next hub"},it="next_step"},{q="What is Bloodhoof Village?",a="Bloodhoof Village is a vital tauren settlement nestled in central Mulgore along the shores of Stonebull Lake. It serves as the primary waystation and crossroads for travelers and young braves journeying north from the Red Cloud Mesa toward Thunder Bluff. Here, the tauren gather to trade, seek spiritual guidance, and tame wild kodo beasts for travel.",al={"what is bloodhoof village","bloodhoof village lore","about bloodhoof village"},it="place",add="0d479a74"},{q="Is Bloodhoof Village dangerous?",a="The village itself is a well-guarded haven, watched over by Baine Bloodhoof and local braves. However, the surrounding plains of Mulgore hold constant dangers, from prowling predators and aggressive gnolls to encroaching Venture Company loggers and shadowy Grimtotem operatives working against the peace of the united tribes.",al={"bloodhoof village danger","is bloodhoof safe","threats to bloodhoof"},it="danger",add="4dcc3235"},{q="Why is it called Bloodhoof Village?",a="The settlement is named in honor of the Bloodhoof tribe, the prominent tauren clan led by High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof. It proudly bears the clan's name and remains under the direct stewardship of Cairne's son, Baine.",al={"bloodhoof village name origin","why bloodhoof village","who is bloodhoof village named after"},it="name",add="39f13758"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For young tauren, entering Bloodhoof Village marks their first real step beyond youth and into broader tribal responsibility and spiritual maturity.",["faction:Horde"]="For the Horde, Bloodhoof Village is a peaceful stopover and a vital link between Mulgore’s southern plains and the Horde capital at Thunder Bluff."},kw={"baine bloodhoof","bloodhoof village","camp narache","kodo","mulgore","stonebull lake","zarlman two-moons"},rel={"npc:baine-bloodhoof","zone:mulgore","subzone:stonebull-lake","npc:zarlman-two-moons","subzone:camp-narache","zone:thunderbluff","quest:767","topic:tauren"},src={"Bloodhoof Village"}}
E["subzone:brambleblade-ravine"] = {t="subzone",n="Brambleblade Ravine",z="mulgore",s="Bristleback quilboar raid Camp Narache from Brambleblade Ravine, cut into Red Cloud Mesa’s northeastern edge. Young tauren braves enter the ravine to defend their homeland and prove their strength.",h="Bristleback quilboar raid Camp Narache from Brambleblade Ravine, cut into Red Cloud Mesa’s northeastern edge.",sec={{t="A Thorn in the Mesa's Side",b="Brambleblade Ravine cuts into Red Cloud Mesa’s northeastern edge, its thorny briars and quilboar camps guarding the Bristleback tribe. From this den, the Bristlebacks raid Camp Narache and threaten young tauren coming of age. Chief Sharptusk Thornmantle leads the shamans and warriors who dwell deep in the ravine with trained battleboars. For the Shu'halo, striking into the canyon is both a rite of passage and a hard need if the plains are to be safe.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Brambleblade Ravine?",a="Brambleblade Ravine cuts into the northeastern edge of Red Cloud Mesa in Mulgore. Bristleback quilboar hold it as a stronghold.",al={"brambleblade ravine","what is brambleblade ravine","brambleblade mulgore"},it="place"},{q="Why are the Bristleback quilboar attacking from here?",a="The Bristlebacks claim the lands around Brambleblade Ravine and see the tauren as intruders. They raid nearby Camp Narache from the ravine.",al={"why quilboar attacking","bristleback motives","quilboar camp narache"},it="motive"},{q="Who leads the quilboar in Brambleblade Ravine?",a="Chief Sharptusk Thornmantle leads the quilboar in Brambleblade Ravine. He lives in a large hut toward the ravine’s back.",al={"brambleblade leader","quilboar boss mulgore","chief sharptusk thornmantle"},it="leader"},{q="Why do young tauren travel to the ravine?",a="Tauren elders and chieftains at Camp Narache send young braves into the ravine to push back the Bristlebacks, reduce their numbers, and defeat their leaders to keep the mesa safe.",al={"tauren quests brambleblade","why go to brambleblade","camp narache quests"},it="why_me"},{q="Who lives in Brambleblade Ravine?",a="The ravine serves as a fortified settlement for the Bristleback quilboar. Alongside aggressive battleboars, tribesmen, and shamans, the Bristleback have built their huts throughout the dirt trails, answering to Chief Sharptusk Thornmantle and his kin.",al={"who lives in brambleblade ravine","brambleblade ravine inhabitants","who is in brambleblade ravine"},it="residents",add="9e3e8e60"},{q="Is Brambleblade Ravine dangerous?",a="It is very perilous for unwary travelers. The quilboar guard their dens aggressively, swarming intruders with trained battleboars and mystic shamans. Anyone descending into the ravine without caution can quickly find themselves cornered against the high canyon walls.",al={"is brambleblade ravine dangerous","how dangerous is brambleblade ravine","brambleblade ravine danger"},it="danger",add="cff45375"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a young tauren coming of age at Camp Narache, you face one of your first tests of martial and spiritual endurance when you strike into Brambleblade Ravine to defend your tribe.",["faction:Horde"]="As a member of the Horde, you help secure its early foothold across Mulgore by clearing the aggressive Bristlebacks from Brambleblade Ravine."},kw={"brambleblade","bristleback","camp narache","mulgore","quilboar","ravine","red cloud mesa","thornmantle"},rel={"topic:quilboar","topic:tauren","zone:mulgore"},src={"Brambleblade Ravine"}}
E["subzone:camp-narache"] = {t="subzone",n="Camp Narache",z="mulgore",s="Young tauren begin their training at Camp Narache, high on Red Cloud Mesa in southern Mulgore. The camp is the Hawkwind tribe’s ancestral home, where elders preserve old ways as quilboar press close and centaur scouts linger.",h="Young tauren begin their training at Camp Narache, high on Red Cloud Mesa in southern Mulgore.",sec={{t="Heart of the Hawkwind Tribe",b="Camp Narache sits at the northern edge of Red Cloud Mesa, the main settlement of the Hawkwind tribe. Hide lodges and pavilions house the wounded, store weapons, bake bread, and offer a place for meditation and communion with ancestors. At the camp’s heart, elders and path trainers gather in a circle. Greatmother Hawkwind guides the newly initiated as they take up their roles as defenders of the Earth Mother.",sp=0},{t="A Proving Ground Under Threat",b="Steep cliffs shield Camp Narache, but threats reach the mesa all the same. Bristleback quilboar from Brambleblade Ravine press into Hawkwind lands, while centaur scouts remain in the distance. Young hunters, shamans, druids, and warriors prove their mettle by thinning aggressive wildlife, guarding the village well, and driving back quilboar raiders. Then they descend the grand path into Mulgore’s wider valleys.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Camp Narache?",a="Young tauren begin their training at Camp Narache, atop Red Cloud Mesa in southern Mulgore. The camp is the Hawkwind tribe’s main settlement, where hunters, warriors, druids, and shamans learn their crafts before traveling to Bloodhoof Village and Thunder Bluff.",al={"what is camp narache","narache lore","tauren starting town"},it="place"},{q="Who leads the village?",a="Greatmother Hawkwind guides the tribe alongside Chief Hawkwind, who leads the braves and coordinates the mesa’s defenses. The elders and trainers gather at the camp’s center to instruct the young.",al={"who leads camp narache","camp narache chief","hawkwind leader"},it="in_charge"},{q="What threats does the camp face?",a="Bristleback quilboar from Brambleblade Ravine to the east harass the camp. Battleboars and striders roam the surrounding bluffs, and centaur bands remain a threat.",al={"camp narache enemies","who attacks camp narache","mesa threats"},it="threat"},{q="Can travelers easily leave the mesa?",a="Steep cliffs bound Red Cloud Mesa, and a guarded mountain path leads north into Mulgore’s plains. Hawkwind braves watch the way to keep hostile beasts and quilboar from ambushing travelers.",al={"leaving red cloud mesa","exit camp narache","path off the mesa"}},{q="What happened at Camp Narache?",a="Camp Narache was founded as a sheltered home for the Hawkwind tribe upon the elevated heights of Red Cloud Mesa. Over generations, it evolved from a simple tribal gathering spot into the principal proving ground for young tauren across Mulgore. Protected by steep cliffs and natural ramparts, the camp allowed fledgling hunters, shamans, and warriors to undergo their traditional rites away from the fierce centaur raids that plagued the open plains below.",al={"camp narache history","history of camp narache","what happened here"},it="history",add="017769ab"},{q="Who lives in Camp Narache?",a="Camp Narache is the primary village of the Hawkwind tribe, led by Chief Hawkwind and his elder mother, Greatmother Hawkwind. Alongside tribal braves, traders, and craftsmen, mentors from across the tauren traditions gather here. Shaman, druids, warriors, and hunters reside in the camp's hide lodges to train young tauren who have arrived to complete their coming-of-age trials.",al={"who lives at camp narache","camp narache residents","who lives here"},it="residents",add="de5ddd4a"},{q="Is Camp Narache dangerous?",a="The settlement itself is peaceful and well-guarded by Hawkwind braves, but the surrounding mesa is increasingly perilous. Aggressive mountain pumas and territorial battleboars prowl the borders, while a growing population of Bristleback quilboar has encroached upon the nearby ravine. While safe within the perimeter of the lodges, wandering far from the camp's hearths brings immediate peril to inexperienced youths.",al={"is camp narache safe","how dangerous is camp narache","danger in camp narache"},it="danger",add="452dc8d4"},{q="Is this where all tauren start out?",a="Yes. Situated high on the protected cliffs of Red Cloud Mesa, Camp Narache serves as the primary training camp for all young tauren. Before you are trusted to venture into the wider valleys of Mulgore or serve the chieftain in Thunder Bluff, you must first prove your strength, wisdom, and respect for nature under the watchful eyes of the elders here.",al={"do all tauren start here","tauren starting area","why do tauren begin here"},it="place",add="602085f2"},{q="Who built Camp Narache?",a="The camp was established by the Hawkwind tribe as their ancestral village on Red Cloud Mesa. Built primarily with sturdy leather tents, carved totems, and natural timber, it was designed according to traditional nomadic techniques, though it has stood as a lasting center for tribal gatherings and the education of tauren youth.",al={"who made camp narache","origin of camp narache","who founded camp narache"},it="origin",add="e8115520"},{q="Is Chief Hawkwind important to the tauren?",a="Chief Hawkwind is the leader of the Hawkwind tribe and the overseer of Red Cloud Mesa. While he governs the camp and directs the defense against the Bristlebacks, his vital cultural role is guiding newly awakened braves through their early trials before they enter the service of the united tribes.",al={"chief hawkwind role","who is chief hawkwind","importance of chief hawkwind"},it="leader",add="9d2ccac7"},{q="Does Chief Hawkwind answer to anyone else?",a="Yes. Like all tribal chieftains in Mulgore, Chief Hawkwind recognizes Cairne Bloodhoof as the High Chieftain of the united tauren tribes. Beyond Cairne, the tauren have pledged their loyalty to Warchief Thrall and the greater Horde, though day-to-day governance of the camp remains in Hawkwind's hands.",al={"who does chief hawkwind answer to","chief hawkwind superior","who rules over hawkwind"},it="in_charge",add="5c34accd"},{q="What are the Bristlebacks and why are they attacking us?",a="The Bristlebacks are a savage tribe of quilboar that inhabit the crags and thorny thickets of the mesa. Fiercely territorial and desperate for resources, they view the tauren settlements as an intrusion into their feeding grounds. Emboldened by their chieftains, they have begun raiding tauren supply lines and threatening Camp Narache directly.",al={"why are bristlebacks attacking","bristleback quilboar","who are the bristlebacks"},it="threat",add="4397ed07"},{q="What is Brambleblade Ravine?",a="Brambleblade Ravine is a jagged, bramble-choked canyon located on the northeastern edge of Red Cloud Mesa. It serves as the primary den and stronghold of the Bristleback quilboar in this region, housing their battleboars and their chieftain, Sharptusk Thornmantle.",al={"brambleblade ravine","where is brambleblade ravine","about brambleblade ravine"},it="place",add="35bda353"},{q="Who is the Earthmother?",a="The Earthmother is the benevolent creator deity revered by the tauren above all else. Tauren tradition teaches that she gave birth to the world, breathed life into the lands, and created their people to live in harmony with the wilds. Her will guides their spiritual practices, shamanic rituals, and reverence for nature.",al={"who is the earth mother","earthmother lore","tauren earthmother"},it="other",add="7ff27be4"},{q="What is Mulgore?",a="Mulgore is the ancestral homeland of the tauren—a vast, verdant expanse of windswept grassy plains, rolling foothills, and towering plateaus. Ringed on all sides by impassable mountain walls, its fertile valleys are home to roaming kodo beasts, wolves, and the permanent tauren capital of Thunder Bluff.",al={"what is the land of mulgore","about mulgore","mulgore lore"},it="place",add="d26e5d41"},{q="Why did the tauren settle in Mulgore?",a="For generations, brutal centaur marauders forced the tauren into a wandering, nomadic existence across the Barrens. After joining forces with Warchief Thrall and the New Horde to drive the centaur out of the region, High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof led his people to settle permanently in the lush, defensible valleys of their ancestral home.",al={"why tauren live in mulgore","why did tauren come to mulgore","settling mulgore"},it="history",add="2ba66ce3"},{q="Is Mulgore safe?",a="Though naturally fortified and peaceful compared to the Barrens, Mulgore is not without danger. Hostile Palemane gnolls, Windfury harpies, aggressive quilboar, and opportunistic goblin miners from the Venture Company continuously disrupt the peace, forcing the tauren to remain vigilant.",al={"is mulgore dangerous","how safe is mulgore","threats in mulgore"},it="danger",add="b543a978"},{q="What is Bloodhoof Village?",a="Bloodhoof Village is a central tauren settlement resting upon the shores of Stonebull Lake in central Mulgore. It serves as a major trade and cultural hub between the proving grounds of Red Cloud Mesa and the soaring heights of Thunder Bluff.",al={"about bloodhoof village","where is bloodhoof village","what is bloodhoof"},it="place",add="2e7cb737"},{q="What is Red Cloud Mesa?",a="Red Cloud Mesa is a high, sheltered plateau rising above southern Mulgore. Flanked by sheer cliffs that keep most predators and invaders from the lowlands at bay, the mesa houses Camp Narache and serves as the secluded proving ground for all young tauren.",al={"red cloud mesa","what is this mesa","about red cloud mesa"},it="place",add="8b10e970"},{q="What is Thunder Bluff?",a="Thunder Bluff is the magnificent capital city of the tauren, perched high atop a series of wind-carved mesas in northern Mulgore. Connected by suspended rope bridges and reached by vast wooden elevators, it was founded by High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof as the united sanctuary for all tauren tribes.",al={"what is thunder bluff","about thunder bluff","tauren capital"},it="place",add="dff4694b"},{q="When did the tauren join the Horde?",a="The tauren allied with the Horde during the Third War, shortly after Thrall and his orcs landed upon Kalimdor. Recognizing a shared spiritual honor and kinship, Thrall helped Cairne Bloodhoof defend his caravan from centaur raids, cementing an enduring friendship that brought the tauren into the New Horde.",al={"tauren joining the horde","how did tauren join horde","when tauren met thrall"},it="history",add="51c45aa6"},{q="How did I end up in Camp Narache?",a="You came to Camp Narache on the secluded heights of Red Cloud Mesa to train and prove yourself among the Hawkwind tribe. While the lower plains of Mulgore can be reached only through a fortified pass, young tauren gather in this sheltered refuge to master their skills under the watchful eye of tribal elders and class trainers before venturing out into the wider world.",al={"how did i get here","how did i end up here","why am i at camp narache"},it="arrival",add="6d7e8195"},{q="What am I supposed to be doing in Camp Narache?",a="Your duty is to aid Chief Hawkwind and the camp elders while learning the rites of your path. Red Cloud Mesa faces constant harassment from aggressive Bristleback quilboars lurking in Brambleblade Ravine and mountain cougars prowling the plateau. By defending the settlement, assisting wounded braves, and performing sacred rituals for the Earth Mother, you will earn the right to descend to Bloodhoof Village and serve the Horde.",al={"what do i do here","what is my purpose here","why am i here"},it="purpose",add="ccbc2a7d"},{q="Why does a new Tauren start here, in Camp Narache?",a="Camp Narache is the ancestral proving ground where young tauren take their first steps toward adulthood. Perched safely atop Red Cloud Mesa away from the roaming centaur and outside threats that haunt central Kalimdor, it offers a secure sanctuary. Here, the elders and trainers of the Hawkwind tribe guide new braves in hunting, honoring the spirits, and learning to defend the new homeland Cairne Bloodhoof secured for your people.",al={"why do tauren start here","why start at camp narache","why is this the tauren starting zone"},it="arrival",rc="Tauren",add="74e7cbb8"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren, you begin your rites of the hunt on this sacred earth and attune yourself to ancestral spirits under the Hawkwind elders’ watch.",["class:Shaman"]="As a young shaman, you take your first steps on the spiritual path here, facing trials to earn your Earth Totem and speak with the mesa’s elementals."},kw={"bristleback","greatmother hawkwind","hawkwind","mulgore","narache","red cloud mesa","spirit lounge","tauren"},rel={"zone:mulgore","npc:greatmother-hawkwind","subzone:brambleblade-ravine","topic:tauren","topic:centaur"},src={"Camp Narache"}}
E["subzone:golden-plains"] = {t="subzone",n="Golden Plains",z="mulgore",s="The Golden Plains form a vast, sun-drenched basin stretching southeast of Thunder Bluff in central Mulgore. Cherished by the tauren, these sweeping grasslands serve as prime hunting territory and a vital grazing habitat for wandering herds of kodo.",h="The Golden Plains form a vast, sun-drenched basin stretching southeast of Thunder Bluff in central Mulgore.",sec={{t="Heart of the Hunt",b="Spreading out beneath the towering mesas of Thunder Bluff, the Golden Plains represent the untamed heart of Mulgore. The shifting sea of golden and green grass offers an abundant sanctuary for local wildlife, making it one of the most treasured traditional hunting grounds of the nomadic tauren. Broad kodo herds roam across the rolling basin, while hunters share stories of legendary quarry, most notably the great kodo Arra'chea, who wanders quietly through the tall stalks.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What are the Golden Plains?",a="The Golden Plains are a broad grassland basin located southeast of Thunder Bluff in Mulgore. They are a beloved cultural hunting ground for the tauren, defined by wide vistas and sprawling wildlife.",al={"golden plains mulgore","where are golden plains"},it="place"},{q="What animals roam the Golden Plains?",a="The basin is famous for its abundant herds of wild kodo, alongside other native creatures of Mulgore. The most famous beast said to wander these grasslands is the elusive and revered kodo named Arra'chea.",al={"golden plains beasts","kodos in golden plains"}},{q="Why is this area important to the tauren?",a="The plains serve as a vital source of game and hides, providing sustenance for the newly founded settlements across Mulgore and celebrating the tauren tradition of tracking and honoring their quarry.",al={"tauren golden plains","why tauren hunt here"},it="history"},{q="Is it dangerous to wander the Golden Plains?",a="For an unwary traveler, the plains pose genuine hazards. While not teeming with hostile armies, the basin is wild territory dominated by herds of massive kodo. Disturbance can easily provoke these colossal beasts into a deadly charge, and legendary, aggressive creatures such as Arra'chea roam freely across the undulating grasslands.",al={"golden plains dangerous","danger golden plains","is golden plains safe"},it="danger",add="977b4a77"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For the tauren, the Golden Plains are an enduring symbol of their homeland's bounty, offering sacred hunting grounds beneath the shadow of Thunder Bluff.",["class:Hunter"]="The vast open expanse and roaming kodo herds make these plains a prime testing ground for trackers seeking legendary beasts like Arra'chea."},kw={"arra'chea","golden plains","hunting grounds","kodo","mulgore","thunder bluff"},rel={"zone:mulgore","zone:thunderbluff","topic:tauren"},src={"Golden Plains"}}
E["subzone:kodo-rock"] = {t="subzone",n="Kodo Rock",z="mulgore",s="Young tauren shamans are directed to Kodo Rock on Red Cloud Mesa, between Camp Narache and Brambleblade Ravine. The stone rises among Mulgore’s grasses, easy to miss against the rolling land.",h="Young tauren shamans are directed to Kodo Rock on Red Cloud Mesa, between Camp Narache and Brambleblade...",sec={{t="A Waypoint on the Mesa",b="Kodo Rock marks the straight path between Camp Narache and Brambleblade Ravine, where hostile quilboar hold territory. Worn earth circles its base. Its profile recalls the kodo beasts that wander the grasslands, though it is smaller than the bluffs around Red Cloud Mesa. For tribesfolk crossing Mulgore’s windswept plains, the stone offers a quiet landmark.",sp=0},{t="Spiritual Significance",b="Among the Shu'halo, natural formations carry spiritual weight. Young tauren shamans beginning their spiritual walk are directed to Kodo Rock for early rites of passage. There, at the solitary stone, novices commune with elemental forces and honor the natural world before they venture into more perilous reaches of the continent.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where is Kodo Rock located?",a="Kodo Rock stands on Red Cloud Mesa in Mulgore, roughly halfway along the straight path between Camp Narache and Brambleblade Ravine. A small clearing of bare dirt marks its base.",al={"kodo rock location","where is kodo rock","find kodo rock"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why is Kodo Rock hard to find?",a="Kodo Rock blends into the mesa’s rolling hills and grasses, and is clear only at close range. Watch the terrain for the patch of dirt beneath it.",al={"cant find kodo rock","kodo rock hidden"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why is Kodo Rock important to tauren shamans?",a="Tauren shamans visit Kodo Rock during their earliest trials out of Camp Narache. Novices are directed there to attune their spirits to Mulgore’s land and elemental forces.",al={"shaman quest kodo rock","why go to kodo rock"},it="purpose"},{q="What dangers are near Kodo Rock?",a="The area around Kodo Rock is generally peaceful. Farther northeast, Brambleblade Ravine holds aggressive Bristleback quilboar.",al={"is kodo rock safe","enemies near kodo rock"},it="danger"},{q="What is Kodo Rock?",a="Kodo Rock is a modest natural monument nestled within the Red Cloud Mesa of Mulgore, standing between Camp Narache and the bristling Brambleblade Ravine. Though unassuming from a distance and easily overlooked in the rolling plains, it holds deep spiritual significance for young tauren shamans beginning their communion with the elemental earth.",al={"what is kodo rock","kodo rock mulgore","kodo rock landmark"},it="place",add="5880b9c2"}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="As a shaman beginning the spiritual path at Camp Narache, you are directed to Kodo Rock to attune with the elemental world.",["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren, you know Kodo Rock as a landmark on Red Cloud Mesa, a stone that reflects the Shu'halo’s sacred bond with Mulgore’s beasts."},kw={"brambleblade ravine","camp narache","kodo rock","monument","mulgore","red cloud mesa","shaman trial","tauren shaman"},rel={"subzone:camp-narache","subzone:brambleblade-ravine","zone:mulgore","topic:tauren"},src={"Kodo Rock"}}
E["subzone:palemane-rock"] = {t="subzone",n="Palemane Rock",z="mulgore",s="Palemane Rock shelters a gnoll camp in Mulgore's western mountains. The Palemane tribe holds the cave and foothills, threatening tauren near Bloodhoof Village.",h="Palemane Rock shelters a gnoll camp in Mulgore's western mountains.",sec={{t="A Gnoll Stronghold",b="Palemane Rock lies along Mulgore's western rim, where open grassland meets the mountains. An encampment fronts a deep cave in the mountainside. Palemane gnolls claim the cave and the foothills outside it as their territory. They attack hunters, travelers, and scouts who come too close, and defend their den fiercely.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Palemane Rock?",a="Palemane Rock is a cave and gnoll settlement in Mulgore's western cliffs.",al={"palemane rock info","palemane cave","where is palemane rock"},it="place"},{q="Who lives at Palemane Rock?",a="Palemane gnolls hold the cave and surrounding camp. They defend their territory against outsiders, including tauren.",al={"who is here","palemane mobs","gnolls at palemane rock"},it="residents"},{q="Why does Palemane Rock matter to the tauren?",a="Palemane Rock lies near central Mulgore and Bloodhoof Village. Hostile raiders from the camp keep tauren hunters and braves busy suppressing them.",al={"why do tauren care","bloodhoof village gnolls","threat to mulgore"}},{q="Is Palemane Rock dangerous?",a="Yes, it is very dangerous. Palemane Rock is heavily infested with aggressive gnolls of the Palemane tribe. They occupy both the exterior camp and the deep cave tunnels winding into the mountainside, and they will fiercely attack anyone who wanders too close to their territory.",al={"is palemane rock dangerous","palemane rock danger","are palemane gnolls hostile"},it="danger",add="e2adfd83"},{q="Why is it called Palemane Rock?",a="The cave and its surrounding crags take their name from the Palemane gnoll tribe that has claimed the area as their home.",al={"why is it called palemane rock","palemane rock name origin","why palemane rock"},it="name",add="3b0205a5"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="The Palemane gnolls in these western caves threaten game and unwary travelers across your people's pastures in Mulgore.",["faction:Horde"]="Keeping Mulgore's borders safe from Palemane gnolls helps protect the tauren homeland."},kw={"bloodhoof","cave","gnoll","mulgore","palemane","rock"},rel={"zone:mulgore","topic:gnoll"},src={"Palemane Rock"}}
E["subzone:ravaged-caravan"] = {t="subzone",n="Ravaged Caravan",z="mulgore",s="Smoke still rises from the Ravaged Caravan on Stonebull Lake's northeastern shore in Mulgore. The Venture Company convoy was bound for Supervisor Fizsprocket's mining operation before tauren defenders destroyed it.",h="Smoke still rises from the Ravaged Caravan on Stonebull Lake's northeastern shore in Mulgore.",sec={{t="Smoke Over Stonebull Lake",b="The ruined wagons sit on a rise above Stonebull Lake, where tauren from Bloodhoof Village stopped the Venture Company. The convoy carried mining supplies, blueprints, and equipment to Supervisor Fizsprocket's dig site, but tauren defenders wrecked it before it could arrive. Venture Co. survivors still cling to the wagons and smoldering crates, guarding what cargo remains.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What happened to this caravan?",a="Local tauren intercepted and wrecked the Venture Company caravan as it moved deep into Mulgore. They sought to stop the goblins from stripping the land, and destroyed the convoy before its cargo reached the nearby mine.",al={"why is the caravan burned","what happened here","ravaged caravan backstory"},it="history"},{q="Who owns the wrecked wagons?",a="The Venture Company owns the wrecked wagons. The goblin mining consortium is trying to establish unpermitted operations in the hills of Mulgore.",al={"whose caravan is this","who owns the caravan","venture company caravan"},it="people"},{q="Where was the caravan heading?",a="The caravan was heading north with tools, machinery, and supplies for Supervisor Fizsprocket, who leads the Venture Company's strip-mining operation at the Venture Co. Mine.",al={"caravan destination","where was it going","fizsprocket supplies"},it="purpose"},{q="Why are there still Venture Co. workers lingering at the wreck?",a="The surviving Venture Co. workers fiercely guard company property. They remain around the charred wagons, trying to salvage what remains of their equipment.",al={"why are goblins still here","venture co survivors","who is guarding the wreck"},it="residents"},{q="What is the Ravaged Caravan?",a="The Ravaged Caravan is the smoldering wreckage of a Venture Company supply convoy resting on a mound along the northeastern shore of Stonebull Lake. What once brought fresh tools and provisions now lies in smoking ruins, surrounded by overturned wagons and scattered cargo.",al={"what is ravaged caravan","ravaged caravan mulgore","ravaged caravan"},it="place",add="71a5a670"},{q="Why is the Ravaged Caravan ruined?",a="The caravan was intercepted and brought down in a violent clash with the local tauren. Watching an unwelcome goblin mining outfit trample through their sacred ancestral lands, the tauren swiftly dismantled the expedition before its mining gear could reinforce operations elsewhere in Mulgore.",al={"why is the caravan destroyed","who attacked the caravan","why is it wrecked"},it="ruin",add="9edfa574"},{q="Is the Ravaged Caravan dangerous?",a="Yes. While the transport itself was wrecked, surviving Venture Company laborers still linger amidst the smoldering debris. Desperate to protect what little remains of their shipment, they attack anyone who approaches the wreckage.",al={"is ravaged caravan safe","ravaged caravan danger","are the workers hostile"},it="danger",add="0b83cd13"},{q="Why is it called the Ravaged Caravan?",a="The site takes its name from the violent devastation left behind after the tauren attack. All that remains of the expedition is a shattered line of broken wagons, charred supplies, and thick black smoke rising over Stonebull Lake.",al={"why is it called ravaged caravan","ravaged caravan name origin","how did it get its name"},it="name",add="87f61f4b"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren, you see the charred wagons as your people's refusal to let foreign industrial syndicates despoil Mulgore's sacred pastures.",["class:Druid"]="As a druid, you see the black smoke over Stonebull Lake as a warning of the harm goblin machinery does to nature's balance."},kw={"mulgore","ravaged caravan","stonebull lake","supervisor fizsprocket","tauren","venture co","venture company","wagon"},rel={"quest:749","quest:764","quest:765","subzone:stonebull-lake","zone:mulgore","topic:venture-company","topic:tauren","topic:horde"},src={"Ravaged Caravan"}}
E["subzone:red-rocks"] = {t="subzone",n="Red Rocks",z="mulgore",s="Red Rocks is a towering sandstone bluff in northeastern Mulgore that serves as a sacred ancestral burial ground for the tauren. Here, the most valiant fallen champions are laid to rest, and young braves travel to its quiet heights to seek the wisdom of their forebears.",h="Red Rocks is a towering sandstone bluff in northeastern Mulgore that serves as a sacred ancestral burial...",sec={{t="Hallowed Stones and Ancestral Graves",b="Rising high above the plains of northeastern Mulgore, the crimson formations of Red Rocks mark a sacred threshold between the living world and the next. Tauren tradition reserves this solemn ground for their most valiant heroes—chieftains, defenders, and champions who gave their lives founding and protecting Thunder Bluff. They are laid to rest here in honor, among the graves of their forebears. It is equally a place of spiritual beginning, where young tauren complete their rites of passage by honoring the ancestral graves and communing with the wind.",sp=0},{t="The Quilboar Desecration",b="The enduring peace of Red Rocks has been shattered by the Bristleback quilboar. Creeping out from their dens, these aggressive scavengers have infested the sandstone ledges and desecrated the ancestral graves with filth. This violation deeply affronts the spiritual traditions of Thunder Bluff. Shaman and lorekeepers now summon aid to cleanse the crags, driving out the Bristlebacks and reclaiming the sanctity of their resting dead.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of Red Rocks?",a="Red Rocks serves as the premier ancestral burial ground and ceremonial rite site for the tauren of Mulgore. Only the most valiant fallen heroes are laid to rest here, and young tauren visit its heights to honor the dead during their trials of adulthood.",al={"why is red rocks important","red rocks purpose","what happens at red rocks"},it="purpose"},{q="Why are there quilboar at Red Rocks?",a="The Bristleback quilboar pushed out from their bramble burrows and overrun the plateau, defiling the burial mounds. Their intrusion is seen by the tauren as an intolerable sacrilege against the resting spirits of their ancestors.",al={"quilboar at red rocks","who is invading red rocks","why are bristlebacks here"},it="threat"},{q="Who is buried at Red Rocks?",a="Burial at Red Rocks is an honor reserved for the most valiant tauren, including great warriors who fought alongside Cairne Bloodhoof to secure Mulgore and build Thunder Bluff, as well as heroes who died serving their tribes.",al={"who is cremated here","red rocks dead","whose graves are these"},it="history"},{q="What ceremonies are performed here?",a="Beyond laying honored heroes to rest, the bluff is a focal point for the Rite of Wisdom, where tauren braves leave offerings and listen for the guidance of ancestral spirits on the wind.",al={"rites at red rocks","tauren rituals red rocks"}},{q="What is Red Rocks?",a="Red Rocks is a prominent formation of red sandstone rising in northeastern Mulgore. It serves the tauren as a revered, sacred ground positioned between the earthly realm of Thunder Bluff and the ancestral realm beyond. Here, tauren youth complete sacred rites to enter adulthood, and the tribes honor their greatest fallen champions.",al={"what is red rocks","about red rocks","red rocks overview"},it="place",add="dabb6ac9"},{q="Is Red Rocks dangerous?",a="While traditionally a quiet and solemn sanctuary, it is currently perilous due to an aggressive incursion of Bristleback quilboar. These invaders have desecrated the burial sites and actively attack anyone who approaches the sacred grounds.",al={"is red rocks dangerous","red rocks danger","enemies at red rocks"},it="danger",add="281a060c"},{q="Why is it called Red Rocks?",a="The site draws its name from its distinctive, naturally occurring cliffs and formations of vivid red sandstone, which distinguish it from the rolling green plains of the surrounding valley.",al={"why is it called red rocks","red rocks name meaning","why red rocks"},it="name",add="63db11a0"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For your people, Red Rocks is sacred soil. The presence of foul quilboar rooting among your honored ancestors' graves is an open outrage that demands cleansing.",["class:Shaman"]="The wind carries the sorrow of unsettled spirits disturbed by quilboar defilement, calling on shamanic rites to restore elemental balance and ancestral peace."},kw={"bristleback","burial ground","earth mother","mulgore","quilboar","red rocks","rite of wisdom","thunder bluff"},rel={"zone:mulgore","npc:lorekeeper-raintotem","quest:833","quest:773","zone:thunderbluff","npc:cairne-bloodhoof","topic:tauren","topic:earth-mother"},src={"Red Rocks"}}
E["subzone:rolling-plains"] = {t="subzone",n="Rolling Plains",z="mulgore",s="The Rolling Plains span the southern expanse of Mulgore, stretching between Bloodhoof Village and the border of the Barrens. These windswept grasslands are home to thriving herds of wildlife, but they are also a point of perpetual vigilance due to prowling centaur raiders and aggressive harpies.",h="The Rolling Plains span the southern expanse of Mulgore, stretching between Bloodhoof Village and the border...",sec={{t="Heart of Southern Mulgore",b="Spanning south from Bloodhoof Village toward the natural earthen gates leading into the Barrens, the Rolling Plains embody the vast open ranges of the tauren homeland. The area teems with wild game essential to the tribes, including prairie wolves, kodo herds, flatland cougars, and swift tallstriders. For young hunters proving their worth to the elders, these sweeping savannahs serve as an indispensable proving ground where tracking, patience, and reverence for nature are put to the test.",sp=0},{t="Encroaching Dangers",b="Despite Mulgore's naturally fortified ring of bluffs, the Rolling Plains remain uniquely exposed to outside threats. It is the only frontier where aggressive centaur outriders have managed to push past defensive perimeters to harass passing travelers and hunting parties. Adding to the peril, a nest of predatory Windfury harpies roosts in the eastern crags, swooping down upon stray game and vulnerable nomadic camps.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What animals can be found on the Rolling Plains?",a="The grasslands support a rich variety of native fauna, including roaming herds of wild kodo, nimble tallstriders, prowling prairie wolves, and stalking flatland cougars. These beasts provide vital meat, hides, and sinew for the tribes of Bloodhoof Village.",al={"rolling plains wildlife","mobs rolling plains","animals mulgore plains"}},{q="Why are centaur found wandering this part of Mulgore?",a="The Rolling Plains border the eastern passes into the Barrens, making them the only region in Mulgore where centaur raiders have managed to breach the perimeter. Marauding outriders constantly scout the plains, probing tauren defenses and threatening isolated hunters.",al={"centaur in mulgore","why centaur rolling plains","centaur outriders"},it="threat"},{q="What other hostile creatures threaten the plains?",a="Aside from roaming centaur scouts, a dangerous enclave of Windfury harpies nests along the eastern crags of the region, preying upon unwary travelers and harassing local wildlife.",al={"harpies rolling plains","dangers rolling plains"},it="threat"},{q="Where do the Rolling Plains begin and end?",a="The plains cover much of southern Mulgore, stretching from Bloodhoof Village to the mountain border adjoining the Barrens.",al={"rolling plains location","where are rolling plains","boundaries rolling plains"},it="place"},{q="What are the Rolling Plains?",a="The Rolling Plains are the expansive, open grasslands stretching across southern Mulgore, reaching from Bloodhoof Village all the way to the border of the Barrens. They serve as rich hunting grounds teeming with wildlife such as kodos, tallstriders, wolves, and cougars, forming the pastoral heartland of tauren territory in southern Mulgore.",al={"what is rolling plains","rolling plains lore","about rolling plains"},it="place",add="711c701d"},{q="Are the Rolling Plains dangerous?",a="While the plains are open hunting grounds, they are not without peril. In addition to prowling cougars and wolves, the region is notable as the only part of Mulgore penetrated by centaur outriders, who roam the open fields. In the east, a nest of aggressive Windfury harpies poses an additional hazard to travelers and hunters alike.",al={"how dangerous are the rolling plains","rolling plains dangers","is rolling plains safe"},it="danger",add="8a6ccc31"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="The Rolling Plains provide vital hunting grounds for the newly unified tribes, but the intrusion of ancient centaur enemies so close to Bloodhoof Village makes guarding this southern corridor a matter of tribal survival.",["class:Hunter"]="With ample herds of kodo, tallstriders, wolves, and cougars, the Rolling Plains offer an ideal wilderness for honing tracking skills, gathering provisions, and taming first animal companions."},kw={"bloodhoof village","centaur","harpies","kodo","mulgore","rolling plains","tallstrider","windfury"},rel={"zone:mulgore","subzone:bloodhoof-village","topic:centaur","zone:barrens"},src={"Rolling Plains"}}
E["subzone:stonebull-lake"] = {t="subzone",n="Stonebull Lake",z="mulgore",s="Stonebull Lake curves around Bloodhoof Village in central Mulgore, giving the tauren fresh water and fish.",h="Stonebull Lake curves around Bloodhoof Village in central Mulgore, giving the tauren fresh water and fish.",sec={{t="Waters of the Plain",b="Stonebull Lake curves around Bloodhoof Village on three sides, shaping the heart of central Mulgore. A wooden bridge crosses its narrowest stretch, carrying the main dirt road between the village and Thunder Bluff on the great mesa to the north. Native fish fill the calm water. Hunters rest on the banks, travelers pause there, and apprentice fishers learn their trade beneath the open sky.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where is Stonebull Lake located?",a="Stonebull Lake lies in central Mulgore, curving around Bloodhoof Village on three sides.",al={"stonebull lake location","where is stonebull lake","mulgore lake"},gp=true},{q="What is the bridge across the lake used for?",a="The bridge crosses the lake's narrowest stretch and carries the main dirt road between Bloodhoof Village and Thunder Bluff to the north.",al={"stonebull bridge","bridge near bloodhoof","road over stonebull lake"},it="purpose"},{q="Why is Stonebull Lake important to the tauren?",a="Stonebull Lake gives Bloodhoof Village fresh water and fish. Its quiet shores also offer young tauren braves in training a place to gather and reflect.",al={"why does stonebull lake matter","purpose of stonebull lake"},it="history"},{q="What is Stonebull Lake?",a="Stonebull Lake is a wide, horseshoe-shaped body of water cradling Bloodhoof Village in central Mulgore. Its cool, fresh waters are renowned across the plains for their abundant fish, providing both a staple food supply and a tranquil haven where tauren often spend quiet hours casting lines from the shore. A sturdy wooden bridge spans its narrowest point, carrying travelers along the main road between Bloodhoof Village and the bluffs to the north.",al={"stonebull lake mulgore","what is stonebull lake","about stonebull lake"},it="place",add="b1753406"},{q="Is Stonebull Lake dangerous?",a="The lake itself is generally peaceful and protected by the proximity of Bloodhoof Village and the tauren braves patrolling nearby. While predators and roaming beasts prowl the broader plains of Mulgore, the waters of Stonebull Lake remain one of the safest fishing and gathering spots in the region, free from any lurking aquatic horrors or hostile settlements along its immediate banks.",al={"is stonebull lake dangerous","threats at stonebull lake","stonebull lake danger"},it="danger",add="641ee038"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren making a home in Mulgore, you find fresh water and sustenance at Stonebull Lake, near your people's capital."},kw={"bloodhoof village","fishing","lake","mulgore","stonebull lake","thunder bluff"},rel={"subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore","zone:thunderbluff"},src={"Stonebull Lake"}}
E["subzone:thunder-bluff"] = {t="subzone",n="Thunder Bluff",z="mulgore",s="On Mulgore’s high mesas, Thunder Bluff shelters the tauren as their capital. Cairne Bloodhoof founded the city after the Third War, bringing a people long scattered across Kalimdor together in a place of refuge and tradition.",h="On Mulgore’s high mesas, Thunder Bluff shelters the tauren as their capital.",sec={{t="A Refuge Upon the Mesas",b="Thunder Bluff rises above Mulgore, where rope bridges link its stone mesas and wooden elevators carry travelers up from the plains. For generations, the tauren wandered across central Kalimdor, pursued by centaur raiding parties. During the Third War, Cairne Bloodhoof joined with Warchief Thrall and the New Horde. Together, they drove back the centaurs and claimed Mulgore’s fertile grasslands. Cairne gathered the scattered tribes and founded the city atop the mesas, where the heights offered shelter and a place for the tauren to trade and gather in worship.",sp=0},{t="Unity and Gathering Mists",b="Cairne Bloodhoof’s followers share Thunder Bluff with tauren who hold to other ways. Magatha Grimtotem and her kin dwell on the Elder Rise, while spirit walkers and shaman seek the ancestors’ will at the Pools of Vision beneath the city. Tribal rivalries persist, yet Thunder Bluff remains a spiritual pillar of Kalimdor’s Horde. Hunters return from the Barrens, artisans travel through, and druids come to commune with the Earth Mother.",sp=0}},faq={{q="How do travelers get up to Thunder Bluff?",a="Travelers ascend to Thunder Bluff by wooden rope and pulley elevators on the southwestern and northeastern bluffs, or fly to the wind rider roost atop the central mesa.",al={"thunder bluff elevator","how to get up to tb","entering thunder bluff"},it="arrival"},{q="Why did the tauren build their city on cliffs?",a="After centuries of centaur raids across the open plains, Cairne Bloodhoof chose the stone mesas for a refuge above Mulgore’s sacred grazing grounds.",al={"why mesas","why build on high bluffs","thunder bluff defense"},it="purpose"},{q="Who rules Thunder Bluff?",a="High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof leads Thunder Bluff. He united the scattered tribes and secured peace in Mulgore, with counsel from tribal leaders and elders across the rises.",al={"leader of thunder bluff","who is the chief","cairne thunder bluff"},it="in_charge"},{q="Are all tauren tribes loyal to Cairne?",a="Most tauren revere Cairne’s wisdom, but some tribes, especially the Grimtotem led by Magatha, doubt the Horde alliance and seek their own course for the tauren people.",al={"grimtotem thunder bluff","tribes of thunder bluff","tauren political rivals"},it="threat"},{q="What is Thunder Bluff?",a="Thunder Bluff is the capital city of the tauren, located atop a network of soaring, windswept mesas in northern Mulgore. Connected by rope bridges and towering elevators, the city serves as a bustling sanctuary of longhouses, tents, and spirit lodges. It represents the first permanent homeland the tauren have claimed after centuries of nomadic wandering.",al={"what is thunder bluff","tell me about thunder bluff","thunder bluff lore"},it="place",add="f3581e0f"},{q="How was Thunder Bluff founded?",a="For generations, the tauren lived as nomadic tribes constantly hunted by marauding centaur. During the Third War, Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof allied with Thrall and the New Horde. Together, they drove back the centaur and secured the lush plains of Mulgore. Upon these towering bluffs, Cairne established the city as a secure refuge to unite the scattered tauren tribes.",al={"thunder bluff history","who founded thunder bluff","origin of thunder bluff"},it="history",add="d7d48e60"},{q="Is Thunder Bluff dangerous?",a="The city itself is a well-defended sanctuary for the Horde, though its sheer cliffs pose a lethal hazard if you misstep near the edges. Beyond the physical drops, internal political tensions linger quietly beneath the peace, as some rival factions like the Grimtotem tribe remain uneasy participants in Cairne's united nation.",al={"is thunder bluff safe","thunder bluff danger","threats in thunder bluff"},it="danger",add="4fba0646"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For the tauren, Thunder Bluff is their first lasting home after generations of wandering, and holds their sacred totems, spirit lodges, and greatest chieftains.",["faction:Horde"]="As a member of the Horde, you have Thunder Bluff as a stronghold in southern Kalimdor, with wisdom and resources alongside Orgrimmar."},kw={"cairne bloodhoof","earth mother","grimtotem","mesas","mulgore","pools of vision","tauren","thunder bluff"},rel={"npc:cairne-bloodhoof","zone:mulgore","topic:tauren","subzone:pools-of-vision","npc:magatha-grimtotem","npc:baine-bloodhoof","topic:horde","zone:barrens"},src={"Thunder Bluff"}}
E["subzone:thunderhorn-water-well"] = {t="subzone",n="Thunderhorn Water Well",z="mulgore",s="At Thunderhorn Water Well, tauren and herds draw water in central Mulgore. The well lies along the path between Bloodhoof Village and Thunder Bluff. The valley’s wells sustain life across the grasslands, and the tauren must keep them clean and defended.",h="At Thunderhorn Water Well, tauren and herds draw water in central Mulgore.",sec={{t="A Life-Giving Spring",b="Thunderhorn Water Well draws from the aquifers beneath central Mulgore. Nomadic tauren and the valley’s settled villages depend on communal wells for water and sustenance. Water is precious across the plains, and control of the wells means control of the land. The Venture Company has repeatedly sought these sources for industrial use, threatening the balance the tribes work to preserve.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where is the Thunderhorn Water Well located?",a="Thunderhorn Water Well lies in central Mulgore, beside the north-south road between Bloodhoof Village and the bluffs of Thunder Bluff.",al={"thunderhorn well location","where is thunderhorn well","thunderhorn water well map"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why is this water well important to the tauren?",a="Mulgore’s wells sustain its herds, pilgrims, and settlements with water from beneath the valley. The tauren regard water as a gift from the Earth Mother and guard it against corruption and greed.",al={"why is thunderhorn well important","tauren water wells meaning","why protect the wells"},it="purpose"},{q="Who has threatened the well?",a="Goblins of the Venture Company tried to seize the well and monopolize the valley’s water for their nearby mining and logging ventures. Local tauren fought back to reclaim and purify it.",al={"who attacked the well","venture co well","enemies at thunderhorn well"},sp=1,it="threat"},{q="What is the Thunderhorn Water Well?",a="The Thunderhorn Water Well is a vital tauren water source set among the rolling grasslands of Mulgore. Situated alongside the northern road connecting Bloodhoof Village to the bluffs of Thunder Bluff, the well provides fresh water crucial for the sustenance of local tribes and travelers crossing the plains.",al={"what is thunderhorn water well","thunderhorn well info","about thunderhorn well"},it="place",add="4280b7a1"},{q="What happened at the Thunderhorn Water Well?",a="Seeking to control the plains' scarce water supply, the opportunistic Venture Company seized the well with their armed workers. Recognizing the dire threat to their homeland, the tauren rallied to drive the interlopers out and reclaim the pristine waters for their people.",al={"thunderhorn well history","what happened at thunderhorn well","venture co thunderhorn"},it="history",add="254832a1"},{q="Is the Thunderhorn Water Well dangerous?",a="While the well belongs to the tauren, the surrounding grasslands are frequented by predatory wildlife and encroaching Venture Company operatives seeking to exploit the land. Travelers walking the road between Bloodhoof Village and Thunder Bluff should keep their guard up.",al={"is thunderhorn well dangerous","thunderhorn well threats","is thunderhorn safe"},it="danger",add="b5bd5549"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren, you depend on wells like Thunderhorn, gifts from the Earth Mother that need guarding against foreign polluters and greedy trespassers.",["class:Shaman"]="As a shaman, you revere Mulgore’s pure springs and protect the water spirits and subterranean currents as a sacred duty."},kw={"bloodhoof","mulgore","thunder bluff","thunderhorn","venture company","water","well"},rel={"zone:mulgore","zone:thunderbluff","subzone:bloodhoof-village","topic:tauren","topic:venture-company"},src={"Thunderhorn Water Well"}}
E["subzone:venture-co-mine"] = {t="subzone",n="Venture Co. Mine",z="mulgore",s="In Mulgore’s eastern cliffs, the Venture Co. Mine cuts into land the tauren refused to surrender. The goblin company digs for minerals without permission, threatening the plains’ natural balance.",h="In Mulgore’s eastern cliffs, the Venture Co.",sec={{t="Unwanted Industry",b="The Venture Company tunnels through Mulgore’s eastern mountain wall despite the tauren’s refusal to grant mining rights. Supervisor Fizsprocket directs the work. Three entrances serve the complex: a ground tunnel guarded by hired muscle, a cliffside overlook, and an upper passage that leads to a small lumber camp. Machines, smoke, and slag heaps scar land the tauren hold sacred.",sp=0},{t="Expanding Ambitions",b="The Venture Company plans to extend its work beyond mining. Mogul Razdunk and the company’s board urged Fizsprocket to begin large-scale timber harvesting, promising advanced goblin shredders to clear the rolling hills. Tauren Outrunners attacked and burned a company supply caravan near Stonebull Lake’s northern shore to delay the expansion. Salvagers soon went to recover its cargo. The tribes now target the mine’s supervisors directly.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Why are goblins mining in Mulgore?",a="The Venture Company wanted to extract minerals from Mulgore, but the tauren refused permission. The cartel disregarded their territorial claims and the land’s balance, sending workers and machines into the eastern cliffs to plunder the hills without authorization.",al={"why are there goblins here","venture co mulgore reason","who runs the mine"},it="why_here"},{q="Who is in charge of this mining site?",a="Supervisor Fizsprocket directs Venture Company operations in Mulgore from his post inside the main entrance. He takes orders from the company’s executive board and Mogul Razdunk.",al={"mine boss","who leads the mine","supervisor fizsprocket"},it="in_charge"},{q="What happened to the supply caravan by the lake?",a="Tauren Outrunners ambushed and burned a Venture Company supply caravan near Stonebull Lake’s northern shore to stop machinery from reaching the mine. Goblin salvage teams soon searched the wreckage for the company’s goods.",al={"ravaged caravan story","stonebull lake caravan","who burned the caravan"},it="conflict"},{q="What else do the goblins plan to build here?",a="Beyond mining ore from the cliffs, the company plans to build a lumber mill and use shredders to clear Mulgore’s trees for profit.",al={"venture co plans","goblin shredders mulgore","lumber camp plans"},it="purpose"},{q="What is the Venture Co. Mine?",a="This is an enormous excavation carved deep into the eastern cliffs of Mulgore. Established without permission by the Venture Company, it serves as an illegal industrial foothold within the tauren homeland. The compound features a main entrance overseen by goblin supervisors, high cliffside passages, and an upper opening leading toward a small logging operation.",al={"venture co mine","what is venture co mine","mulgore mine"},it="place",add="082f8142"},{q="What happened at the Venture Co. Mine?",a="After the tauren flatly denied the Venture Company mining rights in Mulgore, the cartel ignored them and dug in anyway. Tauren Outrunners struck back, raiding and torching a major supply caravan along Stonebull Lake. Enraged by the despoiling of their sacred ancestral lands, the elders of Bloodhoof Village have organized strikes into the mine to drive the trespassers out.",al={"venture co mine history","what happened here","venture co mine lore"},it="history",add="55edfc51"},{q="Is the Venture Co. Mine dangerous?",a="Yes. The tunnels swarm with heavily armed goblin enforcers, laborers, and engineers fiercely protective of their illegal claim. Venture Company guards do not hesitate to shoot trespassers on sight, and navigating the winding, multi-level shafts puts you directly in the crosshairs of their ruthless workforce.",al={"is venture co mine dangerous","venture co mine dangers","venture co hazards"},it="danger",add="a93a58cf"},{q="Why is it called the Venture Co. Mine?",a="It takes its name directly from the notorious goblin syndicate operating it: the Venture Company. Run under the distant directive of Mogul Razdunk, this ruthless enterprise cares nothing for local sovereignty or environmental stewardship, branding their illicit excavation with the company moniker.",al={"why venture co mine","venture co mine name meaning","venture company name"},it="name",add="caf6f18d"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="The mine insults the Earth Mother. Its soot, noise, and scars mark the ancestral valleys the tauren tribes have sworn to protect.",["profession:Engineering"]="Past the mine tunnels and through the higher lumber clearing, Twizwick Sprocketgrind shares mechanical trade secrets away from corporate eyes."},kw={"fizsprocket","goblins","mulgore","ravaged caravan","razdunk","shredders","stonebull lake","venture co mine"},rel={"topic:venture-company","quest:765","quest:764","subzone:ravaged-caravan","zone:mulgore","subzone:stonebull-lake","topic:tauren","topic:goblin"},src={"Venture Co. Mine"}}
E["subzone:wildmane-water-well"] = {t="subzone",n="Wildmane Water Well",z="mulgore",s="At Wildmane Water Well, poisoned waters threaten Mulgore’s northern plains and the herds that depend on them.",h="At Wildmane Water Well, poisoned waters threaten Mulgore’s northern plains and the herds that depend on them.",sec={{t="A Lifeblood Under Siege",b="Across Mulgore, sacred wells sustain the nomadic tauren and their wandering kodo herds. Wildmane Water Well lies at the northern edge of the plains, just beyond Thunder Bluff’s shadow. The Venture Company’s goblins have poisoned its waters to control the regional supply. A tauren-crafted totem is needed for the cleansing ritual that can make the water pure again.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where is the Wildmane Water Well located?",a="Wildmane Water Well lies on Mulgore’s plains, directly north of Thunder Bluff’s mesas. It is the northernmost of the sacred wells that sustain the region.",al={"wildmane well location","where is wildmane well","wildmane water well map"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why is the well important to the tauren?",a="Fresh water sustains life on the plains. The tauren hold the wells sacred because they supply settlements, traveling hunters, and the kodo herds roaming Mulgore.",al={"why do tauren care about the well","wildmane well sacred"},it="purpose"},{q="Who has tried to sabotage or control the well?",a="The Venture Company’s goblins have poisoned Wildmane Water Well to control Mulgore’s water supply. The tauren can cleanse it with a sacred totem.",al={"who attacked the well","wildmane well enemies","who poisoned wildmane well"},it="threat"},{q="What is the Wildmane Water Well?",a="The Wildmane Water Well is the northernmost of the sacred water wells that sustain the tauren of Mulgore, situated just north of the bluffs beneath Thunder Bluff. It provides clean, vital water for the plains and herds, making it an essential lifeline and sacred site for the tribes who have made their home in these ancestral lands.",al={"what is wildmane water well","wildmane water well info","wildmane well mulgore"},it="place",add="6d5f57b0"},{q="What happened at the Wildmane Water Well?",a="The well was seized by the goblins of the Venture Company, who fouled its waters with their poisons in a bid to control Mulgore's water supply. The tauren drove them off and must now cleanse the well with a sacred totem so that clean water can flow again.",al={"wildmane water well history","what happened at wildmane well"},it="history",add="92d8374e"},{q="Is the Wildmane Water Well dangerous?",a="Yes. While the well itself is a peaceful tauren site, its waters have been fouled by the Venture Company's poisons, and prairie wolves and other predators roam the plains around it. Anyone approaching should stay on guard and avoid drinking the tainted water until the well is cleansed.",al={"is wildmane water well dangerous","wildmane well danger","threats at wildmane well"},it="danger",add="d4e90335"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren, you know Mulgore’s sacred wells are gifts from the Earth Mother. Defending Wildmane Water Well from defilement and poison is a sacred duty for every young brave.",["class:Druid"]="As a druid, you know the goblins’ attempts to corrupt Mulgore’s fresh water threaten the plains’ balance. You must remain vigilant and cleanse and heal the land."},kw={"mulgore","sacred well","thunder bluff","venture company","water well","wildmane"},rel={"zone:mulgore","zone:thunderbluff","topic:tauren","topic:venture-company"},src={"Wildmane Water Well"}}
E["subzone:windfury-ridge"] = {t="subzone",n="Windfury Ridge",z="mulgore",s="Windfury Ridge looms over Mulgore’s pastures, where harpies nest among wolves and cougars. Its slopes threaten travelers, while tauren hunters prize the harpies’ feathers.",h="Windfury Ridge looms over Mulgore’s pastures, where harpies nest among wolves and cougars.",sec={{t="A Perilous Perch",b="Windfury Ridge rises above Mulgore’s plains, where Windfury harpies nest among the crags. They swoop on wanderers who pass too close to the hills. Prairie wolves and mountain cougars prowl the rocky slopes, making this border of the tauren heartland dangerous. Tauren warriors hunt the harpies, whose iridescent feathers are sought in Thunder Bluff for ceremonial garments worn at feasts and weddings.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What makes Windfury Ridge dangerous?",a="Windfury Ridge is dangerous because prairie wolves and cougars prowl its slopes, while harpies nest along the heights. Harpies dive from the crags at intruders who come near their nests.",al={"why is windfury ridge dangerous","hazards of windfury ridge","windfury ridge monsters"},it="danger"},{q="Why do the tauren hunt the harpies on Windfury Ridge?",a="Tauren hunt Windfury harpies to defend the borders and gather their brilliant feathers. The plumes are woven into ceremonial headdresses and decorations for feasts and weddings.",al={"why kill windfury harpies","harpy feathers mulgore","hunting harpies"},it="why_wanted"},{q="Where is Windfury Ridge located?",a="Windfury Ridge lies on elevated bluffs in northern Mulgore, beyond Thunder Bluff.",al={"windfury ridge location","where is windfury ridge","windfury ridge map"},it="place",gp=true},{q="What is Windfury Ridge?",a="Windfury Ridge is a rugged highland nestled within the hills of Mulgore. Known primarily as the roost of fierce harpies, it is a perilous stretch of high ground frequented by deadly predators and bypassed by cautious travelers.",al={"what is windfury ridge","windfury ridge info","about windfury ridge"},it="place",add="d25e422a"},{q="Who lives in Windfury Ridge?",a="The ridge is claimed and fiercely defended by the Windfury harpies, who build their nests among the crags. Aside from these winged scavengers, prowling cougars and wild wolves stalk the slopes, preying upon anything wandering too close to the hills.",al={"who lives at windfury ridge","windfury ridge inhabitants","windfury ridge creatures"},it="residents",add="52a38b39"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren, you know the ridge as a recurring nuisance to young hunters and a source of ceremonial ornaments for marriages and seasonal gatherings.",["class:Hunter"]="As a hunter, you can use the steep slopes and cunning predators to practice tracking, beast taming, and range management."},kw={"cougars","feathers","harpies","mulgore","thunder bluff","windfury","windfury ridge"},rel={"zone:mulgore","zone:thunderbluff","topic:tauren"},src={"Windfury Ridge"}}
E["subzone:winterhoof-water-well"] = {t="subzone",n="Winterhoof Water Well",z="mulgore",s="At Winterhoof Water Well, the Venture Company threatens Mulgore's water supply. The well lies south of Bloodhoof Village, at the edge of the Rolling Plains, where tauren tribes and roaming herds depend on its fresh water.",h="At Winterhoof Water Well, the Venture Company threatens Mulgore's water supply.",sec={{t="A Threatened Lifeline",b="Water from deep wells sustains Mulgore's herds and tauren encampments. Winterhoof Water Well draws from aquifers beneath the Rolling Plains and bears the name of one of the plains' respected tribes. Now the Venture Company has targeted this vital source. The goblin cartel has tried to poison the water and gain control over the surrounding territory.",sp=0},{t="Purification and Defense",b="Mull Thunderhorn has made sacred totems to counter the Venture Company's toxins and restore the well's balance. The tauren's young braves must help cleanse it. For the tauren, protecting the well is both a matter of survival and a duty to keep the earth and its waters free from corruption.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Where is the Winterhoof Water Well located?",a="Winterhoof Water Well lies in southern Mulgore, south of Bloodhoof Village at the edge of the Rolling Plains.",al={"winterhoof well location","where is winterhoof well","find winterhoof water well"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Who poisoned the Winterhoof Water Well?",a="The Venture Company poisoned Winterhoof Water Well to control local resources and weaken the tauren.",al={"who poisoned winterhoof well","why is the well poisoned","who ruined the water"},it="threat"},{q="How can the poisoned well be cleansed?",a="Mull Thunderhorn crafted an enchanted totem whose primal magic purges the Venture Company's toxins from the water.",al={"cleanse winterhoof well","fix the well","winterhoof cleansing"},it="purpose",gp=true},{q="Why are water wells so sacred to the tauren?",a="Tauren rely on water for their tribes and roaming herds, and see it as a gift from the Earth Mother. Poisoning it threatens their lives and offends their faith.",al={"why do tauren care about wells","tauren well lore"},it="ties"},{q="What is the Winterhoof Water Well?",a="The Winterhoof Water Well is one of several vital sources of fresh water carved into the earth of Mulgore, situated along the edge of the southern Rolling Plains. For the nomadic tauren and the local wildlife, these wells provide life across the arid grasslands. However, its tranquil presence has recently drawn the unwanted attention of ruthless exploiters looking to turn the land's natural resources into profit.",al={"winterhoof water well overview","what is winterhoof well","about winterhoof well"},it="place",add="8e6c4fef"},{q="What happened at the Winterhoof Water Well?",a="Greedy operatives from the Venture Company seized the well in an underhanded effort to gain control of the local water supply. In the process, they fouled and poisoned its depths, threatening the health of the plains and the herds that depend upon it. Tauren elders and shamans have since called upon brave wanderers to drive out the defilers and use consecrated totems to restore the water's natural purity.",al={"winterhoof well history","what happened at winterhoof well","winterhoof well events"},it="history",add="a848edd2"},{q="Is the Winterhoof Water Well dangerous?",a="Yes. Beyond the toxic corruption seeping into the groundwater, the well site is overrun by armed hirelings of the Venture Company. These ruthless intruders are fiercely protective of their stake in the land and will violently attack any tauren or Horde traveler who approaches to investigate or cleanse the site.",al={"winterhoof well danger","is winterhoof well safe","winterhoof well threats"},it="danger",add="897a0f7c"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As a tauren, defending and purifying ancestral water wells is among the first trials young braves face as they protect the land from outside exploitation.",["class:Shaman"]="As a shaman, cleansing the well calls for elemental harmony and shows how totems can heal wounded water spirits."},kw={"cleansing","mulgore","mull thunderhorn","rolling plains","totem","venture company","water well","winterhoof"},rel={"npc:mull-thunderhorn","quest:748","quest:754","quest:756","subzone:rolling-plains","zone:mulgore","topic:venture-company","topic:tauren"},src={"Winterhoof Water Well"}}
E["zone:mulgore"] = {t="zone",n="Mulgore",z="mulgore",s="Mulgore shelters the tauren in a valley beneath the Stonetalon Mountains. High natural walls surround its emerald grasslands, plateaus, and plains. After Cairne Bloodhoof joined Thrall's Horde and drove out the centaur, the united tribes made Mulgore their homeland, with Thunder Bluff as their spiritual and territorial heart.",h="Mulgore shelters the tauren in a valley beneath the Stonetalon Mountains.",sec={{t="A Land Reclaimed",b="The tauren have reclaimed a home after generations on the move. Centaur warbands hunted and displaced them across the central plains until High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof met Thrall and his displaced orcs. The tauren and orcs shared ideals of honor, shamanism, and strength, and the tauren joined the new Horde. With orcish help, Cairne broke the centaur hold on the region and led his people into Mulgore. They built permanent settlements here for the first time in centuries, and raised their capital, Thunder Bluff, on the highest northern mesas.",sp=0},{t="The Trials of the Earthmother",b="Young tauren face the Great Hunt and honor the Earthmother as they reach maturity. Their training begins at Camp Narache on the southern plateau of Red Cloud Mesa, under Chief Hawkwind. From there, they descend into the Golden Plains and Bloodhoof Village to undertake the Rites of the Earthmother. They learn to commune with ancestral spirits, forage native herbs, and respect the kodos and tallstriders whose herds sustain tauren life.",sp=0},{t="Encroaching Shadows",b="Mulgore's quiet plains face threats from within and beyond its borders. Displaced quilboar have settled in Brambleblade Ravine and defiled the sacred burial grounds of Red Rocks. Windfury harpies hunt wild game in the mountains, while Palemane gnolls strike from hidden caves. Goblins of the Venture Company foul sacred water wells and quarry the eastern hills. Dwarven explorers from Bael Modan have also opened the Bael'dun Digsite to unearth ancient titan artifacts, despite tauren protests.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why did the tauren settle permanently in Mulgore?",a="The tauren settled in Mulgore after joining forces with Thrall's Horde. Centaur marauders had long pursued the nomadic people across Kalimdor, but Cairne Bloodhoof gained the strength to drive them away and reclaim the ancestral valley. The tauren built permanent settlements, including Bloodhoof Village and Thunder Bluff, to give all tribes a unified homeland where they could thrive in peace.",al={"why do tauren live here","tauren homeland history","why settle thunder bluff"},it="history"},{q="How do you leave Mulgore on foot?",a="You can leave Mulgore on foot through the single pass in its southeastern mountain wall. Follow the main dirt highway east past Bloodhoof Village and Morin Cloudstalker's post, through the mountain corridor and into the arid plains of the Barrens.",al={"mulgore exit","how to get to barrens from mulgore","mulgore road out"},gp=true},{q="Who are the Venture Company and why are they here?",a="The Venture Company is a goblin-run commercial cartel seeking Kalimdor's natural riches. In Mulgore, it mines in the eastern mountains and has occupied the vital Thunderhorn, Wildmane, and Winterhoof water wells, defiling the land and threatening the regional water supply.",al={"venture co in mulgore","why are there goblins here","venture co mine"},it="threat"},{q="Why are dwarves digging in tauren territory?",a="Dwarves from the Bael Modan expedition dig at the Bael'dun Digsite in Mulgore's western foothills. The Explorers' League sent them to unearth relics of their Titan creators. They excavate tauren land without permission, despite protests that their work scars the Earthmother.",al={"baeldun digsite","why are dwarves in mulgore","bael dun"},it="threat"},{q="What are the Rites of the Earthmother?",a="The Rites of the Earthmother are sacred trials that young tauren undertake upon reaching adulthood. They test hunting, tracking, vision communion, and knowledge of the natural balance, proving the initiate's devotion to the land and ancestral traditions.",al={"rites of earthmother","tauren maturity quest","rite of the earthmother"},it="purpose"},{q="Who rules Mulgore?",a="High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof rules all tauren lands from Thunder Bluff atop the northern mesas. His son, Baine Bloodhoof, governs Bloodhoof Village and tends to the lower plains' daily concerns under Cairne's wisdom.",al={"mulgore leader","who is the chief of mulgore","cairne bloodhoof leader"},it="in_charge"},{q="What is Mulgore?",a="Mulgore is the verdant, sheltered ancestral homeland of the tauren, nestled within a ring of protective mountains in central Kalimdor. Its rolling golden grasslands and towering windswept mesas offer prime hunting grounds and a peaceful haven, accessible on foot only through a single mountain pass to the east leading into the Barrens.",al={"what is this zone","tell me about mulgore","mulgore overview"},it="place",add="145a41be",nx={8}},{q="Is Mulgore dangerous?",a="While Mulgore is far more peaceful than the Barrens, it faces mounting troubles. Aggressive Bristleback quilboar encroach on sacred grounds, Palemane gnolls lurk in southern caves, and Windfury harpies roost along the ridges. Furthermore, the Venture Company exploits pristine water wells and mountainsides, while dwarven excavators desecrate ancestral lands to the west.",al={"is mulgore safe","threats in mulgore","danger here"},it="danger",add="bf98c20a"},{q="Who are the tauren and how did they end up in Mulgore?",a="The tauren, or shu'halo, are an ancient, nomadic people devoted to the Earth Mother. For generations they wandered the Barrens, endlessly hunted and hounded toward extinction by marauding centaur tribes. After forging a bond of honor with Thrall and the newly arrived orcs during the Third War, High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof led his united people back into their ancestral valley of Mulgore to claim a lasting home.",al={"where did tauren come from","tauren history mulgore","how did tauren get here"},it="arrival",add="148e56bf"},{q="Who is Cairne Bloodhoof?",a="Cairne Bloodhoof is the venerable High Chieftain of the united tauren tribes, leader of the Bloodhoof, and ruler of Thunder Bluff. A peerless warrior and wise elder, Cairne allied his people with Thrall's Horde and guided them through perilous centaur wars to found a lasting sanctuary atop the mesas.",al={"cairne bloodhoof lore","who is the high chieftain","tauren leader"},it="leader",add="dd86ecd9",nx={11}},{q="Is Baine his son?",a="Yes, Baine Bloodhoof is the son of Cairne. Known for his martial courage and diplomatic restraint, Baine oversees Bloodhoof Village in central Mulgore, serving as chieftain of the settlement while his father prepares him to eventually inherit the mantle of high chieftain.",al={"is baine cairne's son","baine bloodhoof relationship","baine bloodhoof"},it="ties",add="96a44cf3"},{q="What is Bloodhoof Village?",a="Bloodhoof Village is a major settlement situated beside the clear waters of Stonebull Lake in central Mulgore. Named in honor of Cairne's tribe and governed by his son Baine, it serves as the primary trade, crafting, and staging hub where young hunters and braves gather after journeying down from Red Cloud Mesa.",al={"bloodhoof village lore","what is bloodhoof","village in mulgore"},it="place",add="24d564cd"},{q="What is Thunder Bluff?",a="Thunder Bluff is the magnificent capital of the tauren nation, constructed high atop a series of windswept, defensible mesas towering over northern Mulgore. Founded by Cairne Bloodhoof following the Third War, its raised plateaus and rope bridges shelter tauren of all tribes and provide a safe bastion against centaur invaders.",al={"thunder bluff lore","tauren capital city","city on the mesas"},it="place",add="dbff83eb"},{q="What are the Palemane gnolls doing in Mulgore?",a="The Palemane tribe of gnolls has carved out dens around Palemane Rock and scattered camps along Mulgore's southern mountains. While tauren philosophy values sharing the Earth Mother's bounty, these aggressive gnolls harass travelers, raid nearby hunting grounds, and attack any braves passing near their territory.",al={"palemane gnolls mulgore","why are gnolls here","palemane rock"},it="threat",add="69b40be7"},{q="How did I end up in Mulgore?",a="If you are a tauren, you were raised here among the nomadic tribes recently united under High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof. Following the Third War, your people settled these verdant ancestral plains to escape the centaur and build a permanent homeland, beginning your training on the southern plateau of Red Cloud Mesa. Travelers from other races arrive through the lone, fortified mountain pass leading from the eastern Barrens, or by hitching passage on a wyvern flight directly up to the sprawling mesa city of Thunder Bluff.",al={"how did i get here","why am i in mulgore","how did i get to mulgore"},it="arrival",add="7f5f34f0"},{q="What am I supposed to be doing in Mulgore?",a="Young tauren must undertake the Rites of the Earthmother to prove their worth, honor ancestral traditions, and learn the ways of the land. Beyond your rites, Mulgore faces encroaching perils that threaten its peace: aggressive Bristleback quilboar swarm the eastern ravines, Palemane gnolls lurk in the hills, and the goblin-run Venture Company defiles sacred water wells and tears into the mountains for ore. Whether answering the call of Chief Hawkwind or aiding Baine Bloodhoof at Bloodhoof Village, your duty is to protect the tribes and defend the new homeland.",al={"what do i do here","what should i be doing here","what is my goal in mulgore"},it="purpose",add="a8ac99e7"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="This valley is your ancestral home, reclaimed through sacrifice and unity. You must preserve its wells and cleanse its sacred grounds in service to the Earthmother.",["faction:Horde"]="Mulgore is the heart of Horde spirituality and agriculture in central Kalimdor. A blood oath of mutual defense binds it to Thrall's people.",["class:Druid"]="Mulgore's rolling plains and untouched groves are among Kalimdor's purest natural sanctuaries. Here, tauren druids study balance and nature's gifts.",["class:Hunter"]="The Great Hunt thrives across Mulgore's plains, where cougars, wolves, and wild kodo test your skill and patience as a tracker."},kw={"bael'dun","baine bloodhoof","bloodhoof village","cairne bloodhoof","camp narache","earthmother","kodo","mulgore","red cloud mesa","red rocks","thunder bluff","venture co"},rel={"zone:thunderbluff","zone:barrens","npc:cairne-bloodhoof","npc:baine-bloodhoof","subzone:bloodhoof-village","subzone:camp-narache","quest:763","subzone:bael-dun-digsite"},m={levels="1-10"},src={"Mulgore (Classic)","Mulgore"}}
